/* The in-app Help Guide. Generated by docs/guide/build.js from docs/guide/*.html: edit those, not this file. */
window.HELP_GUIDE = {"version":"7.10.15","groups":["Start here","Connect the app to your phone","The tabs","Settings and looks","How do I...?","Safety and help"],"sections":[{"id":"welcome","title":"Welcome","group":"Start here","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">ADB Application Manager Pro shows you every app on your Android phone and lets you take charge of them: freeze the ones you never use, remove the ones the phone maker pre-installed, install new ones, and keep backups. It runs on the phone itself, with no account and no ads.</p>\n\n<h4>What the app is</h4>\n<p>Android is careful about one app changing another. The phone's own Settings app can turn off some apps, but it hides many of the apps that run in the background, it works on one app at a time, and it will not let you turn off many of the apps your phone maker added. Developers get around this with a tool called <b>ADB</b> (Android Debug Bridge), normally used from a computer. This app brings that kind of power onto the phone, so for everyday use you do not need a computer.</p>\n<p>To get that power, the app connects to your phone in one of a few ways, called <b>working modes</b>. You pick one, and the app shows its name in a badge at the top right of the screen. <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes: what they are and which to pick</a> explains each one and helps you choose. Until a working mode is set up, the app is in <b>Read-Only</b> mode: you can look at everything and change nothing (see <a href=\"#mode-readonly\">Auto mode and Read-Only mode</a>).</p>\n<p>The title at the top of the screen reads \"Full ADB/Root System Manager\" (on two lines). The name under the icon on your home screen, and in Android's list of apps, is \"ADB Application Manager Pro\". They are the same app.</p>\n\n<h4>What it can do</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>List every app on the phone, including system apps and ones that were already removed, with search, sort and filters (<a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>).</li>\n<li>Freeze (disable) an app, suspend it, stop it, clear its data, uninstall it for your user, or bring a removed system app back. One app at a time, or many together (<a href=\"#apps-menu\">an app's menu</a>, <a href=\"#apps-batch\">batch actions</a>).</li>\n<li>Suggest which pre-installed apps are safe to remove, using the community UAD-NG list (Universal Android Debloater Next Generation), with a review step before anything runs and an Undo afterwards (<a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a>).</li>\n<li>Install .apk files (the files that Android apps are installed from) and the split-file bundles (.apks, .apkm, .xapk) (<a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a>).</li>\n<li>Back up an app, and save \"profiles\" that remember which apps you turned off (<a href=\"#apps-backup\">backups</a>, <a href=\"#apps-profiles\">profiles</a>).</li>\n<li>Look inside an app: its permissions, its app ops, its components and its decoded manifest.</li>\n<li>Browse and manage files, and open zip, 7z, rar and tar archives without unpacking them (<a href=\"#tab-files\">File Manager</a>).</li>\n<li>Run a terminal, with optional AI coding helpers that ask your permission before they run anything (<a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a>).</li>\n<li>Change Android's hidden settings and recolour the system (<a href=\"#tab-settings\">Hidden Settings</a>, <a href=\"#tab-overlays\">RRO/Monet Customization</a>).</li>\n<li>Check for app updates, browse app catalogs, read the device log, watch running processes, and talk to a Wear OS watch (<a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a>, <a href=\"#tab-store\">App Stores</a>, <a href=\"#tab-logcat\">Logcat Viewer</a>, <a href=\"#tab-taskmgr\">Task Manager</a>, <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>What it cannot do</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It cannot change other apps until a working mode is on. Without one it is a viewer.</li>\n<li>It cannot root your phone or unlock it. If you want <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>, the phone must already be rooted (given full administrator access, which you do with other tools).</li>\n<li>It cannot delete a system app from the phone for good. \"Uninstall\" on a system app removes it for your user only; the file stays in the system area, and a factory reset (and sometimes a system update) brings the app back.</li>\n<li>It cannot override the phone maker's locks. A few protected apps refuse to be disabled or removed even in a working mode, and Android's own error message is shown when that happens.</li>\n<li>It does not update Google Play apps itself. Those keep updating through the Play Store or Aurora Store (another app for downloading Play apps) (<a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a>).</li>\n<li>It cannot read other apps' private data, except with Root, which is what lets it back up an app's data (<a href=\"#apps-backup\">Backups</a>).</li>\n<li>It cannot make a risky change safe. It warns you and offers Undo wherever it can, but you decide. Read <a href=\"#safety\">Safety</a> before you remove system apps.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Who it is for</h4>\n<p>It is for anyone who wants to tidy their phone: people who are tired of apps they never chose, who want to stop an app running in the background, or who want a second opinion on what an app is allowed to do. You do not have to know what ADB, Shizuku or root mean. This guide explains every word the first time it comes up, and the <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a> topic collects them in one place, with a longer <a href=\"#glossary\">glossary</a> at the end.</p>\n<p>Be honest with yourself about one thing: the app is powerful. It is possible to turn off something your phone needs. Almost everything the app does can be undone, freezing most of all, and the guide marks the exceptions. Work in small steps, one change at a time.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Android 8.0 or newer.</b> The app will not install on older versions.</li>\n<li><b>An ARM phone</b>, which is nearly every phone. There are three downloads; <a href=\"#install-and-update\">Installing and updating the app</a> says which one to take.</li>\n<li><b>A working mode</b>, for anything beyond looking. You do not need a computer if the phone runs Android 11 or newer (use Wireless Debugging, Android's way of connecting over Wi-Fi, which needs no cable) or if it is rooted (use Root). On Android 8 to 10 without root, you need a computer to switch on ADB over TCP (a setting that lets the app talk to the phone's own ADB service), and you must repeat that after every restart of the phone. <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a> lays this out in a table.</li>\n<li><b>Internet</b>, but only when you open something that needs it: the debloat list, update checks, app catalogs, the app's own update, and a VirusTotal lookup if you add your own key. Nothing is sent to a server of ours, and there is no account (<a href=\"#privacy\">Privacy</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>A map of this guide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Start here</b> (this group): the welcome, a five-minute <a href=\"#quick-start\">Quick start</a>, <a href=\"#install-and-update\">installing and updating</a>, <a href=\"#basics\">the words you will see</a> and <a href=\"#screen-tour\">a tour of the screen</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Connect the app to your phone</b>: every working mode, step by step, starting with <a href=\"#modes-overview\">the overview</a>, then <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>, <a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP</a>, <a href=\"#mode-shizuku\">Shizuku</a>, <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>, <a href=\"#mode-readonly\">Auto and Read-Only</a>, and <a href=\"#app-permissions\">the permissions the app asks for</a>.</li>\n<li><b>The tabs</b>: one topic for each tab in the tab bar, in the same order, beginning with <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Settings and looks</b>: <a href=\"#prefs\">Settings</a>, <a href=\"#feature-list\">turning tabs on and off</a> and <a href=\"#themes\">themes</a>.</li>\n<li><b>How do I...?</b>: short recipes for common jobs, each pointing into the topics above.</li>\n<li><b>Safety and help</b>: <a href=\"#safety\">safety</a>, <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">troubleshooting</a>, <a href=\"#faq\">questions and answers</a>, the <a href=\"#glossary\">glossary</a>, <a href=\"#privacy\">privacy</a> and <a href=\"#credits\">credits</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>How to use this guide</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li><b>Open it.</b> Go to the <b>About</b> tab (the last tab) and tap <b>Help Guide</b>, one of the buttons in the card at the top, between <b>GitHub</b> and <b>What's new</b>. A tall sheet slides up. The first time, the guide takes a moment to load; after that it opens at once, and if you close it and open it again it carries on where you stopped.</li>\n<li><b>Find a topic.</b> At the top of the sheet is a table of contents, grouped like the map above. Tap a topic to jump to it. The <b>Contents</b> button in the top bar, and the <b>Back to the contents</b> link at the end of every topic, bring you back to this list.</li>\n<li><b>Search.</b> Type in the box at the top, for example \"freeze\", \"pairing code\" or \"density\". Only the topics that contain every word you typed stay on the screen, and the words are highlighted when twelve topics or fewer match. Clear the box to get the whole guide back.</li>\n<li><b>Follow links.</b> Underlined words jump to another topic. When one does, a <b>Back to where I was</b> button appears at the bottom for about fifteen seconds; tap it to return to the place you left.</li>\n<li><b>Close it.</b> Tap the X at the top right, tap the dim area outside the sheet, pull the little bar at the top of the sheet down, or use your phone's Back button or gesture.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> You do not always have to go through About. Most tabs, and Settings (the gear), have a small round <b>?</b> button in the top right corner of their first card, and it opens this guide straight at that tab's topic (<a href=\"#screen-tour\">A tour of the screen</a> says which tabs do not).</div>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Button and tab names are written in bold exactly as the English app spells them, and commands and file names look like <code>this</code>. The app itself can be shown in many languages (Settings, Language), but this guide is English only, so if you changed the language, the names you see on screen will differ from the names here.</div>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> If you are new to all of this, read <a href=\"#quick-start\">Quick start</a> next. It takes you from the first launch to one safe, reversible change.</div>"},{"id":"quick-start","title":"Quick start in five minutes","group":"Start here","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">From the first launch to your first safe action: set the app up, look around, freeze one app you do not need, and bring it back. You will know where Undo is before you ever need it.</p>\n\n<h4>Before you start</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The app must be installed. If it is not, see <a href=\"#install-and-update\">Installing and updating the app</a>.</li>\n<li>Steps 1 to 3, 6 and 8 need no working mode and change nothing on your phone. Step 4 sets a working mode up, and steps 5 and 7 need it. If you cannot set one up right now, stop after step 3 (or go on to step 6); you have lost nothing.</li>\n<li>Allow ten minutes the first time, mostly for setting up the working mode. Wireless Debugging in particular has several steps the first time and far fewer after that.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Step by step</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li><b>Open the app.</b> It starts on the <b>Application Manager</b> tab. For a moment the list says \"Querying system packages...\", then every app appears. On a first launch Android may also show its own question about notifications (Android 13 and newer) or about storage (Android 10 and older). Notifications are only used for the pairing-code box, for progress of long file jobs and for one reminder after a system update, so either answer is fine for now. See <a href=\"#app-permissions\">The permissions this app asks for</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Meet the permissions sheet.</b> (Every sheet like it has a small bar at the top: pull it <b>up</b> to give the sheet more room, pull it <b>down</b> to make it smaller again and, once it is small, to close it.) A couple of seconds later a sheet (a panel that slides up over the screen) called <b>Allow a few permissions</b> appears. It lists <b>All files access</b>, <b>Usage access</b>, <b>Display over other apps</b> and <b>Install unknown apps</b>, each with an <b>Allow</b> button and a one-line reason. All are optional. <b>Allow all</b> takes you through the Android screen for each one; <b>Not now</b> skips them. For a first run, <b>Not now</b> is fine. The same sheet is under <b>About</b>, then <b>Permissions</b>, whenever you want it. If another sheet is open when the permissions sheet is due, it waits until that one is closed.</li>\n<li><b>Look at the list.</b> Near the top is an orange banner, \"Active Mode: Read-Only (Unprivileged)\", with a <b>Set Up</b> button and an X in its top right corner (the X puts the banner away until the app is started again, or until Read-Only mode comes back). It means the app can only look for now. Below it are boxes that count your apps (<b>Total Installed</b>, <b>Running</b>, <b>Bloatware (Uninstalled)</b>, <b>Enabled</b>, <b>Frozen</b>, <b>3rd Party</b>, <b>System</b>), a <b>Sort</b> box, a row of filter pills that scrolls sideways, a search box, and then the apps. Try three things: type a few letters in the search box; tap the <b>3rd Party</b> pill to show only apps you installed yourself; tap it again to turn the filter off. Each row has a coloured tag such as <b>USER</b> or <b>SYSTEM</b>. Nothing you have done so far changes any app.</li>\n<li><b>Pick a working mode.</b> Tap the badge at the top right, which now reads \"Setup Mode (Read-Only)\" in orange. (The <b>Set Up</b> button on the banner opens the same sheet.) The sheet <b>Working Modes &amp; ADB Setup</b> slides up with a card for each mode (below a first card about the app's own ADB key). Choose by phone:\n<ul>\n<li>Your phone is rooted: use <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>.</li>\n<li>Not rooted, Android 11 or newer: use <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>. No computer is needed.</li>\n<li>Not rooted, Android 8 to 10: use <a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP</a>, which needs a computer once, or <a href=\"#mode-shizuku\">Shizuku</a> if you already use it.</li>\n</ul>\nNot sure? <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes: what they are and which to pick</a> has a short decision list.</li>\n<li><b>Check that it worked.</b> Close the sheet. The badge turns green (violet for Root) and names the mode, for example \"Wireless ADB (37099)\" or \"ADB TCP (5555)\". The orange banner on the Application Manager tab disappears. If it did not, open <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Look inside an app (no change).</b> On any row, tap the three-dots button (<b>&#8943;</b>) at the right end, or press and hold the row for about half a second. A sheet slides up with the app's name, its package name (its unique ID, explained in <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>) and its version, a grid of buttons (<b>Launch</b>, <b>Force Stop</b>, <b>Freeze</b>, <b>Suspend</b>, <b>Clear Data</b>, <b>Uninstall</b>, <b>Rem Updates</b>, <b>App Info</b>, <b>Extract APK</b>, <b>Backup</b>, <b>Optimize</b>, <b>Logs</b>) and below them a row of tabs (<b>Permissions</b>, <b>App Ops</b>, <b>Activities</b>, <b>Components</b>, <b>Manifest</b>, <b>Raw</b>, <b>Features</b>, <b>Configurations</b>, <b>Signatures</b> and <b>Libraries</b>; swipe the row sideways to reach the last ones). Tap through the tabs only; with a working mode on, the grid buttons act (most at once; <b>Uninstall</b>, <b>Clear Data</b> and <b>Rem Updates</b> ask you to confirm first), so leave them for step 7. Inside the tabs, a small lock on a button means \"needs a working mode\". The grid buttons carry no lock: in Read-Only mode, the ones that change something open a sheet called <b>Working Mode Required</b> instead (see step 7). Close the sheet with the X, by tapping outside it, or with Back. Nothing has changed.</li>\n<li><b>Freeze one app, then bring it back.</b> Pick an app you installed yourself, do not need, and recognise: an old game is ideal. Use the <b>3rd Party</b> pill to find it. Open its menu (step 6) and tap <b>Freeze</b>.\n<ul>\n<li>A short message at the bottom says \"Checking the phone&hellip;\" and then what it found, for example \"Alpha: Frozen\". The <b>Freeze</b> button turns into <b>Enable</b>.</li>\n<li>After a moment the row gets a <b>FROZEN</b> tag, and the app disappears from your home screen and app drawer. Tap the <b>Frozen</b> pill to see it listed.</li>\n<li>Now tap <b>Enable</b>. The <b>FROZEN</b> tag goes and the app is back, exactly as it was. Nothing was deleted at any point.</li>\n</ul>\nIf you are still in Read-Only mode, tapping <b>Freeze</b> opens a sheet called <b>Working Mode Required</b> instead, with a <b>Set Up Working Mode</b> button. That is the app protecting you, not an error.</li>\n<li><b>Know where Undo is.</b> See the list below. You now have all you need to start using the app.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> For your first changes, choose an app marked <b>USER</b> that you recognise. Do not freeze anything marked <b>SYSTEM</b> yet, and never your keyboard, your home screen (launcher), the phone or messages app, Settings, or System UI (the part of Android that draws the status bar and the pull-down panel). Leave <b>Clear Data</b> and <b>Uninstall</b> alone until you have read <a href=\"#apps-menu\">An app's menu</a> and <a href=\"#safety\">Safety</a>: clearing data cannot be undone.</div>\n\n<h4>Where to find Undo</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Right where you did it.</b> In the app's menu, <b>Freeze</b> turns into <b>Enable</b>, <b>Suspend</b> into <b>Unsuspend</b>, and <b>Uninstall</b> into <b>Reinstall</b> (that last one brings back a system app you removed for your user).</li>\n<li><b>History.</b> In the <b>UAD-NG Debloater</b> tab, the <b>History</b> button opens a list called <b>Debloat History</b>. It records every Freeze, Enable, Suspend, Unsuspend, Uninstall and Reinstall done from the Debloater, from an app's menu and from batch actions, with an <b>Undo</b> button on each entry. See <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloater safety</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Backups and profiles.</b> The <b>Backups</b> and <b>Profiles</b> buttons above the list keep copies of apps and of which apps you turned off (<a href=\"#apps-backup\">Backups</a>, <a href=\"#apps-profiles\">Profiles</a>).</li>\n<li><b>Settings changes</b> made in Hidden Settings and in the Overlays tab have their own Undo, described in those tabs.</li>\n<li><b>The last resort</b> for a system app removed for your user is a factory reset, which restores it. Back up what you care about first.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Freezing is the gentlest change: the app keeps all its files and settings, it just cannot run. When you are unsure, freeze instead of uninstalling. You can always uninstall later, after a week of living without the app.</div>\n\n<h4>What next</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Learn the screen: <a href=\"#screen-tour\">A tour of the screen</a> and <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>.</li>\n<li>Tidy up many apps safely: <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a> and <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloater safety</a>.</li>\n<li>Install an app from a file: <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a>.</li>\n<li>Make the connection more convenient: <a href=\"#quick-tiles\">Quick Settings tiles and widget</a> switch the working mode without opening the app.</li>\n<li>Something did not work: <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"install-and-update","title":"Installing and updating the app","group":"Start here","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The app comes as a single APK file from its GitHub releases page, in three flavours for different phone processors. Install it from your browser, and later update it by installing the next version over it, or from inside the app.</p>\n\n<h4>Before you start</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>You need Android 8.0 or newer and some free space on the phone.</li>\n<li>The app is not on Google Play. It is downloaded from <a href=\"https://github.com/Bingblop/ADB-Application-Manager/releases/latest\">its releases page on GitHub</a>.</li>\n<li>Take the download only from that page. Every official release is signed with the same key, which is how you can trust an update, and why a copy from anywhere else is a risk.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>The three downloads</h4>\n<p>Each release has three APK files, whose names end in <code>arm64-v8a.apk</code>, <code>armeabi-v7a.apk</code> or <code>universal.apk</code>, plus a small file called <code>SHA256SUMS.txt</code> that lists a checksum (a fingerprint) for each APK. The file names look like <code>ADB_Application_Manager_Pro-v(version)-arm64-v8a.apk</code>, where (version) is the release number.</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\"><thead><tr><th>Name ends with</th><th>For</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>arm64-v8a.apk</code></td><td>64-bit phones: nearly every phone made in the last several years</td><td>The usual choice.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>armeabi-v7a.apk</code></td><td>32-bit phones: older or very cheap ones, and phones running a 32-bit version of Android</td><td>New, see the note at the end of this topic.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>universal.apk</code></td><td>Either kind</td><td>Carries both versions of the built-in ADB tool, so it is larger. The safe choice if you are unsure.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The difference is the small ADB program that the app carries inside it and starts to talk to your phone. A 64-bit phone needs the 64-bit program, a 32-bit phone needs the 32-bit one, and the universal file has both. Everything you see on screen is the same in all three.</p>\n\n<h4>Which one does my phone need?</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li><b>If you have no idea, take the universal file.</b> It works on both kinds of phone.</li>\n<li><b>If a recent version of this app is already installed</b>, open the <b>About</b> tab. In the <b>This build</b> card, the <b>CPU</b> row shows the first processor type your phone reports. <code>arm64-v8a</code> means take the 64-bit file; <code>armeabi-v7a</code> (or <code>armeabi</code>) means take the 32-bit file. The <b>Device Specs</b> card lower down also lists <b>CPU architecture</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Otherwise</b>, a hardware-information app from your app store usually lists the processor type, often under \"Architecture\" or \"Supported ABIs\" (ABI means processor type). Look for <code>arm64</code> (64-bit) or <code>armeabi</code> / <code>armv7</code> (32-bit).</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> It is the Android system that counts, not just the chip. Some budget phones have a 64-bit chip but ship a 32-bit Android, and they report a 32-bit type. That is why the answer in the <b>CPU</b> row is better than guessing from the phone's age. Phones with an Intel (x86) processor are not what any of the three files was made for.</div>\n<p>If you install the wrong one, Android usually refuses with a message such as \"App not installed\" or that the app is not compatible with your phone. Nothing is harmed; download the other file and try again. Note that a few very recent phones can run 64-bit apps only, so on those the 32-bit file will not install at all.</p>\n\n<h4>Installing from your browser</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li><b>Download the file.</b> On your phone, open the releases page in your browser and scroll to the list of files at the bottom of the newest release. Tap the APK you chose. The browser may warn that this type of file can harm your device; that warning appears for every APK, so tap <b>OK</b> or <b>Download anyway</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Open it.</b> When the download finishes, tap <b>Open</b> in the browser's message, or open your Downloads folder in a file manager and tap the file.</li>\n<li><b>Allow installs from this source, once.</b> Android may say your phone is not allowed to install unknown apps from this source, with a <b>Settings</b> button. Tap it, switch on <b>Allow from this source</b> for the app you used (your browser or your file manager), then press Back. Android calls this \"Install unknown apps\", and the switch belongs to the app you downloaded <i>with</i>, not to this app. (This app has a switch of its own too: the row <b>Install unknown apps</b> in its <b>Permissions</b> sheet. It only matters later, when this app installs a file for you; see \"Updating from inside the app\" below.)</li>\n<li><b>Install.</b> Tap <b>Install</b>. Google Play Protect (Google's built-in app scanner) may offer to scan the app or warn that it is not from a store. Choose <b>More details</b>, then <b>Install anyway</b> (the wording varies a little between phones).</li>\n<li><b>Open it.</b> Tap <b>Open</b>. The first launch is described in <a href=\"#quick-start\">Quick start</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> After installing you can switch \"Allow from this source\" off again for your browser. It is safer to leave it off until you need it.</div>\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> On some recent Samsung phones, a feature called <b>Auto Blocker</b> can block installs that do not come from a store, and nothing else tells you why. If the install is refused with no clear reason, look under Settings, Security and privacy, Auto Blocker.</div>\n<p>To check what you installed, open the <b>About</b> tab. The line under <b>This build</b> says \"Signed with the official release key\" when the app is the real release. If it says \"Not signed with the official release key (a debug, fork or modified build)\", it is a build someone else made.</p>\n\n<h4>If Android says \"App not installed\"</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Wrong file for your phone.</b> Try the universal file.</li>\n<li><b>Different signing key.</b> A copy you built yourself, or got from elsewhere, is signed with another key and cannot replace an official install. You would have to uninstall the old app first, which deletes its saved lists, profiles and settings. Export what you need first (<a href=\"#apps-profiles\">Profiles</a>).</li>\n<li><b>The download broke off.</b> Download it again.</li>\n<li><b>Not enough space.</b> Free some and retry.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Updating in place</h4>\n<p>You never need to uninstall to update. Install the new APK over the old app, the same way as above. Android keeps your settings, saved lists, profiles and permissions, because the new file has the same package name and the same signing key. You can usually go from one flavour to another this way too, for example from <code>universal</code> to <code>arm64-v8a</code>. After an update, a <b>What's new</b> sheet opens once with the changes.</p>\n\n<h4>Updating from inside the app</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open the <b>About</b> tab and tap <b>Check for update</b>. The app opens the <b>App Updater</b> tab at its <b>App update</b> card and asks GitHub for the newest release. (If the <b>App Updater</b> tab is turned off, you get a message saying where to turn it on: <b>Settings</b>, <b>Feature List</b>.)</li>\n<li>The card says either \"You're on the latest version\" or \"Update available\", with the old and new numbers. Buttons: <b>Check for app update</b>, <b>Update now</b> and <b>Release notes</b>.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Update now</b>. A progress bar shows the download. The app picks the right file for your phone by itself: the one named for your processor, else the universal one.</li>\n<li>Before installing, the app checks that the file is this app, is newer, and is signed with the same key. If one check fails it stops and says why.</li>\n<li>With a working mode on, the update installs quietly and the app restarts. Without one, the file is handed to Android's own installer, and the card says \"Confirm the install to update\". The first time, Android asks whether to allow this app to install apps (it lists this app under \"Install unknown apps\"); allow it and follow Android's prompts. You can also allow it beforehand: in <b>About</b>, tap <b>Permissions</b> and press <b>Allow</b> on the row <b>Install unknown apps</b> (see <a href=\"#app-permissions\">The permissions this app asks for</a>). If Android still refuses, install the APK from your browser as described above, or set up a working mode and try again.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>What the App Updater tab does for other apps</h4>\n<p>The same tab has more cards. <b>Updates Available</b> checks the Galaxy Store for Samsung apps, and checks sideloaded open-source apps (apps installed from a file instead of a store) against GitHub, Codeberg, F-Droid, IzzyOnDroid and the Obtainium catalog, with an <b>Update All</b> button. Every download is checked to be the same app, a newer version and signed with the same key before it installs. Google Play apps are not updated here; there is a card that sends you to the Play Store or Aurora Store. See <a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a> and <a href=\"#tab-store\">App Stores</a>.</p>\n\n<h4>About the 32-bit build</h4>\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> The 32-bit build (<code>armeabi-v7a</code>, and the 32-bit half of the universal file) is new, added in version 7.10.0. It carries a different copy of the ADB tool, taken from Termux's package for 32-bit ARM, together with a set of helper libraries that the app, on a 32-bit phone, unpacks into its private folder when it starts after an install or an update. It has seen far less use than the 64-bit build and has not been tried on every 32-bit phone. If a working mode will not connect on such a phone, it may be the build and not you. Please report it with the details from <b>About</b>, <b>Copy debug info</b> (the <b>Report a bug or idea</b> button is in the same tab), so it can be fixed.</div>\n<p>Installing is the same for all three files, and the app itself behaves the same. If the phone gives trouble and it is really a 64-bit phone, the 64-bit file is the better-tested choice; otherwise see <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>.</p>"},{"id":"basics","title":"Words you will see","group":"Start here","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The words this app uses, in plain language, with an example of each. If a word is not here, look in the <a href=\"#glossary\">glossary</a>.</p>\n\n<h4>App</h4>\n<p>Anything you can open from your home screen, such as a game, a camera or a bank. The phone also has many apps that run in the background and have no icon at all. The <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a> lists all of them.</p>\n\n<h4>System app and 3rd Party (user) app</h4>\n<p>A <b>system app</b> came with the phone: the dialer, the clock, the manufacturer's extras, and many pieces of Android itself. A <b>3rd Party</b> app, also called a <b>user</b> app, is one you (or someone setting up the phone) installed afterwards. In the app list each row carries a <b>SYSTEM</b> or <b>USER</b> tag, and there are <b>System</b> and <b>3rd Party</b> filters. A system app that you update from the Play Store is still a system app. Removing system apps is where most of the risk is, so start with user apps.</p>\n\n<h4>Package name</h4>\n<p>The unique ID of an app, shown in small type under its name. It looks like <code>com.google.android.youtube</code>. Two apps can share a display name but never a package name. Search works on both, and a menu has <b>Copy package</b> chips. When the guide or the app talks about \"a package\", it means an app.</p>\n\n<h4>APK and split APKs</h4>\n<p>An <b>APK</b> is the file an app is installed from, with the ending <code>.apk</code>. Many modern apps come as several files that work together: a base file plus small \"split\" files for your processor, your screen density and your language. Those arrive as one bundle with the endings <code>.apks</code>, <code>.apkm</code> or <code>.xapk</code>. The <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a> reads all of them and ticks the splits that fit your phone (<a href=\"#installer-splits\">Splits</a>). <b>Extract APK</b> in an app's menu copies an installed app's file out of the phone.</p>\n\n<h4>Freeze (disable)</h4>\n<p>Freezing turns an app off without removing it. Android calls this \"disabling\". A frozen app cannot run, does not appear in your launcher, and uses no battery, but all its files and settings stay, and one tap brings it back. In this app the button is <b>Freeze</b> (and <b>Enable</b> to undo it); the Debloater calls it <b>Disable</b>, and the history log says \"Disabled\" and \"Enabled\". Frozen apps carry a <b>FROZEN</b> tag. Example: freeze a shopping app you only use at Christmas.</p>\n\n<h4>Suspend</h4>\n<p>Suspending is a lighter pause. The app stays enabled, but Android greys out its icon, will not open it, and hides its notifications. Use <b>Suspend</b> and <b>Unsuspend</b> when you want an app out of reach for a while. Suspended apps carry a <b>SUSPENDED</b> tag.</p>\n\n<h4>Uninstall, and uninstall for the current user only</h4>\n<p>For an app you installed, <b>Uninstall</b> removes it. For a <b>system</b> app, the app can only remove it <b>for the current user</b>, which is you: it disappears from your phone, but its file stays in the protected system area. Such an app is listed under <b>Uninstalled</b> (the <b>Bloatware (Uninstalled)</b> box counts them), and <b>Reinstall</b> in its menu brings it back. A factory reset also restores it, and a big system update sometimes does. <b>Rem Updates</b> is different again: it removes the updates of a system app and returns it to the version the phone shipped with.</p>\n\n<h4>Clear data</h4>\n<p>Wipes everything the app saved: logins, settings, downloads kept inside it. Afterwards the app behaves as if you had just installed it. It cannot be undone, except from a backup that was made with Root. Example: clear the data of a misbehaving app before giving up on it.</p>\n\n<h4>Force stop</h4>\n<p>Ends an app's running processes right now. It is harmless: nothing is deleted and the app can start again. Use it on an app that is stuck or draining battery.</p>\n\n<h4>Permissions</h4>\n<p>What an app is allowed to do: use the camera, see your location, read your contacts. Some are asked about when you use the app (\"runtime\" permissions) and can be switched on and off. Others are fixed when the app is installed (\"install-time\") and cannot be changed by anyone; the app shows a lock on those. An app's menu has a <b>Permissions</b> tab that lists them.</p>\n\n<h4>App ops</h4>\n<p>A finer set of switches that Android keeps behind the permissions, such as \"run in the background\" or \"show over other apps\". Each can be set to allow, ignore, deny, foreground-only or default. They live in the <b>App Ops</b> tab of an app's menu, and changing them needs a working mode.</p>\n\n<h4>Debloat and bloatware</h4>\n<p><b>Bloatware</b> means apps that were pre-installed by the phone's maker or your carrier, that many people never use and cannot easily remove. <b>Debloating</b> is turning them off or removing them for your user. The <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a> shows a community list that rates each app as <b>Recommended</b>, <b>Advanced</b>, <b>Expert</b> or <b>Unsafe</b> to remove. Never remove anything rated <b>Unsafe</b>. See <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloater safety</a>.</p>\n\n<h4>ADB</h4>\n<p>Short for Android Debug Bridge, the tool developers use to send commands to a phone, normally from a computer. It runs commands as a special Android user called \"shell\", whose user number is 2000, and that user is allowed to do things an ordinary app is not. This app carries its own copy of ADB and uses it on the phone itself. See <a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP</a> and <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>.</p>\n\n<h4>Shizuku</h4>\n<p>A separate free app that starts a background helper with ADB-level power, and lets other apps use it once you approve them. If you already use Shizuku, this app can use it as its working mode. See <a href=\"#mode-shizuku\">Shizuku</a>.</p>\n\n<h4>Root</h4>\n<p>Full administrator access to the phone, beyond what even ADB can do. It is not on by default: the phone must be rooted by you with other tools, which usually voids the warranty. If your phone is rooted, this app can use it. See <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>.</p>\n\n<h4>Working mode</h4>\n<p>The way this app gets the power to change other apps: ADB over TCP, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root, or <b>Auto</b>, which uses the first of the first three that is ready (Auto never picks Root; you choose Root yourself). Without one, the app is in <b>Read-Only</b> mode. The current mode is the badge at the top right. See <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>.</p>\n\n<h4>Backup</h4>\n<p>A saved copy of an app: its APK files, its permissions and its app ops, and, with Root, its data. It is a single file you can keep or share, and restoring it puts the app back. See <a href=\"#apps-backup\">Backups</a>.</p>\n\n<h4>Profile</h4>\n<p>A saved list of which apps are frozen, suspended or removed on your phone. You can apply it again later, share it with another phone, and ask the app to <b>Watch</b> it: after a system update it tells you if apps you removed have come back. See <a href=\"#apps-profiles\">Profiles</a>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> The words \"package\", \"app\" and \"application\" mean the same thing in this app. Likewise \"freeze\" and \"disable\".</div>"},{"id":"screen-tour","title":"A tour of the screen","group":"Start here","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">What is on the screen, what the coloured badge at the top means, how the tab bar works, what Back does, and how to select and open apps.</p>\n\n<h4>The header</h4>\n<p>The header is the strip at the top. From left to right:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>The title</b>, \"Full ADB/Root System Manager\", flush against the left edge on two lines. It is as large as the room next to the two buttons allows, so it is a little smaller on a narrow phone. Under it are two small lines in capitals, the <b>device lines</b>: first your phone's maker and model, for example \"SAMSUNG SM-S948U1\", then the Android version and, on a Samsung phone, the One UI version, for example \"ANDROID 17 &#8226; ONE UI 9.0\". On other makes the second line is only the Android version. (For a split second after launch the two lines are empty.)</li>\n<li><b>The Settings gear.</b> It is the same height as the working-mode badge next to it. Tap it for Settings: language, appearance, colours, font, icon pack, the Action Button, the Feature List, a press-and-hold guide and a button that clears the cache of every app (Trim Caches). It is not a tab. See <a href=\"#prefs\">Settings</a>.</li>\n<li><b>The working-mode badge</b>, a small rounded label with a coloured dot. It names the mode in use, and tapping it opens the <b>Working Modes &amp; ADB Setup</b> sheet.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>What the badge says and what its colour means</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\"><thead><tr><th>Badge</th><th>Colour</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>ADB TCP (5555)</td><td>Green</td><td>Connected through ADB over TCP; the number is the port.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wireless ADB (37099)</td><td>Green</td><td>Connected through Wireless Debugging; the number is the connect port.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Shizuku ADB (UID 2000)</td><td>Green</td><td>Using Shizuku, which runs commands as the shell user.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Root (Superuser)</td><td>Violet</td><td>Using root.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Auto &#8226; plus one of the above</td><td>Green</td><td>Auto mode is on and has found a mode that is ready.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Read-Only Mode</td><td>Orange</td><td>You chose Read-Only on purpose.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Setup Mode (Read-Only)</td><td>Orange</td><td>No mode is ready yet. Tap the badge to set one up.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A mode name followed by \"(not ready)\"</td><td>Orange</td><td>You chose that mode but it is not connected right now, for example Wireless ADB after the Wi-Fi changed. For Root it reads \"Root (Superuser) (not ready)\" when a <code>su</code> program exists but your root manager has not allowed this app (see <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Checking Mode...</td><td>-</td><td>The app is still looking. It lasts a moment.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Green and violet mean the app can change things. Orange means it cannot, yet. The colours follow your theme, so on an unusual theme they may differ a little; the words stay the same. <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a> explains how to move from orange to green.</p>\n\n<h4>The tab bar</h4>\n<p>Under the header is a row of tab buttons, most of them with a two-line label. It scrolls sideways: swipe it left and right to reach the others, and the tab you open slides to the middle. From left to right, the tabs are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Application Manager</b> (always first; <a href=\"#tab-apps\">details</a>) and <b>Saved Applications</b> (<a href=\"#tab-saved-lists\">details</a>).</li>\n<li><b>UAD-NG Debloater</b> (<a href=\"#tab-debloater\">details</a>), <b>APK Installer</b> (<a href=\"#tab-installer\">details</a>) and <b>File Manager</b> (<a href=\"#tab-files\">details</a>).</li>\n<li><b>Command-Line Interface</b> (<a href=\"#tab-terminal\">details</a>).</li>\n<li><b>Hidden Settings</b> (<a href=\"#tab-settings\">details</a>) and <b>RRO/Monet Customization</b> (<a href=\"#tab-overlays\">details</a>).</li>\n<li><b>App Updater</b> (<a href=\"#tab-updates\">details</a>) and <b>App Stores</b> (<a href=\"#tab-store\">details</a>). When updates are waiting, the App Updater label shows their number in brackets.</li>\n<li><b>Logcat Viewer</b> (<a href=\"#tab-logcat\">details</a>), <b>Task Manager</b> (<a href=\"#tab-taskmgr\">details</a>) and <b>Connected Devices</b> (<a href=\"#tab-devices\">details</a>).</li>\n<li><b>About</b> (always last; <a href=\"#tab-about\">details</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You can turn tabs off and move them. Open Settings with the gear, find the <b>Feature List</b> card, and use its switches and up and down arrows; <b>Reset to Default</b> puts everything back. Application Manager and About cannot be moved or turned off. See <a href=\"#feature-list\">Feature List</a>. If a link in the app points to a tab you turned off, you get a message saying \"... is turned off. Turn it on in Settings, Feature List.\"</p>\n\n<h4>The ? button on a tab</h4>\n<p>Most tabs, and the Settings screen (the gear), have a small round <b>?</b> button in the top right corner of their first card: tap it and this guide opens straight at that tab's topic. <b>Application Manager</b> and <b>Command-Line Interface</b> have no such card, so no <b>?</b>; reach this guide for them through <b>About</b>, <b>Help Guide</b>.</p>\n\n<h4>The Application Manager tab</h4>\n<p>From the top: the orange Read-Only banner when no mode is on (it has a <b>Set Up</b> button, and an X in its top right corner that puts it away until the app is started again or Read-Only mode returns), the count boxes (tap one to filter the list by it), a <b>Sort</b> box with <b>Share CSV</b>, <b>Profiles</b> and <b>Backups</b> buttons and an <b>Action Button</b> menu, the filter pills, the search box (the three dots at its right end open options for what is searched), and the list of apps.</p>\n<p>Each row shows the app's icon, its name, its package name and tags (<b>RUNNING</b>, <b>FROZEN</b>, <b>SUSPENDED</b>, <b>UNINSTALLED</b>, <b>SYSTEM</b> or <b>USER</b>, and a UAD-NG level when the list rates the app). At the right end are an action button (an icon; you choose what it does in Settings), the three-dots menu button (<b>&#8943;</b>) and a small square checkbox.</p>\n\n<h4>Selecting apps and opening an app's menu</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Tap the checkbox</b>, or the name area of the row, to select the app. The row gets a coloured edge and a tick. A floating round button with a tick and the number of selected apps appears; tap it to open the batch panel (<a href=\"#apps-batch\">batch actions</a>, a panel of buttons that act on all the selected apps at once).</li>\n<li><b>Tap the three-dots button</b> (<b>&#8943;</b>), or <b>press and hold the row</b> for about half a second, to open that app's menu. You feel a short tap when the hold works. The hold does not also select the row.</li>\n<li><b>Press and hold the app's icon</b> to save the icon as a picture. <b>Press and hold the floating button</b> to clear the whole selection at once.</li>\n<li>Settings has a card called <b>Press and hold guide</b> that lists everything in the app that reacts to a long press.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Messages: toasts and sheets</h4>\n<p>A <b>toast</b> is a small rounded message that slides up from the bottom of the screen and fades after a couple of seconds, for example \"Action executed\". It is only a confirmation and cannot be tapped. When something fails, the app also shows a <b>sheet</b> with the full details, which stays until you close it. A sheet is a panel that slides up over the screen: the app's menu, Working Modes, Permissions and this guide are all sheets. Close any sheet with its X, by tapping the dim area outside it, or with Back.</p>\n\n<h4>What Back does</h4>\n<p>The Back button (or gesture) is careful, so you do not leave the app by accident. Each press does the first thing on this list that applies:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Closes an open drop-down list or the search options menu.</li>\n<li>Closes the sheet that is on top, if any.</li>\n<li>Folds the open batch panel, then clears the selection of apps.</li>\n<li>Inside some tabs, steps back inside the tab: clears a Debloater selection, leaves search or selection mode in the File Manager, and so on. When you are looking inside an archive (a zip, 7z or similar file opened in the File Manager), it goes up one folder of the archive at a time and leaves the archive at the top. Back does not go up a folder of the phone's own storage: in the File Manager, tap the \"..\" row (labelled \"Up one folder\") at the top of the list for that.</li>\n<li>Returns to the tab you came from. The app remembers the last eight tabs you visited, and Back retraces them, ending at <b>Application Manager</b>.</li>\n<li>On Application Manager with nothing left to close, the first press shows \"Press back again to exit\". A second press leaves, but only if it is a real second press: at least about three quarters of a second after the first, and within three and a half seconds. A quick double tap does not count.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> If something is still running (an install, an app update download, a file job, a backup, a terminal command, a settings change), the message becomes \"Press back again to leave\" followed by what is running, and the second press asks \"Leave now?\" with a warning that the job would be cut off. Answer Cancel to stay.</div>"},{"id":"modes-overview","title":"Working modes: what they are and which to pick","group":"Connect the app to your phone","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">A working mode is the way this app borrows enough power from Android to freeze, remove and change other apps. You choose one once; the coloured badge at the top right of the screen always shows which one is in use.</p>\n\n<h4>Why the app needs a working mode at all</h4>\n<p>Android keeps every app in its own protected box. An ordinary app, this one included, cannot freeze, uninstall or change the settings of another app. Android reserves those jobs for two special users (a \"user\" here is an account inside Android, not a person): <b>shell</b> (user number 2000), which is the user that ADB runs as, and <b>root</b>. A working mode is simply a way for this app to send its commands as one of those two users.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>ADB over TCP</b> and <b>Wireless Debugging</b> let the app's own built-in copy of ADB talk to the phone's ADB service, from the inside, as the shell user.</li>\n<li><b>Shizuku</b> is a separate app that has already started a helper running as the shell user (or as root) and lets this app use it.</li>\n<li><b>Root</b> runs the commands as root through the phone's <code>su</code> program, which your root manager (Magisk, KernelSU or APatch) controls. The app counts Root as ready only when the root manager has really allowed it.</li>\n<li><b>Read-Only</b> is what you get when none of these is ready: the app can look, and cannot change.</li>\n<li><b>Auto</b> is not a route of its own. It tries some of the above in a fixed order, and uses the first that is ready.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The commands themselves are the ordinary Android ones (<code>pm</code>, <code>am</code>, <code>appops</code>, <code>settings</code> and so on). The app is just a convenient front end.</p>\n\n<h4>The six modes at a glance</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\"><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Needs a computer?</th><th>Android version</th><th>After a restart</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"#mode-readonly\">Auto</a></td><td>No</td><td>Same as the mode it picks</td><td>Picks again by itself</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP (port 5555)</a></td><td>Once, to switch it on, on Android 8 to 10. On Android 11 and newer you can do it from Wireless Debugging instead.</td><td>8 or newer</td><td>Switched off again; the one-time step must be repeated</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a></td><td>No</td><td>11 or newer</td><td>Pairing is kept; switch Wireless debugging on again and connect to the new port</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"#mode-shizuku\">Shizuku</a></td><td>Only if you start Shizuku that way. Starting it from the phone needs Android 11 or newer.</td><td>8 or newer</td><td>Shizuku stops; start it again</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a></td><td>No</td><td>8 or newer, on a rooted phone</td><td>Stays; nothing to redo</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"#mode-readonly\">Read-Only</a></td><td>No</td><td>8 or newer</td><td>Always the same</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Effort, roughly: <b>Root</b> is the quickest if you already have it (one tap and one approval). <b>Wireless Debugging</b> takes about five minutes the first time, because of the pairing, and about a minute after that. <b>ADB over TCP</b> is one tap once the phone is listening, but getting it to listen needs a computer on Android 10 or older. <b>Shizuku</b> means installing and starting another app, and starting it again after each restart. <b>Auto</b> needs nothing, but only helps once one of the others is working.</p>\n\n<h4>Which one should I pick?</h4>\n<p>Read down the list and stop at the first line that fits.</p>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li><b>Is your phone rooted?</b> If yes, pick <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>. It survives restarts and needs no Wi-Fi. (Auto never picks Root on its own, so you must choose it yourself.)</li>\n<li><b>Otherwise, is it Android 11 or newer?</b> Settings, About phone, shows the version. If yes, pick <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>. No computer is needed, only Wi-Fi.\n<ul>\n<li>If you tire of reconnecting every time the Wi-Fi changes, <a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP</a> can be switched on from Wireless Debugging with one button, and does not depend on the Wi-Fi once it is on, but it is switched off by every restart.</li>\n<li>If you already use <a href=\"#mode-shizuku\">Shizuku</a> and it is running, pick that instead; it is a single tap.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><b>Otherwise (Android 8 to 10, not rooted):</b> you need a computer and a USB cable. Use it once to switch on <a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP</a>, or to start <a href=\"#mode-shizuku\">Shizuku</a>. Both have to be redone after every restart. Without a computer, Read-Only is usually all this phone can do with the app.</li>\n<li><b>Not sure, or just browsing?</b> Leave it on <b>Auto</b> or <b>Read-Only</b>. Nothing is lost.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>How to change the mode</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Tap the <b>badge</b> at the top right of the screen. (The orange banner at the top of the Application Manager tab has a <b>Set Up</b> button that opens the same place.)</li>\n<li>A sheet called <b>Working Modes &amp; ADB Setup</b> slides up. Under its title is a line such as \"Selected: Automatic &#8226; Active: Setup Mode (Read-Only)\", which tells you what you chose and what is actually in use.</li>\n<li>Find the card for the mode you want and use its button: <b>Use Automatic</b>, <b>Connect</b> then <b>Use This Mode</b> (ADB over TCP), the pair and connect steps (Wireless Debugging), <b>Authorize &amp; Use Shizuku</b>, <b>Request Root Access</b>, or <b>Continue in Read-Only</b>. The button of the mode you are on changes to <b>Selected</b> (the Shizuku card shows the tag <b>AUTHORIZED</b> instead).</li>\n<li>A message at the bottom says what happened, the badge updates, and the list of apps is reloaded.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>You may switch at any time, and nothing happens to your apps when you do. The app never changes the mode you chose by itself, except that <b>Disconnect</b> on a connected ADB mode drops you back to Auto. A tile in Android's quick settings can also cycle the mode without opening the app (<a href=\"#quick-tiles\">Quick Settings tiles and widget</a>).</p>\n\n<h4>What the cards and their tags mean</h4>\n<p>The sheet starts with a card called <b>This App's ADB Key</b>, tagged <b>PRIVATE</b>. Each install makes its own key, which never leaves the phone. The fingerprint it shows is what Android's \"Allow debugging?\" prompt will display, so you can compare them. <b>Copy Fingerprint</b> copies it; <b>Regenerate Key</b> makes a new one, after which every ADB connection is dropped, Android asks again, and Wireless Debugging must be paired again.</p>\n<p>Then come the six mode cards, in this order: <b>Automatic (Recommended)</b>, <b>ADB over TCP (Port 5555)</b>, <b>Wireless Debugging (Android 11+)</b>, <b>Shizuku ADB Service</b>, <b>Root (Superuser - su)</b> and <b>Read-Only Mode (Unprivileged)</b>. A card has a green edge when that mode is in use, and an orange edge when you chose it but it is not ready. Each card also has a small tag:</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\"><thead><tr><th>Card</th><th>Tag</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Automatic</td><td>SELECTED or AUTO</td><td>SELECTED while Auto is your choice.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ADB over TCP</td><td>CONNECTED, PORT 5555 OPEN, DISCONNECTED</td><td>Connected and ready; the port is open but the app is not connected (often a pending approval); nothing is listening.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wireless Debugging</td><td>CONNECTED or ANDROID 11+</td><td>Connected; or not connected, with a reminder of the Android version needed.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Shizuku</td><td>AUTHORIZED, UPDATE SHIZUKU, RUNNING (UNAUTHORIZED), NOT RUNNING, NOT INSTALLED</td><td>Ready; Shizuku is running but too old; running but this app is not approved; installed but stopped; not on the phone.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Root</td><td>ACTIVE, AVAILABLE, DENIED, NOT FOUND</td><td>In use (Root is your chosen mode and was allowed); a su program was found but Root is not your chosen mode, so the app has not asked it anything yet; Root is your chosen mode but your root manager refused this app; no su found.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Read-Only</td><td>Safe</td><td>Always safe: it changes nothing.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> If you choose a mode that is not ready, the badge turns orange and says \"(not ready)\" after the mode's name. Nothing is broken: open the sheet, read the tag on that card, and finish the setup. For Root, \"Root (Superuser) (not ready)\" together with the tag <b>DENIED</b> means your root manager has not allowed this app (<a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>).</div>\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> A working mode lets this app run powerful commands. Only set one up on a phone you own, and only use an app you trust. Read <a href=\"#safety\">Safety</a> before you remove or disable system apps.</div>\n<p>Next: <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>, <a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP</a>, <a href=\"#mode-shizuku\">Shizuku</a>, <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>, <a href=\"#mode-readonly\">Auto and Read-Only</a>, and <a href=\"#app-permissions\">the permissions the app asks for</a>.</p>"},{"id":"mode-wireless","title":"Wireless Debugging (Android 11 and newer)","group":"Connect the app to your phone","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Wireless Debugging is Android's built-in way to let an ADB tool connect over Wi-Fi. This app pairs with your own phone once, then connects, with no computer at all.</p>\n\n<h4>Before you start</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Android 11 or newer.</b> The feature does not exist on older versions; use <a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP</a> there.</li>\n<li><b>A Wi-Fi connection.</b> The phone must be joined to a Wi-Fi network (one without internet is usually fine). Wireless Debugging does not work over mobile data. If no Wi-Fi is around, joining the hotspot of another phone works on many phones.</li>\n<li><b>Developer options</b> switched on (step 1).</li>\n<li>About five minutes. Pairing is needed once; connecting again later takes a minute.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Step 1: switch on Developer options</h4>\n<p>Android hides its developer settings until you ask for them.</p>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open the phone's <b>Settings</b> and go to <b>About phone</b>. On a <b>Samsung</b> phone with One UI, go to <b>About phone</b>, then <b>Software information</b>.</li>\n<li>Find <b>Build number</b> and tap it <b>seven times</b>, quickly. Midway, a message counts down (\"You are now 3 steps away from being a developer\"). If the phone has a PIN, pattern or fingerprint, it asks for it.</li>\n<li>You see \"You are now a developer!\". A new entry, <b>Developer options</b>, now exists: on Samsung at the bottom of the main Settings list, on a Pixel under <b>System</b>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Names differ by maker. On Xiaomi the item to tap is often called <b>OS version</b> or <b>MIUI version</b>; on OnePlus and Oppo it is under <b>About device</b>, then <b>Version</b>. If you get lost, type \"Build number\" in the Settings search box. The same trick finds \"Developer options\". The app also has an <b>Open Developer Options</b> button on the Wireless Debugging card.</div>\n\n<h4>Step 2: switch on Wireless debugging</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Connect to the Wi-Fi network you will use.</li>\n<li>Open <b>Developer options</b> and scroll to the <b>Debugging</b> section. Switch <b>Wireless debugging</b> on. Android asks \"Allow wireless debugging on this network?\". Tap <b>Allow</b> (tick \"Always allow on this network\" if you trust it).</li>\n<li>Tap the <i>words</i> \"Wireless debugging\", not just the switch. The Wireless debugging screen opens. It shows <b>IP address &amp; Port</b> (something like <code>192.168.1.20:37099</code>: the part before the colon is the phone's address on your Wi-Fi, and the number after it is the port, which picks one service on the phone to talk to), and the entries <b>Pair device with pairing code</b> (on some phones <b>Pair new device with pairing code</b>) and <b>Paired devices</b>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This screen has two different addresses, and mixing them up is the commonest mistake. The table below keeps them apart.</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\"><thead><tr><th>Address</th><th>Where Android shows it</th><th>Where it goes in the app</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Pairing address and 6-digit code</td><td>In the small window that opens when you tap Pair device with pairing code. Changes every time the window opens.</td><td>Step 1 of the card: \"Pairing address\" and \"Code\".</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Connect address (IP address and Port)</td><td>On the main Wireless debugging screen. Changes whenever Wireless debugging is switched off and on, or the phone restarts.</td><td>Step 2 of the card: the IP and Port boxes.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<h4>Step 3: pair this app with your phone</h4>\n<p>Pairing is the one-time handshake in which Android learns to trust this app's key. The six-digit code only lives while Android's pairing window is on screen; if you switch to another app full screen, the window closes and the code stops working. The app gives you two ways around that.</p>\n<h5>Way A: split screen (the app's own tip)</h5>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>In this app, tap the badge to open <b>Working Modes &amp; ADB Setup</b> and find the <b>Wireless Debugging (Android 11+)</b> card.</li>\n<li>Put Developer options and this app side by side, using <b>Split Screen</b> or a <b>Pop-up Window</b> from your phone's recent apps view, so both stay visible.</li>\n<li>In Developer options, tap <b>Wireless debugging</b>, then <b>Pair device with pairing code</b>. A window shows a six-digit code and an address with a port.</li>\n<li>In the app, under <b>STEP 1: PAIR WITH CODE</b>, type the address into <b>Pairing address</b> (for example <code>192.168.1.20:38475</code>; typing only the port also works on many phones) and the six digits into <b>Code</b>. Tap <b>Pair Device</b>.</li>\n<li>A message shows adb's answer. Success reads \"Successfully paired to ...\" followed by an address.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The <b>Auto-Detect Ports</b> button looks for the pairing and connect addresses for you while the pairing window is open and fills in the boxes it finds. If it says \"Nothing found\", keep the Wireless debugging screen open and type them yourself.</p>\n<h5>Way B: pair from a notification</h5>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>In the app, tap <b>Pair via Notification</b>. On Android 13 and newer you must allow notifications first; the app says \"Allow notifications, then tap Pair via notification again.\"</li>\n<li>A notification titled <b>Pair over Wi-Fi</b> appears, with a <b>Pair</b> button that opens a reply box. Go to Developer options, tap <b>Wireless debugging</b>, then <b>Pair device with pairing code</b>, and leave the six-digit window open.</li>\n<li>Pull down the notification shade. If the notification says \"Found pairing service at ...\", tap <b>Pair</b> and type just the six digits. Otherwise tap <b>Pair</b> and type the port, a space, and the code, like <code>37123 123456</code>. When in doubt, type the port and the code.</li>\n<li>The notification changes to \"Pairing...\", then to \"Paired with ... Open the app and connect.\" Failures are written there too, for example \"That doesn't look like a 6-digit code\".</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> You only need to pair once for each key. Android lists this app under <b>Paired devices</b> on the Wireless debugging screen, usually with a name that starts with <code>adbmanager@</code> followed by your phone's model. Regenerating the app's key (<a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>) makes the old pairing useless, and you pair again.</div>\n\n<h4>Step 4: connect</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Back on the main Wireless debugging screen, read the <b>IP address &amp; Port</b>.</li>\n<li>In the app, under <b>STEP 2: CONNECT WIRELESS ADB</b>, fill in the IP box and the Port box. <b>Use My Wi-Fi IP</b> fills in this phone's Wi-Fi address for you; <b>Auto-Detect Ports</b> can fill in the port. If you leave the IP empty, the app uses <code>127.0.0.1</code>, which works on some phones; if it does not, use the Wi-Fi address.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Connect Wireless ADB</b>. A message shows adb's answer, such as \"connected to 192.168.1.20:37099\".</li>\n<li>If the app finds the connection ready, it selects Wireless Debugging as your working mode.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>What success looks like</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The tag on the card changes to <b>CONNECTED</b>, and the button of the mode says <b>Selected</b>.</li>\n<li>The badge turns green and reads \"Wireless ADB (37099)\", with your port in brackets.</li>\n<li>The orange Read-Only banner on the Application Manager tab disappears, and buttons such as <b>Freeze</b> work instead of asking for a mode.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>When the Wi-Fi changes or the phone restarts</h4>\n<p>Android picks a new random connect port every time Wireless debugging is switched off and on, and it often switches it off by itself when you leave the Wi-Fi network or restart the phone. The pairing stays; only the connect address changes.</p>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>The badge turns orange. If you chose Wireless Debugging yourself, it reads \"Wireless ADB (37099) (not ready)\"; on Auto it reads \"Setup Mode (Read-Only)\".</li>\n<li>Switch Wireless debugging back on in Developer options and read the new <b>IP address &amp; Port</b>.</li>\n<li>In the app, open the card, put the new numbers in the boxes (or tap <b>Auto-Detect Ports</b>) and tap <b>Connect Wireless ADB</b>. You do not pair again.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>When the app starts and Wireless Debugging is your chosen mode, it tries the last address once by itself. That works only if the port has not changed.</p>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Many phones let you add a <b>Wireless debugging</b> tile to the quick settings panel (Developer options, Quick settings developer tiles), which makes switching it on quick. If connecting is refused although the address and port are right, Android may have forgotten the pairing; pair again.</div>\n\n<h4>Common problems</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Pairing says it failed.</b> The code is wrong, or the pairing window closed and the code expired. Open the window again, and use split screen or Way B so it stays open. Check that the pairing address is the one in the pairing window, not the connect address.</li>\n<li><b>\"failed to connect\", or a refused connection.</b> The port is out of date, you used the pairing port, Wireless debugging is off, or the phone is on a different Wi-Fi from the one you allowed. Re-read the connect address and try again.</li>\n<li><b>The message ends with \"Target ... is not ready (check the authorization prompt on the device)\".</b> Android is waiting for an approval. Look for a prompt or notification and accept it, then tap Connect again.</li>\n<li><b>Switch on Wireless debugging and it switches itself off.</b> You are not on Wi-Fi, or the Wi-Fi was lost. Join a network first.</li>\n<li><b>Auto-Detect Ports finds nothing.</b> The pairing window must be open for the pairing address to be found, and some phones or networks block the discovery. Type the numbers yourself.</li>\n<li><b>It worked, then stopped.</b> Look at the badge. If it is orange, follow the steps in the section above. If it is green but actions fail, tap <b>Connect Wireless ADB</b> again.</li>\n<li><b>Still stuck.</b> See <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>. On a 32-bit phone, remember that the 32-bit build is new (<a href=\"#install-and-update\">Installing and updating the app</a>).</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"mode-tcp","title":"ADB over TCP port 5555","group":"Connect the app to your phone","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">ADB over TCP makes the phone's ADB service listen on a network port, 5555, so that the app's own copy of ADB can connect to it from the inside. It works on every Android version this app supports.</p>\n\n<h4>What it is</h4>\n<p>Normally ADB talks over a USB cable. The command <code>adb tcpip 5555</code> tells the phone to listen on TCP port 5555 as well (a port is a numbered entry point for network connections). The app then connects to <code>127.0.0.1:5555</code>, which is the phone talking to itself, so no computer is needed once the port is open. The app saves the address and tries it again when it starts, if Auto or ADB over TCP is your mode and the port is open.</p>\n\n<h4>Before you start</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Developer options switched on, as in <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging, step 1</a>.</li>\n<li>One of: a computer and a USB cable; or a Wireless Debugging connection on Android 11 or newer (see Way B below).</li>\n<li>The setting does not survive a restart (see below), so keep this page handy.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Way A: the one-time step from a computer</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>On the computer, get Google's <b>SDK Platform-Tools</b>, a small download that contains the <code>adb</code> program (<a href=\"https://developer.android.com/tools/releases/platform-tools\">download page</a>), and unpack the folder.</li>\n<li>On the phone, in Developer options, switch <b>USB debugging</b> on (it lets a computer send ADB commands over the cable). Plug the phone into the computer with a USB cable. If the phone asks \"Allow USB debugging?\", tick \"Always allow\" and tap <b>Allow</b>.</li>\n<li>On the computer, open a terminal or command window in the platform-tools folder (on Windows, Command Prompt or PowerShell; on a Mac or Linux, Terminal) and run <code>adb devices</code>. Your phone should be listed with the word \"device\" beside it.</li>\n<li>Run <code>adb tcpip 5555</code>. It answers \"restarting in TCP mode port: 5555\".</li>\n<li>Unplug the cable. On the phone, open this app, tap the badge and find the <b>ADB over TCP (Port 5555)</b> card.</li>\n<li>Leave the address <code>127.0.0.1</code> and the port <code>5555</code> as they are, and tap <b>Connect</b>. A message such as \"connected to 127.0.0.1:5555\" appears. If Android asks \"Allow USB debugging?\", compare the fingerprint with the one on the <b>This App's ADB Key</b> card at the top of the sheet, tick \"Always allow\" and tap <b>Allow</b>.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>Way B: without a computer (Android 11 and newer)</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Connect with <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a> first.</li>\n<li>On the <b>ADB over TCP (Port 5555)</b> card, tap <b>Switch to 5555 (tcpip)</b>. The app runs <code>adb tcpip 5555</code> through the Wireless Debugging connection, waits a moment and then connects by itself to the host and port in the boxes of the card (<code>127.0.0.1</code> and <code>5555</code> unless you changed them).</li>\n<li>The Wireless Debugging connection may drop. That is expected, because the ADB service restarted.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If the message is an error such as \"no devices/emulators found\", there was no connection to switch from; connect with Wireless Debugging first. Some phones refuse the switch; the message then shows Android's reason.</p>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> A few custom Android versions (for example LineageOS) have a switch named <b>ADB over network</b> in Developer options that does the same thing as <code>adb tcpip 5555</code>. If yours has it, switch it on and use <b>Connect</b>.</div>\n\n<h4>What the card's buttons do</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Connect</b> connects to the host and port in the boxes. It also makes ADB over TCP your working mode if the connection is ready.</li>\n<li><b>Use This Mode</b> picks ADB over TCP as your working mode. If the app is not connected yet it tries to connect first; if that fails, the mode stays selected, and the badge says \"(not ready)\" when nothing is listening on the port.</li>\n<li><b>Switch to 5555 (tcpip)</b>: described in Way B.</li>\n<li><b>Disconnect</b> drops the ADB connection. If ADB over TCP or Wireless Debugging was your mode, the app falls back to Auto.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Leave the host box at <code>127.0.0.1</code> unless you know why not. The host box accepts another device's address too, but the app's commands would then go to that device while its list shows this phone's apps. To work with another phone or a watch, use <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a> instead.</div>\n\n<h4>Why it is not persistent</h4>\n<p>The phone forgets the setting when it restarts. After every restart, ADB goes back to USB only, and the card shows <b>DISCONNECTED</b>. Repeat Way A or Way B. On Android 8 to 10 that means a computer again after every restart, which is the main reason Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root is nicer where you can have them.</p>\n\n<h4>How to tell it works</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The tag on the card turns <b>CONNECTED</b> (green). <b>PORT 5555 OPEN</b> in orange means the phone is listening but this app is not connected yet, often because an approval is waiting; tap <b>Connect</b>.</li>\n<li>The badge turns green and says \"ADB TCP (5555)\".</li>\n<li>The orange banner on the Application Manager tab is gone and <b>Freeze</b> and friends work.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>If it does not connect</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>\"Connection refused\" or \"failed to connect\".</b> Nothing is listening on 5555: you have not done the one-time step yet, or the phone restarted since. Redo it.</li>\n<li><b>\"Target ... is not ready (check the authorization prompt on the device)\".</b> Approve the \"Allow USB debugging?\" prompt, then tap <b>Connect</b> again. If no prompt appears, in Developer options tap <b>Revoke USB debugging authorizations</b>, then try again.</li>\n<li><b>The prompt shows a fingerprint that does not match</b> the one in the app. Do not allow it; something other than this app is connecting. Tap Deny.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> While port 5555 is open, the phone listens on the Wi-Fi too, not only on the inside. Another device on the same network could try to connect; it would still need your approval in the prompt, so never tick \"Always allow\" for a fingerprint you do not recognise. Use this mode only on a network you trust, and restart the phone when you are finished: that switches it off.</div>"},{"id":"mode-shizuku","title":"Shizuku","group":"Connect the app to your phone","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Shizuku is a separate free app that starts a helper with ADB-level power and lets other apps, this one included, use it. If you already run Shizuku, using it here is a single approval.</p>\n\n<h4>What Shizuku is</h4>\n<p>Shizuku is not part of this app and is not made by the same person. It is a small program you install and start. Once it is running, any app you approve can ask it to run commands as the <b>shell</b> user, \"UID 2000\": the user that ADB runs as, which is allowed to do things an ordinary app cannot. If Shizuku was started with root, commands run as root instead. This app shows \"Shizuku ADB (UID 2000)\" in its badge when it is using it.</p>\n<p>The app also recognises an app called <b>Shizuku Plus</b>, which has its own package name.</p>\n\n<h4>Before you start</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Android 8 or newer. Starting Shizuku on the phone itself, with no computer, needs Android 11 or newer.</li>\n<li>Shizuku installed. Its official site, <a href=\"https://shizuku.rikka.app/\">shizuku.rikka.app</a>, explains where to get it (Google Play or GitHub). Install it like any other app.</li>\n<li>Shizuku must be running before this app can use it. It stops whenever the phone restarts.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Starting Shizuku</h4>\n<p>Open the Shizuku app. Its main screen offers three ways to start; the wording differs a little between versions. Pick one.</p>\n<h5>Method 1: Wireless debugging (Android 11 and newer, no computer)</h5>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Switch on Developer options and join a Wi-Fi network (<a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging, steps 1 and 2</a>).</li>\n<li>In Shizuku, choose <b>Start via Wireless debugging</b>. Its own screen has a button to open Developer options and, on the first run, a <b>Pairing</b> step: Shizuku posts a notification where you type the pairing code from Android's pairing window. This is separate from the pairing this app uses.</li>\n<li>After pairing, tap <b>Start</b>. Shizuku shows a short log and then reports that it is running.</li>\n</ol>\n<h5>Method 2: from a computer</h5>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Set up the computer and USB debugging as in <a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP</a>, Way A.</li>\n<li>Run the command that Shizuku shows on its screen. It is normally <code>adb shell sh /sdcard/Android/data/moe.shizuku.privileged.api/start.sh</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h5>Method 3: root</h5>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>In Shizuku, choose the option for rooted phones and approve the root request from your root manager.</li>\n<li>Shizuku has a setting to start by itself when the phone starts, for rooted phones.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>Connecting this app to Shizuku</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Check that Shizuku says it is running.</li>\n<li>In this app, tap the badge and find the <b>Shizuku ADB Service</b> card. Its tag tells you where things stand.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Authorize &amp; Use Shizuku</b>. Shizuku shows its own question asking whether to allow ADB Application Manager Pro to use Shizuku. Tap the button that allows it (Shizuku's wording may differ a little between versions).</li>\n<li>A message says \"Shizuku authorized. Using Shizuku mode.\". The tag becomes <b>AUTHORIZED</b>, and the badge turns green and reads \"Shizuku ADB (UID 2000)\".</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The card also has <b>Open Shizuku</b>, which opens the Shizuku app, and <b>Check Status</b>, which looks again. Authorizing Shizuku makes Shizuku your working mode, and it works alongside ADB over TCP.</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\"><thead><tr><th>Tag</th><th>What it means</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>NOT INSTALLED</td><td>No Shizuku app was found.</td><td>Install Shizuku.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>NOT RUNNING</td><td>Shizuku is installed but stopped.</td><td>Start it (above), then tap <b>Authorize &amp; Use Shizuku</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>RUNNING (UNAUTHORIZED)</td><td>Shizuku runs, but this app is not approved.</td><td>Tap <b>Authorize &amp; Use Shizuku</b> and allow the question.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>UPDATE SHIZUKU</td><td>The running Shizuku is too old for this app.</td><td>Update the Shizuku app and start it again.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>AUTHORIZED</td><td>Ready.</td><td>Nothing; you are using it.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The app notices Shizuku starting while it is open and refreshes the card by itself. You can take the approval back at any time in the Shizuku app, under its list of authorized apps.</p>\n\n<h4>After a restart</h4>\n<p>Shizuku stops when the phone restarts, unless it was started with root and set to start by itself. After a restart the badge turns orange: \"Shizuku ADB (UID 2000) (not ready)\" if you chose Shizuku, or \"Setup Mode (Read-Only)\" on Auto. Open Shizuku, start it again by the same method, and the badge turns green again without any new approval. The ADB Console in the <a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a> tab also has a Shizuku-only <b>Rish</b> mode.</p>\n\n<h4>If it does not work</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>\"Shizuku is not running. Start it in the Shizuku app, then tap again.\"</b> The app opens Shizuku for you; start it there.</li>\n<li><b>\"This Shizuku version is too old. Please update Shizuku.\"</b> Update the Shizuku app.</li>\n<li><b>Nothing appears when you tap Authorize.</b> Check the notification shade, and that Shizuku has not been stopped by a battery saver.</li>\n<li><b>You denied it by mistake.</b> Tap the button again, or switch the approval on in the Shizuku app.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"mode-root","title":"Root (Superuser)","group":"Connect the app to your phone","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Root means the phone has been modified so that apps can ask for full administrator rights. If your phone is already rooted, this mode is the simplest and the most lasting.</p>\n\n<h4>What root is</h4>\n<p>Android normally stops anyone from changing the system. <b>Rooting</b> adds a program called <code>su</code> (for \"superuser\") and a manager app that decides which apps may use it. The three managers this app names are Magisk, KernelSU and APatch. With root, a command runs as the all-powerful root user, one step above the shell user that ADB uses.</p>\n<div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> This app cannot root your phone, and it will not help you do it. Rooting is done with other tools, usually needs an unlocked bootloader (the program that starts Android, which is normally locked), normally erases the phone, usually voids the warranty and, on Samsung phones, permanently trips the Knox counter (a Samsung record that the phone was modified), which can disable Samsung Pay and Secure Folder. Do not root a phone just to use this app: Wireless Debugging or Shizuku does most of the same work on Android 11 and newer. If you root, back up everything first.</div>\n\n<h4>Before you start</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The phone must already be rooted by you, with a root manager installed.</li>\n<li>Know where the root manager's list of apps is, so you can see and cancel what you approve.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Step by step</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Tap the badge to open <b>Working Modes &amp; ADB Setup</b> and find the <b>Root (Superuser - su)</b> card. The tag says <b>AVAILABLE</b> when the app can see a <code>su</code> program and Root is not your chosen mode, or <b>NOT FOUND</b> when it cannot see one. (<b>ACTIVE</b> and <b>DENIED</b> come later, once Root is your chosen mode: see below.) The app looks in the usual places, such as <code>/system/bin/su</code>, <code>/sbin/su</code>, <code>/data/adb/ksu/bin/su</code> and <code>/data/adb/ap/bin/su</code>, and asks the shell to find <code>su</code>.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Request Root Access</b>. The app runs a test command through <code>su</code> (it asks for <code>id</code>) and waits up to fifteen seconds.</li>\n<li>Your root manager shows its own question. Tap <b>Grant</b> (or <b>Allow</b>). Choose \"forever\" only if you are happy to.</li>\n<li>If the answer comes back as root, the message says \"Using Root (Superuser)\". The tag becomes <b>ACTIVE</b>, and the badge turns violet and reads \"Root (Superuser)\".</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If the answer does not come back as root, the message reads \"Root was denied or is not available\". Root stays your chosen mode anyway, but the app does not treat it as working: the tag turns orange and reads <b>DENIED</b>, the badge turns orange and reads \"Root (Superuser) (not ready)\", and no command that needs privileges runs. See \"If it does not work\" below.</p>\n\n<h4>How the app knows that root really works</h4>\n<p>Having a <code>su</code> program is not enough: your root manager may have refused this app. So, while Root is your chosen mode, the app asks <code>su</code> to run <code>id</code> and counts Root as working only when the answer says <code>uid=0</code> (the root user). The four tags mean:</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\"><thead><tr><th>Tag</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>NOT FOUND</b></td><td>No <code>su</code> program on the phone.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>AVAILABLE</b></td><td>A <code>su</code> program exists, but Root is not your chosen mode. The app has not asked it anything, so no root prompt appears.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>ACTIVE</b></td><td>Root is your chosen mode and <code>su</code> answered <code>uid=0</code>. The badge is violet.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>DENIED</b> (orange)</td><td>Root is your chosen mode, but <code>su</code> did not answer <code>uid=0</code>: your root manager refused this app, or you did not answer in time. The badge reads \"Root (Superuser) (not ready)\".</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The question is asked only while Root is your chosen mode, and the answer is kept. It is asked again only when you check on purpose: <b>Request Root Access</b>, or <b>Check Status</b> on the Shizuku card. The app waits about eight seconds for you to tap <b>Allow</b> in your root manager's prompt (up to fifteen after you tap <b>Request Root Access</b>), so be ready to tap it. If Root is already your chosen mode when the app starts, the root manager may show that question the first time the app checks.</p>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Auto never chooses Root. Its list is ADB over TCP, Wireless Debugging, then Shizuku. On a rooted phone, choose Root yourself with this button, once; the choice is remembered.</div>\n\n<h4>What root unlocks in this app</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Every action runs as root.</b> Freeze, uninstall, permissions, app ops and the rest all go through <code>su</code>, so they are not limited to what the shell user may do. The <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a>, the working-mode shell in the Terminal and the Debloater all work in this mode, and the Installer lets you choose Root as the authorizer.</li>\n<li><b>Backups of app data.</b> Backing up an app's APK, permissions and app ops works in any working mode, but including its <i>data</i> (logins, saved games, settings) is root-only. The Backup sheet says \"Uses Root. The app is stopped while its data is copied.\" Without root, the switch is greyed out with the words \"Needs Root, which was not found on this phone.\" Restoring data needs root too (<a href=\"#apps-backup\">Backups</a>).</li>\n<li><b>Removing system apps directly.</b> With ADB or Shizuku, if Android refuses to remove a system app for your user, the app tries a fallback (a direct call to Android's package service); with root the app does not use that fallback. In every case the app's file stays in the system area.</li>\n<li>Root reaches places the shell user cannot, such as other apps' private folders. That is how data backup works.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Risks</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Anything a root command can do, an app with root can do. Only grant root to apps you trust, and review the root manager's list now and then.</li>\n<li>Root makes it easier to damage the system by mistake, for instance with the <b>Unsafe</b> items of the Debloater or by editing hidden settings. Read <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloater safety</a> and <a href=\"#safety\">Safety</a>.</li>\n<li>Some banking and payment apps refuse to run on a rooted phone.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>If it does not work</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Tag says NOT FOUND.</b> No <code>su</code> was found. The root manager may not be installed or may have been disabled. Fix that, then open the sheet again or tap <b>Check Status</b> on the Shizuku card, which re-checks everything.</li>\n<li><b>\"Root was denied or is not available\".</b> You tapped Deny, or the root manager did not answer in time. Tap <b>Request Root Access</b> again and approve it, or look for the app in the root manager's list and allow it there.</li>\n<li><b>Tag says DENIED, and the badge says \"Root (Superuser) (not ready)\".</b> <code>su</code> exists but did not answer as root for this app, so nothing privileged runs. Open your root manager (Magisk, KernelSU or APatch), find ADB Application Manager Pro in its list of apps, set it to allow, then choose Root again with <b>Request Root Access</b> in Working Modes. If you allowed it after the question had timed out, the tag stays DENIED until you do this.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"mode-readonly","title":"Auto mode and Read-Only mode","group":"Connect the app to your phone","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Auto picks the first working mode that is ready. Read-Only is what remains when none is: you can browse and inspect, but not change anything.</p>\n\n<h4>Auto mode</h4>\n<p>Auto is the default and is called <b>Automatic (Recommended)</b> on its card. It does not create a connection of its own; it looks at what is ready and uses the first of these, in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li><b>ADB over TCP</b>, if the app is connected to <code>127.0.0.1:5555</code>.</li>\n<li><b>Wireless Debugging</b>, if the app is connected to the address you last used.</li>\n<li><b>Shizuku</b>, if it is running and this app has been approved.</li>\n<li><b>ADB over TCP again</b>, if port 5555 is open but the app has not connected yet: it connects quietly and uses it.</li>\n<li>Otherwise <b>Read-Only</b>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Root is never part of this list. The Automatic card lists only \"ADB TCP, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku\". When the app starts with Auto (or ADB over TCP) on and port 5555 open, it connects to it by itself.</p>\n<p>When Auto has found something, the badge reads \"Auto\" followed by a dot and the mode, for example \"Auto &#8226; Wireless ADB (37099)\". Auto changes its mind when things change: if the Wi-Fi drops, it falls back to the next ready mode, or to Read-Only. Pinning a specific mode instead stops that, and the badge goes orange with \"(not ready)\" if that mode is not available.</p>\n\n<h4>Read-Only mode</h4>\n<p>Read-Only is the safe mode. It is what the app starts in on a phone with nothing set up, and you can also choose it on purpose with <b>Continue in Read-Only</b>. The badge then says \"Read-Only Mode\" (chosen) or \"Setup Mode (Read-Only)\" (nothing ready), in orange.</p>\n<h5>What you can do</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>See every app, with search, sort, filters and the count boxes.</li>\n<li>Open an app's menu and read its permissions, app ops, components and its decoded manifest, and use <b>App Info</b> to open Android's own page for the app.</li>\n<li>Export the list or share it as a CSV file, copy package names, make saved lists.</li>\n<li><b>Extract APK</b>: copy an installed app's file out of the phone.</li>\n<li>Browse files that the app is allowed to read (see <a href=\"#app-permissions\">permissions</a>), including added storage such as an SD card.</li>\n<li>Download the Debloater list and read its advice, check for updates, change themes and the app's own settings, and read the Device Specs in About.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>What you cannot do</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Freeze, enable, suspend, force stop, clear data, uninstall, reinstall, or launch an app through the menu.</li>\n<li>Run batch actions, apply profiles, or make or restore backups.</li>\n<li>Switch permissions, app ops or components on and off.</li>\n<li>Read the log in the Logcat Viewer, change Hidden Settings or overlays, or use the working-mode shell in the Terminal. The Task Manager works only in part: its CPU, RAM, battery and network views work, but its process list and GPU view need a working mode.</li>\n<li>Trim the caches of every app (<b>Trim Caches</b> in the SD Maid tab, see <a href=\"#tab-sdm\">SD Maid</a>). The button shows a padlock.</li>\n<li>Install silently. The <b>No privilege</b> choice of the APK Installer's <b>Authorizer</b> setting hands the file to Android's own installer, which asks you to confirm. This app is listed under Android's \"Install unknown apps\" (it has its own row, <b>Install unknown apps</b>, in the <a href=\"#app-permissions\">Permissions sheet</a>), and Android asks whether to allow it the first time an install is made this way.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>The orange banner</h4>\n<p>At the top of the Application Manager tab, in Read-Only, an orange banner says \"Active Mode: Read-Only (Unprivileged)\" and \"All installed apps are shown. To enable Freeze, Uninstall, or ADB commands, connect via ADB TCP 5555, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku, or Root.\"</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Set Up</b> opens the Working Modes sheet.</li>\n<li>The <b>X</b> in its top right corner puts the banner away until the app is started again or Read-Only mode returns. It comes back the next time the app starts, and also if a working mode comes on and later goes away, so Read-Only does not hide itself for good.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>The lock symbol</h4>\n<p>In Read-Only, buttons that would change something show a small lock and look dimmed: the permission buttons (GRANTED, REVOKED, DENIED), the app-op mode buttons (the lock is on the mode that is set), and the ENABLE, DISABLE and STOP buttons of an app's components, and LAUNCH (SHELL) for components that are not exported. A tap still helps: instead of doing nothing it opens a sheet called <b>Working Mode Required</b>, which says which action needs one and offers <b>Set Up Working Mode</b> or <b>Cancel</b>. With a working mode on, the locks go away, except on <i>install-time</i> permissions, which nobody can change (<a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Starting in Read-Only costs nothing and risks nothing. You can look around, learn what your phone has, and set a working mode up when you are ready. See <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a> to choose.</div>"},{"id":"app-permissions","title":"The permissions this app asks for","group":"Connect the app to your phone","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Every Android permission and special access the app asks for, what it is for, what stops working without it, and whether you can say no. Nearly all of them are optional.</p>\n\n<h4>Three kinds of permission</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Ordinary permissions</b> are granted automatically when you install. You never see a question, and Android does not let you switch them off.</li>\n<li><b>Permissions with a pop-up</b> are asked about by Android when needed (\"Allow notifications?\"). You may say no.</li>\n<li><b>Special accesses</b> have no pop-up. Android keeps each on a screen in its Settings. The app offers them in a sheet.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>The permissions sheet</h4>\n<p>The first time the app opens, a couple of seconds after the list appears, a sheet called <b>Allow a few permissions</b> slides up. You can open it again at any time from the <b>About</b> tab, with the <b>Permissions</b> button; there it is called <b>Permissions</b>, and it says \"Allow what you like and skip the rest: every one can be changed later.\"</p>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Each row has a name, a one-line reason, and either an <b>Allow</b> button or a tick and the word \"Allowed\".</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Allow</b> on a row to open the Android screen for it (switch it on, then press Back to return), or, for the ones the app can grant itself, to grant it at once.</li>\n<li><b>Allow all</b>: with a working mode on, the app grants at once everything that has a command (Allow Restricted Settings first, then the rest) and starts again so that it all takes effect. What has no command, or what the phone refused, is opened one Android screen after another; closing the sheet cancels the rest.</li>\n<li><b>Not now</b> closes the sheet. When everything is allowed, the same button says <b>Done</b>. If you allowed something while the first-launch sheet was open, closing it starts the app again so that the change takes effect everywhere.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>On Android 10 and older there is no \"All files\" switch, so <b>Allow</b> shows Android's ordinary storage question instead.</p>\n<p>Separately, when you do something that needs file access and the app has neither that nor a working mode, it opens the same sheet with only that one row, headed \"File access needed\", and carries on once you allow it.</p>\n\n<h4>The special accesses</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\"><thead><tr><th>On screen</th><th>What it is for</th><th>Without it</th><th>Optional?</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>All files access</td><td>Browse storage in the File Manager, find package files on it, install from it.</td><td>The File Manager and the Installer's search cannot read storage unless a working mode is on (a working mode reads it for you). Added storage, such as an SD card, and the file chooser still work.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Allow Restricted Settings</td><td>Lifts Android's block on a few sensitive switches, Usage access among them, for apps installed from outside a store (Android 13 and newer). It sits above Usage access in the sheet, because Usage access may need it first.</td><td>Some switches for this app, Usage access included, stay greyed out as \"Restricted setting\".</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Usage access</td><td>Show how big each app is and when it was last used.</td><td>Sizes fall back to the app's file size, and the storage figures are missing.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Display over other apps</td><td>Lets the app open another app's screen from the background, such as an app you have just installed.</td><td>That kind of \"open it now\" may not appear.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Install unknown apps</td><td>Lets the app install the package files you choose (APK, APKS and XAPK files).</td><td>When the app hands a file to Android's own installer, Android asks first, and refuses until you allow it. Installs made through a working mode are not affected.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Read/Write External Storage</td><td>The older storage permission that some apps still check. On Android 10 and older, it is the storage permission itself.</td><td>Nothing noticeable on Android 11 and newer.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Write Secure Settings</td><td>Lets the app change a few protected system settings used by some advanced features.</td><td>Those advanced features. Nothing in everyday use is known to need it.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>All files access, Usage access, Display over other apps and Install unknown apps are always in the sheet. The others (Allow Restricted Settings, Read/Write External Storage, Write Secure Settings) only appear while a working mode is on, because the app grants them by running a command through that mode (most of them have no screen of their own in Android).</p>\n<p><b>The Install unknown apps row.</b> Tap <b>Allow</b>. With a working mode on, the app grants it at once (if that does not take, Android's screen opens as below). Without one, Android's own <b>Install unknown apps</b> screen for this app opens: switch it on (the switch reads <b>Allow from this source</b> on most phones), press Back, and the row shows a tick. If your copy of the app is an old build that does not report this access, the row is not shown. On a phone older than Android 8 Android has one switch for all apps, so the row would simply say Allowed (this app itself needs Android 8 or newer).</p>\n\n<h4>What the app can grant for itself</h4>\n<p>With a working mode on (ADB, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root), all of these but All files access can be switched on by the app itself, by running a command as the shell user (UID 2000) or as root. Only All files access always needs Android's own screen.</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\"><thead><tr><th>Access</th><th>Command the app runs</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Allow Restricted Settings</td><td><code>appops set com.bloatware.bingblop ACCESS_RESTRICTED_SETTINGS allow</code> (counts as allowed when the command passes)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Usage access</td><td><code>appops set com.bloatware.bingblop GET_USAGE_STATS allow</code> and <code>pm grant com.bloatware.bingblop android.permission.PACKAGE_USAGE_STATS</code>. When you allow Usage access by hand in Android's settings, the app runs the <code>pm grant</code> in the background too.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Display over other apps</td><td><code>appops set com.bloatware.bingblop SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW allow</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Install unknown apps</td><td><code>appops set com.bloatware.bingblop REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES allow</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Read/Write External Storage</td><td><code>pm grant com.bloatware.bingblop android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE</code>, and the same with WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Write Secure Settings</td><td><code>pm grant com.bloatware.bingblop android.permission.WRITE_SECURE_SETTINGS</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>If the grant does not take, the app opens the matching Android screen (or the app's own info page) instead. Write Secure Settings has no screen at all, so for that one nothing opens.</p>\n\n<h4>Permissions that come with a pop-up</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\"><thead><tr><th>On screen</th><th>What it is for</th><th>Without it</th><th>Optional?</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Notifications (Android 13 and newer)</td><td>The code box for <b>Pair via Notification</b>; progress and a Cancel button for long file jobs; a reminder after a system update if you are Watching a profile.</td><td><b>Pair via Notification</b> cannot be used (the app asks again). The update reminder is skipped, though the banner inside the app still tells you.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nearby devices (Bluetooth, Android 12 and newer)</td><td>In <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>, lists the Bluetooth devices this phone is paired with, to find a Wear OS watch, and sends files to one.</td><td>The Bluetooth part of Connected Devices cannot list devices. The Wi-Fi part works.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Run commands in Termux</td><td>The Terminal tab's shell that uses your own Termux. Termux itself must also allow it (the setting <code>allow-external-apps</code> in its properties file).</td><td>That one shell is unavailable. The other shells work.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Storage (Android 10 and older)</td><td>Android's ordinary storage question, the same as All files access above.</td><td>As All files access.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>On Android 11 and older, the Bluetooth permission is an ordinary one, and no question is asked.</p>\n\n<h4>The Shizuku permission</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\"><thead><tr><th>On screen</th><th>What it is for</th><th>Without it</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>A question from the Shizuku app: allow ADB Application Manager Pro to use Shizuku?</td><td>Lets the app run commands through Shizuku. The Android names are <code>moe.shizuku.manager.permission.API_V23</code> and <code>rikka.shizuku.permission.USE_SHIZUKU</code>. It is asked when you tap <b>Authorize &amp; Use Shizuku</b> (<a href=\"#mode-shizuku\">Shizuku</a>).</td><td>Shizuku mode is not available. Every other mode still works.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<h4>Install unknown apps</h4>\n<p>The app declares the permission that Android calls <b>Install unknown apps</b> (its code name is <code>REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES</code>), and the Permissions sheet has a row for it (see the table above). It is used only when the app hands an APK file to Android's own installer, which happens when an install is made without a working mode (for example the APK Installer's <b>No privilege</b> choice, or the app's own update without a working mode). Android lists this app under Install unknown apps, in its Settings, and asks whether to allow it the first time. If you say no, those installs are refused; installs through a working mode are not affected. Allowing it from the sheet beforehand avoids that question.</p>\n\n<h4>Ordinary permissions you are never asked about</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\"><thead><tr><th>Permission</th><th>What it is for</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Internet; network state; Wi-Fi state</td><td>Downloads (the Debloater list, update checks, app catalogs, the app's own update), knowing whether Wi-Fi is on, and finding this phone's Wi-Fi address for <b>Use My Wi-Fi IP</b>. Nothing is sent to a server of ours.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Query all packages</td><td>Seeing the full list of installed apps. Without it, Android 11 and newer would hide most of them.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vibrate</td><td>The short tap you feel on many buttons and on a long press.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wake lock; foreground service (data sync)</td><td>A long file job (copy, extract) keeps running, with a notification and a Cancel button, when you leave the app.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Receive boot completed</td><td>After a restart, notice that the system was updated, so a watched profile can be checked. It only compares the build number and posts a reminder; it changes nothing.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<h4>What it does not ask for</h4>\n<p>The app has no permission for the camera, the microphone, your location, contacts, calendar, messages, call log or accounts. It is not an accessibility service or a device administrator.</p>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Do not mix up the two uses of \"Install unknown apps\". When you download this app, the switch that matters is the one for the app you download <i>with</i>, such as your browser (<a href=\"#install-and-update\">Installing and updating the app</a>). The switch for this app itself (the row <b>Install unknown apps</b> in the Permissions sheet) only matters when it hands a file to Android's installer. Setting up a working mode avoids that step, because the app then installs the file itself.</div>\n<p>To take anything back, open Android's Settings, then <b>Apps</b>, then <b>ADB Application Manager Pro</b>, and look under <b>Permissions</b>. Special accesses are under Settings, Apps, <b>Special app access</b> (the names vary by phone), or just use the sheet in About again. See also <a href=\"#privacy\">Privacy</a>.</p>"},{"id":"tab-apps","title":"Application Manager","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The first tab lists every app on your phone. You can search, filter and sort the list, and pick apps to freeze, suspend, stop or remove, one at a time or many at once.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> the first tab in the tab bar, <b>Application Manager</b>. The app opens on it, and it cannot be switched off or moved.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Looking at the list, searching, filtering, sorting, selecting and exporting work in every mode, even <a href=\"#mode-readonly\">Read-Only</a>.</li>\n    <li>Changing an app (freeze, suspend, stop, clear data, uninstall) needs a <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>: ADB TCP, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root. Without one, a box called <b>Working Mode Required</b> opens instead.</li>\n    <li>The RUNNING badge and the UNINSTALLED apps are only found while a working mode is on, because the app has to ask Android through it. In Read-Only mode the Running count stays at 0.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>Every Android app has a <b>package name</b>, its unique ID, for example <code>com.android.chrome</code>. The list shows it under each app's name, and many features work with it (see the <a href=\"#glossary\">glossary</a>).</p>\n\n  <h4>Freeze, suspend, uninstall: what they mean here</h4>\n  <p>This app has three ways to switch an app off. They are not the same, and each can be undone.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>What happens to the app</th><th>Badge</th><th>Undo with</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Freeze</b></td><td>The app is disabled (the app runs <code>pm disable-user</code>). It vanishes from your launcher, cannot start and cannot run in the background. It stays installed and keeps its data. In a batch, the confirmation box, the progress box and the History call this \"Disable\" or \"Disabled\", while the results box heading still says FREEZE.</td><td>FROZEN</td><td>Enable</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Suspend</b></td><td>The app stays enabled but is paused: it cannot be opened until you unsuspend it. Its icon and name look greyed out in the list.</td><td>SUSPENDED</td><td>Unsuspend</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Uninstall</b></td><td>The app is removed for you (<code>pm uninstall --user 0</code>). The files of a system app stay in the system, so it can be brought back. An app you installed yourself is normally removed for good.</td><td>UNINSTALLED</td><td>Reinstall (system apps only)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>Two more actions are not switches. <b>Force Stop</b> ends the app right now (it can start again later). <b>Clear Data</b> deletes the app's saved data and settings, like Clear storage in Android's own app settings. Clear Data cannot be undone.</p>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Freezing, suspending or uninstalling a system app can break calls, texts, the keyboard, notifications, the camera or even the home screen. Before you touch one, look it up in the <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a> (read <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">its safety page</a> first), or read the UAD-NG chip in the app menu. Try Freeze before Uninstall: you can see whether anything breaks, and one tap on Enable puts the app back.</div>\n  <div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> Uninstall on an app you installed yourself deletes it and its data, and Reinstall cannot bring it back. Clear Data cannot be undone either. If you may want the data again, make a backup first (see <a href=\"#apps-backup\">Backups and restore</a>). The general advice is on the <a href=\"#safety\">Safety</a> page.</div>\n\n  <h4>The boxes at the top</h4>\n  <p>Above the list are three whole boxes and two split boxes with two halves each. Each shows a count, and each is also a filter: tap it and the list shows only those apps, tap it again to turn it off. It lights up while its filter is on, in its own colour: green for Enabled and Running, cyan for Frozen, orange for 3rd Party, purple for System and red for Bloatware (Uninstalled), with a ring around it, so you can tell at a glance which one is in use. The boxes and the filter pills under them are the same filters, so they always agree.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Total Installed</b>: every app in the list. It also counts the UNINSTALLED apps, because they stay in the list so you can bring them back. Tapping it turns all filters off.</li>\n    <li><b>Running</b>: apps that have a live process right now. Needs a working mode.</li>\n    <li><b>Bloatware (Uninstalled)</b>: system apps that were removed for you (by this app or by another tool) but whose files are still in the system. \"Bloatware\" means apps that come pre-installed and that many people do not want. Needs a working mode.</li>\n    <li><b>Enabled | Frozen</b>: a split box with two halves. Enabled counts apps that are on (a suspended app still counts as Enabled, it is only paused). Frozen counts apps that are disabled.</li>\n    <li><b>3rd Party | System</b>: another split box. 3rd Party counts apps that you or a store installed. System counts apps that came with the phone.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>UNINSTALLED apps are counted in Total Installed and in Bloatware (Uninstalled) only, not in the four halves.</p>\n  <p><b>Bloatware (Uninstalled) starts a clean list.</b> Tapping it (or the Uninstalled pill) turns every other filter off first, so you see all the uninstalled apps, not only those that also match a filter you forgot was on. You can still tap another box or pill afterwards to narrow the list, for example System to see only the uninstalled system apps.</p>\n  <p><b>A pair keeps one side.</b> Enabled and Frozen are a pair, and so are 3rd Party and System. Only one half of a pair can be on. Tap Enabled, then Frozen, and Frozen takes the place of Enabled. Tap the half that is on to turn it off.</p>\n  <p><b>Clear filters.</b> When more than one filter is on, a line appears above the pills saying what is combined, for example \"Showing apps that are Running + 3rd Party.\", with a <b>Clear filters</b> chip. Tap the chip (or Total Installed, or <b>All Apps</b>) to turn them all off.</p>\n\n  <h4>The filter pills</h4>\n  <p>Under the Sort row is a strip of pills that scrolls sideways. A tip says so; tap its X to hide it, or just scroll the strip once. Tap a pill to turn it on or off. The number in brackets is how many apps are in that group.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Pill</th><th>Shows the apps that...</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>All Apps</b></td><td>...are in the list. Tapping it turns every other filter off.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Updated 7d</b></td><td>...were installed or updated in the last 7 days.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Running</b></td><td>...have a live process now.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>3rd Party</b></td><td>...you or a store installed. The row badge for these says USER.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>System</b></td><td>...came with the phone.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Enabled</b></td><td>...are on: not frozen and not uninstalled. A suspended app is still Enabled.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Frozen</b></td><td>...are disabled.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Suspended</b></td><td>...are paused with Suspend.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Patched</b></td><td>...look patched or repackaged, for example by ReVanced, or Xposed/LSPosed modules. They carry a teal tag.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Uninstalled</b></td><td>...are system apps removed for you (the UNINSTALLED badge).</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>Filters stack: an app has to match every filter that is on. Running and 3rd Party together show your own apps that are running now. System and Frozen show the system apps you have frozen. 3rd Party, System and Frozen leave out UNINSTALLED apps unless Uninstalled is on as well. Turning Uninstalled on turns every other pill off first (add another one afterwards to combine).</p>\n\n  <h4>Search</h4>\n  <p>The search box sits under the pills. It narrows the list while you type, and it searches inside whatever the filters already show. The X in the box clears the text. If nothing matches you see \"No Applications Found\".</p>\n  <p>Tap the three dots at the right of the box to open <b>Search options</b>. Each option is a switch:</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Include application names</b></td><td>On</td><td>Looks in the names you see.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Include package names</b></td><td>On</td><td>Looks in the package names too. The box then says \"Search by name or package\".</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Use regex matching</b></td><td>On</td><td>Reads what you type as a pattern. Off: the whole name (or the whole package name) has to be typed.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>One of the first two always stays on: switching off the last one turns the other back on. A <b>regex</b> (regular expression) is a small pattern language that lets one search match many things. Plain words work as you would expect, and capital letters do not matter, so <code>cam</code> finds Camera. Special characters match more: <code>mail|chat</code> finds apps with either word, and <code>^com\\.samsung</code> finds packages that start with <code>com.samsung</code>. You can ignore all of this and just type words. If a pattern is not valid, or could take too long, the app searches it as plain words and shows a note under the box. With regex off, <code>cam</code> finds nothing but <code>Camera</code> does. Your choices are remembered.</p>\n\n  <h4>Sort, Share CSV, Profiles, Backups, Action Button</h4>\n  <p>The row above the pills holds a menu, three buttons and the Action Button menu. It is wider than the screen on a small phone: swipe it sideways to reach the end.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Sort</b> (menu)</td><td>Orders the list: <b>Name (A-Z)</b>, <b>Last updated</b>, <b>Install date</b>, <b>Size</b> or <b>Updates first</b> (apps the App Updater has a newer version for come first). For Last updated, Install date and Size, each row also shows the value it is sorted by, such as \"updated Mar 3, 2026\". Size counts the app, its data and its cache once usage access is on, and only the app's file before that. Your choice is remembered.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Share CSV</b></td><td>Builds a CSV file (a plain table that opens in any spreadsheet) of every app, not only the ones the filters show, named like <code>app_list_2026-10-06.csv</code>, and opens Android's share sheet, so you can send it on or save it to a folder.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Profiles</b></td><td>Opens the profile box. See <a href=\"#apps-profiles\">Profiles and drift watch</a>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Backups</b></td><td>Opens the backup box. See <a href=\"#apps-backup\">Backups and restore</a>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Action Button</b> (menu, at the end of the row; it shows the current choice, with no label before it)</td><td>Tap it and a sheet opens with a title, a few words about what it edits, and every choice with a line saying what it does. Chooses what the extra button on every row does: <b>App Settings</b>, <b>Force Stop</b>, <b>App Launcher</b>, <b>Enable / Disable</b>, <b>Install / Uninstall</b>, <b>Unsuspend / Suspend</b>, <b>Permission Manager</b>, <b>Activity Launcher</b>, or <b>None (no button)</b>. It is the same setting as the <b>Action Button</b> card in Settings: change it in either place and the other follows. The rows change at once, and a message names the choice.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>The CSV columns are Name, Package, Version, Installed, Last updated, Type (system or user), State (enabled, disabled, suspended or uninstalled), APK size (bytes) and Update available.</p>\n  <p>The list is drawn 250 rows at a time. More load as you scroll, or when you tap the <b>Show (number) more</b> button at the bottom.</p>\n\n  <h4>What one row shows</h4>\n  <p>From left to right: the app's icon, its name, its package name in grey and a line of badges (plus the date or size you sort by), then the Action Button, the three-dot button and a checkbox. A suspended app has a greyed icon and a faded name.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Badge</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>RUNNING</td><td>The app has a live process now (working mode needed).</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>FROZEN</td><td>The app is disabled.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>SUSPENDED</td><td>The app is paused and cannot be opened.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>UNINSTALLED</td><td>A system app removed for you. It can be brought back with Reinstall. An uninstalled app never also shows FROZEN.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>SYSTEM</td><td>The app came with the phone.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>USER</td><td>You or a store installed it. This is what the filters call 3rd Party.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>UAD-NG and a level (RECOMMENDED, ADVANCED, EXPERT or UNSAFE)</td><td>The UAD-NG community list knows this app and rates how safe it is to remove (see <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a>). Tap it for the full description, exactly as in the app menu. It shows only after the list has been downloaded once, and only for apps the list contains.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Teal tag (ReVanced, Morphe, LSPatch, NPatch, Xposed...)</td><td>The app looks patched or repackaged by that tool (the Patched filter finds them). The app menu checks deeper.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>The two buttons on the right of a row</h4>\n  <p><b>The three-dot button</b> opens the app menu: everything about that one app. See <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>.</p>\n  <p><b>The Action Button</b> is the extra icon next to it: one tap for the thing you do most. It starts as a cog, <b>App Settings</b>, which opens Android's own App info screen for that app. You choose what it does in Settings (the gear at the top right), in the <b>Action Button</b> card. See <a href=\"#prefs\">Settings</a>.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>App Settings</b>: Android's App info screen.</li>\n    <li><b>Force Stop</b> and <b>App Launcher</b> (opens the app).</li>\n    <li><b>Enable / Disable (dynamic)</b>, <b>Install / Uninstall (dynamic)</b> and <b>Unsuspend / Suspend (dynamic)</b>: the icon and the action follow the app's state. A frozen app shows Enable, an enabled one shows Disable.</li>\n    <li><b>Permission Manager</b> and <b>Activity Launcher</b>: open a list sheet for that app, with the same permissions, app ops and activities as the app menu, plus buttons such as Grant all shown and Revoke all shown.</li>\n    <li><b>None (no button)</b>: hides it.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p><b>Press and hold</b> the Action Button for about half a second to get a sheet with every action for that app. In the same Settings card, <b>Hold menu</b>, you choose which actions the sheet lists and in what order; <b>Reset to Default</b> puts it back.</p>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Force Stop, Enable / Disable, Suspend and Reinstall run at once from the Action Button, with no confirmation. Only Uninstall asks \"Uninstall (app name)?\" first. App Settings, App Launcher, Permission Manager and Activity Launcher work to look at in any mode. Every other choice, and changing anything in the two sheets, needs a working mode.</div>\n\n  <h4>Tap, hold and select</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Tap a row</b> (its name or its checkbox) to select it. The row gets a coloured border and the checkbox shows a tick. This does not change the app.</li>\n    <li><b>Press and hold a row</b> for about half a second to open its app menu, the same as the three dots. You feel a short tap when it works. Hold the name or the empty space of the row. The icon, the buttons and the checkbox have their own jobs.</li>\n    <li><b>Press and hold an app's icon</b> to save it as a PNG picture in <code>Download/ADB App Manager/Icons</code> (on Android 8 and 9, in the app's own storage instead). A message names the saved file.</li>\n    <li>Once an app is selected, a round button with a tick and a number appears at the bottom right. It opens the batch actions: see <a href=\"#apps-batch\">Selecting many apps and batch actions</a>.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>The Read-Only banner</h4>\n  <p>When no working mode is ready, a card at the top of the tab says <b>Active Mode: Read-Only (Unprivileged)</b>. Its text says all installed apps are shown, and that to use Freeze, Uninstall or ADB commands you connect through ADB TCP 5555, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root. Its <b>Set Up</b> button opens the Working Modes sheet (see <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>). The X in the top right corner of the banner puts it away for now only: it stays away until the app is started again, or until read-only mode returns after a working mode was on. The banner also goes by itself while a working mode is on.</p>\n\n  <h4>Remember my filters</h4>\n  <p>By default the Application Manager opens on Total Installed, with no filter on, every time the app starts. To open with the filters you left on, open Settings (the gear), find the <b>Lists</b> card and switch on <b>Remember my filters</b>. It also covers the UAD-NG Debloater and Saved Applications filters. The sort order, the Versions pill, the search options and \"Keep selection after running\" are always remembered.</p>\n\n  <h4>Step by step: freeze one app</h4>\n  <p>You need a working mode. The mode badge at the top right shows which one is on (see <a href=\"#screen-tour\">The screen tour</a>).</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Type a few letters of the app's name in the search box. The list shrinks as you type.</li>\n    <li>Tap the three-dot button on its row. The app menu slides up from the bottom.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Freeze</b>. A short message says \"Working on it&hellip;\", then \"Checking the phone&hellip;\", then what the phone reports, for example \"Alpha: Frozen\". The menu stays open.</li>\n    <li>Look at the row behind the menu: it now has a FROZEN badge, and in the menu the Freeze button has turned into <b>Enable</b>. The Frozen count at the top went up by one.</li>\n    <li>To undo, tap <b>Enable</b> in the same menu.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>For <b>Freeze</b>, <b>Enable</b>, <b>Uninstall</b>, <b>Reinstall</b>, <b>Suspend</b> and <b>Unsuspend</b> the message comes from the phone, not from the command: after the command the app reads the list of apps (and, for Suspend, the app's own suspended flag) and tells you what it found: <i>Frozen</i>, <i>Not frozen</i>, <i>Uninstalled</i>, <i>Still installed</i>, <i>Suspended</i> and so on. A command that printed an error for an app that is in fact gone still counts as done. If Android refuses, you see \"Action reported a problem\" (or, for these actions, the finding, such as \"Still installed\") and a <b>Command Result</b> box with Android's own words. Nothing was changed. Some system apps are protected and cannot be frozen. <b>Clear data</b> is read back too, but only in <b>Root</b> mode, where the app's own data folder can be looked at: the app counts the files and the size in it before and after and tells you, for example, <i>Data cleared</i> (12 files, 3.4 MB before, 0 after), <i>Partly cleared</i> or <i>Not cleared</i>. In ADB and Shizuku modes the folder cannot be read, so Clear data and Force stop are judged by what the command answers.</p>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Freeze, Enable, Suspend, Unsuspend, Uninstall and Reinstall are written to a history with one-tap Undo. Open the <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a> tab and tap <b>History</b>. If that tab is missing, turn it on again in the <a href=\"#feature-list\">Feature List</a>.</div>\n\n  <h4>If something is wrong</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>\"No Applications Loaded\".</b> Tap <b>Reload Packages</b>. If it keeps failing, close the app and open it again.</li>\n    <li><b>\"Working Mode Required\" opens when you tap a button.</b> You are in Read-Only mode, or your mode is not ready. Tap <b>Set Up Working Mode</b> and follow <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>. Nothing shows as Running or Uninstalled without a mode either.</li>\n    <li><b>An app you froze or removed came back.</b> A system update can do that. A <a href=\"#apps-profiles\">watched profile</a> tells you and fixes it in one review.</li>\n    <li>More help is in <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>.</li>\n  </ul>"},{"id":"apps-menu","title":"The app menu (one app)","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The app menu is a sheet with everything about one app: its details, buttons to freeze, stop or remove it, and tabs for its permissions, app ops, activities, components, manifest, features, configurations, signatures and libraries.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> in the <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>, tap the three-dot button on an app's row, or press and hold the row for about half a second (not on the icon, a button or the checkbox). Tap the X at the top right, or tap outside the sheet, to close it.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Reading works in every mode: the header, the permission list, activities, components, the manifest, the Raw tab, and the Features, Configurations, Signatures and Libraries tabs.</li>\n    <li>Changing something needs a <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>. Buttons that need one show a small padlock symbol or open the \"Working Mode Required\" box. The table at the end of this page says which is which.</li>\n    <li>The menu stays open after an action button such as Freeze runs, so you can do several things in a row. Its buttons update by themselves: Freeze turns into Enable, and so on.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>The header</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Name and package name.</b> The package name is the app's unique ID. Under them is the version, for example \"Version 1.2.3 (12345)\": the name people see, then the build number in brackets.</li>\n    <li><b>Copy package, Copy name.</b> Two small chips that copy that text to the clipboard.</li>\n    <li><b>Dates.</b> \"Installed (date)\" and, when it is different, \"Updated (date)\".</li>\n    <li><b>Sizes.</b> A line like \"APK 12 MB (3 parts)\", then Data and Cache sizes, with dots between the pieces. The APK is the app's file; \"3 parts\" means a split app. Data and Cache need <b>usage access</b>, a special Android permission. Until it is on, the line ends with a <b>Show data usage</b> link. Tap it and the app turns usage access on through your working mode, or opens Android's settings screen where you switch it on yourself.</li>\n    <li><b>Update line.</b> If the <a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a> has found a newer version, a line says so and shows a button. <b>Update</b> goes to the App Updater tab and starts the update (it needs a working mode). <b>Release</b> opens the release page, when the newest release has no file for your phone. <b>Download</b> shows up only for this app itself and takes you to its own update card. The App Updater tab must be switched on.</li>\n    <li><b>Patched / modified.</b> If the app looks changed by a patching tool, a box lists tags such as ReVanced, Morphe, LSPatch, NPatch, Xposed/LSPosed module or \"Debug-signed (repackaged)\", and who installed it (\"Installed by ...\"). Opening the menu does a deeper check than the list does: it reads the app's file, so a patched app can show a tag here that the list did not show.</li>\n    <li><b>Share</b> (top right, next to the X). Opens Android's share sheet with the app's name, package name and version as text.</li>\n    <li><b>The UAD-NG chip</b> under Share. See below.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h5>The UAD-NG chip</h5>\n  <p>UAD-NG is a community list that rates how safe it is to remove many phone apps. When the app you opened is in that list, a chip under Share shows \"UAD-NG\" and one word: <b>RECOMMENDED</b>, <b>ADVANCED</b>, <b>EXPERT</b> or <b>UNSAFE</b>. Tap it for the \"UAD-NG classification\" box:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>the level and the group the project files it under (such as Oem, Google, Aosp, Carrier or Misc);</li>\n    <li>the project's description of the app, when it has one;</li>\n    <li>warning lines \"Needed by:\" and \"Depends on:\" naming other packages, when the project lists any;</li>\n    <li>buttons <b>UAD-NG Wiki</b>, <b>Removal Levels</b> and <b>Close</b>. <b>Removal Levels</b> opens another sheet with what the four levels mean, the same lines as under <b>Removal Levels</b> in the <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a> tab: Recommended is safe to remove for most people; Advanced breaks minor features or default apps you can replace; Expert breaks widespread or important features, but not basic operation; Unsafe can break vital parts of Android, with a high risk of a boot-loop (the phone keeps restarting and never finishes starting). <b>Close</b> on that sheet brings you back to the classification.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>No chip appears when the app is not in the list, or when you have not yet downloaded the list. Open the <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a> tab and use <b>Update List</b> once. Read <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloater safety</a> before you act on any rating.</p>\n\n  <h4>The action buttons</h4>\n  <p>A grid of buttons sits under the header. Some swap with their opposite: Freeze and Enable, Suspend and Unsuspend, Uninstall and Reinstall.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th><th>Needs mode</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Launch</b></td><td>Opens the app.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Force Stop</b></td><td>Ends the app now. It may start again later. Run as <code>am force-stop</code>.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Freeze</b> / <b>Enable</b></td><td>Freeze disables the app. Enable switches it on again and also unsuspends it. Freeze shows for enabled apps, Enable for frozen ones.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Suspend</b> / <b>Unsuspend</b></td><td>Pauses the app so it cannot be opened, or lifts that. Not offered for an uninstalled app.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Clear Data</b></td><td>Deletes the app's saved data and settings (<code>pm clear</code>). Asks first. Cannot be undone.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Uninstall</b> / <b>Reinstall</b></td><td>Uninstall removes the app for you (it asks first). Reinstall (<code>pm install-existing</code>) appears for an UNINSTALLED system app and brings it back.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Rem Updates</b></td><td>\"Remove updates\": takes the updates off a system app so it goes back to the version it shipped with (<code>pm uninstall-system-updates</code>). Use it only on a system app that has updates. It asks first, and to get the updates back you update the app again from its store.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>App Info</b></td><td>Opens Android's own App info screen for the app.</td><td>No</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Extract APK</b></td><td>Copies the app's installer file to <code>Download/ADB App Manager/APKs</code> as <code>Name_version.apk</code>. A split app becomes one <code>.apks</code> bundle instead, which the <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a> tab can install. A message names the saved file. On Android 8 and 9 the file goes to the app's own storage instead.</td><td>No</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Backup</b></td><td>Closes the menu and opens the backup box for this app. See <a href=\"#apps-backup\">Backups and restore</a>.</td><td>Only to create the backup</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Optimize</b></td><td>Opens \"Dex optimization\": recompiles the app's code (<code>pm compile</code>), which prepares it in advance. This is an advanced tool and most people can skip it. Choose a <b>Compile mode</b> (verify, speed-profile, speed, everything; speed is the default) and whether to <b>Force</b> it (on by default), then tap <b>Run</b>. It can take a while. A progress bar and a <b>Stop</b> button show.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Logs</b></td><td>Closes the menu and opens the Logcat Viewer tab showing only this app's lines. The Logcat Viewer tab must be switched on, and reading the log needs a working mode. See <a href=\"#tab-logcat\">Logcat Viewer</a>.</td><td>Yes, to read the log</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>After a button runs, a short message says \"Action executed\". If Android refuses, you see \"Action reported a problem\" and a <b>Command Result</b> box with Android's own words. Nothing was changed in that case.</p>\n  <div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> <b>Uninstall</b>, <b>Clear Data</b> and <b>Rem Updates</b> ask \"Are you sure?\" first, with a sentence that says what is lost: the question names the app, so check the name. Tap <b>OK</b> to go on or <b>Cancel</b> to stop with nothing changed. The other buttons (Launch, Force Stop, Freeze, Enable, Suspend, Unsuspend, Reinstall) run at once. Freeze, Suspend and Enable can be undone with their opposite button. Clear Data cannot be undone, and neither can Uninstall for an app you installed yourself.</div>\n\n  <h4>The ten tabs under the buttons</h4>\n  <p>A row of pills switches between <b>Permissions</b>, <b>App Ops</b>, <b>Activities</b>, <b>Components</b>, <b>Manifest</b>, <b>Raw</b>, <b>Features</b>, <b>Configurations</b>, <b>Signatures</b> and <b>Libraries</b>. The row is wider than the screen: swipe it sideways to reach the last ones. Most pills show a count in brackets. The tab you pick slides into view. The first six are about what the app may do and is made of; the last four are about what it needs and who made it, and they only read, so they work in every mode.</p>\n\n  <h5>Permissions</h5>\n  <p>A permission is something an app asks Android to let it do, such as use the camera. The tab lists everything the app declares. Type in the search box to find one, and use the chips <b>All</b>, <b>Granted</b>, <b>Denied</b>, <b>Changeable</b> and <b>Install-time</b> to narrow the list. Changeable permissions are listed first.</p>\n  <p>Each row shows the permission's short name and small tags: its kind (normal, runtime, signature, privileged or development), \"app op\" when it has a matching app op, and \"locked\" when it cannot be changed. On the right is a button:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>GRANTED</b> or <b>DENIED</b>: a runtime permission (the kind Android asks you about, like Camera or Location). Tap it to flip it. A message says \"Revoked (name)\" or \"Granted (name)\". Android can close the app when you take a permission away.</li>\n    <li><b>A padlock with GRANTED or NOT GRANTED</b>: an install-time permission. Android fixed it when the app was installed and does not let anyone change it later, so this button does nothing. The padlock is the reason.</li>\n    <li><b>A padlock on a GRANTED / DENIED button</b>: you could change it, but you have no working mode right now. Set one up and the padlock goes.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h5>App Ops</h5>\n  <p>App ops are a second, finer set of switches that Android keeps for each app. They control things such as running in the background or showing notifications. This tab needs a working mode: without one it says \"App ops need ADB, Shizuku or Root\". It has a search box, a <b>Refresh</b> button and the chips <b>All</b>, <b>Allowed</b>, <b>Ignored / Denied</b> and <b>Foreground</b>.</p>\n  <p>Each row names the op and shows its notes, then five mode buttons. The current one is lit.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Allow</b>: the app may do it.</li>\n    <li><b>FG</b>: only while the app is on screen (foreground).</li>\n    <li><b>Ignore</b>: the app's request is quietly refused.</li>\n    <li><b>Deny</b>: the request is refused with an error.</li>\n    <li><b>Reset</b>: back to Android's default for that op.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>Under the list, a field \"Other op, e.g. RUN_ANY_IN_BACKGROUND\", a mode menu and a <b>Set</b> button let you set an op that is not listed.</p>\n\n  <h5>Activities</h5>\n  <p>An activity is one screen of an app. This tab lists every one of them. The heading shows how many there are and how many are unexported, for example \"ACTIVITIES (12 &bull; 9 unexported)\". Use the search box and the chips <b>All</b>, <b>Exported</b> and <b>Unexported</b>. Exported screens are listed first.</p>\n  <p>Each row has small tags: <b>exported</b> (other apps may open it) or <b>unexported</b> (only the app itself normally can), <b>disabled</b> when it is off, and <b>protected</b> when using it needs a permission. The buttons are:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>LAUNCH</b>: opens that screen. For an unexported activity the button reads <b>LAUNCH (SHELL)</b>.</li>\n    <li><b>DISABLE</b> or <b>ENABLE</b>: switches that one screen off or on (<code>pm disable</code> or <code>pm enable</code> on the single component).</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h5>Components</h5>\n  <p>A component is one working part of an app that is not a screen. The tab lists three kinds: <b>Receivers</b> (parts that listen for events such as the phone starting up), <b>Services</b> (parts that work in the background) and <b>Providers</b> (parts that share data with other apps). Each heading shows how many there are, and the tab's pill shows the total. Use the search box and the chips <b>All</b>, <b>Exported</b> and <b>Unexported</b>. The tags are the same as on the Activities tab. The buttons are:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>STOP</b> (services only): stops that service.</li>\n    <li><b>DISABLE</b> or <b>ENABLE</b> (all three kinds): switches that one part off or on. Providers and receivers have only this button.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h5>Launching hidden activities</h5>\n  <p>Many apps have screens that are not meant to be opened from outside, such as a hidden settings page. These are the unexported ones. The app can still open them for you through your working mode. How it works: it briefly sets the screen as your phone's \"assistant\" (and sets the voice-assistant setting aside), presses the assist key so Android itself starts it, waits about a second and a half, then puts your own settings back. An exported screen is first started directly with <code>am start</code>, and only if that fails does the app try the assistant way.</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open the app menu, then the <b>Activities</b> tab.</li>\n    <li>Type part of a screen's name, for example <code>settings</code>, in the search box.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>LAUNCH (SHELL)</b> next to the screen. A message says \"Launching (name)...\".</li>\n    <li>A message says \"Activity launched\" when the request went through, and the screen should open. If Android refused, the message says \"Launch failed\" and a box shows what Android answered. If the message says \"Activity launched\" but no screen opens, the screen probably needs extra information to start.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> This is a trick that does not work on every phone. The app says as much when it fails: the screen may need extra information to open, or another app may be holding the assist gesture. Without a working mode an unexported screen cannot be opened at all, and exported ones are opened by a plain request that the app cannot check.</div>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> If your phone's default assistant app looks different afterwards (for example if the phone was restarted at that moment), set it again in Android's settings, under default apps. If the voice-assistant setting is gone, set it there too. Also, DISABLE on an activity, service or receiver can stop an app from working until you press ENABLE again. Leave a part alone if you do not know what it does.</div>\n\n  <h5>Manifest</h5>\n  <p>The manifest is the app's own description of itself: its screens, permissions and parts, written in a text format called XML. Opening the tab decodes it from the app's file (a message says \"Decoding AndroidManifest.xml...\"). This works in every mode.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The box <b>Find in manifest...</b> searches it. The two arrow chips jump to the previous or next match, and a counter such as \"2 / 7 matches\" shows where you are.</li>\n    <li><b>Matches only</b> (on by default) shows only the matching lines with their line numbers. Turn it off to see the whole file with the matches marked.</li>\n    <li><b>Copy</b> copies the text, <b>Share</b> sends it as a file, and <b>Save to Downloads</b> writes <code>(package)_AndroidManifest.xml</code> into <code>Download/ADB App Manager</code>.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h5>Raw</h5>\n  <p>The raw details the app collected about this app, as plain text: version, folders, SDK levels, installer, tags, every permission and component, and the app-ops text. Useful when you need to copy something exact, or when you report a problem.</p>\n\n  <h5>Features</h5>\n  <p>A feature is a piece of hardware or software that an app says it needs, such as a touch screen, a camera, Bluetooth Low Energy or a certain version of OpenGL ES (the graphics system games use). The tab lists them with the names Android uses, for example <code>android.hardware.camera</code>. The first line says how many there are and how many are not on your phone. Each row has tags:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>required</b> or <b>optional</b>: whether the app asks for the feature as a must-have. An app that requires a feature your phone lacks is hidden from that phone in the Play Store. Optional ones only switch a part of the app off.</li>\n    <li><b>on this phone</b> (green) or <b>not on this phone</b> (orange): whether your phone has it. For the OpenGL ES row the app compares the version it asks for with the version your phone supports.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>Use the search box and the chips <b>All</b>, <b>Required</b>, <b>Optional</b> and <b>Not on this phone</b> to narrow the list. An app with no such entries shows \"This app does not list any hardware or software features it needs\". The list is read once, the first time you open any of the four last tabs for this app.</p>\n\n  <h5>Configurations</h5>\n  <p>This tab gathers what the app says about the screen and the controls it is made for, in small cards:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Input and graphics it asks for</b>: touch screen (any, finger or stylus), keyboard, navigation (D-pad, trackball, wheel) and OpenGL ES version. Most apps ask for nothing here, and the tab says so in a sentence.</li>\n    <li><b>Screens</b>: the smallest screen width it needs in dp (a unit of screen size that does not depend on sharpness), the largest width it supports, and whether it supports small, normal, large and extra large screens, any screen density, resizing and right-to-left languages.</li>\n    <li><b>Android versions</b>: the oldest Android it runs on (minimum), the one it was written for (target) and the one it was built with (compiled against), each as \"API 34 (Android 14)\".</li>\n    <li><b>How it runs</b>: whether it uses hardware acceleration and asks for a large memory heap.</li>\n    <li><b>This phone</b>: the CPU types and OpenGL ES version of your phone, for comparison.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h5>Signatures</h5>\n  <p>Every app is signed by its maker with a key, and Android only allows an update that is signed with the same key. This tab shows that signature:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Signature schemes in the APK</b>: four pills, <b>v1</b>, <b>v2</b>, <b>v3</b> and <b>v3.1</b>, green with a tick when the app's file carries that kind of signature and grey with a dash when it does not. v1 is the old signature kept in the META-INF folder; v2, v3 and v3.1 sit in a block inside the file, and v3 and v3.1 allow the signing key to be changed later. For a split app only the base file is read.</li>\n    <li><b>Signer</b>: a card for the certificate the app is signed with: <b>Subject</b> (who it was made for) and <b>Issuer</b> (who made it; the same for a self-signed certificate, which is what almost every app has), <b>Serial number</b>, <b>Valid from</b> and <b>Valid until</b>, <b>Signature algorithm</b>, <b>Public key</b> (for example RSA, 2048 bits) and three fingerprints: <b>MD5</b>, <b>SHA-1</b> and <b>SHA-256</b>. A fingerprint is a short code that identifies the key. Tap a fingerprint to copy it. Tags show <b>self-signed</b>, <b>expired</b> or <b>not valid yet</b>. Android does not check the dates of an app's certificate, so \"expired\" is only information.</li>\n    <li><b>Earlier certificates</b>: when the maker has changed the signing key (key rotation), the older certificates are listed too.</li>\n    <li><b>Signers</b>: an app can be signed by several keys; each gets its own card.</li>\n    <li><b>Copy all</b> copies the whole thing as text.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>Compare the SHA-256 fingerprint with the one the app's maker publishes to check an app is the real one. The \"Patched / modified\" box in the header also reads the signing key, to spot apps signed with the common debug key (a sign of re-signing by a tool).</p>\n\n  <h5>Libraries</h5>\n  <p>Three lists of code the app uses, with a search box over all of them:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Libraries it asks for</b>: the extra libraries named in the manifest, each tagged <b>shared</b>, <b>static</b> or <b>native</b> and <b>required</b> or <b>optional</b>, with a version for static ones. A required library that your phone lacks stops the app from installing.</li>\n    <li><b>Shared libraries linked in</b>: the library files Android actually linked into the app, with their paths, for example <code>/system/framework/org.apache.http.legacy.jar</code>.</li>\n    <li><b>Native libraries inside the APK</b>: the <code>.so</code> files (machine code that is not Java) in the app's file, grouped by CPU type such as arm64-v8a or armeabi-v7a, each with its size. The line above the list shows the folder where Android puts them and the app's main CPU type. For a split app the files of every part are listed. \"No .so files\" means the app runs on any CPU type or loads its code another way.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>What needs a working mode</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Part of the menu</th><th>Without a mode</th><th>With a mode</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Header, copy chips, Share, dates, sizes</td><td>Works (data and cache need usage access)</td><td>Works</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>App Info, Extract APK, Manifest, Raw, Features, Configurations, Signatures, Libraries</td><td>Works</td><td>Works</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Launch, Force Stop, Freeze, Suspend, Clear Data, Uninstall, Rem Updates, Optimize</td><td>Opens \"Working Mode Required\"</td><td>Works</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Backup, Logs</td><td>Backup opens its box, but Create backup opens \"Working Mode Required\". Logs opens the Logcat Viewer, but it needs a working mode to read the log</td><td>Works</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Permissions</td><td>You can read the list. Toggles show a padlock.</td><td>Runtime ones can be changed</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>App Ops</td><td>Not available</td><td>Works</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Activities, Components</td><td>The lists work. LAUNCH works for exported screens only. STOP and ENABLE / DISABLE show a padlock.</td><td>Everything works</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>Step by step: turn off one permission</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open the app menu for the app, and stay on the <b>Permissions</b> tab.</li>\n    <li>Type <code>camera</code> in the search box, or tap <b>Changeable</b>.</li>\n    <li>Tap the <b>GRANTED</b> button next to CAMERA. It changes to <b>DENIED</b> and a message says \"Revoked CAMERA\".</li>\n    <li>To undo, tap the button again. A message says \"Granted CAMERA\".</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>If Android refuses, the message shows the first line of its answer and nothing changed.</p>\n\n  <h4>If something is wrong</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>A button shows a padlock, or the \"Working Mode Required\" box opens.</b> You have no working mode. See <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>.</li>\n    <li><b>Uninstall says it needs root.</b> Android only lets root remove a system app for one user. The app first tries another way through ADB or Shizuku. If that also fails, the result says so. Switch to <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a> if your phone has it, or use Freeze, which hides the app but keeps it installed.</li>\n    <li><b>The result mentions a device policy or a restriction.</b> A work profile, device owner or parental control blocks it. It cannot be bypassed from here.</li>\n    <li><b>Launch does nothing, or reports a problem.</b> Some apps have no screen to open. Try <b>App Info</b>.</li>\n    <li>More help is in <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>.</li>\n  </ul>"},{"id":"apps-batch","title":"Selecting many apps and batch actions","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">A batch action runs one action, such as Freeze, on every app you have selected, one after the other, with a progress bar and a list of what worked.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> the <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>. Select an app and a round button appears at the bottom right. Tap it to open the batch sheet.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Selecting apps, <b>Save to List</b>, <b>Copy Packages</b>, <b>Share List</b> and <b>Show Apps</b> work in every mode.</li>\n    <li>Every action that changes apps needs a <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>. Without one, the \"Working Mode Required\" box opens.</li>\n    <li>Only one batch can run at a time. If you start another, a message says \"Another batch action is already running\".</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>Selecting apps</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Tap a row to tick its checkbox. Tap again to untick it. The row gets a coloured border while it is selected.</li>\n    <li>A round button with a tick and a number (how many apps are selected) appears at the bottom right of the screen. This button is how you open the batch sheet.</li>\n    <li><b>Hold the round button</b> for about half a second to drop the whole selection at once. A message says \"Cleared (number) selections\".</li>\n    <li>Selected apps stay selected when you change the filters or the search, so you can build a selection in several steps.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>The batch sheet</h4>\n  <p>Tap the round button and a sheet slides up. The top row shows \"(number) Selected\" with three controls:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Select All</b>: selects every app that matches your current filters and search, including those not yet drawn on screen.</li>\n    <li><b>Clear</b>: unselects everything.</li>\n    <li>The <b>X</b>: closes the sheet but keeps the selection. The round button comes back.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>Next come three buttons: <b>Save to List</b> (see <a href=\"#tab-saved-lists\">Saved Applications</a>), <b>Copy Packages</b> (copies the package names, one per line) and <b>Share List</b> (sends the same text through the share sheet). Then a switch, <b>Keep selection after running</b>: \"Run another command on these same apps without reselecting them\". Then twelve action buttons:</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does to each app</th><th>Asks first?</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Freeze</b></td><td>Disables the app (<code>pm disable-user</code>).</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Enable</b></td><td>Switches the app on again and unsuspends it.</td><td>No</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Force Stop</b></td><td>Ends the app now.</td><td>No</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Suspend</b></td><td>Pauses the app so it cannot be opened.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Unsuspend</b></td><td>Lifts the pause.</td><td>No</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Clear Data</b></td><td>Deletes the app's saved data and settings. Cannot be undone.</td><td>No</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Optimize</b></td><td>Opens \"Dex optimization\" for all of them: Compile mode, Force, then <b>Run</b>. Same as the single-app button.</td><td>Its own box</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Uninstall</b></td><td>Removes the app for you.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Reinstall</b></td><td>Brings back removed system apps (<code>pm install-existing</code>).</td><td>No</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Batch Ops</b></td><td>Sets app ops on all of them. See below.</td><td>Its own box</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Show Apps</b></td><td>Lists the selected apps, each with a <b>Remove</b> button.</td><td>Nothing runs</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Command</b></td><td>Runs a command you type on each app. See below.</td><td>Its own box</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> Clear Data, Force Stop, Enable, Unsuspend and Reinstall start at once when you tap them: there is no confirmation list. Clear Data on the wrong selection deletes the saved data of every app in it, and that cannot be undone. Tap <b>Show Apps</b> first to read the list of what is selected.</div>\n\n  <h4>The confirmation list, the progress bar and Stop</h4>\n  <p>Freeze, Suspend and Uninstall open a box first. Its title says \"Confirm Freeze Selected Apps\", \"Confirm Suspend Selected Apps\" or \"Confirm Uninstall Selected Apps\". It lists every app with its package name. Read it. Tap <b>Confirm Freeze</b> (or <b>Confirm Suspend</b>, <b>Confirm Uninstall</b>) to go on, or <b>Cancel</b> to stop with nothing changed.</p>\n  <p>While the batch runs, the same box shows a progress line, for example \"2 of 5: Name (com.example.app)\", a bar, and a <b>Stop</b> button. Stop (or the X at the top of the box) ends the batch once the app being worked on has finished. Every app not reached yet is left as it was. The button changes to \"Stopping...\", and a message later says how many succeeded, failed and were not run.</p>\n\n  <h4>The results box</h4>\n  <p>When the batch ends, a message such as \"Batch Done: 5 succeeded, 0 failed\" appears and a box called <b>Batch Results</b> opens. Its subtitle names the action and the count, for example \"FREEZE &bull; 5 of 5 apps\" (the action keeps the name of the button you tapped). Each app has a card with its name, package name, the answer Android gave (or \"No output returned\") and a tag, <b>Success</b> or <b>Failed</b>. At the bottom:</p>\n  <p><b>The result comes from the phone, not from the command.</b> After <b>Uninstall</b>, <b>Uninstall (keep data)</b>, <b>Reinstall</b>, <b>Freeze</b>, <b>Unfreeze</b>, <b>Suspend</b> and <b>Unsuspend</b>, the app asks the phone what became of each app (the progress sheet says <b>Checking the phone&hellip; (N apps)</b> with a full bar while it does; it reads the list of installed and of frozen apps again, plus each app's suspended flag for Suspend, and waits a second and asks once more if an app is not yet in its new state). The tag then says what it found: <b>Uninstalled</b> or <b>Still installed</b>, <b>Installed</b> or <b>Not installed</b>, <b>Frozen</b> or <b>Not frozen</b>, <b>Enabled</b> or <b>Still frozen</b>, and the counts in the message follow it. Some phones make the uninstall command report a failure although the app is gone; the card then starts with <i>Checked afterwards: uninstalled. The command reported a failure, but the phone says it worked.</i> and the app counts as a success. If the phone cannot be asked (no working shell), the command's own answer is used, as before. <b>Clear data</b> is checked the same way in Root mode (file and size counts of each app's folder before and after, the sheet says <i>Checking the phone&hellip;</i> too); in ADB and Shizuku modes, like <b>Force stop</b>, <b>Batch Ops</b> and <b>Command</b>, it is not checked (the phone offers nothing to read their result from), so their tags come from the command.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Run again</b>: runs the same action on the same apps (Freeze, Suspend and Uninstall ask first again).</li>\n    <li><b>Run again on the (number) that failed</b>: shown when some, but not all, failed.</li>\n    <li><b>Share CSV</b>: shares the results as a CSV file (a plain table that opens in any spreadsheet) with the columns App, Package, Result, Output, Run and Command or action. This is how you export the results.</li>\n    <li><b>Done</b>: closes the box.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>The selection is cleared when the batch ends, unless <b>Keep selection after running</b> is on. The list refreshes by itself a moment later. Every batch is also written to a history: open the <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a> tab and tap <b>History</b> to see it. There, <b>Undo</b> reverses Freeze, Enable, Suspend, Unsuspend, Uninstall and Reinstall. Force Stop and Clear Data cannot be undone.</p>\n\n  <h4>Step by step: freeze five apps</h4>\n  <p>You need a working mode. The example freezes five apps you do not use.</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>In the Application Manager, tap five rows, one by one. The rows get a border, and a round button with a tick and the number 5 appears at the bottom right.</li>\n    <li>Tap the round button. The batch sheet slides up and says \"5 Selected\".</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Show Apps</b> to check the five names. Tap <b>Remove</b> beside any you picked by mistake, then close the box with its X.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Freeze</b>. The box \"Confirm Freeze Selected Apps\" opens with the five apps listed.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Confirm Freeze</b>. A progress box shows \"1 of 5\", \"2 of 5\" and so on, with a bar.</li>\n    <li>When it ends, <b>Batch Results</b> opens. Each app should show <b>Success</b>. Tap <b>Done</b>.</li>\n    <li>The five rows now have a FROZEN badge. To undo, select the same apps and tap <b>Enable</b>, or use <b>Undo</b> in the Debloater's History.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>Some system apps refuse to be frozen. Those rows show <b>Failed</b> with Android's reason, and the other apps are still done.</p>\n\n  <h4>Batch Ops: app ops on many apps</h4>\n  <p>Tap <b>Batch Ops</b> to open \"Batch Ops (App Ops)\". Tick every app op you want to change, and choose a value for each (Allow, FG, Ignore, Deny or Reset; see <a href=\"#apps-menu\">App Ops</a> in the app menu). Presets tick a ready-made set for you: <b>Privacy lockdown</b>, <b>No location</b>, <b>Stop background activity</b>, <b>Silence notifications</b> and <b>Undo Privacy lockdown</b>. You can add an op by name and save your own presets. <b>Apply to (number) apps</b> asks you to confirm, then runs the batch.</p>\n\n  <h4>Command: type your own command</h4>\n  <p>Tap <b>Command</b> to open \"Command on selected apps\". It runs one shell command once for each app. A shell command is a line of text that Android carries out for you, the same kind you can type in the <a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a> tab. Write <code>$package</code> wherever the app's package name belongs; the app swaps in each app's real name. It runs through your working mode exactly as typed.</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Under <b>Run it on</b>, choose \"The selected apps\" or a saved list. A saved list lets you run it on that list instead.</li>\n    <li>Type the command, for example <code>am force-stop $package</code>, or tap an example chip.</li>\n    <li>Read the preview. It shows the exact command for the first app: \"For (package) it will run: ...\". A warning appears if there is no <code>$package</code> in it, because then the same command runs once per app.</li>\n    <li>Switch on <b>Dry run</b> to only list what would run. Nothing is run. The button then says \"Show what would run on (number) apps\".</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Run on (number) apps</b>, and confirm. The confirmation ends with \"There is no undo.\"</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>A dry run opens a box titled \"Dry run: nothing was run\" with each command tagged <b>Would run</b>, and buttons <b>Run it for real</b> and <b>Share CSV</b>. You can also <b>Save this command...</b>, reuse saved and recent commands, and use <b>Export saved commands</b> and <b>Import...</b>.</p>\n  <div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> A command can do anything your working mode is allowed to do, to every app at once, and there is no undo. Always do a dry run first, and never paste a command you do not understand. Commands you type are your responsibility.</div>\n\n  <h4>Select all filtered: a worked recipe</h4>\n  <p>To act on a whole group, for example every frozen app: tap the <b>Frozen</b> pill (add a search such as <code>samsung</code> to narrow it), tap one row so that the round button appears, open the sheet and tap <b>Select All</b>. The number at the top says how many apps are now selected. Open <b>Show Apps</b> to read them before you tap anything else.</p>\n\n  <h4>Getting out of a mistake</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Wrong apps selected: tap <b>Clear</b>, or hold the round button.</li>\n    <li>Wrong action started: tap <b>Stop</b>. Apps not yet reached stay as they were.</li>\n    <li>Action finished: use the opposite button (Enable, Unsuspend, Reinstall), or the Debloater's History Undo.</li>\n  </ul>"},{"id":"apps-profiles","title":"Profiles and drift watch","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">A profile remembers which apps you have disabled, suspended or uninstalled, so you can put the phone back in that state later, or on another phone. A watched profile warns you when a system update brings those apps back.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> the <b>Profiles</b> button in the row under the stat boxes of the <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>. It opens a box called \"App Profiles\".</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Saving, sharing and importing profiles work in every mode. <b>Applying</b> a profile needs a <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>.</li>\n    <li>A profile only records apps that are <i>not</i> enabled: frozen (disabled), suspended or uninstalled ones. An app that is switched on is not saved. Applying a profile never touches an app that is not in it.</li>\n    <li>First do the freezing, suspending and removing you like (see <a href=\"#tab-apps\">the Application Manager</a>), then save.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>Save a profile</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Tap <b>Profiles</b>.</li>\n    <li>Look at the line under the name box. With nothing selected it says \"Every app that is not enabled is saved.\" If you selected apps first it says \"Only the selected apps are saved.\" Either way it adds how many apps would be saved now.</li>\n    <li>Type a name in the box (\"Profile name (e.g. Lean Samsung)\") and tap <b>Save</b>.</li>\n    <li>A message says \"Saved profile (name) ((number) apps)\" and the profile appears in the list.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>If nothing is disabled, suspended or uninstalled, the message says there are no apps to save. A profile with no name is refused with \"Give the profile a name\".</p>\n  <p>Each profile card shows its name and a summary such as \"12 apps &bull; 8 disabled &bull; 3 suspended &bull; 1 uninstalled\" followed by the date. Its five buttons are <b>Apply</b>, <b>Watch</b>, <b>Share</b>, <b>Copy</b> and <b>Delete</b>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Apply a profile</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Tap <b>Apply</b> on the profile. A preview appears. It says what will change, for example \"Will disable 4, suspend 2 ((number) apps)\", then \"(number) already match, (number) are not on this phone and are skipped.\"</li>\n    <li>Read it. Tap <b>Confirm apply ((number))</b> to go on, or <b>Cancel</b>. If nothing needs changing the confirm button is greyed out.</li>\n    <li>A progress box, \"Applying (name)\", shows the apps one by one with a <b>Stop</b> button. It works through the steps in this order: bring back removed apps, re-enable, unsuspend, disable, suspend, uninstall.</li>\n    <li>A \"Profile Results\" box lists every app with <b>Success</b> or <b>Failed</b>. The run is added to the history, where it can be undone (see <a href=\"#apps-batch\">batch actions</a>).</li>\n  </ol>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> A profile can uninstall and disable many apps in one go, including system apps. Read the preview each time, and never apply a profile from someone else without checking its apps. Imported profiles are shown in the same preview, so nothing is applied by itself.</div>\n\n  <h4>Watch a profile (drift watch)</h4>\n  <p>System updates often bring back apps you had removed. \"Drift\" is when the phone no longer matches the profile. To be told about it, tap <b>Watch</b> on a profile. The button then says <b>Watching</b>, and a message says \"Watching (name). You'll be told when its apps come back.\" Only one profile can be watched at a time: watching another stops the first. Tap <b>Watching</b> again to stop.</p>\n  <p>Each time the app list loads, the app compares the phone with the watched profile. If some apps no longer match, a card appears at the top of the Application Manager:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The title says, for example, \"3 apps from \"Lean Samsung\" no longer match it\". If the phone's Android build changed since you last opened the app, it begins \"Your phone was updated.\"</li>\n    <li>The text under it says what would be done, for example \"Uninstall 3. Review before applying.\"</li>\n    <li><b>Review</b> opens the Profiles box with the preview for that profile. You still have to tap <b>Confirm apply</b>. Nothing is applied automatically.</li>\n    <li>The X puts the card away until the app is started again, or until you change which profile is watched.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>After a restart into a new Android build, a notification \"Your phone was updated\" can also appear: \"Open the app to check whether apps from (name) came back.\" On Android 13 and newer this needs Android's notification permission for the app; without it, only the card in the app tells you.</p>\n\n  <h4>Share, copy, import and delete</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Share</b>: sends the profile as a file named like <code>Lean Samsung.adbprofile.json</code> through Android's share sheet.</li>\n    <li><b>Copy</b>: copies the same text to the clipboard.</li>\n    <li><b>Import</b>: paste a copied profile in the box \"Paste a shared profile here to import it\" and tap <b>Import</b>. A message says \"Imported (number) apps\". Text that is not a profile gives \"That is not a valid profile\". Entries for the same app twice are merged, and the last one wins.</li>\n    <li><b>Delete</b>: removes the profile at once, with no question. If it was the watched profile, watching stops.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>When the app cannot keep any more profiles it says \"Profiles are full. Delete one to make room.\"</p>\n\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> A profile is the state of your apps. A <a href=\"#tab-saved-lists\">Saved Applications</a> list is only a list of names, without states. Use a profile to restore a setup, and a list to pick the same group of apps again.</div>\n\n  <h4>If something is wrong</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Apply is greyed out.</b> Nothing needs changing: every app already matches.</li>\n    <li><b>\"(number) are not on this phone and are skipped\".</b> Those apps do not exist on this phone, which is normal when you import a profile from another model.</li>\n    <li><b>The card keeps coming back.</b> Apps on the profile have returned. Use Review, then Confirm apply. If an app fails every time, Android is refusing it; the results box tells you why.</li>\n    <li>No banner appears but you expected one: it only shows for the profile marked <b>Watching</b>, and only while at least one of its apps differs.</li>\n  </ul>"},{"id":"apps-backup","title":"Backups and restore","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">A backup saves an app (its APK, which is the app's installer file), its permission choices and (with root) its data into one file, so you can put it back later or on another phone.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> make a backup from one app's menu with the <b>Backup</b> button (see <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>). The <b>Backups</b> button in the <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a> opens the list of backups, where you restore, share and delete them.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Making and restoring a backup needs a <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>. The app uses it to read the app's permission settings, so in Read-Only mode it refuses.</li>\n    <li>Saving the app's <b>data</b> needs <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>: an app's data is private to it, and only root can read it. The app asks your root manager for permission itself, so the working mode does not have to be Root. Without root, a backup holds the app and its settings, not its data.</li>\n    <li>There is no button to back up many apps at once. A backup is made for one app at a time.</li>\n    <li>Make sure there is enough free storage: a backup is the app's whole APK file plus, with data, its data folders.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>What is inside a backup</h4>\n  <p>A backup is one file ending in <code>.adbbackup</code> (it is a zip file, if you are curious). It holds:</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it is</th><th>Needs</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>The app file(s)</td><td>The APK, and every split part if the app has them.</td><td>Any working mode</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>A details file</td><td>App name, package name, version, the phone it came from, whether it was suspended, the signing key's fingerprints, the dangerous permissions it had been given and the app ops that were changed.</td><td>Any working mode</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The data</td><td>The app's private data folders. Optional, you tick a switch.</td><td>Root</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>The data part copies the app's private folders only. It does not copy files the app keeps in shared storage (such as Android/data or its media), your online accounts, or secrets that an app keeps in Android's secure Keystore. An app restored from data may ask you to sign in again.</p>\n\n  <h4>Where the files go</h4>\n  <p>Backups are saved in the <code>Download</code> folder, inside <code>ADB App Manager</code>, then <code>Backups</code>: <code>Download/ADB App Manager/Backups</code>. The file name is the app name, the version and the date and time, for example <code>Calculator_8.5_20261006-101530-123.adbbackup</code>. On Android 8 and 9 the backup goes to the app's own storage instead, and it is removed if you uninstall this app: copy it somewhere safe first.</p>\n\n  <h4>Back up one app</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>In the Application Manager, open the app's menu and tap <b>Backup</b>. The menu closes and the Backups box opens. At the top is a card \"Back up (app name)\" with its package name and version.</li>\n    <li>Decide about the switch <b>Include app data</b>. With root it says \"Uses Root. The app is stopped while its data is copied.\" Without it, the switch is greyed and says \"Needs Root, which was not found on this phone.\"</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Create backup</b>. A progress line and a bar show: \"Reading (name)...\", \"Backing up data (Root)...\" and \"Saving the backup...\".</li>\n    <li>A note appears: \"Saved (name) ((size), with data) to (path)\". It can add warnings. For example \"Some data changed while it was being read; the backup may be incomplete\", or that the app's app ops could not be read.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>If you tick <b>Include app data</b>, the app is force-stopped first, so do not do this while you are using it.</p>\n\n  <h4>Restore a backup</h4>\n  <p>The list in the Backups box shows each backup the app knows about: the app name and version, the date, the size and \"with data\" or \"APK + settings\". Each one has three buttons: <b>Restore</b>, <b>Share</b> and <b>Delete</b>.</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Tap <b>Restore</b> on a backup. A panel opens: \"Restore (name) (version)?\" It says that it installs the backed-up APK over the installed one, then re-applies its permissions and app ops.</li>\n    <li>If the backup has data and you have root, you can tick <b>Also restore app data</b> \"(replaces its current data)\". It is greyed out with \"(needs Root)\" or \"(none in this backup)\" otherwise.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Confirm restore</b>. A progress bar shows \"Reading the backup...\", \"Installing...\", \"Restoring permissions...\" and \"Restoring data (Root)...\".</li>\n    <li>A note shows the result: for example \"(name): installed 8.5\", then \"Permissions 5 of 6 granted, 2 app ops restored\" and \"Data: restored\". Then the list of apps refreshes.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>What the restore does: if the same version is already installed, the install is skipped, and only the permissions, app ops and data are put back. If the app is a system app that was removed for you, it is brought back instead of installed (\"restored for your user (it is part of the system)\"). Restoring data first moves the current data aside, and puts it back if the restore fails.</p>\n\n  <h4>Restore a file from another phone</h4>\n  <p>Tap <b>Choose backup file...</b> at the bottom of the Backups box, and pick an <code>.adbbackup</code> file. The same \"Restore\" panel opens. A file that is not a backup from this app is refused with \"Not a usable backup\".</p>\n\n  <h4>Share and delete</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Share</b> sends the file through Android's share sheet, so you can store it in the cloud or move it to another phone.</li>\n    <li><b>Delete</b> removes the file at once. There is no question, and no undo.</li>\n    <li>The list only shows backups that this app made and that still exist. Deleting or moving the file with a file manager removes it from the list, but a moved file can still be restored with <b>Choose backup file...</b>.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>Warnings and what can fail</h4>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Some backups cannot be restored. Android refuses to put an older version over a newer one, or an app signed with a different key over the installed copy. The app explains: \"A newer version is installed than the one in the backup. Uninstall the app first (keep its data if you want it restored), then restore\", or \"The installed app is signed with a different key than the backup. Uninstall it first, then restore.\" A backup of a system app is refused on a phone whose own copy of that app is signed with a different key (\"it does not match this device\"), so it mostly helps on the same phone model.</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>\"Not enough storage to install the app\", or \"A split APK is missing from the backup\": free up space, or the file is damaged.</li>\n    <li>\"Another backup or restore is still running\": wait for the first to finish.</li>\n    <li>The \"Working Mode Required\" box opens when you tap <b>Create backup</b> or <b>Confirm restore</b>: you are in Read-Only mode. Set up a working mode.</li>\n    <li>Data restore refuses a damaged archive, or one that contains files outside the app's own folders, and says why.</li>\n    <li>While data is read, a file may change. The note says so, and the backup may then be incomplete.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> Restoring data replaces the app's current data. If the current data matters, make a fresh backup of it first. Only restore backup files you made yourself, or from someone you trust, because a restored app is installed on your phone. Before it installs, the app checks that the file is a backup and that the app's real package name matches. If the app, or the system copy of it, is already on the phone, it also compares the signing keys and refuses a mismatch.</div>"},{"id":"quick-tiles","title":"Quick Settings tiles and the home-screen widget","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Two tiles for your phone's Quick Settings panel, and one home-screen widget, let you switch the working mode and stop a group of apps without opening this app.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> the tiles are in Android's Quick Settings editor, and the widget is in the home screen's widget list. The app tells you how, and chooses the \"quick list\", in the <a href=\"#tab-saved-lists\">Saved Applications</a> tab.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Stop apps</b> needs a <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a> that is ready at the moment you tap it, and a quick list. The <b>Working mode</b> tile does not need a ready mode itself: it looks for one.</li>\n    <li>Tiles and widgets are drawn by Android itself, so the screens differ between phone makers. The names below are exact.</li>\n    <li>They run without showing the app, and the answer comes as a short message at the bottom of the screen.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>What they do</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Name</th><th>What a tap does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Quick Settings tile</td><td><b>Working mode</b></td><td>Switches to the next working mode that is ready, in the order ADB TCP, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku, Root. If none of the others is ready it goes back to Automatic and says \"No other mode is ready. Using Automatic.\" Otherwise it says \"Working mode: (name)\". On Android 10 and newer the tile shows the current mode under its name.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Quick Settings tile</td><td><b>Stop apps</b></td><td>Force-stops every app in your quick list. The tile shows the list's name and size under its name (Android 10 and newer) and is lit when a list is set.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Widget</td><td><b>Mode</b> button</td><td>The same as the Working mode tile.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Widget</td><td><b>Stop apps</b> button</td><td>The same as the Stop apps tile.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>The widget is called <b>ADB App Manager</b>. It shows its name (tap the name to open the app), a line \"Mode: (current mode)\" and a line \"Quick list: (list name) ((number) apps)\", or \"Quick list: No quick list set\". It can be made wider, and it refreshes itself after every action and about every half hour.</p>\n  <p>The answers of Stop apps are short: \"Force-stopped 5 apps\" (with \", 1 failed\" when some did not stop), \"No quick list set. Open Saved Applications in the app and tap Quick list.\", or \"Needs ADB, Shizuku or Root. Set up a working mode first.\"</p>\n\n  <h4>Step by step: set up Stop apps</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>In the Application Manager, select the apps you want to stop together and tap <b>Save to List</b> in the batch sheet (see <a href=\"#apps-batch\">batch actions</a>). Give the list a name.</li>\n    <li>Open the <b>Saved Applications</b> tab and tap <b>Quick list</b> on that list. The button turns to \"Quick list\" with a tick, and a message says \"(name) is now the quick list. Add the \"Stop apps\" tile or the widget to use it.\"</li>\n    <li>Add the tile: swipe down twice from the top of the screen to open the full Quick Settings panel, tap the pencil icon (edit), and drag <b>Working mode</b> and <b>Stop apps</b> into your panel. The wording differs a little between phone makers.</li>\n    <li>Or add the widget: press and hold an empty spot on the home screen, tap <b>Widgets</b>, find <b>ADB App Manager</b> and drag it onto a home screen page.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Stop apps</b>. A message says how many apps were force-stopped.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>To use a different list, tap <b>Quick list</b> on another list: only one list is the quick list. Tap the ticked button again to clear it. If you delete the quick list, it is cleared. If you edit it, the widget's number is refreshed.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Force Stop only ends the apps now, and nothing is changed for good, so this is a safe pair of buttons. It is handy for apps that keep running in the background. Android may ask you to unlock the phone before a tile can act.</div>\n\n  <h4>If something is wrong</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>You cannot find the tiles.</b> The app does not add them for you. Open the Quick Settings editor (swipe down twice, tap the pencil) and look in the list of unused tiles for <b>Working mode</b> and <b>Stop apps</b>, then drag them up.</li>\n    <li><b>\"Needs ADB, Shizuku or Root\".</b> No working mode was ready at that moment, for example Wireless Debugging was switched off. Open the app and set the mode up (see <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>), then try again.</li>\n    <li><b>\"No other mode is ready.\"</b> The Working mode tile found no other connected mode, so it set Automatic.</li>\n    <li><b>The widget shows an old list size.</b> Open Saved Applications; changing or choosing the quick list there refreshes it.</li>\n  </ul>"},{"id":"tab-saved-lists","title":"Saved Applications","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The second tab keeps named lists of apps. Make a list once, then use it again whenever you want to act on the same group of apps.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> the second tab in the tab bar, <b>Saved Applications</b>. It can be turned off, or moved, in the <a href=\"#feature-list\">Feature List</a>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>A list holds package names only (see the <a href=\"#glossary\">glossary</a>). It does not remember whether an app was frozen or not; use a <a href=\"#apps-profiles\">profile</a> for that.</li>\n    <li>Making, editing, copying and deleting lists works in every mode. Acting on the apps in a list needs a <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>.</li>\n    <li>Lists are kept inside this app on this phone. Uninstalling the app, or clearing its storage, deletes them. Use <b>Back up and restore</b> (the card under the New List button) to keep a copy in a folder of your choice, and to bring the lists back.</li>\n    <li>The app has no built-in lists. The tab starts empty, with \"No Saved Lists Yet\".</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>The Quick actions card</h4>\n  <p>At the top of the tab a card called <b>Quick actions</b> explains the <b>Quick list</b> button on each list, the Quick Settings tiles and the widget. See <a href=\"#quick-tiles\">Quick Settings tiles and the home-screen widget</a>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Make a list</h4>\n  <p>There are three ways.</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li><b>From apps you selected.</b> In the Application Manager, select the apps, tap the round button, then <b>Save to List</b>. A box \"Save Selected Apps\" shows how many apps are selected. Type a <b>List name</b>, an optional description, and tap <b>Save Selected Apps</b>. A message says \"Saved selected apps to (name)\". The same idea works in the <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a>, with its own <b>Save to List</b> button.</li>\n    <li><b>By typing.</b> In this tab tap <b>New List</b>. The box \"Create Saved List\" opens. Fill in the name and an optional description, then type the package names in the big box, one per line. Tap <b>Save List</b>. Double entries are removed by themselves. Names separated by commas also work.</li>\n    <li><b>By pasting.</b> Copy a list of package names from anywhere, open <b>New List</b> and paste it into the big box. This is how you bring in a list from another phone, a chat message or a note. A whole set of lists comes back from a backup file instead: see <a href=\"#tab-saved-lists\">Back up and restore</a> further down this page.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>A name is required: without one the message says \"Please enter a list name\". The new list appears at the top.</p>\n\n  <h4>What a list card shows</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The list's name and \"(number) apps\", and its description if you gave one.</li>\n    <li>Up to six package names as small chips, then \"+(number) more\".</li>\n    <li>A small <b>Saved</b> tag, and the buttons <b>Recall</b>, <b>Quick list</b>, <b>Edit</b>, <b>Copy</b> and <b>Delete</b>.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Recall</b></td><td>Selects the list's apps in the Application Manager (replacing any earlier selection) and takes you there. A message says \"Recalled (number) apps from (name)\". Open the round button to run any batch action on them. See <a href=\"#apps-batch\">batch actions</a>. This is how you apply an action to a whole list.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Quick list</b></td><td>Makes this the list that the Stop apps tile and widget force-stop. The button then shows \"Quick list\" with a tick. Tap it again to clear it.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Edit</b></td><td>Opens \"Edit Saved List\" with the name, description and package names. Change them, then tap <b>Save List</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Copy</b></td><td>Copies the package names, one per line, to the clipboard. A message says \"Copied (name)\". This is how you pass on one list as plain text, to paste into a note or a message. To keep all your lists in a file, use <b>Back up and restore</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Delete</b></td><td>Opens \"Delete Saved List\" with \"Are you sure you want to delete this saved list?\" and the name. Tap <b>Delete List</b> to go on, or <b>Cancel</b>. A deleted list cannot be recovered.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>You can also run a command on a list without selecting it first: in the batch sheet's <b>Command</b> box, the menu <b>Run it on</b> offers each saved list.</p>\n\n  <h4>Back up and restore</h4>\n  <p>The card <b>Back up and restore</b> sits under the <b>New List</b> button. It keeps your lists in a small file (JSON) in a place you choose, and brings them back from such a file. Lists are only kept inside the app otherwise, so a backup is what saves them when you uninstall the app, clear its storage or change phone.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>The line at the top of the card</td><td>Says where backups go: <i>Download/ADB App Manager (the default)</i>, or the folder you chose. Under it: when the last backup was made, how many lists it held and its file name, or that the last attempt failed and why.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Choose folder…</b></td><td>Opens Android's own folder picker. Choose any folder it offers: on the phone, an SD card, a USB drive, or a cloud provider that shows up there. The app keeps the permission, so it works again after a restart, and the folder is shown on the card. Backing out changes nothing.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Use Downloads</b></td><td>Shown only while a folder is chosen. Goes back to <i>Download/ADB App Manager</i> (and switches automatic backups off).</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Back up now</b></td><td>Writes a new file named <code>saved-lists-YYYYMMDD-HHMM.json</code> (the date and time of the backup) to that place, so earlier backups stay. A message says how many lists and where. With no saved lists it says so and writes nothing.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Share…</b></td><td>Hands the same file to Android's share sheet: send it by e-mail or chat, or save it in an app of your choice.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Back up automatically</b> (switch, needs a chosen folder)</td><td>A few seconds after a list is saved, edited, copied in or deleted, the folder's <code>saved-lists-latest.json</code> is replaced with the current lists (one file, always the newest state; several changes in a row make one write). Switching it on makes the first one at once. A set of lists that is empty never replaces it, so deleting everything cannot wipe your last backup. The card marks such a backup <i>(automatic)</i>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Restore from a file…</b></td><td>Opens Android's file chooser. Choose a backup file from anywhere.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Restore from the folder…</b></td><td>Needs a chosen folder. Lists the <code>saved-lists…</code> files in it, newest first, with their size and date; tap one.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p><b>What restoring does.</b> The file is checked first. If you have no lists yet, its lists are simply put back. If you have some, a sheet asks:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Add them to my lists</b>: yours stay. A list that is the same as one you have (same name, description and apps) is skipped; one with the same name but other apps comes in as <i>Name (imported)</i>; a list with an id yours already uses gets a new one. A message counts what came in, what was already there and what was renamed.</li>\n    <li><b>Replace my lists with them</b>: yours are removed first (the sheet says how many), then the backup's lists are put in. The <b>Quick list</b> follows the backup when its list is there, and is cleared when the list it pointed to is gone.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p><b>What is refused.</b> A file that is not JSON (<i>That is not a backup file (it is not JSON).</i>), JSON that is not a saved-lists backup of this app (<i>That is not a saved-lists backup from this app.</i>; the presets file of the batch sheet is another kind of file), a backup from a newer version (<i>That backup was made by a newer version of the app (format 2).</i>), a file with no usable list (<i>There are no usable lists in that file.</i>) and a file over 1 MB. Nothing changes then. Inside a good file, entries that are not lists are skipped and counted, names are cut to 80 characters and descriptions to 500, only real package names are kept (letters, digits, dots and underscores, once each), and at most 200 lists of 5,000 apps are taken.</p>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Choose a folder that your cloud app syncs (or an SD card), switch <b>Back up automatically</b> on, and your lists survive losing the phone. If Android says it no longer lets the app use the folder (<i>choose it again</i>), press <b>Choose folder…</b> once more.</div>\n\n  <h4>The filters of this tab</h4>\n  <p>Under the heading \"Show only the apps in each list that match (combine as many as you like)\" is a row of pills: <b>All</b>, <b>Updated 7d</b>, <b>Running</b>, <b>3rd Party</b>, <b>System</b>, <b>Enabled</b>, <b>Frozen</b>, <b>Suspended</b>, <b>Patched</b> and <b>Uninstalled</b>. They are the same filters as in the <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>, including the rule that 3rd Party / System and Enabled / Frozen are pairs that keep only one side.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>With a filter on, each card shows only the apps that match, the count reads \"(number) of (total) apps\", and <b>Recall</b> becomes \"Recall (number)\": only the matching apps are selected.</li>\n    <li>A list with no matching app is hidden. If every list is hidden, the tab says \"No saved list has apps that match these filters.\"</li>\n    <li>An app that is not on this phone never matches a filter.</li>\n    <li>When more than one filter is on, a line says what is combined, with a <b>Clear filters</b> chip. Tap <b>All</b> or the chip to turn them all off.</li>\n    <li>The filters are only remembered when <b>Remember my filters</b> is on. See <a href=\"#tab-apps\">the Application Manager</a> for that switch.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>For example: tap <b>Frozen</b>, then <b>Recall</b> on your \"Samsung junk\" list, and only the apps of that list that are already frozen are selected. Then tap <b>Enable</b> in the batch sheet to bring just those back.</p>\n\n  <h4>Step by step: freeze a group again later</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open the <b>Saved Applications</b> tab and tap <b>Recall</b> on the list. The Application Manager opens with those apps selected and a message \"Recalled (number) apps from (name)\".</li>\n    <li>Tap the round button at the bottom right, check the number and the names with <b>Show Apps</b>, then tap <b>Freeze</b> and confirm.</li>\n    <li>Read the results. Apps that are not on this phone fail; the others are done.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Recall selects every package on the list, even those that are not installed on this phone, and counts them. They simply show <b>Failed</b> in the results. Use the filter pills before you tap <b>Recall</b> if you want only the apps that are present.</div>\n\n  <h4>If something is wrong</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>My list disappeared.</b> Lists live in this app's storage. Clearing the app's data or uninstalling it removes them. If you made a backup, use <b>Restore from a file…</b> or <b>Restore from the folder…</b>.</li>\n    <li><b>The backup says it failed.</b> The card says why in the line under <i>Backups go to</i>. <i>Choose it again</i> means Android took the folder's permission away: press <b>Choose folder…</b>. <i>No space left</i> (the default place) means the phone is full.</li>\n    <li><b>Recall selected fewer apps than I expected.</b> A filter is on. Tap <b>All</b> and recall again.</li>\n    <li><b>A package name is wrong.</b> Tap <b>Edit</b> and fix it. The batch results show a failure for names that are not real apps.</li>\n    <li>The Quick Settings tile does nothing: see <a href=\"#quick-tiles\">the tile page</a>.</li>\n  </ul>"},{"id":"tab-debloater","title":"UAD-NG Debloater","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">This tab lists the apps on your phone that a community project has studied, tells you in plain words how risky each one is to remove, and lets you uninstall, disable or restore them after a review step, with a history you can undo.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> the third tab in the usual order, named <b>UAD-NG Debloater</b>. If it is missing, it has been switched off: turn it on again in the <a href=\"#feature-list\">Feature List</a>.</p>\n\n  <h4>What debloating is</h4>\n  <p><b>Bloatware</b> means apps that came with the phone but that you did not choose: extras from the phone maker, your mobile network or Google. Some are useful. Others you never open, yet they take space or run in the background. <b>Debloating</b> means removing or switching off the ones you do not want (see the <a href=\"#glossary\">glossary</a>).</p>\n  <p>The danger is that some preinstalled apps are parts of Android itself. Removing the wrong one can break calls, the keyboard, or even the start-up of the phone. So this tab tells you, for each app, how safe it is to remove. Read <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloating safely, step by step</a> before you remove anything.</p>\n\n  <h4>What UAD-NG is</h4>\n  <p>UAD-NG stands for <b>Universal Android Debloater Next Generation</b>, a free community project. It keeps a long list of Android package names (a package name is an app's unique ID, such as <code>com.example.app</code>). For each package the list gives a short description, a <b>removal level</b> from safe to dangerous, the list it belongs to (phone maker, Google, mobile network and so on), and which other packages need it.</p>\n  <p>This app does not contain the list. It downloads it from the project's GitHub page, keeps a copy on your phone, and compares it with your apps. The levels and descriptions are the project's advice, not a promise that nothing will break on your phone.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Looking</b> at the list needs no working mode. <b>Uninstall, Disable, Restore and Undo</b> need one: ADB over TCP, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root (see <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>). In Read-Only mode, tapping one opens a sheet called <b>Working Mode Required</b> with a <b>Set Up Working Mode</b> button.</li>\n    <li>The list is downloaded the first time you open the tab (about 1.6 MB), so the first visit needs the internet. After that the tab works offline from the saved copy, and the app refreshes it when it is over a week old.</li>\n    <li>Make a backup first. Steps are in <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloating safely</a> and <a href=\"#apps-backup\">Backups</a>.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>The top card and the list file</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Status line</b></td><td>Says what is happening (\"Downloading the latest UAD-NG list (about 1.6 MB)...\", \"The UAD-NG list has not been downloaded yet.\"), or, when all is well, how many packages the list knows, when it was updated and how many are on this phone.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Update List</b></td><td>Downloads the list again now. The button is greyed out while a download runs.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Removal Levels</b></td><td>Opens or closes a panel that explains the four levels (also in <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloating safely</a>).</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>UAD-NG Wiki</b></td><td>Opens the project's wiki in your browser.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>History</b></td><td>Opens the log of everything you did here, with Undo (see below).</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>When you open the tab, the app downloads the list by itself if it has none or if the copy is over a week old. If a download fails (no internet, GitHub not reachable) you see \"UAD-NG list update failed ... (using cached list)\" and your old copy stays. A damaged or incomplete download is thrown away, so a bad file never replaces a good one. With no good copy yet, the list area says \"Waiting for the UAD-NG list...\" until a download works: connect to the internet and tap <b>Update List</b>.</p>\n  <p>Only apps that are both on your phone and in the project's list are shown. This includes apps that were already uninstalled for you, so you can bring them back. An app that is not in the list never appears here; you can still manage it in the <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Finding the apps you want</h4>\n  <p>Under the card are rows of filter chips. Tap a chip to switch it on or off.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Row</th><th>Choices</th><th>How it works</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Removal level</td><td><b>Recommended</b>, <b>Advanced</b>, <b>Expert</b>, <b>Unsafe</b></td><td>You can switch several on. Only Recommended is on at first. With all four off, every level is shown.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>List</td><td><b>All vendors</b>, <b>OEM</b>, <b>Google</b>, <b>AOSP</b>, <b>Carrier</b>, <b>Misc</b></td><td>One at a time. OEM is the phone maker, Google is Google's own apps, AOSP is the plain Android system, Carrier is added by your mobile network, Misc is the rest. Anything the project files under another name shows only under All vendors.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Brand</td><td><b>All brands (n)</b>, then one chip per brand</td><td>One at a time. Your phone's brand comes first and is marked, then the others, biggest first. Tap your own brand to see only your maker's apps; nothing is filtered by brand until you tap a chip, and the chip you pick is remembered. The project has no brand field, so the app guesses the brand from the package name, and can be wrong now and then.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>State</td><td><b>On device</b>, <b>Enabled</b>, <b>Disabled</b>, <b>Uninstalled</b>, <b>All</b></td><td>One at a time. On device (the start choice) means everything that is not uninstalled. Disabled means switched off. Uninstalled means removed for you.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Also show only</td><td><b>All</b>, <b>Updated 7d</b>, <b>Running</b>, <b>3rd Party</b>, <b>System</b>, <b>Suspended</b>, <b>Patched</b></td><td>Combine as many as you like. 3rd Party and System exclude each other. When two or more are on, a line \"Showing apps that are ...\" appears with a <b>Clear filters</b> chip.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>The search box (\"Search package, name or description...\") looks in the package name, the app name, the description and the brand. The line above the list says how many rows are shown and how many are ticked.</p>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> <b>Clear filters</b> clears only the \"Also show only\" pills; <b>Clear</b> next to <b>Select shown</b> clears your ticks. Level, list, state and brand are remembered between launches; the pills only if you turn on <b>Remember my filters</b> in <a href=\"#prefs\">Settings</a>.</div>\n\n  <h4>Reading a row</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Tick circle</b> on the left: tap it to select the app.</li>\n    <li><b>Name and package name</b>, then small tags: the removal level, the brand, the list (for example Oem), and the state if it is not Enabled (\"disabled\" or \"uninstalled\"). \"suspended\" means the app is paused.</li>\n    <li><b>Description</b> from the project. It shows two lines; tap the row to read it all. Tapping also shows <b>Depends on</b>, the packages this app needs.</li>\n    <li><b>Needed by</b> in orange lists installed apps that need this one. Take it as a warning.</li>\n    <li><b>The icon button on the right</b> is the Action Button: by default it opens the app's Android settings, and holding it lists more actions. Change it under Action Button in <a href=\"#prefs\">Settings</a>.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>Rows are sorted from Recommended to Unsafe, then by name.</p>\n\n  <h4>Step by step: removing or switching off apps</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Check that the status line shows a list and that a working mode is active (the badge in the header). Leave Recommended on.</li>\n    <li>Read the description of each app you are thinking about, then tick a few. <b>Select shown</b> ticks every row that passes your filters, so use it only when you have read them all.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Uninstall</b>, <b>Disable</b> or <b>Restore</b>. A sheet called <b>Review: Uninstall Packages</b> (or Disable, or Restore) opens. It lists every app that the action will touch, with its level. Nothing has happened yet.</li>\n    <li>If the selection holds Expert or Unsafe apps, a red line warns that they can break important features and, for Unsafe, can make the phone boot-loop (restart again and again without ever finishing starting). Press <b>Cancel</b> if in doubt.</li>\n    <li>Press the main button at the bottom of the sheet, for example <b>Uninstall 3</b>. A message says \"Running on 3 packages...\". When it ends, a <b>Debloater Results</b> sheet shows each app as Success or Failed with the phone's own reply. Press <b>Done</b>.</li>\n    <li>Restart the phone and use it normally before you remove more.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>What each button does</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What happens</th><th>Skips</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Uninstall</b></td><td>Removes the app for your user only. A system app stays in the phone's protected storage, so it can come back with Restore. Its settings and data for you are deleted.</td><td>Apps already uninstalled</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Disable</b></td><td>Switches the app off. It disappears from the app drawer and stops running, but stays installed.</td><td>Apps that are not Enabled</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Restore</b></td><td>Brings back an Uninstalled app, or switches a Disabled app on again.</td><td>Apps already enabled</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Save to List</b></td><td>Asks for a name (it suggests \"Debloat\" and the date) and saves the ticked apps as a list in <a href=\"#tab-saved-lists\">Saved Applications</a>. It changes no app.</td><td>Needs at least one tick</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> <b>Uninstall</b> on an app that is not a system app (one installed like any other app) can remove it for good, and Restore may then have nothing to bring back. Install it again from its store if you want it.</div>\n\n  <h4>The log and Undo</h4>\n  <p>Press <b>History</b> to open <b>Debloat History</b>. Every action is listed, newest first, with the date, how many items worked, and a line for each app. The last 150 entries are kept on the phone. Batch actions from the Application Manager are logged here too.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Undo</b> on an entry reverses what worked in it: uninstalled apps are restored, disabled apps are enabled again. You are asked to confirm (\"N packages will be reverted\"), and an <b>Undo Results</b> sheet follows. The undo itself is also logged. Undo needs a working mode.</li>\n    <li><b>Copy Log</b> copies the history as text. <b>Clear History</b> deletes it, after a question; after that, the old entries can no longer be undone.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>The app menu in the Application Manager shows a small <b>UAD-NG</b> tag for apps that are in the list; tap it for the same description (see <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>).</p>\n\n  <h4>When something goes wrong</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>What it means and what to do</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>\"No packages match these filters.\"</td><td>Your chips are too narrow. Turn on more levels, or set State to All.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"The UAD-NG list has not been downloaded yet.\"</td><td>The first download did not work. Connect to the internet and tap <b>Update List</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"Nothing to restore: the selected packages are already enabled\"</td><td>Restore acts only on Uninstalled and Disabled apps. Set State to one of them.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Failed, with \"only root can delete system app for a particular user\"</td><td>Android would not let a normal shell remove a system app. The app already tried a second way. Use <b>Disable</b> instead, or switch to Root mode.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Failed, with a message about a device policy or a restriction</td><td>A work profile, device owner or parental control blocks removing apps. It cannot be bypassed from here while it is set.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>"},{"id":"debloater-safety","title":"Debloating safely, step by step","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Removing the wrong app can make the phone behave oddly or even refuse to start. This page is the careful way to do it: back up, start with the safest group, change a few apps at a time, test, and know how to put things back.</p>\n  <p><b>Needs:</b> a working mode (ADB over TCP, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root, see <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>), a battery charged above half, and about an hour without hurry. Know your screen lock and your Google account password: after a factory reset you will need them.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> A mistake can leave the phone stuck on its logo (a boot loop). Work in small steps, back up first, and never remove an app you cannot explain. If you cannot tell what an app is for, leave it alone.</div>\n\n  <h4>What the four levels mean</h4>\n  <p>The levels come from the UAD-NG project. The text is the one the tab shows under <b>Removal Levels</b>.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>In plain words</th><th>What can go wrong</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Recommended</b></td><td>Safe to remove for most people, including apps you can reinstall from an app store. Still worth a quick review, since everyone uses their phone differently.</td><td>You lose an app you did use, such as a preinstalled game, video or social app.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Advanced</b></td><td>Breaks minor or obscure features, overlays, or default apps (keyboard, gallery, launcher) that a better alternative can replace.</td><td>Typing, photo viewing or the home screen stops working until you install another app for the job. A small setting stops working.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Expert</b></td><td>Breaks widespread or important features, but not basic operation. It should not boot-loop the phone unless the description says so, with no guarantee.</td><td>Features you rely on can stop: for example calling extras, backup, or the camera's extra modes. This is hard to track down later.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Unsafe</b></td><td>Can break vital parts of Android, or may be illegal to remove where you live (for example emergency alerts). High risk of a boot-loop.</td><td>The phone may not start, or may lose emergency functions. Do not remove these.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>Step by step</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li><b>Back up your own things.</b> Copy photos, contacts and messages to your cloud account or a computer, the way you normally do. Removing an app deletes that app's own data, and a factory reset (the last resort below) deletes everything on the phone.</li>\n    <li><b>Back up the apps you plan to touch.</b> In the Application Manager, the Backups box saves an app's APK (its installer file), its permissions and its app ops in any working mode; the app's data needs Root (see <a href=\"#apps-backup\">Backups</a>).</li>\n    <li><b>Open the UAD-NG Debloater tab</b> and let the list update. Check that the header badge shows a working mode.</li>\n    <li><b>Keep only Recommended on.</b> Leave State on On device. Tap your phone's brand chip to shorten the list. You will see rows with the app name, the package name and a description.</li>\n    <li><b>Read every description.</b> Tap a row to read all of it and to see Depends on. Read the orange \"Needed by\" line. If the text is missing, strange, or mentions something you use, skip the app.</li>\n    <li><b>Tick only three to five apps.</b> A short batch is easy to test and to undo. Do not use Select shown on a long list.</li>\n    <li><b>Prefer Disable when unsure.</b> Disable keeps the app on the phone, only switched off, and is the lightest change. Uninstall for your user is stronger. Neither frees much storage for a system app (its file stays on the protected system part). The gain is less background activity and a tidier phone.</li>\n    <li><b>Read the review sheet</b> and confirm. Check the results are all Success.</li>\n    <li><b>Test the phone.</b> Restart it. Then check: a call, a text message, the camera, Wi-Fi and mobile data, Bluetooth, the keyboard, fingerprint or face unlock, notifications, an alarm, charging, and anything you use every day. Use the phone normally for a day.</li>\n    <li><b>Keep a record.</b> History lists what you did, and <b>Copy Log</b> copies it as text, which you can paste into a note. Before a batch you can also use <b>Save to List</b>, so you have the names later.</li>\n    <li><b>Repeat</b> with the next small batch. Only when you are comfortable with Recommended should you look at Advanced, and then one app at a time.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>Putting one app back</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open the UAD-NG Debloater tab. Set State to <b>Uninstalled</b> (for an uninstalled app) or <b>Disabled</b>, or to <b>All</b>. Turn all the level chips off, so that every level is shown.</li>\n    <li>Find the app (use the search box) and tick it.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Restore</b>, check the review sheet, and confirm. An uninstalled app is reinstalled from the phone's own storage, a disabled app is enabled.</li>\n    <li>Other ways: open <b>History</b> and tap <b>Undo</b> on the entry that removed it, or open the app's menu in the <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a> (filter <b>Uninstalled</b> to find it), which offers <b>Reinstall</b> or <b>Enable</b>.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>A restored app acts like a freshly installed one: its old settings and data are usually gone, and it may ask for permissions again.</p>\n\n  <h4>Disable or uninstall for your user</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Disable</th><th>Uninstall (for your user)</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>What it does</td><td>Switches the app off. It stays installed.</td><td>Removes the app for your user account only.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The app's data</td><td>Kept.</td><td>Deleted.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Undo with</td><td>Restore (or Enable).</td><td>Restore (reinstalls from the phone's own storage).</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>A system app</td><td>Stays on the phone, switched off.</td><td>Stays on the protected system part, hidden from you.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>An app you installed</td><td>Works the same.</td><td>May be removed completely: reinstall from a store.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> A big Android or phone-maker update can bring back apps you removed, and it can switch disabled apps on again. This differs from phone to phone. If it happens, remove them again. A saved list (<b>Save to List</b>) makes that quick.</div>\n\n  <h4>What never to remove</h4>\n  <p>There is no complete list, because every phone is different. These roles are essential on almost every phone. Do not remove, disable or uninstall an app that does one of these things unless the description clearly says it is safe and you have a replacement:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The system interface: the status bar, notification shade, lock screen and navigation (often called System UI).</li>\n    <li>The phone and calling parts, contacts storage, and the messages app if you use texts.</li>\n    <li>Settings, the app installer, and the permission controller.</li>\n    <li>Your home screen app (launcher) and your keyboard, unless you have already installed and chosen another one.</li>\n    <li>Google Play services and the Play Store, if you use Google apps; the Android System WebView, which many apps use to show web pages.</li>\n    <li>Anything for emergency alerts, SIM card, mobile data, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, fingerprint or face unlock, and the camera.</li>\n    <li>Every app tagged <b>Unsafe</b>, and any app with a \"Needed by\" line that lists apps you use.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>If the phone will not start: boot loop</h4>\n  <p>A <b>boot loop</b> is when the phone restarts again and again, or stays on the logo, and never reaches the home screen. It happens when something the system needs at start-up is gone. Try these in order, and stop at the first one that works:</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li><b>Wait and restart.</b> Hold the power button until the phone turns off, wait ten seconds, and start it again.</li>\n    <li><b>If it reaches the home screen, even for a moment,</b> open this app and use <b>History</b> then <b>Undo</b> on the last entry, or Restore the apps you removed.</li>\n    <li><b>Safe mode.</b> It starts the phone with only its built-in apps. How to start it depends on the phone: often you hold Volume Down while the logo shows, or press and hold <b>Power off</b> on the power menu. If the phone starts in safe mode, an app you installed is probably the trouble; restart normally afterwards. Safe mode does not undo a removal, and this app does not run in safe mode.</li>\n    <li><b>Computer with ADB (advanced).</b> If USB debugging was on, <code>adb shell pm install-existing PACKAGE</code> brings an app back. A phone stuck on its logo often does not allow this.</li>\n    <li><b>Factory reset, as the very last resort.</b> From the phone's recovery menu, choose to wipe data. This deletes everything stored on the phone but puts back the system apps you removed, because they never left the system part. You will need your screen lock and account passwords.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Because you only change a few apps at a time and restart after each batch, a boot loop almost always points to those few apps. Tell them to whoever helps you.</div>\n\n  <h4>When a removal fails</h4>\n  <p>A <b>Failed</b> row means the phone refused. Nothing changed for that app. Read the reply on the Debloater Results sheet; the tab adds a note for the common refusals (system app needing root, a device policy, a restriction). Choose <b>Disable</b> instead, or leave the app.</p>"},{"id":"tab-installer","title":"APK Installer","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Install an app from a file on your phone: a plain <b>APK</b> (an Android app file), or a bundle (<b>APKS</b>, <b>APKM</b>, <b>XAPK</b>) made of several parts. The tab reads the file first and shows what it is, then lets you choose how to install it.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> the fourth tab in the usual order, named <b>APK Installer</b> (switch it on in the <a href=\"#feature-list\">Feature List</a> if it is missing).</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Choosing and reading a file needs no working mode. Installing works in two ways: with <b>ADB</b>, <b>Shizuku</b> or <b>Root</b> (see <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>) you get every option; with <b>No privilege</b> Android's own install screen asks you to confirm and most options are switched off. In that case Android can also ask you to allow installs from this app first (Android calls the setting <b>Install unknown apps</b>): switch it on for this app when asked, then press <b>Install</b> again if nothing happens. You can allow it beforehand: <b>About</b>, <b>Permissions</b>, then <b>Allow</b> on the row <b>Install unknown apps</b> (<a href=\"#app-permissions\">The permissions this app asks for</a>).</li>\n    <li>The app first copies the file into its private cache and unpacks the parts, so keep free space of up to twice the file size. The cache is emptied after the install.</li>\n    <li>Only install files from sources you trust. An APK can do anything a normal app can.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>Step by step</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open the tab. The first time, the app also searches your storage for package files (see Find APKs below).</li>\n    <li>Pick a file in one of three ways: <b>Choose APK / APKS / APKM / XAPK</b> (Android's file picker opens), a row in the found-files list (tap it), or <b>Install XAPK (APK + OBB data)</b> (the same picker, with a reminder that game data is copied afterwards). You can also open a package from another app and choose this app; press <b>Always</b> to make it the default installer. The tab then opens by itself, and switches itself back on if you had turned it off.</li>\n    <li>The hint under the buttons says \"Reading package...\" and then, for example, \"Loaded APK - review and install below.\" The page scrolls to a card with the app's name. More cards appear: <b>VirusTotal scan</b>, <b>Splits</b> (bundles only), <b>Game data (OBB)</b> (only when the file has some), and <b>Install options</b>.</li>\n    <li>Read the package card (next table).</li>\n    <li>Optional: scan the file with VirusTotal (below).</li>\n    <li>Check <b>Install options</b>. The start values suit most files, and <b>Authorizer</b> (the part that carries out the install) is already set to your active mode. If no working mode is on, it starts on No privilege.</li>\n    <li>Press <b>Install</b>. A message says \"Installing ...\". Below the button, a grey line shows the command that will run, for example <code>pm install -r (1 file)</code>. With No privilege it says instead that a system install dialog will ask you to confirm.</li>\n    <li>A result sheet opens, titled \"Install\" plus the package name, with the method used. On success it offers <b>Launch Application</b>, <b>Application Settings</b> and, for a file on your storage (for example one loaded from the found-files list, and only while <b>Offer to delete the file afterwards</b> is on), <b>Delete the installer file</b>. Press <b>Done</b>.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>The package card</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>It means</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Name and package name</td><td>The app's label, and its unique ID under it.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Version</b></td><td>Version name, and the version number in brackets. A bigger number is newer.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Installed</b></td><td>Shown only if the app is already on the phone: the version you have now.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Min SDK</b>, <b>Target SDK</b></td><td>The oldest Android the app runs on, and the one it was built for. 26 is Android 8, 29 is Android 10, 30 is Android 11, 33 is Android 13, 34 is Android 14.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Size</b>, <b>Splits</b></td><td>Total size of all parts, and how many files the package holds.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Signature line</td><td>Every app is signed with its maker's key, and Android only accepts an update signed with the same key. The line says one of: \"Signed. Not currently installed.\"; \"Signature matches the installed app.\"; \"Signed with a different key than the installed app - Android will reject this as an update.\"; or a warning that Android cannot verify the signature (edited after signing, or unsigned).</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>The card does not list the permissions the app asks for. After installing, you can check them in the <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>. The switches <b>Display SDK information</b>, <b>Display package size</b> and <b>Display package version</b> hide or show the chips.</p>\n  <p><b>VirusTotal scan (optional).</b> Paste your own free API key from virustotal.com, then press <b>Scan this package</b>. Only the file's SHA-256 fingerprint (a short code for its exact contents) is sent. The result says \"Clean - no engine flagged this file\", or how many engines flagged it as malicious or suspicious, with counts and a link to the full report. If VirusTotal has not seen the file, a button <b>Upload it for analysis</b> appears: that sends the whole file, so do not use it for anything private. The key is stored on this device only. For a bundle, only the base APK is checked.</p>\n\n  <h4>Install options</h4>\n  <p>The app always replaces an installed copy of the same app and keeps its data (the <code>-r</code> flag); there is no switch for that. The lines marked * are greyed out and cleared when the Authorizer is No privilege.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>What it does</th><th>Start</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Authorizer</b></td><td><b>ADB</b>: through the app's own ADB link (Wireless Debugging or TCP). <b>Shizuku</b> and <b>Root</b>: the same install through that backend. <b>No privilege</b>: Android's install dialog asks you; install reason and package source are passed on where Android allows.</td><td>Your active mode</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>*<b>Grant all permissions</b></td><td>Gives the app every permission it asks for.</td><td>Off</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>*<b>Bypass low target SDK block</b></td><td>Lets a very old app install on Android 14 or newer.</td><td>Off</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>*<b>Allow downgrade</b></td><td>Installs an older version over a newer one.</td><td>Off</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>*<b>Allow test packages</b></td><td>Accepts apps marked as test-only.</td><td>Off</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>*<b>Install for all users</b></td><td>Installs for every user of the phone.</td><td>Off</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>*<b>Request update ownership</b></td><td>Asks Android 14 to make this app the owner of future updates.</td><td>Off</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Install reason</b>, <b>Package source</b></td><td>Labels the install (Default, Unknown, Policy, Device restore, Device setup, User; and Default, Unspecified, Store, Local file, Downloaded file, Other). Package source needs Android 13.</td><td>Default</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>*<b>Install source / installer package</b>, <b>Install requester / originating URI</b></td><td>Records which store or web address the app \"came from\". The arrow button lists common choices.</td><td>Empty</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>*<b>Manual dex optimization</b>, <b>Dex optimization mode</b>, *<b>Force optimization</b></td><td>Compiles the app after install (verify, speed-profile, speed, everything). Mostly for experts.</td><td>Off, mode speed</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Block signature mismatch</b></td><td>Refuses an update signed with a different key than the installed copy.</td><td>On</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Block unknown signature</b></td><td>Refuses a package that is unsigned or unreadable.</td><td>On</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Auto-delete package after install</b></td><td>Deletes the chosen file once it installs. This is permanent and has no Undo.</td><td>Off</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Offer to delete the file afterwards</b></td><td>After success, offers to delete the file; it goes to a hidden folder first, with an Undo.</td><td>On</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Select all splits by default</b></td><td>Ticks every part of a bundle (see <a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split packages</a>).</td><td>Off</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Leave the two <b>Block</b> switches on. Turn them off only if you know why, because they stop you from replacing a real app with a look-alike signed by someone else.</div>\n\n  <h4>Find APKs on this device</h4>\n  <p>The tab can list the package files already on your phone. It runs by itself the first time you open the tab, and again whenever you press <b>Find APKs on this device</b>. It needs All-files access (<a href=\"#app-permissions\">Permissions</a>) or a working mode; without them the status line offers a <b>Grant access</b> button. With a working mode it also looks in Android/data and Android/obb. It covers internal storage and SD cards, skips trash folders, and stops after about 2,000 files or 25 seconds, saying \"search stopped early\".</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Each row shows a badge (APK, APKS, APKM or XAPK), the file name, size, age, the folder, and for plain APKs the package and version. <b>Duplicate</b> means the same content as another found file; <b>Older version</b> means a newer one of the same app is also there.</li>\n    <li>Use the box <b>Filter found files...</b> and the type drop-down (All types, APK, APKS, APKM, XAPK). Tap a row to load that file into the installer.</li>\n    <li><b>Delete one file:</b> press and hold the row. A sheet <b>Delete ...?</b> shows the path; press <b>Delete from this device</b>. The file moves to a hidden folder and an Undo bar stays for a few seconds. After the bar goes, the file is gone for good.</li>\n    <li><b>Delete many:</b> press <b>Select</b>, tick rows, or use <b>Select duplicates and older versions</b> or <b>All</b>. Then <b>Delete selected</b> asks once and gives one Undo for the whole batch. <b>Done</b> leaves selection.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>What happens during the install, and errors</h4>\n  <p>The app checks the signature gates, then installs all chosen files in one step through the Authorizer. With game data, a line shows \"Copying data file 1 of 2: ...\" afterwards. The result sheet quotes Android's own reply: <b>Success</b>, or a line starting with <code>Failure</code>. The app adds a \"Note:\" line for the common ones.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Message</th><th>Meaning and fix</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><code>INSTALL_FAILED_UPDATE_INCOMPATIBLE</code>, \"signatures do not match\"</td><td>The installed copy has a different signing key. Only uninstalling it first fixes this, which deletes its data. Back it up first.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>INSTALL_PARSE_FAILED_NO_CERTIFICATES</code> and similar</td><td>Android cannot verify the file's signature (edited, or unsigned). Installing it from the <a href=\"#tab-files\">File Manager</a> signs a copy with this app's key for you.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>INSTALL_FAILED_VERSION_DOWNGRADE</code></td><td>The file is older than the installed app. Turn on <b>Allow downgrade</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>INSTALL_FAILED_TEST_ONLY</code></td><td>A test-only app. Turn on <b>Allow test packages</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>INSTALL_FAILED_DEPRECATED_SDK_VERSION</code></td><td>An app built for a very old Android. Turn on <b>Bypass low target SDK block</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>INSTALL_FAILED_OLDER_SDK</code></td><td>The app needs a newer Android than yours.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>INSTALL_FAILED_NO_MATCHING_ABIS</code></td><td>The app has no code for this phone's processor. Find another build.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>INSTALL_FAILED_INSUFFICIENT_STORAGE</code></td><td>Not enough free space.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>INSTALL_FAILED_VERIFICATION_FAILURE</code></td><td>Play Protect refused or timed out. Turn off its app scanning, or \"Verify apps over USB\" in Developer options, and try again.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"install files are no longer available - pick the package again\"</td><td>The cache was cleared. Choose the file again.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"Install cancelled\" (No privilege)</td><td>You pressed Cancel in Android's install dialog.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> No privilege hands the file to Android's own installer. This app has not been tried that way on every phone, and some phones block it. If Android asks to allow installs from this app (Install unknown apps), switch that on for this app: the row <b>Install unknown apps</b> in <b>About</b>, <b>Permissions</b> does the same. If you still see an install blocked message, switch the Authorizer to ADB, Shizuku or Root.</div>\n  <p><b>To undo an install:</b> in the Application Manager, open the app's menu and uninstall it. Its data is deleted, so back up first if it matters.</p>"},{"id":"installer-splits","title":"Split packages, APKS, APKM, XAPK and game data","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Many apps come as several files, not one. This page explains what that means, which file types the installer accepts, how it chooses the parts your phone needs, how game data is copied, and what to try when a bundle will not install.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> in the <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a> tab, in the <b>Splits</b> and <b>Game data (OBB)</b> cards. They appear only when the file you picked has those parts.</p>\n\n  <h4>What a split APK is</h4>\n  <p>A simple app is one APK file. A <b>split</b> app is cut into parts: a <b>base APK</b> (the core of the app) and extra files for one thing each. Most extra files are <b>config splits</b>, made for one processor type (for example arm64-v8a), one screen density (for example xxhdpi) or one language (for example en). Others are <b>feature modules</b>, optional parts of the app. All parts of one app must be installed together, in a single step.</p>\n  <p>Developers do this to keep downloads small: Google Play sends each phone only the parts it needs. When you get an app outside Play, those parts are packed into one file called a <b>bundle</b>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Which files the installer accepts</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>APK</b></td><td>One app file.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>APKS</b></td><td>A bundle made by Google's bundletool and similar tools: a ZIP file of APKs.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>APKM</b></td><td>The bundle format of APKMirror, also a ZIP file of APKs.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>XAPK</b></td><td>A ZIP with one or more APKs, a manifest, and usually game data (OBB) files.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>The app decides by the file's content, not its name. If the file opens as an installable APK, it is an APK. If not, it is opened as a ZIP of APKs, and called XAPK if it has an XAPK manifest or game data, APKM if it carries APKMirror's info file, and APKS otherwise. After picking, the hint says \"Loaded APKS\", \"Loaded XAPK with 2 game data files\" and so on. A file with no APK inside shows \"Could not read that package: no APKs found inside this archive\". A very large file (over 8 GB) is refused as too large to be an app package.</p>\n\n  <h4>The Splits card</h4>\n  <p>Each part is a row with a switch. The first row is <b>Base APK</b>; it is always included and cannot be switched off. Under every name you see the file name, its size and a note about your phone. Three links sit above the list.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Link</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Match this phone</b></td><td>Ticks the base and the parts that fit your phone (see below).</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Select all</b></td><td>Ticks every part.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Only base</b></td><td>Leaves just the base.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>The switch <b>Select all splits by default</b> in Install options makes every package open with all parts ticked.</p>\n\n  <h4>How it picks the parts for your phone</h4>\n  <p>The app asks the phone for its processor types (best first), its screen density, and its language list. Then it ticks:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>CPU:</b> one split only, the first type on your phone's list that the bundle has. The note reads \"fits this phone (CPU arm64-v8a)\"; the others read \"another CPU\". If no CPU split fits, none is ticked, and you may need another build of the app.</li>\n    <li><b>Screen density:</b> the one Android itself would use for your screen, usually the closest. The rest read \"another screen density\".</li>\n    <li><b>Language:</b> every language in your phone's language list (Settings, Languages). The rest read \"another language\". English is not added if it is not on your list.</li>\n    <li><b>Feature modules and other extras</b> are not ticked: the note reads \"feature module (optional)\", \"for a feature module\" or \"optional extra\".</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>The line under the Install button shows how many files will go in, for example <code>pm install-multiple -r (4 files)</code>.</p>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Some apps need a feature module just to start. If the app crashes at once, or the install says a split is missing, press <b>Select all</b> and install again.</div>\n  <p>When you send a bundle to another device in the Connected Devices tab, the app picks the parts for that device by its own rules, not these: it always sends the feature modules too, and it adds English. See <a href=\"#devices-send\">Sending apps to the device</a>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Signing and splits</h4>\n  <p>Every part must be signed with the same key. A bundle you downloaded from its maker or from APKMirror already is. The signature line on the package card is read from the base APK. If you edited a bundle, its signature can no longer be checked; the card then says so, and Android may refuse it. Installing from the <a href=\"#tab-files\">File Manager</a> signs a copy for you. See <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a> for the two <b>Block</b> switches.</p>\n\n  <h4>Game data (OBB)</h4>\n  <p>Big games keep their data outside the APK. In a bundle, those files live in folders such as <code>Android/obb/PACKAGE/</code> (sometimes <code>Android/data/PACKAGE/</code>). When the file has some, the <b>Game data (OBB)</b> card lists each one with its destination, for example <code>/sdcard/Android/obb/PACKAGE/</code>, and its size.</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Keep the switch <b>Copy data files after install</b> on (it is on at first).</li>\n    <li>Press <b>Install</b>. The app installs the parts first.</li>\n    <li>Only if the install succeeded, a line shows \"Copying data file 1 of 2: ... (1.2 GB)...\" for each file.</li>\n    <li>The result sheet adds \"Game data (OBB):\" with, for example, \"2 of 2 data files copied (1.2 GB) to Android/obb/PACKAGE/\".</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>On Android 11 and newer, an app cannot write into Android/obb or Android/data itself, so the copy needs <b>ADB</b>, <b>Shizuku</b> or <b>Root</b>. With No privilege you may see \"Android doesn't let this app create that folder without ADB, Shizuku or Root. Copy it by hand to ...\". Only files whose folder names the same package as the app are copied, and unsafe paths are refused. A multi-gigabyte copy takes time: keep the phone awake and plugged in. Free space is needed twice: the app keeps a copy of the bundle in its own cache while it works, and then the final files are written as well.</p>\n  <p>If the data copy fails, the app is still installed, but the game may ask to download its data. You can open the file in the <a href=\"#tab-files\">File Manager</a> without unpacking it and copy the data folder by hand (see <a href=\"#files-archives\">Archives</a>).</p>\n\n  <h4>Several packages</h4>\n  <p>The installer takes one package at a time, but all its ticked parts go in together. To install many apps, load one, install it, then load the next from the found-files list. While an install runs, another cannot start (\"An install is already running\").</p>\n\n  <h4>If a bundle fails</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Read the result sheet and match it with the error table in <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a>.</li>\n    <li>Press <b>Select all</b> and try again. If it works, the app needed a part that was not ticked.</li>\n    <li>Press <b>Match this phone</b> and look for a row marked \"fits this phone (CPU ...)\". If there is none, the bundle may not support your phone.</li>\n    <li>Check free space. Try a different Authorizer, for example ADB instead of No privilege.</li>\n    <li>\"no APKs found inside this archive\" or \"could not read the base APK of this package\" usually means a damaged or incomplete download. Download it again.</li>\n    <li>If the app is already installed with another signing key, uninstall it first. Back up what you need.</li>\n  </ol>"},{"id":"tab-files","title":"File Manager","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Browse the files and folders on your phone, read and edit text files, copy, move, rename, delete and share things, and look inside zip files. You do not need a computer.</p>\n<p><b>Where to find it:</b> the tab named <b>File Manager</b>, next to <b>APK Installer</b> (you can move or hide tabs in <a href=\"#feature-list\">Feature List</a>).</p>\n\n<h4>Before you start</h4>\n<p>Most of this tab works on your own storage and needs no <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a> (a way for this app to run commands with more rights than an ordinary app: ADB, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root). It needs permission to see your files instead.</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Place</th><th>What it needs</th><th>What you can do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Your storage, <code>/storage/emulated/0</code> (Download, Pictures, DCIM and so on)</td><td><b>All-files access</b> (a switch in Android's settings that lets an app see all your files; Android 11 and later) or the storage permission (Android 10 and earlier)</td><td>Everything on this page</td></tr>\n<tr><td>System places such as <code>/system</code> or <code>/data/local/tmp</code></td><td>A working mode</td><td>Look, read, and change what that mode is allowed to change</td></tr>\n<tr><td>An SD card, a USB drive or another app's folder</td><td>Nothing extra</td><td>Added with <b>+ Add storage</b>: see <a href=\"#files-storage\">SD cards, USB drives and other storage</a></td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>With no working mode (<a href=\"#mode-readonly\">Read-Only</a>) the tab still works on your own storage once All-files access is on. See <a href=\"#app-permissions\">Permissions</a>.</p>\n\n<h4>Open the tab and find your way</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Tap <b>File Manager</b>. The list shows your storage folder. If a box says <i>Storage looks inaccessible</i>, tap <b>Grant storage access</b>. Android opens its <i>All files access</i> screen for this app (on Android 10 and older it shows a storage permission box instead): switch it on or allow it, press Back, and the list fills in by itself.</li>\n<li>Tap a folder to open it. Folders come first, then files. The row at the very top, <b>..</b> with the words <i>Up one folder</i>, goes one level back up.</li>\n<li>To jump somewhere, type a path in the box under the title (it starts as <code>/storage/emulated/0</code>) and tap <b>Go</b>. <code>/sdcard</code> works too. The small path above the list shows where you are.</li>\n<li>Tap a file to see what you can do with it, or press and hold a row to select things (both below).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Each row has an icon (a small picture for photos and videos), the name (folder names end with a slash) and a grey line such as <i>-rw- • 1.2 MB</i>. A first letter <b>d</b> means a folder. The other letters say what this app may do with it: r read, w write, x run (for a folder: enter). For system places listed through a working mode you see the longer form that the <code>ls</code> command prints, such as <i>drwxrwx--x</i>. The button with three dots at the right of a row opens the same actions as tapping a file, for folders too.</p>\n<p>Under the path box are shortcuts: <b>Storage</b>, <b>/data/local/tmp</b> and <b>/system</b> (these two need a working mode), <b>+ Folder</b> and <b>+ File</b> (make a new one here), <b>Refresh</b> and <b>Select</b>. Storages you add appear in the row above them.</p>\n\n<h4>What you can do with a file or folder</h4>\n<p>Tap a file, or the three-dot button on any row. A sheet opens with the name, the path and these buttons (only the ones that fit are shown):</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Install</b>, <b>Sign...</b></td><td>For .apk, .apks, .apkm and .xapk files: hand the file to the <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a>, or sign an .apk with this app's own key</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>View</b> or <b>View contents</b></td><td>Show the file in a viewer. For an archive, <b>View contents</b> opens it like a folder (see <a href=\"#files-archives\">archives</a>)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Edit</b></td><td>Open a text file in the built-in editor</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Open with...</b></td><td>Ask Android which app should open the file (files up to 400 MB)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Share</b></td><td>Send a copy of the file with Android's share sheet</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Extract...</b> or <b>Open as archive</b></td><td>Unpack an archive, or try to read any other file as one</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Compress...</b></td><td>Pack the item into a zip, 7z or tar file</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Rename</b>, <b>Copy</b>, <b>Move</b>, <b>Delete</b></td><td>Change the name, put a copy or the item itself in another folder, or remove it</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Viewing and editing</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Text.</b> View shows the first 128 KB in the sheet, with colours for code and settings files (.xml, .json, .py, .sh, .prop and more). A file that is not text shows as unreadable characters: use Open with... instead.</li>\n<li><b>Pictures</b> (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp, heic, heif, avif) open with <b>Fit / actual size</b>. <b>PDF</b> files show one page at a time with <b>Previous</b> and <b>Next</b>. <b>Fonts</b> (.ttf, .otf, up to about 6 MB) show a sample sentence.</li>\n<li><b>Video and music</b> have no player in this app: View hands them to <b>Open with...</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>To change a text file:</p>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Tap the file, then <b>Edit</b>. A tall editor sheet opens. A file over 2 MB is refused (<i>too big to edit here</i>), and so is one that is not text.</li>\n<li>Type your changes. The line at the bottom says <i>Unsaved changes</i>, counts characters and shows your line and column.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Save</b>. The line says <i>Saved</i>. The text goes to a temporary file first and is swapped in only when that worked, so a failure keeps the old text. Line endings are kept.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Close</b>. If you have not saved, the app asks <i>Close without saving your changes?</i></li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Wrap lines</b> and <b>Colors</b> are two tick boxes at the top of the editor (both on). If something else changed the file while you were editing, Save asks whether to save your version over it. A new text file made with <b>+ File</b> opens here at once.</p>\n\n<h4>Copy, move or delete several things</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Press and hold one row for half a second, or tap <b>Select</b>. A bar appears: <i>1 selected</i>, with <b>All</b>, <b>Copy</b>, <b>Move</b>, <b>Compress</b>, <b>Delete</b> and a close button. Tap rows to add or remove them. <b>All</b> picks everything in the folder; tap it again to clear.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Copy</b> or <b>Move</b>. A bar says <i>3 items to copy: open the target folder, then tap Paste</i>.</li>\n<li>Open the target folder. The bar stays while you browse.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Paste here</b>. If a name is already used there, a box titled <i>A name is already taken</i> asks what to do. Tapping outside it cancels the paste.</li>\n</ol>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>A name that is already taken</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Replace</b></td><td>The new item goes over the old one. Two folders with the same name are merged.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Skip</b></td><td>The old one stays. The item counts as skipped.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Keep both</b></td><td>The new one gets another name, such as <code>photo (1).jpg</code>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For <b>Delete</b>, tap it in the selection bar and confirm in the box that lists the first names. Folders go with everything in them.</p>\n\n<h4>Progress, the notification and Cancel</h4>\n<p>While a job runs, a line above the list shows where it is, for example <i>Copying 2 of 5: movie.mp4 - 45% · 4.2 MB/s · 8 s left</i>, with a <b>Cancel</b> button. The same text is in a notification called <i>Copying 5 items</i>, with a progress bar and its own <b>Cancel</b>. The notification keeps the job alive if you leave the app (on Android 13 and later, allow notifications to see it). If nothing moves for 20 seconds the line says so.</p>\n<p>Cancel stops the job soon and the result says <i>stopped</i>. A copy that is stopped never leaves a half-written file under the real name. Only one file job runs at a time (<i>A file job is still running</i>). At the end you get a message such as <i>Copied 5 of 5</i>, with counts of skipped and failed items, and a box lists each failure with its reason. Moving inside one storage is instant; moving to another storage copies first and then removes the original. Jobs on places this app cannot write itself go through the working mode and show no speed or time left.</p>\n\n<h4>New folder, new file, rename</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>+ Folder</b> and <b>+ File</b> ask for a name. A name cannot be empty, hold a slash, or be <code>.</code> or <code>..</code>, and names that differ only in capital letters count as the same (<i>That name is already used here</i>).</li>\n<li><b>Rename</b> shows the name in a box: change it and tap <b>Confirm</b>. The ending (such as .txt) is just part of the name: changing it does not convert the file.</li>\n<li><b>Copy</b> and <b>Move</b> in the sheet ask for a destination path, filled in with the item's own path. Change it to a folder that already exists, for example <code>/storage/emulated/0/Download</code>. A same-named item already there is replaced without asking, so for anything important use Select, Copy and Paste here (it asks what to do when a name is taken).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Options and order</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Show hidden files</b> (off): also lists names that start with a dot (file managers usually hide these). While it is off, a line at the end of the list says how many hidden items are not shown. The search uses this switch too.</li>\n<li><b>Show thumbnails</b> (on): small pictures for photos and videos on your storage (none for pictures over 80 MB or for added storage). They are cached, up to 48 MB; <b>Clear thumbnail cache</b> empties it.</li>\n<li>The list is always folders first, then files, each in alphabetical order. There is no sort menu and no Properties screen. The size is under each name; to find the biggest or newest files use <a href=\"#files-search\">search</a> with <code>size:</code> and <code>date:</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> There is no recycle bin. Delete removes a file, or a folder with everything in it, at once. In a file's sheet, <b>Delete</b> shows a red button <i>Delete NAME?</i> (with the real name), and one tap on that button deletes at once. There is no undo: the only way back is a copy you made earlier. Before deleting something you did not make, open it and check what it is.</div>\n<div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> With a working mode you can reach folders that belong to Android itself (<code>/system</code>, <code>/data</code>, <code>/vendor</code>). Renaming, moving or deleting things there can stop the phone from starting or make apps crash. First copy anything you might need to a safe folder. The app refuses to delete or move the root, a top-level folder, a whole storage or a system tree (<i>System location: not touched</i>), but the files inside them are not protected. If the phone no longer starts normally, you may need a computer or a factory reset: see <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>.</div>\n\n<h4>If something does not work</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><i>Can't read this folder...</i>, or a folder that looks empty though it is not</td><td>All-files access is off, or it is a system folder and no working mode is connected. Grant the access, or set up a <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><i>The app cannot write here...</i></td><td>This folder is not writable for the app: grant All-files access, or connect a working mode.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><i>A folder cannot go inside itself</i></td><td>You tried to copy or move a folder into itself.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"id":"files-search","title":"Searching files","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Find a file by part of its name, its ending, size, date or kind, by words inside it, or by the names inside zip files. The search is part of the <a href=\"#tab-files\">File Manager</a>.</p>\n<p><b>Where to find it:</b> in the File Manager, the box that says <i>Search files and folders</i> (under the path box), with the <b>Search</b> button next to it.</p>\n\n<h4>Before you start</h4>\n<p>The search is done by this app itself, not through a <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>. It therefore needs All-files access to see your storage (the app asks when it is missing, unless a working mode is on); a working mode alone does not help, because the search reads the files itself. In storage you added yourself (see <a href=\"#files-storage\">SD cards and other storage</a>) it needs nothing extra.</p>\n\n<h4>Step by step</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Choose where to look in the drop-down next to the tick boxes. It starts as <b>This folder</b>. The other choices are <b>Internal storage</b>, <b>Downloads</b>, <b>Camera and photos</b>, <b>Pictures</b>, <b>Documents</b>, <b>Music</b> and <b>Movies</b> (only those folders that exist), <b>SD card or drive</b> with a code when the phone shows one, and <b>Whole phone</b>.</li>\n<li>Set the two tick boxes. <b>Include subfolders</b> (on) looks in every folder below; off, it looks only at what is directly in the folder. <b>Search inside archives</b> (off) also looks at names inside zip files.</li>\n<li>Type what you look for and tap <b>Search</b> (or press Enter). If the box is empty you see <i>Type what to search for</i>.</li>\n<li>Watch the line <i>Searching /storage/emulated/0/Download · 1200 looked at · 3 found</i>. The Search button becomes <b>Stop</b>, and the line has a <b>Cancel</b> button.</li>\n<li>The results replace the folder list. A line says how many were found and how long it took, for example <i>12 found · 0.8 s</i>. Tap <b>Back to the folder</b> to return.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>What you can type</h4>\n<p>You can type plain words, or add the special words below. Several parts together all have to fit: <code>report ext:pdf date:30d size:&gt;1mb</code>. Tap <b>Search help</b> for a short reminder on screen.</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You type</th><th>It finds</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Words</td><td>Names that contain every word. Capital letters do not matter.</td><td><code>holiday beach</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>* and ?</td><td>* stands for any run of letters, ? for exactly one</td><td><code>IMG_20*.jpg</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"quoted words\"</td><td>The words together, with the space</td><td><code>\"my notes\"</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>-word</td><td>Leaves out names that contain the word</td><td><code>report -draft</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>ext: or a dot</td><td>Files that end in one of these endings</td><td><code>ext:jpg,png</code> or <code>.pdf</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>type:</td><td>image, video, audio, text, doc, archive, apk, font, folder or file (several with commas)</td><td><code>type:video</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>size:</td><td>Bigger, smaller or between. Units: b, k, m, g, t (k is 1024 bytes). A folder never matches a size.</td><td><code>size:&gt;10mb</code>, <code>size:1k..5m</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>date:</td><td>When it was last changed: today, yesterday, week, month, year; 7d, 2w, 3m, 1y (days, weeks, months, years back); a day; a range; before or after a day</td><td><code>date:7d</code>, <code>date:2025-03-01..2025-03-31</code>, <code>date:&lt;2024-01-01</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>content:</td><td>Text files that contain the word. With several content: words, all must be in the same file.</td><td><code>content:invoice</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>archive:</td><td>Entries inside zip-type archives whose name contains the word</td><td><code>archive:classes.dex</code></td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A part the app cannot understand (for example <code>size:abc</code>) is reported in a note above the results, and the rest of the search still runs.</p>\n\n<h4>Reading the results</h4>\n<p>Each result shows the name, where it is (for an archive entry: the archive, then the entry), the size and the date, and, for a <code>content:</code> search, the first matching line with its line number. Tap a result:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a file opens the same sheet as in the folder list (View, Edit, Share, Rename...);</li>\n<li>a folder opens that folder;</li>\n<li>an archive entry opens the archive and puts the entry's name in its search box.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <b>In folder</b> button at the right of a result closes the results and shows the folder that holds it. After you rename or delete something from the results, the search runs again by itself.</p>\n\n<h4>The pills above the results</h4>\n<p>A row of pills sorts the results: <b>All</b>, <b>Images</b>, <b>Video</b>, <b>Audio</b>, <b>Documents</b>, <b>APKs</b>, <b>Archives</b>, <b>Folders</b>, <b>Over 10 MB</b>, <b>Over 100 MB</b>, <b>Last 7 days</b>, <b>Last 30 days</b>. They work on the results you already have, by the ending of each name.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The kinds combine with \"or\": Images and Video together show both.</li>\n<li>Size (10 or 100 MB) and age (7 or 30 days) take one choice each: tapping the other one swaps it.</li>\n<li>A kind, a size and an age all have to fit at once.</li>\n<li>With two or more pills on, a line says what is shown, for example <i>Showing Images or Video, Over 10 MB.</i>, with a <b>Clear filters</b> button. The count adds how many are shown. <b>All</b> turns every pill off.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Searching an SD card or other added storage</h4>\n<p>Open the added storage first, then leave the drop-down on <b>This folder</b>. Names, endings, kinds, sizes, dates and <code>content:</code> (text files up to 4 MB) all work there. <code>archive:</code> is skipped, with a note saying so, and <b>Search inside archives</b> does nothing there.</p>\n\n<h4>What is not searched, and how long it takes</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Names inside archives are searched only with <code>archive:</code> or the <b>Search inside archives</b> tick box, and only in zip-type files (zip, apk, apks, apkm, xapk, jar, aar, epub, docx, xlsx, pptx and similar). The inside of 7z, rar and tar files is never searched. With that tick box on, <code>content:</code> also reads text entries up to 1 MB.</li>\n<li><code>content:</code> reads only text files up to 4 MB. Pictures, videos, music, archives, empty files and files that are not text are skipped.</li>\n<li>Names that start with a dot are skipped unless <b>Show hidden files</b> is on. Links to folders are not followed, and the system folders <code>/proc</code>, <code>/sys</code> and <code>/dev</code> are skipped.</li>\n<li>Files and folders this app is not allowed to read are skipped without a message.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The search does not go deeper than 40 folder levels. It stops by itself at 1000 results, after 400,000 files and folders looked at, or after 90 seconds, and the line then says <i>stopped at the limit</i>. Tap <b>Stop</b> (or <b>Cancel</b>) to stop earlier: the line says <i>stopped</i> and the results found so far stay. Only one search runs at a time (<i>A search is still running</i>).</p>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Name searches are quick. <code>content:</code> and <code>archive:</code> searches open files and are slow on a big storage. Make them smaller: choose a folder in the drop-down instead of <b>Whole phone</b>, and add <code>ext:</code>, <code>type:</code>, <code>size:</code> or <code>date:</code> to cut down the files that have to be opened.</div>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Nothing found?</b> Check the drop-down (the folder you are looking at, or all of storage), whether the word is in a hidden name (turn on <b>Show hidden files</b>), and whether All-files access is on. Capital letters never matter, but spelling does.</div>"},{"id":"files-archives","title":"Zip, 7z, rar, tar and other archives","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">An archive is one file that holds many files (a zip file is the best known). The <a href=\"#tab-files\">File Manager</a> can look inside one without unpacking it, unpack all or part of it, make new ones, and change what is inside some of them.</p>\n<p><b>Where to find it:</b> tap an archive in the File Manager, then <b>View contents</b>, <b>Extract...</b> or <b>Open as archive</b>. To make one, select files and tap <b>Compress</b>.</p>\n\n<h4>Before you start</h4>\n<p>This works on files the app can read itself (your storage with All-files access) and, through a copy, on added storage. Changing an archive needs a folder this app can write to. No <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a> is needed for your own storage.</p>\n\n<h4>Which formats work</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Open and extract</th><th>Make new</th><th>Change inside</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Zip and its relatives: .zip, .apk, .apks, .apkm, .xapk, .jar, .aar, .aab, .war, .epub, .docx, .xlsx, .pptx, .odt, .ods, .odp, .ipa, .xpi, .crx, .kmz, .cbz, .whl, .nupkg, .vsix and more</td><td>Yes</td><td>As .zip</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>7z (.7z, .cb7)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>rar (.rar, .cbr: RAR4 and RAR5)</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>tar: .tar, .tar.gz (.tgz), .tar.bz2 (.tbz, .tbz2), .tar.xz (.txz), .tar.zst (.tzst), .tar.lz4 (.tlz4)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>One compressed file: .gz, .bz2, .xz, .zst, .lz4</td><td>Yes (shown as the one file inside)</td><td>Yes, one file only</td><td>No</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Passwords work for zip (ZipCrypto and AES), 7z (AES-256) and for opening rar. A rar split into several parts is not supported. Reading rar files is new and was tested mostly with test files, not with many real archives: if one will not open, unpack it on a computer. The zst formats depend on the phone: if the phone refuses them, a file of that kind may not open, and Compress says <i>This format isn't available in this build yet</i>.</p>\n\n<h4>Look inside an archive</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>In the File Manager tap the archive. In the sheet tap <b>View contents</b>. For a file that does not look like an archive, tap <b>Open as archive</b>; if it is not one you see <i>Not a readable archive</i>.</li>\n<li>The folder list is replaced by an archive card. At the top: the name, and a line such as <i>12 files • 3.4 MB • 7Z (solid: files are packed together) • password-protected</i>, then where the archive is. Under it: a search box (<i>Search inside the archive...</i>), a path trail you can tap, and the buttons <b>Up</b>, <b>Extract this folder</b>, <b>+ Add file</b>, <b>+ Folder</b>, <b>Compare</b> (and <b>Sign</b> for an .apk).</li>\n<li>Tap a folder to go in. Each row says the size and, for files, <i>42% packed</i> (or <i>stored</i>, or <i>encrypted</i>) and the date. Long lists show 400 rows; tap <b>Show more</b> for the rest. A search shows at most 5000 matches.</li>\n<li>Tap a file for its sheet. <b>View</b> shows text with colours (first 256 KB), a picture (up to 4 MB), an app's compiled XML turned back into readable XML (read only), or, for anything else, the first 512 bytes as hexadecimal numbers (a plain way of writing raw data). A line shows size, packed size, CRC (a check code for the contents) and date.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Close</b> at the top right to go back to the folders. <b>Open inside</b> opens an archive that is inside this one; that one is view only.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>Extract</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Tap <b>Extract...</b> in a file's sheet (the whole archive), in an entry's sheet (that file or folder), or <b>Extract this folder</b> in the archive card.</li>\n<li>In <b>Extract into</b> choose <b>This folder</b> (where the archive is), <b>A new folder named after the archive</b> (the starting choice), or <b>Another folder</b> (type its full path).</li>\n<li>Under <b>If a file is already there</b> choose <b>Replace it</b>, <b>Skip it</b> or <b>Keep both</b> (the starting choice: the new file becomes <code>name (1).ext</code>).</li>\n<li>Optionally tick <b>Delete the archive afterwards</b> (whole archives on your storage only). The app asks again, and deletes it only if every file was extracted with nothing skipped.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Extract</b>. A progress line with <b>Cancel</b> and a notification show speed and time left. At the end: <i>Extracted 12 files (3.4 MB) to /storage/emulated/0/Download/photos</i>. Names that are unsafe (for example ones that climb out of the folder) are skipped and listed.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If the folder is not writable for the app you see <i>This app can't write to ...</i>: choose a folder on shared storage.</p>\n\n<h4>Passwords</h4>\n<p>A box called <b>Password needed</b> asks when an archive or one file in it is locked. Type it (tick <b>Show the password</b> to check it) and tap <b>Open</b>, <b>Extract</b> or <b>Unlock</b>. A wrong one gives <i>That password did not work. Try again.</i> The box says the password is used only for this archive, only while it is open, and is never saved. A zip opens without a password; only the locked files ask. A 7z or rar with locked names asks before it opens.</p>\n\n<h4>Make an archive</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Select the files and folders (see <a href=\"#tab-files\">File Manager</a>) and tap <b>Compress</b>, or tap <b>Compress...</b> in the sheet of one item. Added storage does not offer it.</li>\n<li>In the <b>Compress</b> box set the name, the <b>Format</b> (Zip opens everywhere; 7z packs smallest; the tar formats; the single-file formats appear only when one file is picked), and <b>Packing</b>: Store (none, fastest), Fast, Normal (starting choice) or Maximum.</li>\n<li>For zip or 7z you can add a <b>Password</b>. For zip choose <b>Protection</b>: AES-256 (strong, the starting choice), AES-128 or ZipCrypto (weak but opens everywhere). 7z always uses AES-256 and hides the file names too.</li>\n<li>Choose <b>Save into</b> (this folder or another full path) and what to do if the name is taken (<b>Keep both</b> or <b>Replace it</b>), then tap <b>Compress</b>.</li>\n<li>The result reads like <i>Created photos.zip (12 files, 3.4 MB to 2.1 MB, password-protected)</i>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> If you lose the password of an archive, the files cannot be recovered. Write it down somewhere safe.</div>\n\n<h4>Change what is inside</h4>\n<p>For zip, 7z and tar archives, the entry sheet has <b>Edit text</b> (plain UTF-8 text up to 256 KB, with the same kind of line ending all through), <b>Rename / move</b> (change the folder part of the path to move it) and <b>Delete</b>; the card has <b>+ Add file</b> (type the path of a file on storage, then the name to give it inside) and <b>+ Folder</b>. The archive is rewritten next to the original and swapped in only when that worked and the result can be read back, so a failure keeps the original.</p>\n<p>It is refused for installed and system packages (under <code>/system</code>, <code>/vendor</code>, <code>/data/app</code> and similar: copy the file to storage first), for archives opened from inside another archive, and for files with extra data in front of the archive (such as self-extracting ones). rar and single compressed files cannot be changed: if the buttons show, an attempt ends with <i>This archive can't be edited here</i>. Compare lets you check two archives, or an archive and an installed app, entry by entry.</p>\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Changing an .apk breaks its signature, so Android will not install it until it is signed again. The app asks before the first change and then shows a red banner with <b>Sign it now...</b>. The signature uses a key made on this phone; an installed app signed by someone else cannot be updated with it, and removing the installed one first deletes its data. There is no undo for an edit: copy the archive first.</div>\n\n<h4>Big archives</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Opening reads only the list of names, so it is quick even for a big file.</li>\n<li>In a solid 7z or rar, or a compressed tar, reading one file means unpacking the ones before it. Extracting everything is faster than many single files.</li>\n<li>One archive job runs at a time (<i>Another archive job is still running</i>). Make sure there is free space for the result.</li>\n<li>A file on added storage is first copied into the app's cache (up to 300 MB). <b>Open inside</b> and <b>Install</b> refuse a single entry over 2 GB: extract it instead.</li>\n<li>If the app cannot read the file itself, it copies it through the working mode first (up to 3 GB).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>If it fails.</b> <i>Not a readable archive</i>: the file is damaged, cut short, or not an archive. <i>That password did not work</i>: type it again (a damaged archive gives the same message). <i>This file changed after it was opened</i>: close the archive, open it again and repeat the change.</div>"},{"id":"files-storage","title":"SD cards, USB drives and other storage","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Add an SD card, a USB drive or a folder that another app shares (Termux, or a cloud app that offers its files to Android, for example), so the <a href=\"#tab-files\">File Manager</a> can work on it. You do it once, through Android's own picker.</p>\n<p><b>Where to find it:</b> File Manager, the row of links under the options, button <b>+ Add storage</b>.</p>\n\n<h4>Why Android asks you to pick the folder</h4>\n<p>Android does not let an app open any folder it likes on a card, a drive or another app's space. It must be given a folder by you, in a system screen (the Storage Access Framework). The app then holds that one folder only. This needs no <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>, no root and no All-files access.</p>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> On some phones, once All-files access is on, a card can also be opened by typing its path, <code>/storage/</code> followed by a code like <code>1A2B-3C4D</code>, in the path box. The search drop-down lists such a card as <b>SD card or drive</b>. If that works for you, you may not need <b>+ Add storage</b>. If it does not work, or you want to reach a drive or another app's folder, add it as below.</div>\n\n<h4>Add a storage</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Plug in or insert the card or drive. In the File Manager tap <b>+ Add storage</b>.</li>\n<li>Android shows its own file picker (its look differs from phone to phone). Open its side menu, choose the card or drive, then use its button that allows access to this folder (often <i>Use this folder</i> and then <i>Allow</i>).</li>\n<li>The File Manager opens the storage and a button with its name appears in the row, with an X next to it. The grant stays after you restart the app.</li>\n<li>Tap the name button at any time to come back to the top of that storage. Inside a folder, the <b>..</b> row goes one level up; the top of a storage has no such row. The name on the button is the one Android gives the storage (for a card it can be a code such as <code>1A2B-3C4D</code>).</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>What works on added storage</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Works?</th><th>How</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Browse, new folder, new file, rename, delete</td><td>Yes</td><td>As on the phone's storage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Copy and move</td><td>Yes</td><td>Copy or Move, open the target folder, <b>Paste here</b>: into, out of and between storages, with the name choices and Cancel</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Select several items</td><td>Yes</td><td>Press and hold, or <b>Select</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>View and edit text</td><td>Yes</td><td>In place, up to 2 MB</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pictures, PDF, fonts, archives, Open with, Share, Extract</td><td>Yes</td><td>The file is copied once into the app's cache (up to 300 MB) and reused until the original changes. You see <i>Getting the file...</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Search</td><td>Mostly</td><td>Names, endings, kinds, sizes, dates and <code>content:</code>; not <code>archive:</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Compress, Install, Sign, thumbnails</td><td>No</td><td>Copy the file to your phone's storage first</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These tasks work on a copy, so a change made to an archive that sits on a card may not reach the original. To change one, copy it to the phone's storage, change it there, and copy it back with Copy and Paste here.</p>\n\n<h4>Remove a storage</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Tap the X next to the storage's name.</li>\n<li>Confirm the question <i>Remove \"name\" from the file manager? This only takes away this app's access to it - nothing on it is deleted.</i></li>\n<li>The button goes away. If you were inside it, the File Manager goes back to your storage. To use it again, tap <b>+ Add storage</b> and pick it again.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>Find APKs is somewhere else</h4>\n<p>The <b>Find APKs on this device</b> button is not here. It lives in the <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a> tab and searches the phone's own storage by path, not storages added here.</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Delete on a card or drive is just as final as on the phone: there is no recycle bin. Before you take a card or drive out, let Android eject it (in its own settings) so a copy that is still being written is not cut off.</div>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>If it fails.</b> The picker does not show the card: remove and re-insert it, or look in the picker's side menu. A folder lists as empty or gives an error: the card may be out or locked; put it back and tap its button again. <i>This file is over 300 MB, too big to open from added storage here</i>: copy it to the phone with Copy and Paste here instead. <i>Could not read this file</i>: the provider refused it; try again, or copy it first.</div>"},{"id":"tab-terminal","title":"Command-Line Interface","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Type commands to your phone and read the answers, in one of three shells. A second pane, the <b>ADB Console</b>, is the older command box of this app. Coding assistants are covered in <a href=\"#terminal-agents\">Coding agents in the terminal</a>.</p>\n<p><b>Where to find it:</b> the tab named <b>Command-Line Interface</b>. Two big buttons at the top, <b>Terminal</b> and <b>ADB Console</b>, switch between the panes; the tab remembers the one you used last.</p>\n\n<h4>Before you start</h4>\n<p>A command is a line of text that tells the phone to do one thing. A shell is the program that reads your lines and runs them. Which shell you pick decides what the commands may do. Pick it with <b>Shell</b> above the screen. Words such as ADB and root are explained in the <a href=\"#glossary\">Glossary</a>.</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Shell</th><th>Runs as</th><th>Needs</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>This app (sandbox)</b></td><td>This app, in its private home folder</td><td>Nothing</td><td>File and text commands. It cannot touch other apps, and <code>pm</code> or <code>dumpsys</code> usually do not work</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Working mode (ADB, Shizuku, Root)</b></td><td>The ADB shell user, or root with Root</td><td>A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a></td><td><code>pm</code>, <code>am</code>, <code>dumpsys</code>, <code>settings</code>, <code>cmd</code>, logcat</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Termux (bash and packages)</b></td><td>The Termux app, in its home folder</td><td>Termux from F-Droid or GitHub, and a one-time setup</td><td>Real bash, <code>pkg install</code>, git, python</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Termux is a separate free app that gives you a full command line (bash is its shell, and <code>pkg</code> installs programs into it). The first time, the tab picks Termux if it is set up, otherwise the working mode if one is connected, otherwise the sandbox (you can change that in the gear, under <b>Shell when the Terminal opens</b>). Each shell has its own screen and stays open while you use other tabs, so <code>cd</code> (change folder) and variables carry over. <b>Restart shell</b> starts a fresh one.</p>\n<p>Next to the drop-downs at the top (<b>Coding Agents</b>, <b>Model</b>, <b>Effort</b>) are three small buttons: the gear (<b>Terminal settings</b>, which holds your keys and defaults), <b>MCP</b> and <b>Help</b> (a short explanation of the Terminal inside the app).</p>\n\n<h4>Run your first command</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open the <b>Terminal</b> pane. The black screen prints a welcome and a line saying which shell is ready, for example <i>sh ready through ADB, running as the shell user (uid 2000) in</i> and a folder. The uid is the user number: 2000 is the ADB shell user, 0 is root.</li>\n<li>Tap the box at the bottom, type <code>getprop ro.product.model</code> and tap <b>RUN</b> (or Enter on the keyboard).</li>\n<li>Your line is echoed as <i>folder $ command</i> and the answer appears under it: the model name of your phone. The prompt shows <i>#</i> instead of <i>$</i> when the shell is root.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Harmless commands to start with (they only read):</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Shows</th><th>Best in</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><code>getprop ro.build.version.release</code></td><td>The Android version</td><td>Any shell</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>id</code></td><td>Which user the shell runs as</td><td>Any shell</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>df -h</code></td><td>Free space on each storage</td><td>Any shell</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>date</code></td><td>The date and time</td><td>Any shell</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>pm list packages -3</code></td><td>Package names of the apps you installed</td><td>Working mode</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Typing, keys and history</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>RUN</b> sends the line. While a command runs it turns into <b>STOP</b>. The small button at its left shows <b>$</b> (or <b>#</b>): it switches between commands and the AI chat of an agent, and is dim until you pick one.</li>\n<li>The key rows under the box work like Termux's: <b>ESC</b> clears the line, <b>TAB</b> completes a file name (several matches are listed), <b>CTRL</b> stops what is running, the up and down arrow keys go through earlier commands (the last 120 are remembered), the left and right arrow keys, <b>HOME</b> and <b>END</b> move the cursor, and | / - ~ are there because phone keyboards hide them. <b>SHIFT</b> swaps the row for a second row of symbols: backtick, $ ; : \" ' \\ { } ( ) * &amp; #. Tap SHIFT again to go back.</li>\n<li><b>Suggestions.</b> While you type, a faint grey completion shows on a line just above the box (the part you have not typed yet, after what you have). It comes from your earlier commands first, then from the packages on your phone (after <code>pm</code>, <code>am</code>, <code>dumpsys</code> and similar commands) and a list of common commands. <b>Press the right arrow key</b> with the cursor at the end of the line to accept it, or <b>tap the suggestion line</b> (phone keyboards often have no right arrow). Keep typing to ignore it. It only suggests; nothing runs until you tap <b>RUN</b>.</li>\n<li><code>clear</code> empties the screen. <code>exit</code> ends the shell; press Enter to start a new one.</li>\n<li>Programs that need the whole screen (nano, vim, top, a Python prompt, ssh) cannot run here. The screen says it needs a real terminal and offers <b>Open in Termux</b> or <b>Run here anyway</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Stop a command, copy the output</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Tap <b>STOP</b>, or the <b>CTRL</b> key, to end a running command. A command is stopped by itself after 30 minutes. If a command prints more than 2,000,000 characters the screen says <i>output cut off here</i>; the command keeps running until you stop it. The screen keeps about the last 300,000 characters (<i>older output trimmed</i>).</li>\n<li>Below the screen: <b>Real terminal</b> opens a full-screen terminal that runs programs needing a real terminal (see below). <b>Full screen</b> makes the Terminal cover the whole app: the screen takes all the room above the input line and the extra keys, and follows the on-screen keyboard so nothing hides behind it. Everything stays: the shell, agent, model and effort menus, settings, the cheat sheet and the extra keys. Tap <b>Exit full screen</b> or use Back to come out; leaving the tab does too. <b>Clear</b> empties it, <b>Copy</b> copies the text you selected or, with nothing selected, the whole screen, <b>Cheat Sheet</b>, <b>Open in Termux</b>, <b>New chat</b> (only with an agent) and <b>Restart shell</b>. Press and hold on the screen text to select part of it.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>The cheat sheets</h4>\n<p><b>Cheat Sheet</b> opens a searchable list of ready-made commands that depends on the shell. Tap one: it lands in the input box, and the first part in angle brackets such as <code>&lt;package&gt;</code> is selected so you can type over it. Nothing runs until you tap RUN.</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Shell</th><th>Title</th><th>Groups</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>This app</td><td>Sandbox Cheat Sheet</td><td>Files, Text, Process / system</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Termux</td><td>Termux Cheat Sheet</td><td>Packages, Files, Text, Git, Network, Process / system</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Working mode, and the ADB Console</td><td>ADB Cheat Sheet</td><td>Device info, Current app / focus, Packages, App control, Permissions, Activity Manager / intents, Input, Key events, Screen, Connectivity, Battery (testing), Logs, Files, Reboot</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The ADB sheet also has a button, <b>Load the full reference from the gist</b>, that loads a longer list live from a public page on GitHub (Pulimet's ADB gist). Pressing it contacts GitHub.</p>\n\n<h4>Set up Termux</h4>\n<p>Open the gear, then <b>Termux setup</b> (it also opens by itself when you pick Termux and it is not ready). It lists five steps and puts a tick on each one that is done (the second step never gets a tick):</p>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li><b>Install Termux</b> from F-Droid (a free app store for open-source apps) or GitHub. The old Google Play build is not suitable.</li>\n<li><b>Open Termux once</b>, so it finishes installing itself.</li>\n<li><b>Let Termux take commands from other apps</b>: tap <b>Copy</b>, open Termux, paste and run the line shown (it adds <code>allow-external-apps=true</code> to Termux's settings).</li>\n<li><b>Allow this app</b>: Android asks whether this app may <i>Run commands in Termux environment</i>.</li>\n<li><b>Test the connection</b>: <b>Test</b> sends a harmless command and says <i>Termux answered</i>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Then tap <b>Use Termux as the shell</b>. <b>Sync with Termux</b> makes this shell match your own Termux: it starts the Termux shell again, reads your aliases, functions and exports from <code>~/.bashrc</code> (also when that file starts with the usual \"only for interactive shells\" line) and prints what it took over: the bash version, home folder, package folder, how many aliases and functions, and whether phone storage is set up. <b>Set up storage in Termux</b> opens Termux and runs <code>termux-setup-storage</code> only when phone storage is not set up yet (Android may ask you to allow it); when it is, Termux just says so and nothing is rebuilt.</p>\n<p><b>Set up Termux now</b> (the same sheet, enabled once Termux is installed and allowed) is the one-tap setup of a working command line. It runs these steps in the Termux shell, where you can watch each one: update the package lists (<code>pkg update</code>), install <b>vim</b>, <b>nano</b>, <b>git</b>, <b>python</b>, <b>openssh</b>, <b>curl</b>, <b>wget</b> and <b>htop</b> (<code>pkg install</code>), write a starter <code>~/.bashrc</code>, <code>~/.vimrc</code> and <code>~/.nanorc</code>, and check that each tool is there. <b>Choose tools</b> lets you tick others (tmux, ripgrep, jq, zip, unzip, rsync, nodejs, clang, make, man) or leave some out, and turn any of the three starter files off; the choice is remembered. Then tap <b>Open the real terminal</b> to use them.</p>\n<p>The starter <code>.bashrc</code> gives you a colour prompt that shows the folder and the git branch (and turns its <code>$</code> red after a command that failed), a long history shared by all your terminals, aliases (<code>ll</code>, <code>la</code>, <code>..</code>, <code>gs</code>, <code>gl</code>, <code>py</code>, <code>sd</code> for shared storage, <code>dl</code> for Downloads, <code>update</code>), and small helpers: <code>mkcd</code> (make a folder and go in), <code>extract</code> (open any archive) and <code>serve</code> (share the current folder over HTTP). The <code>.vimrc</code> turns on syntax colours, line numbers, the mouse and four-space tabs; the <code>.nanorc</code> line numbers, the mouse and syntax colours. Only a marked block is written (it starts with <code># >>> ADB App Manager starter >>></code>): your own lines above or below it stay, and running the setup again replaces the block instead of adding a second one. If you have no <code>~/.bash_profile</code>, one is made so that login shells (the real terminal's) read <code>~/.bashrc</code>; if you have one, it is left alone. If a step fails (no internet, for example), the setup stops there and says which step; run it again when it is fixed, the finished steps are quick the second time.</p>\n\n<h4>The real terminal (full screen)</h4>\n<p>The line-based Terminal cannot run programs that draw on the whole screen. <b>Real terminal</b> (the button below the screen) can: it runs your shell on a genuine pseudo-terminal, as Termux does, so <b>vim</b>, <b>nano</b>, <b>top</b>, <b>htop</b>, <b>less</b>, <b>ssh</b>, a Python prompt, colours, the arrow keys, Ctrl-C and window resizing all work. It covers the whole app, follows the on-screen keyboard, and keeps what you did when you close it with the X or Back (the session goes on until you type <code>exit</code> or tap <b>Restart</b>; after <code>exit</code>, press Enter to start a new shell).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Shell</b> (top left): <b>This app (sandbox)</b> is Android's own shell in this app's folder, with only the tools Android has (toybox): no vim or nano. <b>Working mode</b> is a shell with the power of ADB, Shizuku or Root (for ADB it is the phone's own <code>adb shell</code> on a terminal). <b>Termux</b> is Termux's bash with everything you installed there with <code>pkg</code>, vim and nano included; it needs the Termux set-up of the Terminal's settings. Changing the shell restarts the terminal.</li>\n<li><b>Keys</b> (bottom): <b>ESC</b>, <b>TAB</b>, the arrows (hold to repeat), <b>HOME</b>, <b>END</b>, <b>PGUP</b>, <b>PGDN</b>, <b>/</b> and <b>&minus;</b>, a row of symbols (<code>| ~ $ ; : \" ' \\ { } [ ] ( ) &lt; &gt; * &amp; # = + _ ? ! @ % ^ . ,</code> and the backtick), <b>FN</b> for F1 to F12, and the keyboard key that shows or hides the keyboard. <b>CTRL</b> and <b>ALT</b> are sticky: tap once and the next key you type gets it (CTRL then <b>c</b> is Ctrl-C); tap twice to lock it (it shows a line under it), a third time to free it.</li>\n<li><b>A&minus;</b> and <b>A+</b> change the text size (remembered). <b>Copy</b> copies the selected text, or with nothing selected the whole screen. <b>Paste</b> types the clipboard in. <b>Restart</b> ends the session and starts a new one.</li>\n<li>Behind it is a small helper program that comes with the app (<code>libptyexec.so</code>), for 64-bit and 32-bit phones; Android does not let an app open a terminal by itself.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>The ADB Console pane</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A card shows the active working mode, with <b>Configure</b> and <b>Working Modes</b>, and buttons <b>Rish mode</b> (a long-lived Shizuku shell where <code>cd</code> sticks), <b>UID (id)</b>, <b>Connect 5555</b>, <b>tcpip 5555</b>, <b>Dev Options</b> and <b>Cheat Sheet</b>. See <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>.</li>\n<li>Each command is run on its own through the active mode, so <code>cd</code> does not carry over (Rish mode does). Without a working mode the commands run as this app, like the sandbox.</li>\n<li><b>Find in output...</b> highlights matches (<i>2 / 7 matches</i>) with arrows to jump; <b>Copy</b> puts the whole output on the clipboard and <b>Share</b> sends it as a text file.</li>\n<li><b>Suggestions.</b> The ADB Console completes as you type, the same way the Terminal does: a grey line above the box shows the rest of the command, from your history first, then from the packages on your phone and a list of common <code>adb</code>/shell commands. <b>Right arrow</b> (cursor at the end) or a <b>tap on the line</b> accepts it. Nothing runs until you tap <b>Run</b>.</li>\n<li><b>History</b> lists the last 80 commands (tap one to edit it, <b>Run</b>, <b>Save</b>, or the X). <b>Saved</b> keeps named commands or several-line scripts; <b>Pin</b> turns up to six into one-tap buttons.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Safety: what some commands do</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th><th>Undo</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><code>rm file</code></td><td>Deletes the file at once. There is no recycle bin.</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>rm -r folder</code></td><td>Deletes the folder and everything in it</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>pm uninstall --user 0 name</code></td><td>Removes an app for you</td><td><code>pm install-existing name</code> for a system app; other apps must be installed again</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>pm clear name</code></td><td>Erases the app's data: logins, saves, settings</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>pm disable-user --user 0 name</code></td><td>Freezes an app</td><td><code>pm enable name</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>pm list packages</code></td><td>Only lists. Harmless.</td><td>Not needed</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> A command does exactly what it says, with the rights of the shell you picked. Never paste a command you do not understand, even from a friendly website. Start with commands that only read (<code>list</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>ls</code>, <code>df</code>).</div>\n<div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> In the working mode shell, and above all as root, commands such as <code>rm -r</code> on a system folder, <code>settings put</code>, <code>setprop</code> or <code>reboot</code> can stop the phone from starting or lose your data. Back up first (<a href=\"#apps-backup\">App backups</a>) and read <a href=\"#safety\">Safety</a>. If an app stops working after a command, undo it with the matching command above.</div>\n\n<h4>If it fails</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><i>No working mode is connected: connect ADB, Shizuku or Root first</i>: tap <b>Working Modes</b> in the message, or choose another shell.</li>\n<li><i>Termux is not installed</i> or <i>This app may not run commands in Termux yet</i>: follow <b>Termux setup</b> above.</li>\n<li><i>The shell is still busy with an earlier command: CTRL-C ends it</i>: tap STOP.</li>\n<li><i>The shell is not running: press Enter to start it again</i>, or tap <b>Restart shell</b>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"terminal-agents","title":"Coding agents in the terminal","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">A coding agent is an AI assistant you chat with inside the Terminal. It can suggest commands, read files and propose changes to files. You approve each step before anything runs.</p>\n<p><b>Where to find it:</b> <a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a>, <b>Terminal</b> pane, the drop-down <b>Coding Agents</b> at the top. Choose <b>None</b> to use the Terminal on your own.</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> An agent can be wrong, and it runs commands with the rights of the shell you chose. In the working mode shell or as root, one command can remove apps, erase data or change settings. Read every command and every file change before you press <b>Run</b> or <b>Apply</b>. Do not press <b>Always allow in this chat</b> unless you are ready to accept whatever follows. Before you start, make a backup (<a href=\"#apps-backup\">App backups</a>). Commands cannot be undone; only file changes can (<code>/undo</code>).</div>\n\n<h4>Before you start</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>You need the internet and an account with the provider (to get an API key), or the provider's own tool in Termux, or a server of your own. Providers bill the key's account for what you use.</li>\n<li>What you chat about is sent to that provider (see Privacy below).</li>\n<li>The agent works in the shell you picked. The sandbox is the safest to try.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>The agents in the list</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Agent (as named in the list)</th><th>How it connects</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Gemini (Google), Claude (Anthropic), ChatGPT (OpenAI)</td><td>An API key, or the provider's own tool (Gemini CLI, Claude Code, Codex) in Termux</td><td>A subscription works only through the provider's own tool</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cursor (SpaceX)</td><td>An API key for Cursor's cloud agents, or Cursor CLI in Termux</td><td>Cloud agents work in Cursor's cloud on a Git repository you may name. They cannot see your phone's files</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Copilot (GitHub)</td><td>GitHub's Copilot CLI in Termux, signed in with GitHub or with a token</td><td>Needs a Copilot plan</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Perplexity, Grok (SpaceX), Muse (Meta), Deepseek</td><td>An API key only</td><td>Same steps as below. Their saved keys show in the gear like the others</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Jan.ai, AnythingLLM, Ollama (on-device)</td><td>The address of your own server. Jan.ai and Ollama need a key only if the server asks for one; AnythingLLM needs a key from its own settings</td><td>Free. Jan.ai and AnythingLLM run on a computer; Ollama runs on the phone through Termux</td></tr>\n<tr><td>OpenCode</td><td>Installed in Termux from its sheet</td><td>Signs in to a provider of your choice</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DroidMind, Kilo Code, Leon.ai</td><td>Not chat agents</td><td>Choosing one opens a sheet that explains what it is (DroidMind's sheet also has an install button for Termux)</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Add an API key</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open the <b>Coding Agents</b> drop-down and choose an agent under <b>API Key Required</b>. If it is not connected yet, a sheet named after the agent opens (for Gemini, Claude, ChatGPT and Cursor it has two tabs: <b>API key</b> and <b>Sign in with subscription</b>; stay on <b>API key</b> for these steps).</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Get a key</b>. The provider's website opens. Create a key there (a long secret text, like a password). The provider may ask for billing details.</li>\n<li>Come back, tap the key box and paste the key. Tap <b>Show</b> to check it.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Test and save</b>. The status says <i>Testing...</i>. The app sends one small request to the provider with your key, asking for its list of models. If it works, the key is saved, the sheet closes and the screen prints <i>[Claude (Anthropic): it works, 8 models]</i>.</li>\n<li>If it fails, a sheet titled with the agent's name and <i>the test failed</i> gives the reason (for example <i>The key was not accepted</i>) and the exact reply, with <b>Try again</b>, <b>Save anyway</b> and <b>Copy details</b>. Use Save anyway only if you are sure the key is right and the test itself failed.</li>\n<li>To remove a key later: open the agent's sheet and tap <b>Forget the key</b>, then confirm. The gear lists every key as <i>key sk-ant-...a1b2</i>, with <b>Change</b>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>How the key is kept.</b> Each key is encrypted with AES-256-GCM (a strong standard way of scrambling data) under a key held in the Android Keystore (Android's protected store for secrets), and kept in this app's private settings. App backups are off, so the key is not copied into a phone backup. The screen you see never gets the key back; only the start and the last four characters are shown. The app adds the key to a request itself, and only when the request goes to that provider's own address (or, for your own server, the address you saved with the key). If the Keystore is reset, the sheet says <i>it can no longer be read: enter it again</i>.</p>\n\n<h4>Model and effort</h4>\n<p><b>Model</b> lists the provider's models. The list is read from the provider after a key works (at most once a day, or when you type <code>/models</code>); until then a short built-in list is shown. <b>Effort</b> is <b>Low</b>, <b>Balanced</b> (the starting choice) or <b>High</b>: how hard the agent is asked to think. Higher is slower and uses more of your paid quota. It changes anything only for Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini and Grok chatting through a key.</p>\n\n<h4>Chat with an agent</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Pick the agent. The small button left of the input switches to <b>AI</b> and the button says <b>SEND</b>. The state line under the Shell box shows the agent and, after a reply, how many tokens (pieces of text) were sent and received, for example <i>1.2k in / 300 out</i>.</li>\n<li>Type a request, such as <i>Which of my apps use the most storage?</i>, and tap <b>SEND</b>. The answer streams in after <i>claude ›</i> (the agent's name).</li>\n<li>Start a line with <code>!</code> to run it yourself as a command (<code>!ls</code>). Lines starting with <code>/</code> are chat commands: <code>/help</code>, <code>/new</code> (start over), <code>/undo</code>, <code>/stop</code>, <code>/agent name</code>, <code>/model name</code>, <code>/models</code>, <code>/effort high</code> and <code>/settings</code>.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>STOP</b> to end the reply, and the command it started. <b>New chat</b> forgets the conversation.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>How approvals work</h4>\n<p>The agent works through four kinds of step: run a command, read a file, write a file, change part of a file. The app shows a card for the ones that matter and waits for your tap.</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>The card says</th><th>Buttons</th><th>What a tap does</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><i>Claude wants to run this in Working mode (/folder):</i> and the command</td><td><b>Run</b>, <b>Skip</b>, <b>Always allow in this chat</b></td><td>Run executes it and the output goes back to the agent. Skip runs nothing; the agent waits for you.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><i>wants to change</i> (or <i>replace</i>, or <i>create</i>) a file, with the changed lines, red for removed and green for added</td><td><b>Apply</b>, <b>Skip</b>, <b>Always allow in this chat</b></td><td>Apply writes the file. You can take it back with <code>/undo</code>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><i>wants to read a file outside the working folder:</i> and the path</td><td><b>Allow</b>, <b>Skip</b></td><td>Allow lets it read that one file.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>Reading files inside the folder you are in needs no OK.</li>\n<li><b>Always allow in this chat</b> stops the questions for the rest of the chat. For commands it is remembered per shell and user, so allowing in the sandbox does not allow commands as root. <b>New chat</b> ends it.</li>\n<li>After a Skip the screen says <i>[tell Claude what to do instead]</i>. After 25 steps (you can choose 10, 25, 50 or 100) it pauses; send <i>go on</i> to continue.</li>\n<li>The gear holds the switches <b>Ask before running commands</b> (on), <b>Ask before changing files</b> (on) and <b>Read files outside the working folder without asking</b> (off). Turning the first two off lets the agent act with no question at all. Leave them on.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The agent is told not to run destructive commands (<code>rm -r</code>, <code>pm uninstall</code>, <code>pm clear</code>, <code>settings put</code>, <code>setprop</code>, <code>reboot</code>) unless you asked for exactly that. That is a request to the AI, not a lock.</p>\n\n<h4>Agents that sign in through Termux</h4>\n<p>For the tools that run in Termux (Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Codex CLI, Copilot CLI, Cursor CLI, OpenCode), the app sends your message to the tool and shows its answer. The approval cards above do not apply: the tool follows its own rules. The switch <b>Let them act without asking</b> (in the gear, under <i>Sign-in tools in Termux</i>, off by default) passes each tool's own option to skip its approvals (Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Codex CLI, Copilot CLI and Cursor CLI have one; OpenCode does not). With it off, a tool only does what needs no approval. Leave it off. Its sheet has the steps <b>Termux</b>, <b>Install</b>, <b>Sign in</b> and <b>Chat here</b> under <b>Sign in with subscription</b>. Installing takes several minutes and up to about 1 GB. The providers do not support Android officially, so a tool may not run on every phone; the Terminal then shows its exact message. The <b>MCP</b> button adds extra tools (such as access to files or the web) to these agents; MCP is a standard way of giving an AI assistant such tools.</p>\n\n<h4>The AI button in the ADB Console</h4>\n<p>In the ADB Console pane, the small <b>$</b> button switches to <b>AI</b> and asks the same agent, model and effort for help with a command. It cannot read or change files. A reply may propose one command, shown with <b>Run</b> and <b>Skip</b>; Run sends it through the console's working mode (with <b>Ask before running commands</b> switched off it runs at once). It needs an agent that is connected with a key or server, set up in the Terminal pane first (<i>Pick a Coding Agent in the Terminal first</i>).</p>\n\n<h4>Privacy: what leaves the phone</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Every message you type, and the earlier messages of the conversation.</li>\n<li>A short description of your phone: Android version, make and model, processor type, the folder you are in, and today's date.</li>\n<li>What the agent reads or runs: the lines of files it reads and the output of commands it runs. Long output is shortened, and text that looks like an API key is hidden.</li>\n<li>Nothing else of your files is sent unless a step reads it. The requests go from the phone straight to the provider; this app has no server in between.</li>\n<li>Gemini's free tier: Google may use what you send to improve its products. Cursor's cloud agents receive your last few messages. An address that starts with <code>http://</code> is not encrypted on your network. Ollama on the phone stays on the phone.</li>\n<li>The conversation is kept on the phone (the last 60 messages) so it survives a restart. Turn <b>Keep the conversation between launches</b> off, or tap <b>Clear the conversation</b>, to remove it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>See also <a href=\"#privacy\">Privacy</a>. Never ask an agent to read files that hold passwords or private data.</p>\n\n<h4>If it fails</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><i>not connected</i> after the agent's name in the state line: open the agent's sheet and add the key or sign in.</li>\n<li><i>The key was not accepted</i>: check the key, or make a new one. <i>The account has no credit left</i>: add credit with the provider. <i>Not found: the model may not exist</i>: pick another model, or tap <b>Reload models</b>. <i>The conversation is too long</i>: tap <b>New chat</b>.</li>\n<li><i>No connection</i>: check the internet. <b>Details</b> shows the exact reply.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"tab-devices","title":"Connected Devices","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Look after another Android device from this phone: a Wear OS watch first, but a tablet or a second phone works the same way. You can manage its apps, send apps to it, run commands on it, read its log, browse its files and change its settings and screen density, all through ADB (the tool that talks to an Android device, see the <a href=\"#glossary\">glossary</a>).</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> the tab bar, after <b>Task Manager</b> and before <b>About</b>. If it is not there, it has been switched off in the <a href=\"#feature-list\">Feature List</a>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>On this phone:</b> you do not need to set up a working mode when you reach the other device over Wi-Fi. The app carries its own copy of adb and uses it for this tab, so nothing from <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a> has to be on. The one exception is the Bluetooth link to a Wear OS watch, which needs ADB in a Working mode (see <a href=\"#devices-add\">Adding a device</a>).</li>\n    <li><b>On the other device:</b> Developer options turned on (a hidden menu in its Settings, opened by tapping <b>Build number</b> seven times: <a href=\"#devices-wear\">Wear OS, start to finish</a> shows how), and debugging turned on inside it. On a phone or tablet that is <b>Wireless debugging</b>. On a Wear OS watch it is <b>ADB debugging</b> and then <b>Debug over Wi-Fi</b>.</li>\n    <li><b>For the Wi-Fi way:</b> both devices on the same Wi-Fi network.</li>\n    <li><b>A few minutes and the other device in your hand.</b> The first connection needs you to read a number off its screen and to tap <b>Allow</b> on it.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> If you have never done this, open <a href=\"#devices-wear\">Wear OS, start to finish</a> first. It walks through a watch from a blank start to an installed app, one screen at a time.</div>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Whatever you do in this tab happens on the device you picked at the top, not on this phone. The other tabs of the app act on this phone; this one never does. Before you disable, uninstall, delete or change something, read the device name in the card at the top so you are sure which device you are looking at.</div>\n\n  <h4>The top of the tab</h4>\n  <p>The first card is titled <b>Connected Devices</b>. Under the title there is one row:</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>The device picker (a drop-down list)</td><td>Lists every device adb can see, by name. A device that has not allowed this phone yet shows <b>(not allowed yet)</b> after its name, and one that does not answer shows <b>(offline)</b>. Pick one to work with it. When adb sees nothing, the list is grey and says <b>No device</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>+ Add device</b></td><td>Opens the sheet where you pair, connect, scan or link a watch over Bluetooth. See <a href=\"#devices-add\">Adding a device</a>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Refresh</b></td><td>Asks adb for the list again right now. A small message at the bottom says how many it found: <b>1 device</b>, <b>2 devices</b> or <b>No device found</b>.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>You do not have to press Refresh to see changes. While you look at this tab the list is read again every 6 seconds, so a device that connects, drops or is allowed on its own screen shows up by itself. Leaving the tab stops this.</p>\n  <h4>A device that dropped</h4>\n  <p>Every device that was connected is remembered (up to twenty, see Saved devices below). When one is no longer connected, because its Wi-Fi went to sleep, the cable came out, its Wireless debugging was switched off or it restarted, it does not just vanish: a toast says <b>Lost the connection to ...</b> and a card stays under the picker with the device's name, how it was connected (Wi-Fi, USB or Bluetooth link) and when it was lost. The card has two buttons:</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Reconnect</b></td><td>Does what that kind of connection needs. <b>Wi-Fi</b> (an address such as 192.168.1.20:5555): disconnects, connects again and, if that fails, looks for the device on the same address by its Wireless debugging announcement, because the port changes every time Wireless debugging is switched on; a new port is found and used by itself. <b>Wireless debugging by name</b>: looks the name up on the network and connects to the address it announces. <b>USB</b>: asks adb to reconnect offline devices; if the device does not answer, check the cable, unlock it and make sure USB debugging is on. <b>Bluetooth link</b>: sets the link up again through this phone's own ADB, then connects. When it cannot, the card says why in words. Nothing else changes, and you can press it again.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Forget</b></td><td>Takes the device off the list for good. You can add it again with Add device.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p><b>Reconnect all.</b> When two or more devices are not connected, a line above the cards says <b>2 devices not connected</b> and has a <b>Reconnect all</b> button. It tries each one in turn (they share one adb, so not at the same time), shows <i>Trying...</i> while it works, and ends with a message such as <i>Reconnected 1 of 2. The rest says why where it is listed.</i> or <i>Reconnected 2 devices</i>. A device that could not be reached keeps its card with the reason, so you can fix it and press its own <b>Reconnect</b>. With one device not connected there is no such button: its own Reconnect does the same.</p>\n  <p><b>On its own.</b> When a device drops while you look at the tab, the app also tries to bring it back by itself: right away, after 8 seconds and after 25 seconds (not for USB, and not while the app is in the background). If one attempt works it stops there; if all three fail the card tells why and waits for you. The line <b>Reconnect on its own when a device drops: On</b> under the card switches this off and on, and <a href=\"#prefs\">Settings &gt; Reconnecting devices</a> has the same switch and lets you set the <b>retry timings</b> (how many attempts, and how many seconds before each one). <b>Reconnect</b> is also the first item in the device menu (the three dots on the device card) and the button in the note under the card of a device that is <b>offline</b>. <b>Disconnect</b> in the device menu is a decision, not a drop: the device is forgotten and nothing is tried.</p>\n  <h4>Saved devices</h4>\n  <p>Under the picker, once a device has been connected, a line says <b>Saved devices (2)</b>. Tap it to open the list: every device this app has seen connected, newest first, with how it was connected (<b>Wi-Fi</b>, <b>USB</b> or <b>Bluetooth link</b>) and when it was last connected, or <b>connected now</b>. A device that is not connected has two buttons: <b>Connect</b> (the same as Reconnect above) and <b>Delete</b>; a device that is connected has only Delete.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Delete</b> asks first, then takes the device off the list. If it is connected at that moment, it stays connected and is not saved again until you connect it again.</li>\n    <li><b>Delete all</b> (under the list) does the same for every saved device.</li>\n    <li><b>Remember the devices I connect</b> (under the list) can be turned <b>Off</b>: devices you connect after that work as usual but are not saved, and a dropped one is not reconnected by itself.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>The list is kept in this app only, on this phone, and holds the name and the address or serial of each device, nothing else.</p>\n  <p><b>This phone is never in the list</b>, even when its own ADB or Wireless Debugging working mode is on. The tab leaves out this phone's own addresses on purpose, so you cannot manage the wrong device by mistake.</p>\n\n  <h4>When no device is connected</h4>\n  <p>You see a card with a plus sign, the words <b>No device connected</b> and a <b>+ Add device</b> button. Under it, once you have connected to something before, a line starting with <b>Recent:</b> lists the addresses you used. Tap one to connect again at once. If adb itself cannot run, the card shows adb's complaint in place of the usual text.</p>\n\n  <h4>The device card</h4>\n  <p>Once a device is picked, a card under the picker describes it:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>A square icon: <b>W</b> for a watch, <b>A</b> for any other Android device.</li>\n    <li>The name, made from the maker and the model (for example Samsung SM-R930).</li>\n    <li>A grey line: the Android version and its API level, the processor type, the screen size and the density in dpi (dots per inch, how sharp and how big things are drawn). Under it, the device's address.</li>\n    <li>Badges, explained below.</li>\n    <li>Three dots at the right, which open the device menu.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Badge</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Connected</b> (green)</td><td>The device answers and has allowed this phone. All the tools below are available.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Not allowed yet</b> or <b>offline</b> (yellow)</td><td>See the table of states further down.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Wi-Fi</b>, <b>USB</b> or <b>Bluetooth link</b></td><td>How this phone reaches the device (<b>USB</b> means a cable). <b>Bluetooth link</b> shows for a watch you linked with the Bluetooth tab. The Add device sheet offers two ways to connect, Wi-Fi and the Bluetooth link, and this guide covers those two.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Wear OS</b></td><td>The device says it is a watch.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Battery 87%</b>, or <b>Battery 87% charging</b></td><td>The charge level when the details were last read. Use <b>Reload details</b> to read it again.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>The three-dot menu of the device</h4>\n  <p>Tap the three dots in the card. A sheet slides up with the device's name at the top and these buttons:</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th><th>Asks first?</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Reload details</b></td><td>Reads the name, Android version, screen and battery again.</td><td>No</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Reboot</b></td><td>Restarts the device. The connection drops for a moment. A device reached over Wi-Fi may come back with a new address, so you may have to connect again.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Reboot to recovery</b></td><td>Restarts into recovery mode (a small maintenance screen).</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Reboot to bootloader</b></td><td>Restarts into the bootloader (the tiny program that runs before Android and is used for flashing).</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Power off</b></td><td>Turns the device off. It has to be started again by hand.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Copy address</b></td><td>Copies the device's serial or address, the text shown under its name, such as 192.168.1.20:5555.</td><td>No</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Disconnect</b></td><td>Drops the Wi-Fi or Bluetooth-link connection. Shown only for a device that is reached by an address. The device stays paired, so you can connect again later.</td><td>No</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> <b>Reboot to recovery</b> and <b>Reboot to bootloader</b> take the device out of normal Android. A device in the bootloader cannot be reached over Wi-Fi, so from this phone you have no way to bring it back: you have to restart it by hand, usually by holding its power button. <b>Power off</b> works the same way. Only use these when you know why. If the device does not come back, check its maker's instructions for starting it.</div>\n\n  <h4>The seven sub-tabs</h4>\n  <p>Below the card there is a row of rounded buttons that scrolls sideways. It shows when the device is <b>Connected</b>. The tab remembers the last one you used, and the last device.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Sub-tab</th><th>What it is for</th><th>Read more</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Apps</b></td><td>List the device's apps; enable, disable, uninstall, reinstall; pull an APK.</td><td><a href=\"#devices-apps\">Apps of the device</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Send</b></td><td>Install APK, APKS, APKM or XAPK files, or apps from this phone, on the device.</td><td><a href=\"#devices-send\">Sending apps</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Console</b></td><td>Type commands for the device and see what it answers.</td><td><a href=\"#devices-console\">Device console</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Logcat</b></td><td>Read the device's own log, for example to see why an app crashed.</td><td><a href=\"#devices-logcat\">Device's log</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Files</b></td><td>Browse the device's storage; send files to it and pull files from it.</td><td><a href=\"#devices-files\">Device's files</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Hidden Settings</b></td><td>Read and change the device's system, secure and global settings.</td><td><a href=\"#devices-settings\">Device's hidden settings</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Display</b></td><td>Change the screen density and size, with an automatic undo.</td><td><a href=\"#devices-display\">Screen density and size</a></td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>The states a device can be in</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>What it means</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Connected</b></td><td>The device answers and trusts this phone.</td><td>Nothing. Pick a sub-tab.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Not allowed yet</b> (adb calls this \"unauthorized\")</td><td>The device has not accepted this phone's key. The tools stay hidden, and a note under the card says so.</td><td>Look at the device's own screen. Tap <b>Allow</b> and tick <b>Always allow</b>, then press <b>Refresh</b> here. If no question appears, turn debugging off and on again on the device and connect again. The same happens after you make a new ADB key in this app: every device has to trust the new key.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>offline</b></td><td>adb knows the device but it does not answer: its Wi-Fi went to sleep, it moved out of range, or it restarted.</td><td>Tap <b>Reconnect</b> in the note under the card (or in the device menu, or on the card of the dropped device). If it stays offline, turn Wireless debugging (or Debug over Wi-Fi) off and on on the device, then use <b>Connect</b> in Add device.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>authorizing</b> or <b>connecting</b></td><td>The connection is being set up. The note under the card words it like the next row (\"Most tools need it started normally\"), and the tools stay hidden until the card says <b>Connected</b>.</td><td>Wait a few seconds. The list updates by itself.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>recovery</b>, <b>sideload</b>, <b>bootloader</b></td><td>The device is not running Android normally. The note says \"Most tools need it started normally.\"</td><td>Restart the device by hand.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Not in the list at all</td><td>adb has forgotten it, or never knew it (for example after you disconnected, or after this phone's adb restarted).</td><td>Tap <b>+ Add device</b> and connect again, or tap its address under <b>Recent:</b>.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>Good to know</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>When you switch to another device in the picker, everything read from the first one (its app list, its log, its files) is thrown away and read again for the new one.</li>\n    <li>Pressing <b>Back</b> closes an open sheet first. After that it takes you to the tab you came from, as everywhere in the app. Leaving this tab does not stop a send or a command that is already running, but it does stop the 6-second device check and the live log. Only when <b>Back</b> is about to close the app does it ask first, if apps are being sent, a command is running or a batch change is being applied: it says \"Press back again to leave\" and then \"Leave now?\", because closing the app would cut the work short.</li>\n    <li>The live log (Play in the Logcat sub-tab) stops by itself when you leave the Logcat sub-tab or this tab.</li>\n    <li>Everything runs over adb between this phone and the device. Nothing goes through the internet.</li>\n  </ul>"},{"id":"devices-add","title":"Adding a device: pair, connect, scan, Bluetooth","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Before this phone can manage a device it has to be introduced to it. The <b>Add device</b> sheet has three ways: type the address yourself (<b>Wi-Fi</b>), let the app look for devices on your network (<b>Nearby</b>), or link a Wear OS watch through its Bluetooth connection to the phone (<b>Bluetooth</b>).</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> in <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>, tap <b>+ Add device</b> (next to the device picker, or in the middle of the screen when no device is connected). The <b>Send</b> sub-tab also has a <b>Bluetooth link</b> button that opens the sheet on the Bluetooth tab.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The other device has Developer options on (how to turn them on is in <a href=\"#devices-wear\">Wear OS, start to finish</a>; on a phone or tablet, tap <b>Build number</b> seven times in its About screen).</li>\n    <li>For Wi-Fi and Nearby: debugging over Wi-Fi is on in the device, and both devices are on the same Wi-Fi network. This phone needs no working mode.</li>\n    <li>For Bluetooth: ADB must be on in this phone's own Working mode (<a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB TCP</a> or <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>; tap the mode badge at the top right of the app to open the Working Modes sheet), and the watch must be connected to this phone through the Wear OS app.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>The sheet</h4>\n  <p>A sheet slides up titled <b>Add a device</b>, with the line \"Pair and connect over Wi-Fi, or link a watch over Bluetooth\". Three tabs sit under it: <b>Wi-Fi</b>, <b>Nearby</b> and <b>Bluetooth</b>. At the bottom is a grey box that shows what adb answered to your last action, in adb's own words. Read it when something does not work. Tap the cross or the dark area outside to close the sheet.</p>\n\n  <h4>Wi-Fi: two steps, pair and then connect</h4>\n  <p>Wireless debugging uses two different addresses on the other device. <b>Pairing</b> is a one-time handshake that uses a 6-digit code. <b>Connecting</b> is what you do every time. They use different ports (the number after the colon), so do not copy one into the other's box.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Where the pairing code and its address are</th><th>Where the connect address is</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Phone or tablet (Android 11 or newer)</td><td>Developer options, <b>Wireless debugging</b> on, then the button for pairing with a pairing code. A box shows a 6-digit code and an address with a port.</td><td>The main <b>Wireless debugging</b> screen, line <b>IP address &amp; Port</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Wear OS watch</td><td>Developer options, <b>ADB debugging</b> on, <b>Debug over Wi-Fi</b> on, then <b>Pair new device</b> (the pairing screen with a code). Menu names differ a little between makers.</td><td>The <b>Debug over Wi-Fi</b> line shows the watch's address and port.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <h5>Step 1 of 2: Pair (first time only)</h5>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>On the other device, open the pairing screen. It shows a code and an address like 192.168.1.20:37215. <b>Keep that screen open</b>: the code only works while it is shown.</li>\n    <li>In the sheet, on the <b>Wi-Fi</b> tab, type the address and port into the first box (the label starts with \"1. Pair\") and the 6 digits into <b>Pairing code</b>.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Pair</b>. The grey box says <b>Pairing...</b>, then shows adb's answer. On success it says <b>Successfully paired</b> and a message at the bottom says <b>Paired. Now connect.</b> If the second box was empty, it gets the device's address filled in up to the colon, with the cursor ready for the port.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <h5>Step 2 of 2: Connect</h5>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Close the pairing screen on the device. Look at its main wireless screen and read the address and port (not the pairing port).</li>\n    <li>Type it into the second box, the one labelled \"2. Connect\" (for example 192.168.1.20:5555).</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Connect</b>. After a moment the sheet closes, the device appears in the picker, a message says <b>Connected to</b> and its address, and the device card shows.</li>\n    <li>The device may ask <b>Allow debugging?</b> on its own screen. Tap <b>Allow</b> and tick <b>Always allow</b>. Until you do, the card says <b>Not allowed yet</b>.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Some older watches show only <b>Debug over Wi-Fi</b> with an address and no pairing screen. If yours does, skip step 1 and go straight to <b>Connect</b>. On a phone or tablet the connect port usually changes each time you switch Wireless debugging off and on, so read it again each time.</div>\n  <p>If the address or code is typed wrongly the app tells you before it runs anything: <b>Type the address and port, like 192.168.1.20:37215</b>, <b>The pairing code has 6 digits</b>, or <b>Type the address and port, like 192.168.1.20:5555</b>. The last one only appears when the connect address is empty or has odd characters; if the port is missing, adb decides what to do with the address.</p>\n\n  <h4>Nearby: let the app look</h4>\n  <p>Devices that offer wireless debugging announce themselves on the Wi-Fi network (the method is called mDNS). The <b>Nearby</b> tab asks adb who is announcing.</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Turn on Wireless debugging (or Debug over Wi-Fi) on the other device. If it is not paired yet, open its pairing screen and leave it open.</li>\n    <li>In the sheet, tap <b>Nearby</b>, then <b>Scan the network</b>. It says <b>Looking...</b> for up to 15 seconds.</li>\n    <li>Each device found is a row with its address in large letters and a grey line with its name and one of two states: <b>waiting for a pairing code</b> (button <b>Pair</b>) or <b>ready to connect</b> (button <b>Connect</b>).</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Pair</b>: the sheet jumps to the Wi-Fi tab with the pairing address already typed, and the cursor is in <b>Pairing code</b>. Type the code and continue as above. Tap <b>Connect</b>: the app connects at once.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>If nothing is found you read <b>Nothing found. Both devices must be on the same Wi-Fi network, with wireless debugging on. You can also type the address by hand under Wi-Fi.</b> Some routers hide devices from each other (guest networks, \"client isolation\"), and not every device announces itself, so a failed scan does not mean the device cannot be reached: type the address by hand.</p>\n\n  <h4>Bluetooth: a Wear OS watch with no Wi-Fi</h4>\n  <p>A Wear OS watch is linked to its phone by Bluetooth through the Wear OS app. The Wear OS app can pass adb messages along that link. This app does it in two moves. First it opens a numbered entry point (a \"port\") on this phone that leads to the Wear OS app's adb hub. Then it connects to that entry point as if it were a device at 127.0.0.1 (the address that always means \"this phone itself\").</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>On the watch: <b>Settings, Developer options, ADB debugging</b> on, then <b>Debug over Bluetooth</b> on.</li>\n    <li>In the Wear OS app on this phone: <b>Watch settings, Advanced, Debugging over Bluetooth</b> on.</li>\n    <li>Turn on <b>ADB</b> in this app's Working mode, so this phone can reach itself (<a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB TCP</a> or <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>).</li>\n    <li>In the sheet, tap the <b>Bluetooth</b> tab. Leave <b>Local port</b> at 4444 (a free number on this phone: any number from 1024 to 65535 is accepted, except one that this phone's own ADB uses) and tap <b>Connect over Bluetooth</b>.</li>\n    <li>The grey box says <b>Looking for this phone's own ADB...</b>, then <b>Linking to the watch through Bluetooth...</b>. When it works the sheet closes and a message says <b>Connected over Bluetooth. Allow it on the watch if it asks.</b> The card shows the badge <b>Bluetooth link</b>.</li>\n    <li>Look at the watch and tap <b>Allow</b> if it asks.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>If this phone has no working ADB mode, the grey box says <b>This phone is not connected to its own ADB. Turn on ADB in this app's Working mode first (Working Mode button at the top), then try again.</b> The \"button at the top\" is the mode badge at the top right of the app. If you typed a port that this phone's own ADB uses, a message says <b>That port is used by this phone's own ADB. Use another, like 4444.</b> If the link fails, adb's words are shown, followed by a reminder to check that <b>Debugging over Bluetooth</b> is on in the Wear OS app and on the watch.</p>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> The Bluetooth link relies on the Wear OS companion app and on the watch's Debug over Bluetooth setting. It was written to follow the method Google documents for Wear OS, but it has not been tried on every watch. A watch that uses a different companion app, or that has no Debug over Bluetooth option, will not work this way. Use Wi-Fi then. Also keep the port at 4444 or another free number: the app refuses a port that this phone's own ADB uses (5555 by default), because that address stands for this phone itself and is kept out of the device list.</div>\n\n  <h4>Recent addresses</h4>\n  <p>Each time <b>Connect</b> works, the address is remembered (the last 8). When you open the sheet with an empty Connect box, the newest one is typed in for you. With no device connected, the same addresses are on the empty screen after <b>Recent:</b>; tap one to connect straight away. A Bluetooth link is not listed there: connect it again from the Bluetooth tab.</p>\n\n  <h4>Disconnect</h4>\n  <p>Open the device's three-dot menu and tap <b>Disconnect</b>. The device vanishes from the picker, and the empty screen returns if it was the only one. The device is still paired, so you can connect again with <b>Connect</b> or from <b>Recent:</b>.</p>\n\n  <h4>What the messages mean</h4>\n  <p>Messages that come from the app:</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Message</th><th>What it means</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Type the address and port, like 192.168.1.20:37215</td><td>The pairing address is empty, has odd characters or no port.</td><td>Type it as the device shows it, with the colon and the port.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The pairing code has 6 digits</td><td>The code box does not hold exactly 6 digits.</td><td>Type the 6 digits shown on the device.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Use a port from 1024 to 65535</td><td>The Bluetooth local port is not in that range.</td><td>Use 4444.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>That port is used by this phone's own ADB. Use another, like 4444.</td><td>The Bluetooth local port is one that this phone's own ADB already uses.</td><td>Use 4444 or another free number.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Could not connect</td><td>adb gave no clearer answer.</td><td>Read the grey box, then check the device.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nothing found...</td><td>The scan saw no device.</td><td>Check the Wi-Fi and the setting on the device, or type the address.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>Words that come from adb itself (the exact text differs a little between adb versions):</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>adb says</th><th>What it usually means</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Connection refused</td><td>Nothing listens at that address and port: debugging is off, or the port changed.</td><td>Read the address on the device again.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Connection timed out, No route to host</td><td>The device did not answer: asleep, other network, or the router blocks it.</td><td>Wake the device, check both are on the same Wi-Fi.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Wrong password...</td><td>The pairing code is wrong or expired.</td><td>Look at the pairing screen again; it may have made a new code.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>[Process timed out after ...ms]</td><td>adb did not finish in time (pairing waits 40 seconds, connecting 25).</td><td>Try again with the device's screen on.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>already connected</td><td>Not an error: the device is already there.</td><td>Pick it in the device picker.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>More help is in <a href=\"#devices-wear\">Wear OS, start to finish</a> (troubleshooting table) and in <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>.</p>"},{"id":"devices-apps","title":"Managing the apps of the device","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The <b>Apps</b> sub-tab lists every app on the other device and lets you switch one off, remove it, bring a removed system app back, stop it, clear its data or copy its APK to this phone. It is the same idea as the <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>, but for the other device.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>, sub-tab <b>Apps</b> (the first one). It reads the list by itself the first time you open it.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The device must show <b>Connected</b> (see <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>).</li>\n    <li>Reading the list can take a while on a slow watch: up to 90 seconds are allowed. The line above the list says <b>Reading the apps of the device...</b> until it is done.</li>\n    <li>Nothing here needs root on the device, not even removing a system app (see \"Uninstalling a system app\" below). The device's own rules still apply, so it will refuse some changes (see \"When the device refuses\" below).</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Removing the wrong system app from a watch can break its clock face, its notifications or its pairing with the phone. Read <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloater safety</a> before you remove anything you do not recognise, and <b>Disable</b> an app before you decide to uninstall it.</div>\n\n  <h4>The list</h4>\n  <p>Each app is a row with:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>An icon: the real icon when this phone has the same app, otherwise a square with the app's first letter.</li>\n    <li>The <b>name</b>. When this phone has the same app installed, the name is the one this phone shows. Otherwise the app makes a name from the package name (the app's unique code name): it takes the last part, adds the part before it for very generic words such as app or wear, splits joined words and gives it a capital letter. So com.google.android.deskclock becomes <b>Deskclock</b>.</li>\n    <li>The <b>package name</b> in small letters under it.</li>\n    <li>Badges: <b>UNINSTALLED</b> or <b>DISABLED</b> when that applies, and always <b>SYSTEM</b> or <b>USER</b>.</li>\n    <li>A round-edged button on the right (<b>Disable</b>, <b>Enable</b> or <b>Reinstall</b>) and three dots for the menu.</li>\n    <li>A tick box on the left. Tap the box, or the name, to select the row.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>The list is sorted by name. Under the search box a line tells you how many apps are shown (<b>12 apps of 87</b>). Only the first 600 rows are drawn; if there are more, a note says how many are hidden and suggests you search or filter. <b>Reload</b>, next to the search box, reads the device again.</p>\n\n  <h4>Search and filters</h4>\n  <p>The search box (<b>Search apps on the device</b>) matches the name or the package name as you type. Under it is a row of filters, each with a count:</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Filter</th><th>Shows</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>All</b> (with the total)</td><td>Everything. It is on while no other filter is.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>3rd Party</b></td><td>Apps that were installed by a person (not part of the device's system).</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>System</b></td><td>Apps that came with the device.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Enabled</b></td><td>Apps that are installed and not disabled.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Disabled</b></td><td>Apps that are installed but switched off.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Uninstalled</b></td><td>Apps removed for the device's user but still known to it (normally system apps that you can bring back).</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>The rules are simple:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>3rd Party</b> and <b>System</b> are one choice: tapping one turns the other off. <b>Enabled</b> and <b>Disabled</b> are another one.</li>\n    <li>You can combine one of each pair, for example <b>System</b> and <b>Enabled</b>. A line then says \"Showing apps that are System + Enabled.\" with a <b>Clear filters</b> button.</li>\n    <li>An <b>uninstalled</b> app counts only as <b>Uninstalled</b>. It is not in the 3rd Party, System, Enabled or Disabled counts. With <b>Uninstalled</b> on, you see only uninstalled apps, and <b>3rd Party</b> or <b>System</b> narrow that list. <b>Enabled</b> or <b>Disabled</b> together with <b>Uninstalled</b> show nothing, because an uninstalled app is neither.</li>\n    <li>Tap an active filter again to turn it off. <b>All</b> or <b>Clear filters</b> turns all of them off.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>The button on each row</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>Shown when</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Disable</b></td><td>The app is enabled</td><td>Switches the app off. It stays on the device with its data, but cannot run and is hidden from the launcher. The row gets the <b>DISABLED</b> badge.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Enable</b></td><td>The app is disabled</td><td>Switches it back on.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Reinstall</b></td><td>The app is uninstalled</td><td>Brings back a removed system app from the copy still kept in the device's system. No download or file is needed.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>After the command, the app asks the device what became of the app (it lists the apps again; <i>Checking the device&hellip;</i> shows meanwhile, unless you turned off <a href=\"#prefs\">Progress messages</a>), and the answer is what the device says now, not what the command printed. A message such as <b>Disabled: Fit</b> appears and the row changes in place. If the app is not in the wanted state (<i>Still installed</i>, <i>Not disabled</i>, <i>Not installed</i>), the sheet <b>That did not work</b> opens with <i>Checked afterwards: still installed.</i> and the device's own answer, and a command that printed an error for an app that is in fact gone still counts as done. For several apps at once the device is looked at once for all of them, and the sheet names only those that are not as wanted.</p>\n\n  <h4>The three-dot menu of an app</h4>\n  <p>Tap the three dots. A sheet slides up with the app's name and package. For an app that is installed it has these buttons:</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Open</b></td><td>Starts the app on the device.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Force stop</b></td><td>Stops the app if it is running. Nothing is deleted.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Clear data</b></td><td>Deletes the app's data (its settings, accounts and files) on the device. It asks first.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Enable</b> / <b>Disable</b></td><td>Same as the row button.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Uninstall</b></td><td>Removes the app for the device's user. It asks first. For a system app that the device first refuses, the app tries a second way by itself (see \"Uninstalling a system app\" below).</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Pull APK</b></td><td>Copies the app's file (and its split files) from the device to this phone. See below.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Details</b></td><td>Shows what the device knows about the app: version, first install and last update times, the installer, the target and minimum Android versions, the user id, the app's folder and its flags. It is the device's own text, shortened to the first lines.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>App page on device</b></td><td>Opens that app's system settings page on the device's own screen.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Select</b> / <b>Deselect</b></td><td>Adds or removes the app from the selection for a batch.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Copy package</b></td><td>Copies the package name to the clipboard.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>For an app that is already <b>uninstalled</b> the menu is shorter: <b>Reinstall</b>, <b>Select</b> and <b>Copy package</b>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Selecting several apps</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Tap the box or the name of a row. The row gets a tick and a bar appears at the bottom of the list: <b>1 selected</b>.</li>\n    <li>Tap more rows. The count goes up. A filter or a search can help you find them.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Enable</b>, <b>Disable</b>, <b>Uninstall</b> or <b>Reinstall</b> in the bar. The cross at the right clears the selection.</li>\n    <li>While it runs the bar shows <b>1 of 5...</b>, <b>2 of 5...</b>. The apps are done one after the other.</li>\n    <li>When it ends you see a message such as <b>Disabled 5 apps</b>. If some failed, a sheet titled for example <b>1 of 5 did not work</b> lists each failure with the device's reason, and the others went through.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p><b>Uninstall</b> always asks first. <b>Disable</b> asks first when more than one app is selected. The bar does not check the state of each app: tapping <b>Enable</b> on an app that is already enabled is harmless, but it is pointless.</p>\n\n  <h4>What the commands really are</h4>\n  <p>The names on the buttons stand for these commands, which the app sends to the device. You can run the ordinary ones yourself in the <a href=\"#devices-console\">console</a> (the file transfer of the second try is done by the app, not by you).</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>Command on the device</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Disable</td><td><code>pm disable-user --user 0 PACKAGE</code></td><td>Switches the app off for the main user. Reversible.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Enable</td><td><code>pm enable PACKAGE</code></td><td>Switches it back on.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Uninstall</td><td><code>pm uninstall --user 0 PACKAGE</code></td><td>Removes the app for the main user. A system app's file stays in the device's system area.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Uninstall, second try (only when the device answers that only root can delete a system app)</td><td>A helper file is sent with <code>adb push</code> to <code>/data/local/tmp</code>, run with <code>app_process</code>, then deleted with <code>rm -f</code></td><td>The helper calls Android's package manager directly, with the delete-system-app flag. The result is the same as the first way: the app is gone for the main user.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Reinstall</td><td><code>cmd package install-existing PACKAGE</code></td><td>Makes a removed app available again from the copy that is still on the device.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>If <code>cmd package install-existing</code> fails, the app tries the older <code>pm install-existing</code> before it gives up.</p>\n\n  <h4>Uninstalling a system app</h4>\n  <p>Uninstall on the other device works the way it does on this phone. The app first runs <code>pm uninstall --user 0</code> on the device, and for most apps that is all it takes. Some devices answer a system app with <i>only root can delete system app for a particular user</i>. When that happens the app carries on by itself, with nothing more for you to tap:</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>It sends a tiny helper file (about 6 KB, built into this app) to the device's <code>/data/local/tmp</code> folder with <code>adb push</code>.</li>\n    <li>It runs the helper on the device with <code>app_process</code>. The helper calls Android's package manager directly, with the \"delete system app\" flag. It is the same trick this app uses on its own phone.</li>\n    <li>It deletes the helper from the device again, whatever the result was.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>You see what you saw before: a message such as <b>Uninstalled: Fit</b> and an <b>UNINSTALLED</b> badge when it worked. If it still fails, the sheet <b>That did not work</b> shows the device's own words and a note. The note says either to <b>Disable</b> the app instead, or, when the device reports it, that a device policy or a user restriction blocks removing apps, which cannot be changed from here. The question before <b>Uninstall</b> is the same as before. In a batch, each system app of this kind gets the helper sent, run and removed in turn, so a long list takes longer. A removed system app can be brought back with <b>Reinstall</b>.</p>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Reinstall is for system apps.</b> A system app is only hidden by <b>Uninstall</b>, because its file lives in the part of the device you cannot erase, so <b>Reinstall</b> works for it. An app that a person installed is normally removed completely: it disappears from the list after a <b>Reload</b>, and <b>Reinstall</b> cannot bring it back. Until you press <b>Reload</b>, its row may still show <b>UNINSTALLED</b> and a <b>Reinstall</b> button, and tapping it will most likely fail. To get it back, send its APK again with the <a href=\"#devices-send\">Send</a> sub-tab.</div>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> <b>Disable</b> first. A disabled app does nothing and shows no icon, but all its data is kept. Live with it for a few days. If nothing breaks, you can <b>Uninstall</b>. If something does, <b>Enable</b> undoes it in one tap.</div>\n\n  <h4>Pull APK: copy an app to this phone</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Tap the three dots of an installed app, then <b>Pull APK</b>.</li>\n    <li>A message says <b>Pulling 1 file...</b> (or more, when the app has split files, see <a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split APKs</a>).</li>\n    <li>When it finishes you read <b>Saved to Download/ADB App Manager/Devices/</b> followed by the device's folder.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>The files go to the <code>Download/ADB App Manager/Devices/&lt;device&gt;/</code> folder of this phone, where <code>&lt;device&gt;</code> is the device's address with the colon changed to an underscore (for example <code>192.168.1.20_5555</code>). The main file is named <code>PACKAGE.apk</code>. Each split file is named <code>PACKAGE_</code> and then its own file name. You can find the folder with the <a href=\"#tab-files\">File Manager</a>. If the message says the folder could not be made, the app has no permission to write there: see <a href=\"#app-permissions\">App permissions</a>.</p>\n\n  <h4>When the device refuses</h4>\n  <p>Some apps are protected. The device may answer with words such as <b>Failure</b>, <b>Permission Denial</b>, <b>Operation not allowed</b> or <b>Shell cannot change component state</b>. The sheet <b>That did not work</b> shows that answer. This is the device saying no, not a fault of the app. What you can do:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Leave that app alone. It is protected on purpose, often because the device needs it to run.</li>\n    <li>Try <b>Force stop</b> instead, which is allowed for most apps.</li>\n    <li>If a system app will not uninstall, the app has already tried its second way (the helper, described above) before the sheet opened. <b>Disable</b> the app instead: it stops running and is hidden, and <b>Enable</b> brings it back.</li>\n    <li>For a batch, the others still went through. Select just the failed ones if you want to try again.</li>\n    <li>If an app you disabled makes the device behave oddly, tap <b>Enable</b> on it. Use the <b>Disabled</b> filter to find it.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> Uninstalling or disabling a system app can leave a watch stuck on a boot screen or without its watch face. Before you remove anything, make sure you can undo it: <b>Disable</b> can be undone with <b>Enable</b>, and an uninstalled system app can come back with <b>Reinstall</b>. If a watch no longer starts properly, a factory reset (done on the device) is the last resort and wipes it.</div>"},{"id":"devices-send","title":"Sending apps to the device","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The <b>Send</b> sub-tab installs apps on the other device. You can choose package files stored on this phone, or send an app that this phone already has. It goes over the same connection the device uses now: Wi-Fi, USB or the Bluetooth link.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>, sub-tab <b>Send</b> (the second one).</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The device must show <b>Connected</b>. Otherwise tapping <b>Send to device</b> says <b>Choose a connected device first</b>.</li>\n    <li>Have the files ready on this phone. The app accepts <b>APK</b>, <b>APKS</b>, <b>APKM</b> and <b>XAPK</b> files (and a ZIP that holds APKs). If you do not know the difference, read <a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split APKs and bundles</a>.</li>\n    <li>For a watch, use an app made for Wear OS. A phone app may install but not be usable on a round, small screen.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Install only files you trust. An app sent to a watch runs with the same powers as one installed any other way. The device may also refuse an app that is meant for a newer Android than it has.</div>\n\n  <h4>Step by step</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open the <b>Send</b> sub-tab. The card <b>Send apps to this device</b> says <b>Nothing chosen yet.</b></li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Choose package files</b>. Android's file chooser opens. Pick one or more files and confirm. The files are copied into the app first, so a big file takes a moment.</li>\n    <li>Or tap <b>From this phone</b> to send an app that this phone already has (see below).</li>\n    <li>The chosen items appear as rows, each with a name and a grey line, and a cross to remove it. <b>Clear list</b> removes them all.</li>\n    <li>Set the four switches, if you need to (below).</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Send to device</b>. A bar and a line show how far it is: for example <b>1 of 3 &middot; face.apk &middot; Installing 2 files</b>.</li>\n    <li>When it ends each row says <b>Installed</b> in green, or shows the end of the device's error in red. A line says <b>2 of 3 packages installed</b> and a message says <b>Sent to the device</b> or <b>Some packages did not install</b>.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>What the chosen items look like</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>You chose</th><th>The row says</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>One <b>.apk</b></td><td>APK and its size</td><td>Installed as one app.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Several <b>.apk</b> files where one is named base.apk or the names contain split or config</td><td>Split APK, with the number of files</td><td>Treated as one app made of parts and installed together.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Several unrelated <b>.apk</b> files</td><td>One row each</td><td>Installed one after the other.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>.apks</b>, <b>.apkm</b>, <b>.xapk</b> or <b>.zip</b></td><td>Package file and its size</td><td>Opened by the app; only the parts the device needs are installed (see below).</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Anything else</td><td>A message \"not an APK, APKS, APKM or XAPK file\"</td><td>Not added.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>From this phone</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Tap <b>From this phone</b>. A sheet opens: <b>Apps on this phone</b>, \"Choose the ones to send to the device\".</li>\n    <li>Use the search box and the filters <b>3rd Party</b>, <b>System</b> and <b>All</b>. Tap rows to tick them. Only the first 300 are drawn: search to find the rest.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Add to the list</b> (it says <b>Add 2 to the list</b> when two are ticked). With nothing ticked you read <b>Tick the apps to send</b>.</li>\n    <li>Each app becomes a row that says <b>From this phone</b> and the number of files. An app that this phone keeps in several parts (\"splits\", see <a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split APKs</a>) says <b>(splits made for this phone, they may not fit the device)</b>.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>The app sends exactly the files this phone has for that app. It does not trim them, so an app that this phone keeps in parts for its own processor and screen may not fit a watch. If the install fails, get the app as an APK or bundle made for the device and use <b>Choose package files</b> instead.</p>\n\n  <h4>The four switches</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Switch</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Replace an app that is already there</b></td><td>On</td><td>Installs over an existing copy and keeps its data (adb install -r). Turn it off only if you want the install to fail when the app is already there.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Allow an older version</b></td><td>Off</td><td>Lets you install a version older than the one on the device (adb install -d). Needed when the device says the version is a downgrade.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Grant every permission</b></td><td>Off</td><td>Gives the app all the permissions it asks for at install time (adb install -g), so it will not ask later. Use it only for apps you trust.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Allow test apps</b></td><td>Off</td><td>Lets you install an app that its developer marked as a test build (adb install -t).</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>The switches apply to the whole list in one go.</p>\n\n  <h4>Progress, Stop and the results</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>While sending, <b>Send to device</b> is replaced by <b>Stop</b>, the crosses on the rows are greyed and the bar creeps along. If you press the phone's Back button until the app is about to close, it asks before leaving.</li>\n    <li><b>Stop</b> ends the adb step that is running. The line says <b>Stopping...</b>. The item that was running fails (its row shows <b>Failed</b>, or whatever adb had printed so far), and every item after it fails with <b>Error: stopped</b>. Items that already said <b>Installed</b> stay installed.</li>\n    <li>An install may take up to 10 minutes per item before it is cut off; copying one game data file may take up to an hour.</li>\n    <li>The result stays on the rows until you send again, remove a row or clear the list. Rows are not removed by themselves, so you can read the errors.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>Messages you may see in a red row (they are Android's own words):</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Message contains</th><th>It usually means</th><th>What to try</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>INSTALL_FAILED_OLDER_SDK</td><td>The app needs a newer Android than the device has.</td><td>Find an older version of the app.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>INSTALL_FAILED_VERSION_DOWNGRADE</td><td>The device has a newer version.</td><td>Turn on <b>Allow an older version</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>INSTALL_FAILED_ALREADY_EXISTS</td><td>The app is there and replacing is off.</td><td>Turn on <b>Replace an app that is already there</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>INSTALL_FAILED_UPDATE_INCOMPATIBLE</td><td>The one on the device was signed by someone else.</td><td>Uninstall the old one on the device first. Its data is lost.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>INSTALL_FAILED_NO_MATCHING_ABIS</td><td>The app has no code for the device's processor.</td><td>Get a build for the right processor.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>INSTALL_FAILED_INSUFFICIENT_STORAGE</td><td>The device is full.</td><td>Free some space on it.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>How bundles are trimmed to the device</h4>\n  <p>An APKS, APKM, XAPK or ZIP bundle can hold a base app and dozens of \"config\" parts: one for each processor type, each screen density and each language. A watch needs only a few. When you send a bundle, the app asks the device what it has and keeps:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>the base app and any feature parts, always;</li>\n    <li>of the processor parts, the first one that matches the device's processor list (best first); if none matches, all of them stay, because it is better big than broken;</li>\n    <li>of the density parts, the nearest one to the device's current density (the larger one on a tie); if the density is unknown, all stay;</li>\n    <li>of the language parts, the device's own language and English.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>The device's language, density and processors are read when you pick the device. If you changed the density on the <a href=\"#devices-display\">Display</a> sub-tab, the new value is the one used. The row for such a bundle says <b>Reading the package file</b> while it is being opened, then <b>Installing 3 files</b> (the count after trimming).</p>\n  <h5>Game data (OBB) in an XAPK</h5>\n  <p>An XAPK can carry extra files for a game or app, stored under <code>Android/obb/</code> or <code>Android/data/</code>. After the app has installed successfully, the app copies these files into the device's <code>/sdcard/Android/...</code> folders, one by one, and the line says <b>Copying</b> and the file's name. If copying fails, the row is marked as failed even though the app itself is installed.</p>\n\n  <h4>Send with Bluetooth</h4>\n  <p>The second card, <b>Send with Bluetooth</b>, is a different route that does not use ADB. It hands the queued files to Android's own Bluetooth sharing.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Show paired devices</b></td><td>Lists the Bluetooth devices this phone is paired with, each marked <b>watch or wearable</b>, phone, computer, audio or other. On Android 12 or newer it first asks for the Bluetooth permission: allow it and tap the button again. It also tells you if the phone has no Bluetooth, if Bluetooth is off, or if nothing is paired.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Send queued files</b></td><td>Opens Android's Bluetooth sharing (or its share sheet) with the files of the list. You pick the paired device there. It says <b>Choose something to send first</b> if the list is empty.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Bluetooth link</b></td><td>Opens the Add device sheet on its Bluetooth tab (see <a href=\"#devices-add\">Adding a device</a>).</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Most watches, Wear OS included, cannot receive files this way. Android's Bluetooth sharing will start, but the watch will not accept an APK. For a Wear OS watch, use the Bluetooth link under Add device (or Wi-Fi) and send over ADB with <b>Send to device</b>. The Bluetooth card is meant for devices that do accept shared files, such as another phone or a tablet.</div>\n\n  <h4>If it does not work</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Choose a connected device first:</b> the card shows <b>Not allowed yet</b> or <b>offline</b>. Fix that first.</li>\n    <li><b>A red row with a long message:</b> read its last lines, then use the table above.</li>\n    <li><b>The whole list failed at once:</b> the connection dropped. Check that the device is still <b>Connected</b>, then send again. Rows that said <b>Installed</b> are not undone.</li>\n    <li><b>Nothing happens after Send to device:</b> a watch with its screen off may be slow to answer. Wake it and wait.</li>\n  </ul>"},{"id":"devices-console","title":"The device console","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The <b>Console</b> sub-tab is a small command line for the other device. You type a command, press <b>Run</b>, and the answer appears on a black screen. It also lets you give commands to adb itself, for example to copy a file between the device and this phone.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>, sub-tab <b>Console</b> (the third one). It is the device's counterpart of the <a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a> tab, which runs commands on this phone.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The device must show <b>Connected</b>. If it does not, Run says <b>Choose a connected device first</b>.</li>\n    <li>Commands run as the device's \"shell\" user (the one adb uses). That is not root: it can do a lot, but not everything.</li>\n    <li>You do not need to know commands. The row of chips under the input fills in ready-made ones (see below).</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> A command is carried out at once, on the real device, and there is no undo. Commands such as <code>rm</code> (delete files), <code>pm clear</code> (erase an app's data), <code>settings put</code> (change a setting) and <code>reboot</code> do exactly what they say. Copy only commands you understand. If you are not sure what one does, look it up first, or use the buttons in the other sub-tabs, which ask before they do anything risky.</div>\n\n  <h4>What is on the screen</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Title <b>ADB console</b> with <b>Copy</b>, <b>Share</b>, <b>Clear</b></td><td><b>Copy</b> copies the whole session (every command and its answer) to the clipboard. <b>Share</b> sends it as a text file named device_console.txt through Android's share sheet. <b>Clear</b> wipes the screen but not the history of commands. With nothing on the screen, Copy and Share say <b>Nothing to copy</b> or <b>Nothing to share</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The black screen</td><td>Your commands in blue, the answers in white, errors in red. While a command is running it says <b>running ...</b>. The last 60 commands are kept on the screen. You can select text on it.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The mode button, <b>$</b> or <b>adb</b></td><td>Chooses where your lines go (see next section). Tap to switch.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The input box</td><td>Type a command. The <b>Enter</b> key of a keyboard runs it; <b>Shift</b> and <b>Enter</b> start a new line.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Run</b> / <b>Stop</b></td><td>While a command runs, the button turns red and says <b>Stop</b>. Tap it to end the command.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Previous</b> / <b>Next</b></td><td>Step back and forward through the lines you ran before (the last 100, kept even after you close the app). The up and down arrow keys of a keyboard do the same.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The suggestion line</td><td>Just above the input box a grey line completes what you are typing: first from your earlier lines, then, in the shell, from common commands and (after <code>pm path</code>, <code>am force-stop</code> and the like) from the package names that this device's <b>Apps</b> sub-tab has read; in adb mode, or after <code>adb </code>, from adb's own commands (<code>pull</code>, <code>push</code>, <code>install</code>, <code>uninstall</code>, <code>logcat</code>, <code>forward</code> ...) and, after <code>shell</code>, the device's shell. <b>Right arrow</b> (cursor at the end) accepts it; so does a tap on the line. It never runs anything.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The chips</td><td>A row of ready-made commands. Tapping one types it into the input box and puts the cursor there. It does <b>not</b> run it: you check it, then tap <b>Run</b>.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>Two kinds of line: for the device, and for adb</h4>\n  <p>A line is sent in one of two ways.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Mode <code>$</code> (the default).</b> The line is run in the device's own shell, as if you were typing on the device. Pipes (the vertical bar), quotes and searches work the way the device understands them, for example <code>ps -A | head -25</code>.</li>\n    <li><b>A line that starts with <code>adb </code>.</b> In <code>$</code> mode, if the line begins with the word <code>adb</code> and a space, the rest goes to adb itself, not into the device's shell. This is how you copy files: <code>adb pull</code>, <code>adb push</code>, <code>adb forward</code>.</li>\n    <li><b>Mode <code>adb</code>.</b> Tap the <b>$</b> button and it turns into <b>adb</b>. Now every line goes to adb itself, and the hint in the box becomes <code>pull /sdcard/Download/a.txt /sdcard/</code>. You do not need to type the word adb in this mode; if you do, the app drops it. A command for the device's own shell then needs the word <code>shell</code> in front, for example <code>shell ls /sdcard</code>. Tap the button again to go back to <code>$</code>.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>For adb lines the app adds the chosen device for you (<code>adb -s THE-DEVICE ...</code>). A few commands do not belong to one device and run without it: <code>devices</code>, <code>connect</code>, <code>disconnect</code>, <code>pair</code>, <code>mdns</code>, <code>version</code>, <code>help</code>, <code>reconnect</code> and <code>keys</code>. So does a line that starts with <code>-s</code> and a device name: you chose the device yourself. Quotes in an adb line are understood, so a name with spaces can be wrapped in quotes.</p>\n\n  <h4>Which adb commands are blocked, and why</h4>\n  <p>This app uses a single adb server (the background program that carries every adb connection) for everything it does: this tab, the Working modes, installs and the rest. If you stopped or restarted that server, every connection would drop at once, including the app's own working mode. So an adb line (one that starts with <code>adb</code>, or any line in adb mode) is not sent when:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>it has <code>kill-server</code>, <code>start-server</code>, <code>server</code> or <code>nodaemon</code> anywhere before the word <code>shell</code> (they stop, start or change the server; what comes after <code>shell</code> is for the device and is not checked);</li>\n    <li>its first word starts with <code>-P</code>, <code>-L</code> or <code>-H</code> (they point adb at a different server).</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>Instead of running it, the console prints <b>That one would stop the adb this app runs on, so it is not sent.</b> in red. Nothing happened on the device. If you really need to restart the connection to one device, use <b>Reconnect</b> (shown under the card when a device is offline) or <b>Disconnect</b> in the device menu, then connect again.</p>\n\n  <h4>Step by step: your first command</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open the <b>Console</b> sub-tab. The screen says <b>Type a command for the device and press Run.</b></li>\n    <li>Tap the chip <code>getprop ro.product.model</code>. It appears in the box.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Run</b>. The screen shows <code>$ getprop ro.product.model</code> in blue, then the device's model name, for example <code>SM-R930</code>.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Previous</b> to bring the line back, or <b>Copy</b> to keep the session.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>Examples to try</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it does</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><code>getprop ro.product.model</code></td><td>Prints the device's model.</td><td>Harmless.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>pm list packages -3</code></td><td>Lists the apps a person installed.</td><td>One line per app, each starting with package:.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>dumpsys battery</code></td><td>Shows the battery level, charging state and temperature.</td><td>Harmless.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>input keyevent 26</code></td><td>Presses the power key: the screen turns off, or on.</td><td>Handy to wake a sleeping watch. <code>input keyevent 3</code> is Home and <code>4</code> is Back.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>wm size</code> and <code>wm density</code></td><td>Print the screen size and density.</td><td>See <a href=\"#devices-display\">Screen density and size</a>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>screencap -p /sdcard/screen.png</code></td><td>Takes a screenshot and saves it on the device.</td><td>Then pull it from the <a href=\"#devices-files\">Files</a> sub-tab.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>adb pull /sdcard/Download/a.txt /sdcard/Download/</code></td><td>Copies a file from the device to this phone.</td><td>The second path is on this phone and must be a place the app may write. The Files sub-tab is easier and uses a fixed folder.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>adb push /sdcard/Download/a.txt /sdcard/</code></td><td>Copies a file from this phone to the device.</td><td>The first path is on this phone.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>adb forward tcp:8080 tcp:8080</code></td><td>Passes a network port on this phone to the same port on the device.</td><td>For developers.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>adb devices -l</code></td><td>Lists every device adb knows, with details.</td><td>Runs without choosing a device.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>wm density reset</code></td><td>Puts the screen density back to the device's own.</td><td>The rescue command from the Display sub-tab.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>The built-in chips are: <code>getprop ro.product.model</code>, <code>pm list packages -3</code>, <code>dumpsys battery</code>, <code>dumpsys activity activities | grep mResumedActivity</code> (which app is in front), <code>df -h /data</code> (free space), <code>ps -A | head -25</code> (what is running), <code>wm size</code>, <code>wm density</code>, <code>settings list global</code>, <code>ip addr show wlan0</code> (the Wi-Fi address), <code>cat /proc/meminfo | head -6</code> (memory), <code>logcat -d -t 40</code> (the last 40 log lines), <code>input keyevent 26</code>, <code>adb devices -l</code> and <code>adb pull /sdcard/Download/</code> (an unfinished pull: add the file name and a destination).</p>\n\n  <h4>What the output looks like</h4>\n  <p>Plain text, exactly as the device prints it. A command that prints nothing, such as one that worked silently, shows nothing under it. If the output starts with <b>Error:</b>, or adb complains in its own way (output that starts with <b>adb:</b> or <b>error:</b>), the answer is drawn in red. Output longer than 60,000 characters is cut, and the screen says <b>... (the rest is cut)</b>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Time limits and commands that never end</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>A device command may run for up to <b>2 minutes</b>. An adb line (a pull or a push of a big file) may run for up to <b>10 minutes</b>. After that adb is stopped and the answer ends with <b>[Process timed out after ...ms]</b>.</li>\n    <li>Some commands never finish by themselves: <code>logcat</code> without <code>-d</code>, <code>top</code> without <code>-n 1</code>, <code>adb logcat</code>, <code>ping</code> without a count. Tap <b>Stop</b> to end them. Use the chips' forms, which end by themselves.</li>\n    <li>The console has no keyboard link to the device, so programs that wait for you to type (an editor, a login, <code>su</code>) cannot be used.</li>\n    <li>While a command runs, pressing Back until the app is about to close asks first, because closing the app would cut the command short.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>If it does not work</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Nothing happens on Run:</b> a line that is empty is ignored.</li>\n    <li><b>\"Permission denied\" or \"not found\":</b> the shell user may not be allowed that command, or the device lacks it. Not every command exists on a watch.</li>\n    <li><b>Red error that starts with adb's own words:</b> read it. A typo in an adb word gives an answer such as <b>adb: unknown command</b>.</li>\n    <li><b>adb says \"unknown command\" for something that works in the device's shell (such as <code>ls</code>):</b> you are in adb mode, where every line goes to adb itself. Put <code>shell</code> in front, or tap the button to go back to <code>$</code>.</li>\n  </ul>"},{"id":"devices-logcat","title":"The device's log","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The <b>Logcat</b> sub-tab reads the other device's own log: the running diary in which Android and every app write what they do, including why something failed. It is the quickest way to see why an app crashes on a watch.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>, sub-tab <b>Logcat</b> (the fourth one). It looks and works like the <a href=\"#tab-logcat\">Logcat Viewer</a> tab, which reads this phone's log. Opening the sub-tab reads the log once by itself.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The device must show <b>Connected</b>.</li>\n    <li>A device's log is a short, circular buffer: when it is full, the oldest lines are overwritten. Old problems may already be gone.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>The controls</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Level (drop-down)</td><td>How serious a line has to be to show. Choices: <b>Verbose</b>, <b>Debug</b>, <b>Info</b> (the default), <b>Warn</b>, <b>Error</b>, <b>Fatal</b>. A level shows itself and everything more serious: <b>Info</b> shows Info, Warn, Error and Fatal; <b>Error</b> shows Error and Fatal. Changing it reads the log again.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Number of lines (drop-down)</td><td>How many of the most recent lines to read: <b>200</b>, <b>500</b> (the default) or <b>1000</b>. Changing it reads again.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>filter text / tag</b> box</td><td>Shows only the entries that contain what you type, in the tag or in the message. Capitals do not matter and the text is taken as it is (no special codes). Every match is highlighted. It works at once on what is already loaded and does not read the device again.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Play</b> (green)</td><td>Follows the log live. It reads once now, then again every 2.5 seconds, and the button turns orange and says <b>Pause</b>. Each live read asks for at most 300 lines. Tap <b>Pause</b> to stop.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Refresh</b></td><td>Reads the log once, now.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Clear</b></td><td>Empties the device's log buffer (the command <code>logcat -c</code>) and the screen, and says <b>Log cleared on the device.</b> The old lines cannot be brought back.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Copy</b></td><td>Copies everything that was read to the clipboard as plain text. That includes entries the filter box hides or the 700-row limit leaves off the screen.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Share</b></td><td>Sends the same text as a file named device_logcat.txt through Android's share sheet.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Live reading keeps the watch's Wi-Fi busy and uses its battery. Pause when you have what you need. Playing stops by itself when you leave the Logcat sub-tab or the Connected Devices tab.</div>\n\n  <h4>How the rows are drawn</h4>\n  <p>Exactly like in the Logcat Viewer tab. Each entry is one row:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>A small coloured badge at the left with the level letter: <b>V</b>, <b>D</b>, <b>I</b>, <b>W</b>, <b>E</b> or <b>F</b>. The row has a coloured stripe on its left in the same colour.</li>\n    <li>The <b>tag</b> in bold: the part of the system or the app that wrote the line.</li>\n    <li>The time, and two numbers such as <b>1234/1256</b>: the process and the thread that wrote it.</li>\n    <li>The message below, in the colour of its level. Several lines that belong to one log call, such as the lines of a crash report, are joined into one row.</li>\n    <li>Warnings have an amber background, errors a red one and fatal lines a purple one, and error text is bold.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>At most 700 rows are drawn. If there are more, the first line of the list says how many earlier entries are not shown; use a higher level or the filter box to narrow it. If you were at the bottom of the list, it follows new lines. If you scrolled up to read, it leaves you where you are. Unlike the Logcat Viewer tab, this one has no per-app picker, no colour-key buttons to hide a level, no <b>Save</b> button and no 2000-line choice.</p>\n\n  <h4>Reading a crash on a watch</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Set the level to <b>Error</b> and the number of lines to <b>1000</b>.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Clear</b>, so only fresh lines show.</li>\n    <li>Make the app crash on the watch (open it again).</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Refresh</b>. You see a few red rows.</li>\n    <li>Look for a row with the tag <b>AndroidRuntime</b> whose message starts with <b>FATAL EXCEPTION</b>. The lines joined under it are the crash report: a line with <b>Process:</b> and the package name, then the name of the problem (for example <code>java.lang.NullPointerException</code>) and a list of lines that start with <code>at</code>. A line starting with <b>Caused by</b> is usually the real reason.</li>\n    <li>Type the app's package name in the filter box to cut the noise. A crash in native code looks different: search for <b>Fatal signal</b>.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Copy</b> or <b>Share</b> to send the text to the app's developer.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>If it does not work</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>The area shows adb's complaint instead of rows:</b> the device dropped or went offline. The live reading stops by itself. Fix the connection (see <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>) and tap <b>Refresh</b>.</li>\n    <li><b>No log lines:</b> the level is too high, or you just tapped <b>Clear</b>. Lower the level or wait.</li>\n    <li><b>Nothing matches the filter:</b> the word is not in the lines that were read. Raise the number of lines or lower the level.</li>\n  </ul>"},{"id":"devices-files","title":"The device's files","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The <b>Files</b> sub-tab is a small file manager for the other device. You can look through its folders, read a text file, copy files to it and from it, make folders, rename and delete.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>, sub-tab <b>Files</b> (the fifth one). It opens on the device's shared storage. For this phone's own files see the <a href=\"#tab-files\">File Manager</a>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The device must show <b>Connected</b>.</li>\n    <li>Everything is done as the device's shell user (see <a href=\"#devices-console\">The device console</a>). That user can read and write shared storage, but not the private data of other apps.</li>\n    <li>Pulled files are saved on this phone, so the app needs permission to write there (see <a href=\"#app-permissions\">App permissions</a>).</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> <b>Delete</b> removes the file or folder from the device for good. There is no bin and no undo. Deleting a folder removes everything inside it. Do not delete things in system folders unless you know what they are.</div>\n\n  <h4>The screen</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Up</b> and the path box</td><td>The box shows the folder you are in, for example <code>/sdcard</code>. <b>Up</b> goes to the folder above it. At the very top (<code>/</code>) it stays.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The place chips</td><td>Jump to a well-known folder: <b>Storage</b> (<code>/sdcard</code>), <b>Download</b> (<code>/sdcard/Download</code>), <b>Temp</b> (<code>/data/local/tmp</code>, a folder adb can normally write to, handy for scratch files), <b>All storage</b> (<code>/storage</code>), <b>System</b> (<code>/system</code>) and <b>Root</b> (<code>/</code>).</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Refresh</b></td><td>Reads the folder again.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Send a file here</b></td><td>Copies files from this phone into the open folder (a push). See below.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>New folder</b></td><td>Makes a folder inside the open one. It asks for a name; the name cannot contain a slash. A message says <b>Folder made</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Go to...</b></td><td>Asks for a full path, such as <code>/sdcard/Download</code>, with the current path filled in. Tap <b>Go</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The list</td><td>Folders (and links) first, then files, each sorted by name. A line above says <b>12 items</b>. Each row has a small label (<b>DIR</b>, <b>FILE</b> or <b>LNK</b> for a link), the name, the permissions and, for a file, its size. A link shows an arrow and where it points. An empty folder says <b>This folder is empty.</b></td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>Tapping a row</h4>\n  <p>Tap a <b>folder</b> and it opens. Tap a <b>file</b> and its menu opens. Tap a <b>link</b> and the app first checks what it points to: a folder opens, anything else gets the menu. The three dots at the right of a row open the same menu.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Menu button</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Open</b> (folders)</td><td>Opens the folder.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>View as text</b> (files)</td><td>Shows the start of the file as text in a box. See the limits below.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Pull to this phone</b> (or <b>Pull folder to this phone</b>)</td><td>Copies it from the device to this phone. See below.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Rename</b></td><td>Asks for a new name (no slash allowed) and renames it on the device.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Delete</b></td><td>Asks <b>Delete NAME?</b> and then removes it from the device. A folder is removed with everything in it.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Copy path</b></td><td>Copies the full path of the row to the clipboard.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>Step by step: get a file from the watch to this phone</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open <b>Files</b>. The list shows the device's storage.</li>\n    <li>Tap folders until you see the file (for example <b>Download</b>).</li>\n    <li>Tap the file, then <b>Pull to this phone</b>.</li>\n    <li>A message says <b>Pulling NAME...</b>, then <b>Saved to Download/ADB App Manager/Devices/</b> and the device's folder.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>Pulled files always go to the <code>Download/ADB App Manager/Devices/&lt;device&gt;/</code> folder of this phone. The folder is named after the device's address with the colon turned into an underscore, such as <code>192.168.1.20_5555</code>. The file keeps its own name. A pull may run for up to an hour. Open the folder with the <a href=\"#tab-files\">File Manager</a>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Step by step: send a file to the device</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open the folder where the file should go.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Send a file here</b>. Android's file chooser opens. Pick one or more files.</li>\n    <li>For each file a message says <b>Sending NAME...</b>. At the end: <b>2 files sent to /sdcard/Download</b>, or the reason it failed.</li>\n    <li>The list refreshes by itself and shows the new files.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>Limits to know</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>View as text</b> shows only the first 40 KB of a file. If the file is bigger, a note under the box says <b>Showing the first 40 KB.</b></li>\n    <li>A file over 5 MB is not opened at all. The message says <b>A file this large is better pulled to this phone</b>. Use <b>Pull to this phone</b>.</li>\n    <li>If a file looks like something other than text, the box says <b>This looks like a binary file. Pull it to this phone instead.</b></li>\n    <li><code>/data</code> and <code>/system</code> need the device to allow it. On a normal device the shell user cannot read the private data of apps under <code>/data</code>, and cannot change <code>/system</code>. You will see a red message such as <b>Permission denied</b>. Reading parts of <code>/system</code> usually works. Changing anything in it is refused unless the device is rooted or a developer build.</li>\n    <li>A path that does not exist shows a red message such as <b>No such file or directory</b>.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> A screenshot of the watch: run <code>screencap -p /sdcard/screen.png</code> in the <a href=\"#devices-console\">console</a>, then come here, tap <b>Storage</b>, tap <b>screen.png</b> and <b>Pull to this phone</b>. Delete the file from the watch afterwards.</div>"},{"id":"devices-settings","title":"The device's hidden settings","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The <b>Hidden Settings</b> sub-tab lets you read and change the other device's system, secure and global settings: the long list of switches and numbers that Android keeps behind its Settings screens. Many things a watch's menus never offer live here.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>, sub-tab <b>Hidden Settings</b> (the sixth one).</p>\n\n  <h4>This one edits the other device</h4>\n  <p>The app also has a main <a href=\"#tab-settings\">Hidden Settings</a> tab. That one edits <b>this phone</b>, needs a working mode on this phone, explains many settings in plain words, flips on/off values with a press and hold, and keeps a journal with Undo (see <a href=\"#settings-journal\">the journal</a>). This sub-tab edits the <b>device you picked</b> in Connected Devices, over adb, and it is plainer: a name, a value and, when the app knows the setting, the same description and values as the main tab (also an About box in its editor). It has no one-tap switches and no journal or Undo. Look at the device card before you save anything, so you are sure whose settings these are.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The device must show <b>Connected</b>.</li>\n    <li>Know what you want to change. Do not experiment on a device you rely on.</li>\n    <li>The subtitle on the card already says it: <b>A wrong value can make a device misbehave: change one at a time.</b></li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> There is no undo here. A wrong value in the <b>secure</b> or <b>global</b> table can turn off the screen, the Wi-Fi, the keyboard or the touch input of a watch, or stop it from starting normally. Before you change a value, tap its key and use <b>Copy value</b> (or write the old value down). That is how you put it back. Change one setting, look at the device, then go on.</div>\n\n  <h4>The three tables</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>What lives there (examples)</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>global</b></td><td>Settings for the whole device, such as airplane mode, animation speeds and developer options.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>secure</b></td><td>Security-related settings of the current user, such as which keyboard is used and which accessibility services run.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>system</b></td><td>Everyday preferences, such as screen brightness and how long the screen stays on.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>Android reads these three lists from three places, so the same name can exist in more than one. Always check which of the three chips is on.</p>\n\n  <h4>The screen</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>global</b>, <b>secure</b>, <b>system</b>: three chips; the one that is on is the table you see. The first time you open a table, the app reads it from the device (\"Reading global settings...\"). Each table is read once and kept until you tap <b>Reload</b> or pick another device.</li>\n    <li><b>Search keys and values</b>: type part of a name or a value. The list narrows as you type. The count line says, for example, <b>3 settings match</b>.</li>\n    <li><b>+ New</b>: makes a setting (see below).</li>\n    <li><b>Reload</b>: reads the table again and says how many settings it found.</li>\n    <li>The count line says <b>120 settings in global</b> when nothing is typed in the search box.</li>\n    <li>The list is sorted by key. Each row shows the key in bold and its current value under it in colour. A long value is cut to about three lines in the list; tap it to see all. Only the first 400 rows are drawn: search to find the rest.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>Changing a setting: step by step</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Choose the table (<b>global</b>, <b>secure</b> or <b>system</b>).</li>\n    <li>Type part of the key into the search box, for example <b>animation</b>.</li>\n    <li>Tap the row. A sheet slides up with the key as its title, <b>global setting</b> under it, a box holding the value, and the warning <b>A wrong value can make the device misbehave. Delete takes the setting away so the system falls back to its default.</b></li>\n    <li>First tap <b>Copy value</b> to keep the old value on the clipboard.</li>\n    <li>Change the text in the box and tap <b>Save</b>. The sheet closes, a message says <b>Saved</b> and the key's name, and the list shows the new value.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Button in the sheet</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Save</b></td><td>Writes the value to the device (<code>settings put TABLE KEY VALUE</code>). If the device answers with an error, a sheet <b>That did not work</b> shows its words and nothing is changed in the list.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Delete</b></td><td>Asks <b>Delete KEY?</b> first, then removes the setting from the device (<code>settings delete</code>). The system then uses its own default for that key, or none.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Copy value</b></td><td>Copies the value to the clipboard.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>Making a new setting</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Tap <b>+ New</b>. A box asks for <b>New global setting</b> (or secure, or system, whichever table is on), with two fields: <b>Key</b> and <b>Value</b>.</li>\n    <li>Type both and tap <b>Add</b>. A key can hold only letters, digits and the signs <code>. _ : -</code>; otherwise you read <b>A key holds letters, digits and . _ : -</b>.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>Making a key that the system does not know has no effect: the setting is stored, but nothing reads it. It is useful only when you know that some app or part of the system looks for that exact key.</p>\n\n  <h4>Harmless things to start with</h4>\n  <p>These are well-known Android settings. A watch's software may ignore some of them, and the names can differ between makers, so read the key's current value first.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Table and key</th><th>What it does</th><th>Normal value</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>global, <code>window_animation_scale</code></td><td>How slowly windows animate. Lower is snappier, 0 turns the animation off.</td><td>1.0</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>global, <code>transition_animation_scale</code></td><td>The same for transitions between screens.</td><td>1.0</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>system, <code>screen_off_timeout</code></td><td>How long the screen stays on, in milliseconds (30000 is 30 seconds).</td><td>30000 or 60000</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>Searching the <b>global</b> table for <b>adb</b> shows the debugging settings of the device. Read them if you are curious, but do not change them from here: turning off the debugging that this phone uses ends the connection.</p>\n\n  <h4>Putting it back</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>A value you changed: tap its row, put the old value back (paste it with a long press in the box), tap <b>Save</b>.</li>\n    <li>A setting you deleted: tap <b>+ New</b>, type the same key and the old value, tap <b>Add</b>. If you did not write it down, many default values come back by themselves when the device restarts, but not all.</li>\n    <li>A setting you made: tap it and use <b>Delete</b>.</li>\n    <li>If the device behaves badly and you cannot tell why, restart it and look at the table again.</li>\n  </ul>"},{"id":"devices-display","title":"Screen density and size","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The <b>Display</b> sub-tab changes how big everything looks on the other device (its <b>density</b>) and the size of the picture it draws (its <b>screen size</b>). On a watch, a small change in density can fit much more on the screen. Every change undoes itself after 15 seconds unless you tap <b>Keep</b>. That countdown runs inside the app's page, so it may not happen if the app is paused or closed.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>, sub-tab <b>Display</b> (the last one).</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The device must show <b>Connected</b>. If the first line of the <b>Screen density</b> card says <b>Reading the device...</b>, wait a moment: the current values are being read.</li>\n    <li>Have the device in front of you, with its screen on, so you can see the result.</li>\n    <li>Know the way back (see \"If the screen becomes unreadable\" below).</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>What density is, and why it matters on a watch</h4>\n  <p>Density is measured in <b>dpi</b> (dots per inch). It is the number Android uses to decide how big to draw buttons, text and pictures. The screen itself has a fixed number of tiny dots. A <b>lower</b> density draws everything smaller, so more fits on the screen. A <b>higher</b> density draws everything larger and fits less.</p>\n  <p>On a small, round watch screen this is a big deal. At the maker's default, text can look huge and a list may show only two rows. Lowering the density by 20 or 40 can show a whole message at once. Raising it makes things easier to read and to tap. The sharpness does not change: only the size of things.</p>\n\n  <h4>The Screen density card</h4>\n  <p>The card has a line that tells you where you are: <b>Now 280 dpi. The device's own is 320.</b>, or <b>Now 320 dpi (the device's own).</b> when you have not changed it.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>The drop-down list</td><td>Offers every value from 120 to 640 in steps of 5. The device's own value is marked <b>(default)</b> and the one in use now is marked <b>(now)</b>; both are in the list even if they are not a multiple of 5. Picking a value does <b>not</b> apply it.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>&minus;</b> and <b>+</b></td><td>Move the drop-down 5 lower or 5 higher. They do not apply anything either. They work down to 72 and up to 1000.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Apply selected</b></td><td>Applies the value that is in the drop-down.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The box <b>Your own number</b> and <b>Apply number</b></td><td>Type a whole number from 72 to 1000 and tap <b>Apply number</b> (the Enter key does the same). You read <b>Type a whole number, like 280</b> or <b>Use a number from 72 to 1000</b> if it is not accepted. If the number is already in use you read <b>That is the density now</b>. Picking from the drop-down empties this box.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Reset to default</b></td><td>Puts the device's own density back at once, with no countdown. A message says <b>Density is back to the device's own</b>.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>Step by step: make a watch show more</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open <b>Display</b> and read the first line, for example <b>Now 320 dpi (the device's own).</b></li>\n    <li>Tap <b>&minus;</b> several times until the drop-down shows a value about 20 or 40 lower (for example 280 or 240).</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Apply selected</b>. The device redraws at once: its screen looks different and some apps restart.</li>\n    <li>A yellow banner appears at the top of the sub-tab: <b>The density is now 280 dpi. Keep it? It goes back by itself in 15 s.</b> with two buttons, <b>Keep</b> and <b>Put it back</b>. The number counts down every second.</li>\n    <li>Look at the device. If you like it, tap <b>Keep</b> (a message says <b>Kept</b>). If not, tap <b>Put it back</b>, or do nothing: when the countdown ends the app puts it back by itself and says <b>Put back</b>.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>\"Back\" means the value before this change: your earlier override if you had one, otherwise the device's own. The same safety net works for a change made with <b>Apply number</b> and for a screen size.</p>\n\n  <h4>The Screen size card</h4>\n  <p>The card says <b>The resolution the device draws at, width x height in pixels.</b> and shows the current size: <b>Now 450x450 (the device's own).</b></p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>The box (hint <b>450x450</b>)</td><td>Type the width, the letter x and the height, for example <code>400x400</code>. Spaces are ignored. Each number has 3 to 5 digits, or you read <b>Type it like 450x450</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Apply</b></td><td>Sets that size. The same yellow <b>Keep</b> / <b>Put it back</b> banner appears, with 15 seconds.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Reset</b></td><td>Puts the device's own size back at once. A message says <b>Screen size is back to the device's own</b>.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Leave the size alone unless you know why you need it. On a round watch a size that is not the real one can leave the picture off-centre or cut off at the edges. Density is the safer change.</div>\n  <div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> The drop-down goes from 120 to 640, but a typed number may be anything from 72 to 1000. A very low number draws everything so small that you cannot read or touch it; a very high one makes screens overflow. Stay inside the drop-down range. A density or size change normally stays after the device restarts, so do not count on a reboot to fix it.</div>\n\n  <h4>If the screen becomes unreadable</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li><b>Wait 15 seconds.</b> The change undoes itself. The banner counts down on this phone.</li>\n    <li>Or tap <b>Put it back</b> in the banner, or <b>Reset to default</b> (or <b>Reset</b> for the size).</li>\n    <li>Or open the <a href=\"#devices-console\">Console</a> and run <code>wm density reset</code> and, if you changed the size, <code>wm size reset</code>. These work as long as the card says <b>Connected</b>, even when you cannot read the device's screen.</li>\n    <li>If the connection is gone, connect again with <b>+ Add device</b> (see <a href=\"#devices-add\">Adding a device</a>) and then use step 3.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>The countdown runs in this app, on this phone. If you close the app, or the phone pauses it, or the connection drops during the 15 seconds, the automatic undo may not happen. That is why steps 2 to 4 exist. Switching to another device in the picker does not stop the undo: it still goes to the device that was changed.</p>"},{"id":"devices-wear","title":"Wear OS, start to finish","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">This is a walk through for a Wear OS watch, from nothing to a managed watch: turn on developer mode, connect, then remove an app you never use, install an app, change the screen density, read the log and copy a file to this phone.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>A Wear OS watch (the examples are written for Wear OS 3 or newer) that is charged. Put it on its charger for this: debugging uses Wi-Fi, which drains the battery.</li>\n    <li>This phone, with this app, on the same Wi-Fi network as the watch. The watch itself must be joined to that Wi-Fi network, not only linked to the phone by Bluetooth.</li>\n    <li>About ten minutes. No working mode is needed on this phone for the Wi-Fi way.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Menu names on watches vary with the maker and the Wear OS version. If a name below is not exactly what you see, look for the nearest one: the idea is the same.</div>\n\n  <h4>Part 1: turn on developer mode on the watch</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>On the watch, open <b>Settings</b>.</li>\n    <li>On most Wear OS watches go to <b>System</b>, then <b>About</b>, then <b>Versions</b>. On some it is <b>About watch</b> and the build is listed under software details. Where it sits varies, so look in Settings for About or System.</li>\n    <li>Find <b>Build number</b> and tap it <b>7 times</b>. A short message says that developer mode is on.</li>\n    <li>Go back to <b>Settings</b>. A new entry, <b>Developer options</b>, is now there (on many watches inside <b>System</b>).</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>Part 2: turn on debugging</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open <b>Developer options</b>. Turn on <b>ADB debugging</b> and confirm the question.</li>\n    <li>Turn on <b>Debug over Wi-Fi</b>. The watch joins the Wi-Fi network if it has to, then shows an address with a port, such as <b>192.168.1.20:5555</b>. Leave that screen reachable: you need the address.</li>\n    <li>Check that this phone is on the same Wi-Fi network.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>Part 3: pair and connect</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>On the watch, under <b>Debug over Wi-Fi</b>, tap <b>Pair new device</b>. The watch shows a 6-digit code and a second address with another port, such as <b>192.168.1.20:37215</b>. Do not close this screen.</li>\n    <li>On this phone open the <b>Connected Devices</b> tab and tap <b>+ Add device</b>. On the <b>Wi-Fi</b> tab type the pairing address in the first box and the code in <b>Pairing code</b>. Tap <b>Pair</b>. You read <b>Successfully paired</b>.</li>\n    <li>Look at the watch's Debug over Wi-Fi address again (the first one, such as 192.168.1.20:5555). Type it into the second box and tap <b>Connect</b>.</li>\n    <li>The sheet closes and the watch appears. If the watch asks <b>Allow debugging?</b>, tap <b>Allow</b> and tick <b>Always allow</b>.</li>\n    <li>The card shows the watch's name, the badges <b>Connected</b>, <b>Wi-Fi</b>, <b>Wear OS</b> and <b>Battery</b>, and the seven sub-tabs.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>Instead of typing, you can tap <b>Nearby</b>, then <b>Scan the network</b>: the watch shows up with <b>Pair</b> or <b>Connect</b>. If your watch has no pairing screen (some older ones), skip the pairing part and use <b>Connect</b> only. More about the sheet in <a href=\"#devices-add\">Adding a device</a>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Example 1: remove a preinstalled app you never use</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open the <b>Apps</b> sub-tab. The list reads from the watch. Tap <b>3rd Party</b> or <b>System</b> to narrow it, or type a word in the search box.</li>\n    <li>Find the app. If its name means nothing to you, leave it: use <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloater safety</a> as a guide to what is safe.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Disable</b> on its row. The row gets the <b>DISABLED</b> badge and the app is hidden from the watch's own app list.</li>\n    <li>Use the watch for a few days. If anything breaks, tap <b>Enable</b>.</li>\n    <li>If nothing broke and you want the space, tap the three dots, then <b>Uninstall</b> and confirm. A system app now shows <b>UNINSTALLED</b>; <b>Reinstall</b> brings it back. If the watch first answers that only root can remove a system app, the app tries a second way by itself (<a href=\"#devices-apps\">Managing the apps of the device</a> explains it).</li>\n  </ol>\n  <div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> Never remove the watch face, <b>Settings</b>, the Google or Wear OS services, the keyboard or the Bluetooth and phone-pairing apps. A watch that loses these may not work until it is factory reset, which erases it. When unsure, Disable, and only that.</div>\n\n  <h4>Example 2: install an APK on the watch</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Have an APK built for Wear OS on this phone.</li>\n    <li>Open the <b>Send</b> sub-tab and tap <b>Choose package files</b>. Pick the file.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Send to device</b>. The bar moves and the row says <b>Installed</b>.</li>\n    <li>Open the watch's app list. The app is there. If the row is red, read its message and see the table in <a href=\"#devices-send\">Sending apps to the device</a>.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>Example 3: change the screen density</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open the <b>Display</b> sub-tab.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>&minus;</b> until the drop-down shows a value 20 to 40 lower than the one marked <b>(default)</b>. Tap <b>Apply selected</b>.</li>\n    <li>Look at the watch. Tap <b>Keep</b> within 15 seconds if you like it, or let it undo itself. Details: <a href=\"#devices-display\">Screen density and size</a>.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>Example 4: read a log</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open the <b>Logcat</b> sub-tab. Set the level to <b>Error</b>.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Clear</b>, make the problem happen on the watch, tap <b>Refresh</b>.</li>\n    <li>Read the red rows, or type the app's package name in the filter box. See <a href=\"#devices-logcat\">The device's log</a>.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>Example 5: copy a screenshot or a file to this phone</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open the <b>Console</b> sub-tab, tap <b>Run</b> after typing <code>screencap -p /sdcard/screen.png</code>.</li>\n    <li>Open the <b>Files</b> sub-tab. Tap <b>Storage</b>, then <b>screen.png</b>, then <b>Pull to this phone</b>.</li>\n    <li>Look in <b>Download</b>, <b>ADB App Manager</b>, <b>Devices</b>, then the watch's folder on this phone. Any other file works the same way, see <a href=\"#devices-files\">The device's files</a>.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>The Bluetooth route, with no Wi-Fi</h4>\n  <p>If the watch cannot use Wi-Fi, you can reach it over its Bluetooth link to this phone. It needs more things in place, and it is the least tested part of this tab.</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>On the watch: <b>Settings, Developer options, ADB debugging</b> on, then <b>Debug over Bluetooth</b> on.</li>\n    <li>In the Wear OS app on this phone: <b>Watch settings, Advanced, Debugging over Bluetooth</b> on.</li>\n    <li>In this app, turn on ADB in a Working mode (<a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB TCP</a> or <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>).</li>\n    <li>In Connected Devices tap <b>+ Add device</b>, open the <b>Bluetooth</b> tab, leave the port at 4444 and tap <b>Connect over Bluetooth</b>. Allow it on the watch if it asks.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> This depends on the Wear OS companion app. It follows the method that is documented for Wear OS but has not been tried on every watch, so it may not work on yours. If it does not, use Wi-Fi.</div>\n\n  <h4>Troubleshooting</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>What you see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Watch not found: the scan finds nothing, or <b>Connection refused</b></td><td>Debug over Wi-Fi is off, the address or port is wrong, or the phone and watch are on different networks.</td><td>Check <b>Debug over Wi-Fi</b> is on and read the address again. Check both are on the same Wi-Fi (not a guest network). Type the address by hand.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pairing fails (<b>Wrong password</b>)</td><td>The code is wrong or the pairing screen closed, so the code expired.</td><td>Open <b>Pair new device</b> again and use the new code and address.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Paired, but Connect fails</td><td>You used the pairing address for connecting. They have different ports.</td><td>Use the address on the Debug over Wi-Fi screen.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Not allowed yet</b></td><td>The watch has not accepted this phone.</td><td>Look at the watch, tap <b>Allow</b>, tick <b>Always allow</b>, then press <b>Refresh</b>. If no question comes, turn Debug over Wi-Fi off and on and connect again.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>offline</b></td><td>The watch stopped answering.</td><td>Tap <b>Reconnect</b> under the card. If that fails, wake the watch, turn Debug over Wi-Fi off and on, and tap <b>Connect</b> again.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The connection drops after a while</td><td>Many watches turn Wi-Fi off to save battery, especially when the screen is off.</td><td>Put the watch on its charger, keep its screen on while you work, and if it has a Wi-Fi mode setting, choose one that keeps Wi-Fi on. Then <b>Connect</b> again.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The address changed after the watch restarted</td><td>The router gave it a new address, and the port may be new too.</td><td>Read the new address on the watch, or use <b>Nearby</b>. The old one under <b>Recent:</b> will not work.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bluetooth link fails</td><td>Debugging over Bluetooth is off in the Wear OS app or on the watch, this phone has no working ADB mode, or the watch does not support it.</td><td>Check both switches and the Working mode. If it still fails, use Wi-Fi.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>For general problems see <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>. Everything here can be undone: <b>Enable</b> brings back a disabled app, <b>Reinstall</b> a removed system app, <b>Reset to default</b> the density, and <b>Disconnect</b> ends the connection.</p>"},{"id":"tab-morphe","title":"Morphe Patcher","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Patch apps with <b>Morphe</b> on this phone: pick the patches, let the app build and sign the APK, and install it. The patcher is the Morphe project's own engine, built into this app, and everything works the way <b>Morphe Manager</b> works: patch sources, a community finder, Simple and Advanced patching, a live log, and a list of patched APKs.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> the tab bar, after <b>Connected Devices</b> and before <b>About</b>. If it is not there, it has been switched off in the <a href=\"#feature-list\">Feature List</a>.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> A patched app is not the app from the store. It is signed with a key made on this phone, so Android will not install it over the original unless the original is removed first (the app asks, and says that its data goes with it). Patches belong to their authors: only add sources you trust.</div>\n\n  <h4>The top of the tab</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Sources</b></td><td>The patch bundles you have, and where to add more. See <a href=\"#morphe-sources\">Patch sources</a>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Morphe Helper</b></td><td>Finds and downloads the original APK a patch needs, with an optional VirusTotal check. See <a href=\"#morphe-helper\">Morphe Helper</a>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Patched APKs</b></td><td>Every APK you have patched, with its log. See <a href=\"#morphe-patched\">Patched APKs</a>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Settings</b></td><td>Simple or Advanced by default, what happens after a patch, where patched APKs are kept, the signing key. See <a href=\"#morphe-patching\">Patching an app</a>.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>Under the card there are two pages: <b>Apps</b> lists the apps your sources have patches for, <b>Community</b> is the finder for more sources. The credit line on the card names the people whose work this is; the same names are in <a href=\"#credits\">Credits and licences</a>.</p>\n\n  <h4>The first time</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open the tab. The Apps page offers <b>Download Morphe Patches</b>, the official source (about 11 MB). Tap it.</li>\n    <li>The app reads the patches (a few seconds, in a process of its own). The apps they fit appear, the ones installed on this phone marked <b>Installed</b>.</li>\n    <li>Tap an app, choose the patches, tap <b>Patch</b>.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> If a card at the top says this build of the app does not contain the Morphe engine, patching is switched off in that build. Sources, the finder and downloads still work. A release build has the engine.</div>"},{"id":"morphe-sources","title":"Patch sources","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">A patch source is a bundle of patches, a <code>.mpp</code> file. Morphe Patches, the official one, is there from the start; you can add as many more as you like.</p>\n  <p>Open <b>Sources</b>. Each card shows the name, the repository, the version, the number of patches, when it was updated and its size, with badges: <b>Pre-installed</b>, <b>Local file</b>, <b>Off</b>, <b>Not downloaded</b>, <b>Needs a newer Morphe</b> (the bundle was made for a newer patcher than this app carries), <b>Metadata not available</b> (the address could not be read, the bundle you have still works) and <b>Update</b> followed by a version.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Update</b> / <b>Download</b></td><td>Fetches the newest version of a remote source.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Turn off</b> / <b>Turn on</b></td><td>Keeps the source without using its patches.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Pre-releases</b></td><td>Switches between stable and early releases for that source. Press Update to move to the other channel.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Rename</b>, <b>Delete</b></td><td>For sources you added. The official source can be neither renamed nor deleted. Apps you already patched stay as they are.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Open</b></td><td>Opens the repository.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <h4>Adding a source</h4>\n  <p>Tap <b>+ Add a source</b>. On <b>Remote</b> paste the address of a GitHub or GitLab repository, or of a <code>.json</code> metadata file: <code>github.com/owner/repo</code>, <code>gitlab.com/owner/repo</code>, <code>example.com/patches-bundle.json</code>. The box tells you at once whether the address is usable. A link from the finder, <code>morphe.software/add-source?github=owner/repo</code>, works too. Tick <b>Pre-release patches</b> to follow the early releases. On <b>Local file</b> pick a <code>.mpp</code> from storage; a local source never updates on its own.</p>\n  <div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Only add sources you trust.</b> A patch source decides what ends up inside your patched apps. The app downloads the bundle over https only and refuses a file that is not a patch bundle, but it cannot tell you what a patch does.</div>\n  <p><b>Check for updates</b> asks every remote source whether a newer version exists and marks the ones that have one.</p>"},{"id":"morphe-community","title":"Community patch finder","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The <b>Community</b> page lists the patch bundles other people publish for Morphe. It shows what <a href=\"https://morphe-patches.software\">morphe-patches.software</a>, the finder of the Morphe project, publishes, loaded when you open the page and kept for six hours (offline, the last copy is shown and says so).</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>By app</b> lists apps that have community patches, with their category and how many bundles patch them. Tap one for its description, the versions the patches name and the bundles that patch it. The <b>featured</b> apps are along the top.</li>\n    <li><b>Bundles</b> lists the bundles: name, repository, author, stars, number of patches, the apps they patch.</li>\n    <li><b>Search</b>, the <b>category</b> drop-down and the sort (<b>Popular</b>, <b>Most stars</b>, <b>Recently updated</b>, <b>Name A-Z</b>) narrow the list.</li>\n    <li><b>Only patches for apps installed on this phone</b> keeps what fits the apps you have.</li>\n    <li>A bundle you have already added shows <b>Installed</b> in green with a glow. An app on this phone shows <b>On this phone</b>.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>Tap <b>Add</b> on a bundle (or <b>Add this source</b> in its sheet). The app asks first, downloads it and lists it under <b>Sources</b>. The finder is community-maintained: its bundles are not checked by the Morphe project or by this app.</p>"},{"id":"morphe-patching","title":"Patching an app","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Tap an app on the Apps page (or <b>Patch a file</b> to patch an APK, APKS, APKM or XAPK you have) to open its patch page.</p>\n  <h4>The APK</h4>\n  <p>The top card shows the app, whether it is <b>Installed on this phone</b> or <b>From a file</b>, its version, and whether the version is one the patches were made for (<b>Supported version</b> in green, or <b>Other version</b>). Most patches work with only the versions they list. If yours is not one, use <b>Get the APK with Morphe Helper</b> (see <a href=\"#morphe-helper\">Morphe Helper</a>) or <b>Choose an APK file</b>.</p>\n  <h4>Simple and Advanced</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Simple</b> chooses for you: the patches of the first source that has some for this app which are on by default and fit the version. You see the list and the Patch button.</li>\n    <li><b>Advanced</b> lists every patch with a tick box: <b>App-specific</b> patches for this app and <b>Universal</b> patches that fit any app, with a search box and <b>All</b>, <b>None</b> and <b>Defaults</b>. A patch with options has a gear: text, numbers, switches, choices, sliders, colors and files. With more than one source, each has its own tab.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <h4>Patch</h4>\n  <p>The <b>Patch</b> button has two boxes next to it. <b>Install when finished</b> (on) installs the patched app through the working mode, silently over ADB, Shizuku or Root; with none, the system installer asks you. <b>Delete the APK after installing</b> (off) removes the patched APK once the install worked.</p>\n  <p>While it runs you see the steps (Preparing, Loading, Unpacking, Patching, Rebuilding, Signing, Saving, Installing), a progress bar and a log in the style of logcat, with a level filter, <b>Follow</b>, <b>Copy</b> and <b>Share</b>. A notification keeps it going if you leave the app and has Cancel. When it is done you get the file name and size and <b>Install</b>, <b>Share</b>, <b>Save to Downloads</b>.</p>\n  <h4>When it fails</h4>\n  <p>When one patch fails, the patches that need it fail too (\"<i>depends on ..., which raised an exception</i>\"). The card leads with the first error, whose details are open, and folds the others into one line, <b>N more failed only because a patch they need failed</b>, with <b>Show</b> to name them. Fix the first one and the rest go away.</p>\n  <p>The result card names the step and the patch that failed, shows its full details (the error and stack trace), says in plain words what usually causes it, and offers <b>Back to the patches</b>, <b>Copy the log</b>, <b>Share the log</b> and <b>Retry, skipping failed patches</b>. The usual causes: the version is not supported, the phone ran out of memory (try <b>Strip unused native libraries</b>), or the bundle needs a newer Morphe.</p>\n  <h4>Settings</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Patching mode</b></td><td>Simple or Advanced when a patch page opens.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Install when finished</b>, <b>Delete the APK after installing</b></td><td>The two boxes next to Patch.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Keep patched APKs in</b></td><td>The app folder (private), <b>Downloads</b> (<code>Download/Morphe Patcher</code>, easy to reach), or both.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Strip unused native libraries</b></td><td>Keeps only the libraries for this phone's CPU: less memory, a smaller APK.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Stop when a patch fails</b></td><td>Off: the patches that work are applied and the failed ones are reported.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Patch unsupported versions</b></td><td>Applies patches even to a version they do not list. It may fail or break the app.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Signer name</b>, <b>Signing key</b></td><td>Patched apps are signed with a key made on this phone the first time. Keep using the same key to update a patched app in place: <b>Export the key</b>, <b>Import a key</b> or make <b>New key</b>.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>"},{"id":"morphe-helper","title":"Morphe Helper","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Most patches need one exact version of the original app. <b>Morphe Helper</b> finds that APK on the sources you choose, downloads it, checks it, and lets you install it or patch it. It follows the open-source app <b>Helper for Morphe</b> by rushiranpise, built into this app.</p>\n  <h4>Download</h4>\n  <p>Enter the package and the version (empty: the newest), choose the CPU and the source, then: (a version may carry its build flavour, such as Gboard's <code>18.0.3.954559732-release-arm64-v8a</code>; Helper finds the release the source lists as <code>18.0.3.954559732</code>)</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Fast mode</b> walks every source that is on until one delivers.</li>\n    <li><b>This version</b> looks for exactly that version; <b>Newest</b> for the newest.</li>\n    <li><b>All versions</b> lists what a source has, with format and size, and a Download button for each. For APKMirror, whose app page shows only the latest releases, Helper also reads the pages of its <i>See more uploads</i> list (up to eight) to reach older versions; <b>This version</b> does that too when the version is not on the app page. If APKMirror blocks those pages, you get the newest ones only: use <b>Open the site</b>.</li>\n    <li><b>Open the site</b> opens the source in a browser, for the ones that cannot be downloaded from here (they say why). By default that is the app's own browser (see below); with the setting <b>Open the sources in: The phone's browser</b> it is your phone's browser, where you save the file yourself and, when you come back, Helper looks in Downloads by itself.</li>\n    <li><b>Find in Downloads</b> lists the APK, APKM, APKS and XAPK files in your Downloads folder (and the folders directly inside it) that hold the package you typed, newest first, each with its version, format and number of parts. Helper opens every file to read the package, so the name does not matter (<code>method.latin_18.0.3..._apkmirror.com.apkm</code> is found for <code>com.google.android.inputmethod.latin</code>). <b>Use</b> takes a file. This needs <b>All files access</b>; without it the list says so and offers to grant it.</li>\n    <li><b>Pick a file</b> opens Android's file picker, for a file anywhere else.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p><b>Bundles.</b> Many sources hand out an app as a bundle: <b>APKM</b> (APKMirror), <b>APKS</b> and <b>XAPK</b>, a zip of the base APK and its split APKs. Helper downloads them like an APK (a source's list says which format each version has), reads the package and version from inside, and shows <i>APKM, 5 parts</i>. The patcher merges the parts itself, so <b>Patch this file</b> works on a bundle as it is. <b>Install all parts</b> puts every part on the phone in one session: through the working mode silently, or, without one, through Android's installer, which asks you once.</p>\n  <p>A downloaded file is checked: its hash against the one the source published, and its real manifest (package, version, build) against the request. A file that does not match is refused. You then get <b>Install this APK</b> (<b>Install all parts</b> for a bundle; through the working mode), <b>Patch this file</b>, <b>Share</b>, and, when VirusTotal is on, <b>Scan with VirusTotal</b>.</p>\n  <h4>The browser inside the app</h4>\n  <p>Some sites, APKMirror above all, put a browser check in front of their downloads that a plain download cannot pass: Helper then says the source blocked it. <b>Open it here, in the app</b> (shown with that message), and <b>Open the site</b>, open a full-screen browser inside this app. It identifies itself the way Chrome does, so the check passes as it does in a browser. Use the site as usual: pick the version and the variant and tap its download button. Whatever the page hands over as a file (<code>.apkm</code>, <code>.apks</code>, <code>.xapk</code>, <code>.apk</code>) is saved by the app itself, with the same cookies, straight into Helper's folder; the bar at the bottom shows the name and the progress, and when it is done the browser closes and Helper opens the file (package, version, number of parts) as if it had downloaded it. A page that is not a file is never saved. <b>Back</b>, <b>Forward</b>, <b>Reload</b> and <b>Close</b> are at the top; the Android Back key goes back in the page and closes the browser at the first page.</p>\n  <p>The pages you visit cannot reach the app: the browser gives them no way to call it, and only web addresses (http and https) open. Its cookies stay in this app. The setting <b>Open the sources in</b> (<i>This app</i> or <i>The phone's browser</i>) chooses which one <b>Open the site</b> uses.</p>\n  <h4>Downloads from the browser</h4>\n  <p>Everything the in-app browser downloads is listed under <b>Downloads from the browser</b> in Helper, with its size, speed and time left, and it goes on when you close the browser. Up to three download at a time; the others wait their turn.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Pause</b> stops and keeps what was downloaded. <b>Resume</b> asks the site for the rest only. If the site cannot do that, or the file changed meanwhile, the file starts again from the beginning; either way the finished file is checked against the size the site named.</li>\n    <li><b>Retry</b> (shown when a download stopped, for example because the connection broke) is the same as Resume: the row says how far it got. If the site answers that the link has expired or wants its browser check again, open the page in the browser again and press its download button; the same file continues instead of starting a second copy.</li>\n    <li><b>Cancel</b> stops and removes the unfinished part. <b>Use this file</b> (on a saved one) makes it Helper's result, ready to install or patch. <b>Remove</b> takes a row off the list (a saved file stays in the folder); <b>Clear finished</b> removes all saved and cancelled rows.</li>\n    <li><b>In the background.</b> While anything downloads, a notification (<b>Downloading ...</b>) shows how many files, how far, how fast and how long is left, with <b>Pause all</b> and <b>Cancel</b>, and the downloads keep going when you leave the app or the screen goes off (the app is kept running as a foreground service of the data-transfer kind, and the phone's processor is kept awake only while something downloads). When the last one is over the notification is replaced by a short note (<i>keyboard.apkm is saved</i>, <i>2 downloads finished</i>, or <i>1 finished, 1 stopped. Open the app to retry</i>); tap it to open the app. <b>Pause all</b> pauses every download that is going or waiting (they stay in the list to Resume); <b>Cancel</b> stops them and removes the unfinished parts. The switch <b>Keep downloading in the background</b> under the list turns all this off (then the downloads stop when the app is left, as before); it is on by default. If notifications are off for the app, the downloads still keep going. On Android 15 the system ends a data-transfer service after six hours: what is going is then paused, not lost.</li>\n    <li>The list is remembered: after the app was closed, a paused or broken download is still there to resume. A download that was running when the app was stopped comes back as Paused.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <h4>Settings</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Sources</b>: switch each of the ten (APKMirror, Uptodown, APKPure, APKCombo, Aptoide, Evozi, Mi9, APK Downloader, Aurora, Play) on or off, and pick the default. Some can be downloaded directly; the others open in the browser.</li>\n    <li><b>Connection</b>: Wi-Fi and mobile data, Wi-Fi only, or mobile data only. <b>Save downloads</b>: the app's cache or <code>Download/Helper for Morphe</code>. <b>Clear after use</b>. <b>Try the other sources automatically</b>: when the source you chose does not have the version or does not work, <b>This version</b>, <b>Newest</b> and <b>All versions</b> go on with the next source until one delivers (off by default). <b>Fast mode: version</b>.</li>\n    <li><b>VirusTotal</b>: optional. Paste your API key (a free one allows 4 lookups a minute and 500 a day), press <b>Test</b>, and choose <b>Never</b>, <b>Ask</b> or <b>Always</b>. A file VirusTotal already knows is looked up by its SHA-256 and not uploaded again (a cached report). Bundles are opened and each APK inside is checked. The quota bars show what is used. The key is kept on this phone and is sent only to VirusTotal.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Good to know.</b> Helper is independent of the sites it uses. Their pages, versions and formats can change at any time, and a source that changed says so and sends you to the browser.</div>"},{"id":"morphe-patched","title":"Patched APKs","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The <b>Patched APKs</b> button lists every APK you have patched, newest first, with the app, the version, the date, the size, the patches that were applied, and <b>Installed</b> once it was installed.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Install</b> installs it again. If Android refuses because the installed app has another signature, the app offers to uninstall the installed copy first and says that its data goes with it.</li>\n    <li><b>Share</b> sends the APK to another app. <b>Save to Downloads</b> copies it to <code>Download/Morphe Patcher</code>.</li>\n    <li><b>Log</b> shows the log of that run. <b>Delete</b> removes the APK and its log; the app on the phone is not touched.</li>\n  </ul>"},{"id":"tab-sdm","title":"SD Maid","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Clean up what is not needed any more: <b>SystemCleaner</b> removes superfluous files, <b>AppCleaner</b> the expendable data of apps (caches and the like), <b>CorpseFinder</b> the leftovers of apps that are gone, and <b>Deduplicator</b> files that exist twice. Scan, look at the list, take out what you want to keep, then delete.</p>\n  <p>The tools are a port of the four tools of <b>SD Maid SE</b> by darken (GPL-3.0): the filters, the rules, the expendables databases and the automation steps are from there. This is not the SD Maid app, and it has none of its icons, logo or translations. If you want everything else SD Maid can do, the buttons in the card open <b>SD Maid SE on GitHub</b> and <b>on Google Play</b>.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> the tab bar, after <b>Morphe Patcher</b>. If it is not there, it has been switched off in the <a href=\"#feature-list\">Feature List</a>.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Deleting is final.</b> There is no trash and no way back for what a tool deletes, except what you restore from your own backup. Always look at the list first, and exclude what matters (see <a href=\"#sdm-exclusions\">Exclusions</a>).</div>\n\n  <h4>The top of the tab</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The working mode, and the <b>data areas</b> this phone lets the tools read: public storage, public app media, public app data, public app resources, private app data and portable storage. An area marked <b>working mode</b> is read through ADB, Shizuku or Root; one marked <b>not available</b> is skipped, and the hint under your finger says what it needs. Android 11 and newer hide the other apps' folders in <code>Android/data</code> from normal apps, and private app data needs Root, so the more privileged your working mode is, the more the tools can find.</li>\n    <li><b>Scan all</b> starts every tool. <b>Delete all results</b> deletes what every tool found, after one question. <b>Cancel all</b> shows up while something works.</li>\n    <li><b>History</b>, <b>Exclusions</b> and <b>Settings</b> open the pages described below.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>A tool's card</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>What you see</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Scan</b></td><td>Looks for what the tool is made for. Nothing is deleted. While it works the card shows the step, a bar, how many are done, how much was found so far and the folder it is in. <b>Cancel</b> stops it and throws the partial result away.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Details</b></td><td>The list of what was found. See <a href=\"#sdm-review\">Looking at the results</a>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Delete</b></td><td>Deletes everything in the list that is still ticked, after a question. When it is done the card shows what was freed, with an X to dismiss it.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>1-tap scan and delete</b></td><td>A checkbox, <b>off</b> by default. When it is on, the Scan button becomes <b>Scan and delete</b>: it scans and deletes everything it finds, with no list and no question. Turning it on asks once and says what it does. What you excluded is skipped, and everything deleted is written to History.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The gear</td><td>The settings of that tool. See <a href=\"#sdm-settings\">Settings</a>.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p><b>Two at a time.</b> At most two tools work at the same time; a third one waits and says <b>In queue</b>. The <b>live status</b> that stays at the top while anything works shows every tool that is working, its step, a bar and its path, and how many of the two places are in use.</p>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> The work goes on in the app's own process while you look at another tab. The results of a scan stay in memory, so rotating the phone or changing the theme does not lose them; closing the app does.</div>"},{"id":"sdm-review","title":"SD Maid: looking at the results","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Tap <b>Details</b> on a card to see what a scan found, and take out whatever you want to keep.</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>The first list shows the groups: the filters (SystemCleaner), the apps (AppCleaner), the remnants (CorpseFinder) or the sets of identical files (Deduplicator), each with its size. Every group starts ticked. Untick a group to leave all of it out. <b>All</b> and <b>None</b> tick or untick the whole list, and the search box narrows it.</li>\n    <li>Tap a group to see what is inside: every path with its size. Untick single items. In Deduplicator the copy that is kept is marked <b>Kept</b> and cannot be ticked; the others are the copies that will be deleted.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Delete selected</b>. The question says how many items and how much space, or, when nothing was taken out, the question of that tool (\"Delete all app remnants?\").</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p><b>Exclude</b> on a row is the lasting version of unticking: it adds an exclusion, takes the row out of the list at once and shows a bar with <b>Undo</b>. An app is excluded for AppCleaner as a whole; everything else by its path. What you untick is only remembered until the next scan or delete.</p>\n  <p>Before anything is deleted, every path is looked at again, because a scan can be hours old. A path that is already gone counts as deleted. A path that is not inside a data area, or that is a data area itself, is never deleted.</p>\n  <h4>What each tool does</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>SystemCleaner</b> walks storage once with its filters (log files, trash, thumbnails, empty folders, macOS and Windows leftovers, lost-and-found, temporary files, advertisements, analytics, usage and more) and groups what matches by filter. The filters you switch off in its settings are not used.</li>\n    <li><b>AppCleaner</b> finds the caches and other expendable data of every app, by the rules SD Maid ships (browser caches, thumbnails, logs, code caches, and the app-specific rules for many popular apps). It also sees the cache an app keeps in its private folder, as far as Android shows its size; the caches no file access can reach are cleared with a working mode (all apps at once) or with the accessibility service (one app after the other).</li>\n    <li><b>CorpseFinder</b> finds the folders and files of apps that are no longer installed, using the owner of each folder and a database of known leftovers. Each remnant shows how risky it is to delete.</li>\n    <li><b>Deduplicator</b> finds files with identical content: it groups by size, compares a first block, then the SHA-256 of the whole file. Small files below the minimum size are not looked at.</li>\n  </ul>"},{"id":"sdm-exclusions","title":"SD Maid: exclusions","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">An exclusion keeps an app, a file or a folder out of the tools you choose, so it is never listed and never deleted.</p>\n  <p>Open it with <b>Exclusions</b>. The list shows every exclusion with the tools it applies to. The ones marked <b>default</b> come with the app and can be removed; <b>Restore defaults</b> brings them back.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>What it matches</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>App</b></td><td>One installed app. AppCleaner leaves its data alone; CorpseFinder does not call its folders remnants.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Path</b></td><td>One file or folder and everything inside it. Write the absolute path, for example <code>/storage/emulated/0/DCIM</code>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Segment</b></td><td>A part of a path, like <code>DCIM/Camera</code>, wherever it is. <b>Allow partial matches</b> lets the first and last name be a part of a longer name; <b>Ignore casing</b> ignores capitals.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p><b>Affected tools</b> chooses where it applies: <b>All tools</b>, or any of the four. A folder that contains an excluded item is not deleted either, so that the excluded item stays. <b>Export</b> copies all exclusions to the clipboard; <b>Import</b> reads them back (and the exclusion files of SD Maid SE).</p>"},{"id":"sdm-history","title":"SD Maid: history","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Everything the tools delete is written down: which tool, when, how much, and the paths.</p>\n  <p>Open <b>History</b>. Each report shows the tool, the time, <b>Done</b>, <b>Partial success</b> (for example, a run that stopped because the screen turned off) or <b>Failure</b>, and what it says on the card afterwards. Tap a report to see the paths that were deleted. Scans are never written, because nothing is deleted by them. The top shows how much space has been freed in all.</p>\n  <p>Reports are kept for 30 days and the paths for 7 days; both can be changed in the general <b>Settings</b> (0 keeps no paths). <b>Reset all</b> deletes all reports and sets the totals to zero.</p>"},{"id":"sdm-settings","title":"SD Maid: settings and the accessibility service","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Every tool has its own settings behind the gear on its card, and the tab has general settings behind <b>Settings</b>. They are the settings of SD Maid SE, with its texts; a change is used by the next scan.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The filters of SystemCleaner and AppCleaner can be switched on and off one by one; some need Root, and say so.</li>\n    <li>CorpseFinder lets you choose which areas are searched and how careful it is with files that may belong to something.</li>\n    <li>Deduplicator has the minimum size and the kinds of files.</li>\n    <li><b>Dry run</b> in the general settings scans and reports as usual but deletes nothing. It is the safe way to try a tool the first time.</li>\n    <li><b>Reset to defaults</b> puts one screen back to what the app ships with.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <h4>The accessibility service</h4>\n  <p>Only AppCleaner uses it. Android does not let an app clear the cache of other apps, so SD Maid, and this tab, press the button for you: the service opens the <b>App info</b> screen of each app, finds <b>Storage</b> and taps <b>Clear cache</b>, while a cover over the screen shows what it is doing, with a way to cancel. It stops when the screen turns off or locks. Nothing is read or sent anywhere.</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>In the <b>Accessibility service</b> card, tap <b>Enable the service</b>. The card explains what it does and asks for your consent, then opens Android's accessibility settings. Turn the service on there.</li>\n    <li>On Android 13 and newer, an app installed from a file may be blocked from using an accessibility service: open this app's <b>App info</b>, the three dots, <b>Allow restricted settings</b>, and try again.</li>\n    <li>With a working mode you can also tap <b>Enable with the working mode</b>, which turns the service on for you.</li>\n    <li>Once the service is on and you gave consent, AppCleaner's Delete uses it after you confirm. The phone is asked whether the service is ready at the moment you tap Delete, so a service you turned on a minute ago counts at once. (Before v7.10.10 the service switched itself off again when it was turned on before the consent was given, and AppCleaner then skipped the caches that need it. It now stays on, and only acts once you have agreed.)</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p><b>Clear the rest with accessibility.</b> After a Delete, or whenever AppCleaner has results, the card has this button above Details. It clears only the caches that no file access reaches (what the trim of all apps through a working mode, and the file deletions, left), by tapping Clear cache in the settings of each of those apps. If the service is not ready (no consent, switched off, or not connected yet), the button says which and offers to set it up instead of doing nothing.</p>\n  <p><b>Withdraw consent</b> in the card stops AppCleaner from using it. The automation follows the screen texts of the system settings, in the languages SD Maid has tables for; on some phone makers' systems it can fail, and then it says so and the rest of the cleanup still works.</p>\n\n  <h4>Trim Caches in All Applications</h4>\n  <p>A card of this tab (it was in Settings until v7.10.10), next to the cleaning tools because it does the same kind of job for every app at once. It has one button, <b>Trim Caches in All Applications</b>, and a long explanation under it. The button clears the cache of every app in one go, to free some storage.</p>\n  <p><b>What it needs.</b> A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a> (ADB over TCP, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root). Without one the button shows a padlock, and tapping it opens the sheet <b>Working Mode Required</b>.</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open the <b>SD Maid</b> tab and find the card <b>Trim Caches</b>, under the <b>Accessibility service</b> card.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Trim Caches in All Applications</b>. A question opens: \"Trim the caches of every app now? Only cache files are deleted. Apps, their data, logins and your files stay as they are.\" Tap <b>OK</b> to go on, or <b>Cancel</b> and nothing happens.</li>\n    <li>The app runs <code>pm trim-caches 128G</code> through your working mode, which is the same as typing <code>adb shell pm trim-caches 128G</code> on a computer. The button turns grey and reads \"Trimming caches&hellip;\", and a line under it says that this can take up to a minute or two. The app stays usable while it runs.</li>\n    <li>When it ends, a box titled <b>Trim Caches in All Applications</b> opens. On success it says <b>Caches trimmed</b>, shows the command tagged <b>SUCCESS</b>, and gives a line such as \"Free space on the phone: 120 GB before, 123 GB after (2.4 GB freed).\" When nothing more could be freed, the line ends \"(nothing more could be freed)\" instead. The command prints nothing when it works, so the box also says \"Done. The command printed nothing, which is what it does when it works.\"</li>\n    <li>If it did not work, the box says <b>It did not finish</b>, shows the command tagged <b>FAILED</b>, and quotes Android's own words. See <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>, \"Trim Caches says it did not finish\".</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h5>What it does</h5>\n  <p><code>pm trim-caches</code> asks Android to free storage by deleting app caches until the phone has the amount of free space you give it. 128 GB is more free space than any phone has, so the target can never be reached. Android therefore keeps going and clears every cache it is allowed to clear, in every app, instead of stopping after the first few. Which apps it starts with, and in what order, is Android's own choice.</p>\n\n  <h5>What is trimmed</h5>\n  <p>Only cache: the temporary files that each app keeps in its own <i>cache</i> folder and its <i>code cache</i> folder, on the phone's internal storage and in the app's folder under <code>Android/data</code>. That means things like downloaded thumbnails and images, web pages a browser kept for next time, map tiles, video and music that was only buffered, and temporary files an app made while working. Android treats all of it as safe to throw away, and the app makes it again when it needs it. (<a href=\"#glossary\">Glossary</a>: cache.)</p>\n\n  <h5>What is not touched</h5>\n  <p>Your apps themselves, their data and settings, logins and accounts, saved games, photos, videos, downloads and every other file in your folders. Nothing is uninstalled and no app is reset.</p>\n\n  <h5>What you may notice</h5>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The first start of an app afterwards can be a little slower, and it may use mobile data to fetch images again.</li>\n    <li>An app that keeps offline downloads in its cache folder (some music, video and map apps do) may need to download them again.</li>\n    <li>Apps that are running right now may keep some of their cache until they close.</li>\n    <li>How much it frees depends on how much cache your apps hold. The box shows the free space before and after, so you can see what it did.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> This does not delete files, downloads or app data, so it will not free a lot on a phone whose space is used by big apps or files. For that, see <a href=\"#recipe-find-big-apps\">Find the apps that take the most space</a>. The steps again, in short: <a href=\"#recipe-trim-caches\">Free up space by clearing every app's cache</a>.</div>"},{"id":"tab-settings","title":"Hidden Settings","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Android keeps hundreds of small settings that its own Settings app never shows. This tab lists them, lets you read any one, change it, add your own or delete it, and puts the old value back when you change your mind.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> the tab named <b>Hidden Settings</b> (two lines on the tab bar: Hidden and Settings), between Command-Line Interface and RRO/Monet Customization in the default order. If it is missing, it has been switched off: see <a href=\"#feature-list\">Turning tabs on and off and moving them</a>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>You need a working mode.</b> The app reads and changes these settings by running Android's own <code>settings</code> command through your working mode (ADB over TCP, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root; see <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a> and the <a href=\"#glossary\">glossary</a>). In Read-Only mode the tab cannot even show the list. With no mode ready, a card says <b>Needs ADB, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root</b> and its <b>Set Up</b> button opens Working Modes. Connect one, come back, and the list loads.</li>\n    <li><b>No computer is needed</b>, and the tab does not use the app's own \"Write Secure Settings\" permission (see <a href=\"#app-permissions\">App permissions</a>). What you can change is decided by the mode and by Android.</li>\n    <li>Which settings exist depends on your Android version and phone maker. One your phone does not have is not in the list.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> These are the settings Android reads while it runs. A wrong value can cut the connection this app uses, lock you out of the keyboard or screen, or upset the system. The app marks the worst ones <b>careful</b> and asks first, but others can also cause trouble. If you do not know what a setting does, leave it alone. <b>Before you change one</b>, tap its row and use <b>Copy value</b> to keep the original in a note. Every change you make can be put back (see <a href=\"#settings-journal\">Undoing settings changes</a>), but a lock-out can stop you reaching the app, so read \"If you lose the connection\" below first.</div>\n\n  <h4>The three tables</h4>\n  <p>A <b>setting</b> is a name with a value, such as <code>screen_off_timeout</code> with <code>60000</code>. Android keeps them in three groups called <b>tables</b>, and the tab has one sub-tab for each.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>What lives there</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Global</b></td><td>Settings for the whole phone.</td><td><code>adb_enabled</code>, <code>airplane_mode_on</code>, <code>window_animation_scale</code>, <code>device_name</code></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Secure</b></td><td>Settings of the current user that only the system, or the shell, may change: keyboards, accessibility, location mode.</td><td><code>default_input_method</code>, <code>location_mode</code>, <code>ui_night_mode</code></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>System</b></td><td>The classic per-user settings: brightness, screen timeout, text size, rotation, volumes.</td><td><code>screen_off_timeout</code>, <code>font_scale</code>, <code>volume_music</code></td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>Android reads many settings only at certain moments, and some phone makers overwrite a few with their own, so a change that \"does nothing\" is not always a fault.</p>\n\n  <h4>What the screen shows</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Global, Secure, System</b> (three wide buttons) choose the table. The number under each name is its count of settings: an ellipsis while loading, an exclamation mark if it could not be read, and \"matches / total\" while a search or filter is on.</li>\n    <li><b>Search settings...</b> (the box, with an x to clear it), <b>＋</b> (create a setting) and <b>⋯</b> (More: sort order, reload, your changes, copy and share).</li>\n    <li>Four chips: <b>All</b>; <b>Switches</b> (values such as 1 or 0, true or false, on or off, yes or no); <b>Described</b> (every setting the app can describe: about 1,100 of Android's own; see \"What a setting does and what its values mean\" below); <b>Edited</b> (the ones you changed with this app).</li>\n    <li>A status line: the count, the time it was read, and a <b>Refresh</b> link.</li>\n    <li>The list. Each row has the name, what the setting does and which values it takes when the app knows them, and the value (long values are cut after two lines). A dot after the name means you changed it. An orange edge and the word \"careful\" mark a risky one. <i>(empty)</i> means nothing is stored, <i>(blank)</i> only spaces. A switch at the right means the value is switch-like. The list shows 120 rows at a time; more load as you scroll or with <b>Show more</b>.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>What a setting does and what its values mean</h4>\n  <p>Under the name of each setting the list says <b>what it does</b>, and below that <b>Values:</b> which values it takes. The value the setting holds now is in <b>bold</b> in that line, so <code>Values: <b>0 = off</b>; 1 = priority interruptions only; 2 = total silence; 3 = alarms only</code> tells you at a glance that Do Not Disturb is off. Tap a row and the sheet has an <b>About</b> box with the same, one value to a line, and a <b>Now:</b> line that follows what you type (type <code>2</code> and it says <i>Now: 2 = total silence</i>).</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Where the text comes from.</b> The app knows what about 1,100 of Android's own settings do (the Global, Secure and System tables of Android's source code). The ones people meet most often have been written out in plain words; the others carry the short text from Android's documentation.</li>\n    <li><b>Reading the values.</b> <code>0 = off; 1 = on</code> is a switch. A time is in <b>milliseconds</b> (1000 is one second, 60000 one minute). A decimal such as <code>1.0</code> is a scale: 1.0 normal, 0.5 half, 2.0 double. <code>percent</code>, <code>Hz</code> (refresh rate) and <code>kelvin</code> (colour warmth) are units. A list uses <code>,</code> or <code>:</code> between its items; <code>package/class</code> is an app component.</li>\n    <li><b>Only real switches flip.</b> A setting the app knows takes more than 0 and 1 (for example <code>wifi_on</code>, <code>zen_mode</code>) is never flipped by a press and hold: tap it and pick one of its values.</li>\n    <li><b>The search</b> also looks in the values text, so <code>milliseconds</code> or <code>total silence</code> finds settings.</li>\n    <li><b>A setting without a description.</b> Phone makers and apps add many settings of their own (a name starting <code>sec_</code> often comes from Samsung, <code>miui_</code> from Xiaomi, a name like <code>com.example.app.key</code> from that app). For these the editor says <i>What it probably is</i> and guesses from the name and the value: an on/off switch, a time in milliseconds, a web address, a package name. It is only a guess; if you cannot tell what a setting does, leave it alone.</li>\n    <li><b>The <b>How to read this list</b> link</b> under the title of the tab opens a short explanation of the three tables, a row and the kinds of value. The <b>?</b> next to the title opens this guide topic.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>Find a setting</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open <b>Hidden Settings</b>. Grey bars show while the tables are read; then each button shows its count.</li>\n    <li>Tap a table, or just search: the counts on all three buttons change to \"matches / total\".</li>\n    <li>Type in the search box. It looks in the <b>name</b>, the <b>value</b> and the <b>description</b>, ignores capitals, and every word you type must match. <code>timeout</code> finds <code>screen_off_timeout</code>.</li>\n    <li>Narrow the list with a chip, or tap <b>⋯</b> and choose <b>Sort by</b>: <b>Name A-Z</b> (default), <b>Name Z-A</b> or <b>Value</b>.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p><b>What you will see:</b> the list shrinks to the matches, with the matching letters highlighted. If nothing matches, the page says <i>Nothing matches \"text\" in Global.</i> and offers <b>Create \"text\" in Global</b> (only when what you typed is one word that can be a setting name) and <b>Show everything</b>. Back clears the search first. The table, filter and sort order you used last are remembered; the search text is not.</p>\n\n  <h4>Read a value</h4>\n  <p>The value is on the row. To see all of it, tap the row: a sheet opens with the value in a box. The app also asks the phone for the real value while it opens, because the list may be a little old; if it differs, the box is updated and a message says <i>Showing the value the phone holds now</i>. The sheet's <b>Reload</b> link does the same on demand.</p>\n\n  <h4>Change a value</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Tap the row. A sheet slides up with the name, the table, the description and, for a risky setting, an orange box explaining the danger. Read it.</li>\n    <li>Type in the <b>Value</b> box, or tap a quick value. If the setting is documented, its own values come first, each with its meaning (for example <b>2 · total silence</b>); then <b>0</b>, <b>1</b>, <b>true</b>, <b>false</b>, <b>null</b>, <b>(empty)</b>, <b>-1</b>. A line under the box says what kind of value it looks like and what it was before.</li>\n    <li>A red line means it cannot be saved yet. The limits are 20,000 characters, no control characters (invisible special codes, apart from a tab or a line break), and a size that fits in one command sent through ADB (about 62,000 bytes once quoted). <b>Save</b> stays grey until the problem is gone.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Save</b>. For a careful setting your phone first asks you to confirm.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p><b>What you will see:</b> the sheet stays open while the phone applies the change, so a refusal never loses what you typed. On success it closes, the row shows the new value with a dot, and an <b>Undo</b> bar appears for about 7 seconds. Saving the same value says <i>No change</i>. The word <code>null</code> is stored as that text; use <b>(empty)</b> for nothing and <b>Delete</b> to remove the setting. The sheet's other links: <b>Web search</b> (looks the setting up on the web by its table and name), <b>Explain (AI)</b> (asks the Coding Agent you chose in the <a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a> tab what the setting is and whether it is safe to change; the setting's name, table and current value are sent to that agent's company, only when you tap the button, and many settings belong to the phone maker, so the answer may be a careful guess), <b>Copy name</b>, <b>Copy value</b> and <b>Copy command</b> (the exact <code>settings put</code> line, to paste in the ADB Console pane of the <a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a> tab).</p>\n\n  <h4>Flip a switch</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li><b>Tap the switch</b> at the end of a row, or <b>press and hold the row</b> for about half a second (you feel a short tap).</li>\n    <li>Confirm if it is a careful setting.</li>\n    <li>The switch moves and the Undo bar says, for example, <code>show_touches: 0 → 1</code>.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>Capitals are kept (True becomes False). Settings that look like switches but are really a choice among several values, such as <code>zen_mode</code>, <code>ui_night_mode</code> or <code>screen_off_timeout</code>, have no switch (holding one says <i>Not a switch: tap it to edit</i>). In the editor, a switch-like setting has a <b>Flip</b> button that only changes the box; you still tap <b>Save</b>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Add a new setting</h4>\n  <p>Android uses a setting only if something reads it by that exact name, so an invented one does nothing. Add settings you have a real name for, from a guide or an app's documentation.</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Tap <b>＋</b>. A sheet titled <b>New setting</b> opens.</li>\n    <li>Choose <b>Global</b>, <b>Secure</b> or <b>System</b> (it starts on the table you were in).</li>\n    <li>Type the <b>Name</b>: up to 256 characters, not starting with a dash, with no equals sign, spaces or control characters. A red line says what is wrong.</li>\n    <li>Type the <b>Value</b>, or leave it empty to create the setting with nothing in it.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>＋ Create</b>. If the name exists, the button reads <b>Replace</b>, a note shows the current value, and you must confirm. The app asks the phone first, so a name missing from an old list is still caught.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p><b>What you will see:</b> the list jumps to that table with the name in the search box, and the Undo bar says \"＋ Created name\". Undo deletes it again.</p>\n\n  <h4>Delete a setting</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Tap the row, then <b>Delete</b> (red, at the bottom of the sheet).</li>\n    <li>Confirm. The box says Android and apps then behave as if it was never set.</li>\n    <li>The row disappears and the Undo bar says \"Deleted name\". <b>Undo</b> brings it back with its old value.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>The settings marked \"careful\"</h4>\n  <p>Twelve settings carry the warning. The app asks before every change, flip, delete or revert of them.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Warning</th><th>Settings</th><th>Risk</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Cuts the connection</td><td><code>adb_enabled</code>, <code>adb_wifi_enabled</code>, <code>airplane_mode_on</code>, <code>wifi_on</code> (Global)</td><td>It can cut the ADB connection this app uses, so you may not be able to change it back from here.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Locks you out</td><td><code>accessibility_enabled</code>, <code>enabled_accessibility_services</code>, <code>touch_exploration_enabled</code>, <code>default_input_method</code>, <code>enabled_input_methods</code> (Secure)</td><td>It can lock you out of the screen, the keyboard or accessibility.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Breaks setup</td><td><code>device_provisioned</code> (Global), <code>user_setup_complete</code>, <code>android_id</code> (Secure)</td><td>It can break setup, apps or the system. Leave the two setup settings at 1.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>Other settings can also be dangerous. When in doubt, search the web for the exact name first.</p>\n\n  <h4>Harmless and useful examples</h4>\n  <p>Easy to see, easy to undo. Try one at a time; your phone maker's software may ignore some.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Setting (table)</th><th>What the app says</th><th>Try</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><code>window_animation_scale</code>, <code>transition_animation_scale</code>, <code>animator_duration_scale</code> (Global)</td><td>1 = normal, 0 = off.</td><td><code>0.5</code> makes animations quicker; <code>0</code> turns them off.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>screen_off_timeout</code> (System)</td><td>Screen timeout in milliseconds.</td><td><code>60000</code> is one minute.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>stay_on_while_plugged_in</code> (Global)</td><td>1 AC, 2 USB, 4 wireless; 7 = all.</td><td><code>7</code> keeps the screen on while charging.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>show_touches</code> (System)</td><td>Show taps (developer option).</td><td>Flip it on, then off.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>font_scale</code> (System)</td><td>Text size (1.0 = normal).</td><td><code>1.15</code> for slightly larger text. Note the old value first.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Tap <b>Described</b> to see only the settings the app can explain. It is a safe way to learn what is there.</div>\n\n  <h4>The ⋯ button</h4>\n  <p>The sheet <b>Hidden settings tools</b> has <b>Sort by</b>, <b>Reload all</b>, <b>Changes</b> (your history; see <a href=\"#settings-journal\">Undoing settings changes</a>), <b>Copy shown</b> and <b>Share shown</b> (the rows currently shown, one <code>name=value</code> per line, by copy or by the share sheet), plus a short value guide. The lists can hold personal details such as the device name, Bluetooth address and Android ID, so do not post one without reading it.</p>\n\n  <h4>If a change is refused or nothing happens</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Meaning and what to do</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Red box <b>Could not read the global settings</b>, and \"!\" on the button</td><td>No answer, or the list was cut short. Tap <b>Try again</b>, or <b>Working modes</b> to check the connection (<a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>).</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>A sheet <b>Change refused</b> (or <b>Could not delete</b>)</td><td>Android said no, in its own words, with advice. Some keys are locked by the system or maker. On Xiaomi, Redmi and POCO, turn on <b>USB debugging (Security settings)</b> in Developer options.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"No answer from the device (timed out)\"</td><td>The change may still have been applied. The list is read again in a moment: check the row before you retry.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The old value comes back by itself</td><td>The app reads the value back after every change, so this is what the phone holds. The system or the maker's software owns that setting.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The new value shows but nothing looks different</td><td>Try turning the screen off and on, restarting the app that should react, or restarting the phone. The setting may not apply to your phone.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>If you lose the connection</h4>\n  <p>If you switched off <code>adb_enabled</code> or <code>adb_wifi_enabled</code> (or Wi-Fi, or switched on airplane mode) while the app was using ADB, the app can no longer reach the phone to change them back. Open your phone's own Settings, go to <b>Developer options</b> (a hidden part of Android's Settings; if you cannot find it, search the Settings for its name), switch <b>USB debugging</b> or <b>Wireless debugging</b> back on (and Wi-Fi), then reconnect in <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working Modes</a>. If another mode (Shizuku or Root) still works, <b>Revert</b> the entry in <b>Changes</b> instead. If a keyboard or accessibility setting locks you out, use your phone's own Settings to choose a working keyboard and switch accessibility services back, then revert the entry.</p>"},{"id":"settings-journal","title":"Undoing settings changes: the Undo bar and the history","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Hidden Settings keeps two ways back: an <b>Undo</b> bar that appears right after a change, and a history called <b>Changes</b> where every change you made can be reverted later.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> the Undo bar appears at the bottom of the screen in the <a href=\"#tab-settings\">Hidden Settings</a> and <a href=\"#tab-overlays\">RRO/Monet Customization</a> tabs. The history is under the <b>⋯</b> button of Hidden Settings, in the <b>Changes</b> button.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Putting a value back is a change like any other, so it needs a working mode (see <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>).</li>\n    <li>The history only knows about changes made <b>with this app</b>. If another app, Android itself or a computer changed a setting, it is not listed, and the app cannot tell you what the old value was.</li>\n    <li><b>The page has no backup and no restore for settings.</b> There is no button that saves a whole table to a file or loads one back. The history is the safety net, and the section \"Keep your own copy\" below shows a manual way to save a list before you experiment.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>The Undo bar</h4>\n  <p>After a change, a small bar slides up from the bottom of the screen. It shows a short line in a typewriter font and an <b>Undo</b> button. It stays for about 7 seconds and then slides away.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>You did this</th><th>The bar says</th><th>Undo does this</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Saved a value in the editor</td><td>Saved name</td><td>Writes the previous value back.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Flipped a switch</td><td>name: 1 → 0</td><td>Flips it back.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Created a setting</td><td>＋ Created name</td><td>Deletes the setting again.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Replaced an existing setting</td><td>Saved name</td><td>Writes the old value back.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Deleted a setting</td><td>Deleted name</td><td>Creates it again with its old value.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Applied or reset a theme (RRO/Monet tab)</td><td>Theme applied / Theme reset to the default</td><td>Puts the old theme setting back.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Switched an overlay on or off (RRO/Monet tab)</td><td>On: name / Off: name</td><td>Switches it back.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Make a change.</li>\n    <li>Within about 7 seconds, tap <b>Undo</b> on the bar.</li>\n    <li>A message says <i>Put back: name</i> (or <i>Removed: name</i> when the setting did not exist before), and the row shows the old value again.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p><b>What you will see:</b> the bar disappears when you tap Undo, when 7 seconds have passed, when a newer change takes its place (only the newest change has an Undo bar), or when you leave the Hidden Settings and RRO/Monet Customization tabs. After the bar is gone, use <b>Changes</b>.</p>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> If you change a theme and Android restarts the app (this happens when the colours change), the app comes back to the RRO/Monet tab by itself with the Undo bar waiting. This works for changes made in the last 20 seconds.</div>\n\n  <h4>The Changes history</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open <b>Hidden Settings</b> and tap <b>⋯</b> next to the search box.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Changes</b>. A sheet titled <b>Changes</b> opens, with the line \"Made with this app, newest first. Revert puts the old value back.\"</li>\n    <li>Each entry shows the setting name, the table and how long ago (for example \"Global · 3 min ago\"), and the change as <i>old value → new value</i>. A value that was not there before shows as <i>(not set)</i>; an empty one as <i>(empty)</i>.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Revert</b> under the entry you want to undo.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>If nothing has been changed yet, the sheet says <i>Nothing was changed with this app yet.</i> The same history is behind the <b>Edited</b> filter chip and the small dot after the name of every changed setting in the list.</p>\n\n  <h4>Revert one change</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>In <b>Changes</b>, find the entry and tap <b>Revert</b>.</li>\n    <li>If the setting has changed again since (by you, by Android, or by another app), a box says <i>name has changed since: it is now ... (this change left it ...). Put the old value back anyway?</i> Choose OK only if you want the old value back regardless.</li>\n    <li>If the setting is marked careful, you are asked to confirm once more (see <a href=\"#tab-settings\">Hidden Settings</a>).</li>\n    <li>The sheet closes and a message says <i>Put back: name</i> (or <i>Removed: name</i> when the entry was a setting you created, so there is nothing to put back).</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>Reverting is recorded as a new change, so it appears at the top of the history and can itself be reverted.</p>\n  <p>If the old value was longer than 2,000 characters, the entry shows <i>(too long to keep)</i> in its place and has no Revert button, because the old text was not stored. For those, use the Undo bar right after the change, or fix the setting by hand.</p>\n\n  <h4>Revert many changes</h4>\n  <p>There is no \"revert all\" button. Revert the entries one at a time, <b>starting from the top (the newest)</b>, so that every entry finds the setting in the state it left it. Going out of order is allowed, but you will get the \"has changed since\" question for entries that depend on a later one.</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open <b>⋯ → Changes</b>.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Revert</b> on the first (newest) entry. The sheet closes after each revert.</li>\n    <li>Open <b>⋯ → Changes</b> again and repeat. Check your phone from time to time, so you stop as soon as it behaves normally.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>What is kept, and where</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>The last 150 changes.</b> Older ones are forgotten as new ones arrive.</li>\n    <li><b>Where:</b> inside this app's own storage on the phone. The app does not write a file to Downloads or anywhere else, and nothing is sent to anyone. The history survives closing the app and restarting the phone. Because it lives in the app's own storage, clearing the app's data or uninstalling the app removes it (and with it your way back).</li>\n    <li><b>Theme changes too.</b> A Material You theme change made in the RRO/Monet tab is a change of the secure setting <code>theme_customization_overlay_packages</code>, and is listed here with that name. Reverting it puts the old theme setting back. Overlay on/off changes are not listed in Changes, only in the Undo bar.</li>\n    <li><b>What is in each entry:</b> the table, the name, when it happened, the old value and the new value.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>Clear the history</h4>\n  <p>At the bottom of the <b>Changes</b> sheet, <b>Clear history</b> asks <i>Forget the history of changes? The settings themselves stay as they are.</i> If you confirm, the list is emptied, the small dots and the <b>Edited</b> filter forget everything, and nothing on the phone changes. You cannot get the entries back, so do this only when you are happy with how the phone behaves. The button only shows while there is something to clear.</p>\n\n  <h4>Keep your own copy before you experiment</h4>\n  <p>The history cannot restore a whole table, and it does not know settings you did not change. If you are about to try several things, save the original values first.</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open <b>Hidden Settings</b> and choose the table (<b>Global</b>, <b>Secure</b> or <b>System</b>).</li>\n    <li>Clear the search box and tap <b>All</b>, so that every setting is shown.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>⋯</b>, then <b>Copy shown</b> (a message says <i>Copied 340 settings</i> or similar), and paste into a notes app. Or tap <b>Share shown</b> and send the text to yourself.</li>\n    <li>Do the same for the other tables if you want to.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>The copy is plain text with one <code>name=value</code> line per setting. To restore something later, find the line and set the value by hand (tap the row, type, <b>Save</b>), or create the setting with <b>＋</b> if it was deleted. The app cannot read that text back in for you.</p>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> The lists can hold personal details, such as the device name, the Bluetooth name and address, and the Android ID. Keep the copy private. If a very long list does not go through the share sheet, narrow it with the search or a filter first.</div>\n\n  <h4>If something goes wrong</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Revert says it could not change the setting.</b> A sheet shows Android's own answer, the same as for any change. Read it; see \"If a change is refused or nothing happens\" in <a href=\"#tab-settings\">Hidden Settings</a>.</li>\n    <li><b>The history is empty but you know you changed things.</b> The changes were made outside this app, or the app's data was cleared since. Set the values back by hand from your own copy.</li>\n    <li><b>The phone is unusable after a change and you cannot reach the app.</b> Use your phone's own Settings (Developer options, language and input, accessibility) to undo what you can, reconnect in <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working Modes</a>, then revert the entry. See also <a href=\"#safety\">Safety</a>.</li>\n  </ul>"},{"id":"tab-overlays","title":"RRO/Monet Customization","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Change the colours Android uses for its own screens and for apps that follow it (the \"Material You\" theme), and switch the system's look-changing add-ons, called overlays, on or off.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> the tab named <b>RRO/Monet Customization</b> (two lines on the tab bar), right after Hidden Settings. If it is missing, it is switched off: see <a href=\"#feature-list\">Turning tabs on and off and moving them</a>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Two words first</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Overlay (RRO).</b> RRO means Runtime Resource Overlay: a small package that changes the look of another app, or of Android itself (colours, icon shapes, fonts, the navigation bar), without touching that app. Each overlay is on or off. The app it changes is its <b>target</b>.</li>\n    <li><b>Material You, or \"Monet\".</b> From Android 12, Android builds all its system colours from <b>one source colour</b>: your wallpaper's, or one you choose. A <b>style</b> decides how vibrant the palette is. Google apps and apps that support Material You follow it. Monet is the name of that colour engine.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>The tab has two halves, chosen with the two buttons under the title: <b>Theme</b> (with \"Material You\" under it) and <b>Overlays</b> (with the number of overlays under it).</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Changing anything needs a working mode</b> (ADB over TCP, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root; see <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>). Without one, a card says <b>Needs ADB, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root</b> with a <b>Set Up</b> button. The <b>colours in use</b> are still shown, because the app reads them from Android directly.</li>\n    <li><b>The Theme half needs Android 12 or newer.</b> On Android 11 and older the tab opens on the Overlays half (unless you chose a half before), <b>Apply theme</b> and <b>Default</b> are greyed out, and the page says <i>Material You needs Android 12 or newer.</i></li>\n    <li><b>Phone makers differ.</b> The app writes the setting that Android's own wallpaper picker writes, so phones close to stock Android are the most likely to follow it. Some makers add their own palette on top (the page says so), and a few refuse changes from a shell. The app cannot tell in advance. Try one colour, and use <b>Undo</b> or <b>Default</b> if you do not like it.</li>\n    <li><b>Samsung.</b> If the phone has Samsung's \"wallpaper colours\" switch (a setting called <code>wallpapertheme_state</code>), <b>Apply theme</b> also sets it to match (off for a colour you chose, on for the wallpaper) and reads it back; <b>Undo</b> restores it. If it will not change, a message says the phone's own palette may still win. The app does not talk to Samsung's theme store or to Good Lock (Samsung's own customisation app); it only writes Android's standard theme setting and, where it exists, that one switch.</li>\n    <li>On Xiaomi, Redmi and POCO a change may need <b>USB debugging (Security settings)</b> switched on in Developer options.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> A theme change repaints the whole system and Android usually <b>restarts apps</b>, this one included. Save your work elsewhere first. The app notices the restart and brings you back to this tab with the Undo bar waiting. Overlays can also change the navigation bar or system interface, so the wrong one can make the screen harder to use. Every change here can be undone.</div>\n\n  <h4>The Theme half</h4>\n  <h5>Colors in use</h5>\n  <p>The top card shows what Android uses now: a swatch and a title, either <b>System default</b>, <b>From your wallpaper</b> (with the style), <b>Custom color</b> (with a code such as <code>#6750A4 · Tonal Spot</code>) or <b>Another theme</b> (set by something else; applying here replaces it), plus a time such as \"2 min ago\". Below are five rows of squares, <b>Accent 1, Accent 2, Accent 3, Neutral 1</b> and <b>Neutral 2</b>, eleven shades each from light to dark. <b>Tap a square</b> to copy its code (<i>Copied #6750A4 (Accent 1 500)</i>). <b>Refresh</b> reads the colours again; after a change they redraw by themselves a few seconds later.</p>\n\n  <h5>Choose the colour source</h5>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Under <b>Color</b>, tap <b>Wallpaper</b> (Android builds the palette from your home-screen wallpaper, its default) or <b>Custom color</b> (from one colour you choose).</li>\n    <li>With <b>Custom color</b> a box appears with a big square and a code field (a hex code is six letters and digits that name a colour). Pick the colour in one of the ways below.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Type the code:</b> <code>6750A4</code>, <code>#6750a4</code>, <code>#abc</code> or <code>FF6750A4</code> (the first two digits, transparency, are dropped). A red line says <i>Use six hex digits, like 6750A4</i> if it is invalid.</li>\n    <li><b>Move the sliders:</b> <b>Hue</b> (0 to 359), <b>Saturation</b> and <b>Lightness</b> (0 to 100). The code and the square follow.</li>\n    <li><b>Tap a Recent colour:</b> the last eight you applied.</li>\n    <li><b>Open the presets:</b> tap <b>Preset colors 657</b>, search by name or code, narrow with the chips <b>All, Neutral, Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Teal, Blue, Purple, Pink</b>, and tap a round swatch. Its name and code show under the grid. The grid shows 96 at a time with a <b>Show more</b> button. Tapping a preset also switches the source to <b>Custom color</b>.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h5>Choose a style</h5>\n  <p>Under <b>Style</b>, tap one of six options (a round button marks the active one):</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>What the page says</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Tonal Spot</b> (Default)</td><td>Complementary colors in a balanced blend.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Vibrant</b></td><td>Vibrant complementary colors with a strong background tint.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Expressive</b></td><td>A colorful theme that pairs unexpected accent colors.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Fruit Salad</b></td><td>A saturated theme that uses a wide range of colors.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Rainbow</b></td><td>A strong accent that stands out against no background tint.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Spritz</b> (Muted)</td><td>A muted theme with a blend of desaturated colors.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h5>Apply a colour scheme</h5>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Choose the source, the colour (if custom) and the style.</li>\n    <li>Read the line under the buttons: <i>Android repaints in a moment, and apps may restart.</i> If it says <i>This is already what the phone uses.</i>, there is nothing to apply. With an invalid code, <b>Apply theme</b> stays grey.</li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Apply theme</b>. It reads <b>Applying...</b> while the app reads the current setting, writes the new one and reads it back.</li>\n    <li>Android repaints within a second or two and may restart the app. The Undo bar appears for about 7 seconds with <i>Theme applied</i>, and the colours in use update.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>The app keeps other choices stored in the same setting (a font, icon shape or icon pack on Pixel-like phones) and replaces only the colour entries. It writes one setting, the secure setting <code>theme_customization_overlay_packages</code>, and lists the change in <a href=\"#settings-journal\">Changes</a> in Hidden Settings, with a Revert. <b>Default</b> asks <i>Go back to the system default?</i>; if you confirm, the theme setting is cleared (wallpaper colours, Tonal Spot) and Samsung's switch is left alone. The <b>How this works</b> card has <b>Copy the setting</b> (the raw setting text, for a bug report) and <b>Reload</b>.</p>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> The app's own look can follow these colours: the <b>Material You</b> palette in <a href=\"#themes\">Themes and colours</a> uses them, so this app may change colour too.</div>\n\n  <h4>The Overlays half</h4>\n  <p>Tap <b>Overlays</b>. The page asks Android's overlay service for its list (what <code>cmd overlay list</code> prints) and shows it:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Search overlays...</b> filters by name or target; <b>Reload</b> reads the list again. Chips: <b>All</b>, <b>On</b>, <b>Off</b> and <b>Theme</b> (names that look like theme, colour, icon pack, navigation bar, font or shape). Your chip is remembered.</li>\n    <li>A status line, such as <i>83 overlays · 21 on</i>, the time read, and <b>Refresh</b>.</li>\n    <li>The list is in three parts, in this order: <b>Enabled</b> first, then <b>Disabled</b>, then <b>Installed, not changeable</b> (overlays Android lists but cannot switch), each with its count. Inside a part, overlays are grouped under their target (such as <code>android</code> or <code>com.android.systemui</code>) with a count; those with no target are under <b>Other</b>.</li>\n    <li>Each row shows the name, <b>a very short description of what it is for</b> (for example <i>Gesture navigation: swipes instead of buttons</i>), its state (<b>On</b>, <b>Off</b> or <b>Unavailable</b>) and a switch. The description is a best guess made from the overlay's name and target, so treat it as a hint, not a promise. Unavailable means Android lists it but it cannot be switched (its target app is missing, or it is not approved); the row is dimmed. 120 rows are drawn at a time.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h5>Switch an overlay on or off</h5>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Find the overlay (search or a chip).</li>\n    <li><b>Tap its switch</b>, or <b>press and hold the row</b> for about half a second (you feel a short tap).</li>\n    <li>The app runs the change and reads the whole list again, so the row shows what Android reports, not what was asked for.</li>\n    <li>The Undo bar says <i>On: name</i> or <i>Off: name</i>. Tap <b>Undo</b> within about 7 seconds to put it back.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p><b>The detail sheet.</b> Tap a row (not the switch) to open a sheet with the full name, state and target, a note, <b>Switch on</b> or <b>Switch off</b>, and the links <b>Copy name</b>, <b>Copy target</b> and <b>Copy command</b> (the exact <code>cmd overlay</code> line).</p>\n  <p><b>When an overlay will not switch.</b> Some overlays are fixed on or off by the system or belong to another app. Android 12 and newer answer with a bare \"commit failed\", and the app says so: <i>Android refused the change and gave no reason</i>. A sheet titled <b>Could not switch it on</b> (or off) shows Android's words and advice. If no answer comes in time, the phone may still apply it and the list is read again shortly. Overlays are switched for the user the phone's shell uses by default, which is the owner on nearly every phone.</p>\n\n  <h4>If a colour looks bad: undo and recovery</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li><b>Tap Undo</b> on the bar (about 7 seconds). The old theme is written back and a message says <i>Theme put back</i>.</li>\n    <li><b>Or tap Default</b> and confirm: wallpaper colours and Tonal Spot again.</li>\n    <li><b>Or use the history:</b> <b>Hidden Settings</b>, <b>⋯</b>, <b>Changes</b>, find <code>theme_customization_overlay_packages</code>, <b>Revert</b> (see <a href=\"#settings-journal\">Undoing settings changes</a>).</li>\n    <li><b>Or delete the setting:</b> in <b>Hidden Settings</b> open <b>Secure</b>, search <code>theme_customization</code>, tap the row and <b>Delete</b>. Android then uses its default.</li>\n    <li><b>An overlay is the problem:</b> in the Overlays half tap <b>On</b>, find it and switch it off.</li>\n    <li><b>The screen is hard to use:</b> use your phone's own wallpaper and style settings (if it has them) to choose a normal colour, restart the phone if the colours stay wrong, then revert the entry in Changes.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>The app needs a working mode to write, so reconnect in <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working Modes</a> first if it dropped.</p>\n\n  <h4>Troubleshooting</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Could not read the theme</b> / <b>Could not read the overlays</b></td><td>No answer, or this phone has no overlay manager. Tap <b>Try again</b> and check <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working Modes</a>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Theme change refused</b></td><td>Android did not keep the new theme. Read the answer under it; on Xiaomi, Redmi and POCO turn on USB debugging (Security settings). Some phones do not allow it.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"No answer: the theme may still change\"</td><td>The phone was slow, or the app restarted before the answer. Wait a few seconds: the tab reads the state again.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Applied, but the colours look the same</td><td>Your phone maker's own palette wins, or apps have not repainted. Reopen an app or restart the phone; if nothing helps, your phone does not follow this setting.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>"},{"id":"tab-updates","title":"App Updater","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">This tab checks whether newer versions exist for the apps you installed outside the Play Store, and for Samsung's own apps. It can download and install them for you, after checking each file.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> the tab named <b>App Updater</b>. When updates are waiting, its name shows a number in brackets, for example \"App Updater (3)\".</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Checking needs the internet and no working mode.</li>\n    <li>Installing an update needs a working mode: ADB over TCP, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root (see <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>). Without one, a sheet <b>Working Mode Required</b> opens. The only exception is this app's own update, which can use Android's install screen. Android can then ask you to allow installs from this app first (Android calls the setting <b>Install unknown apps</b>): switch it on for this app when asked, or beforehand in <b>About</b>, <b>Permissions</b> (see <a href=\"#app-permissions\">The permissions this app asks for</a>).</li>\n    <li>Play Store apps are not checked here (see the last card below).</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>Where updates come from</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Which apps</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Galaxy Store</b></td><td>Samsung system apps (package names starting com.samsung., com.sec. or com.osp.) and apps installed from the Galaxy Store. Only apps Samsung distributes appear.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>GitHub</b>, <b>Codeberg</b></td><td>Sideloaded apps whose releases live on those sites (Codeberg is a website for sharing code, like GitHub). The app reads the newest release and picks the APK for your processor.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>F-Droid</b>, <b>IzzyOnDroid</b></td><td>Sideloaded apps found by package name in these catalogs of free, open-source apps.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Obtainium</b></td><td>Obtainium is a separate open-source app that fetches updates from developers' pages. Its community catalog says where an app's releases live, and you can import your own Obtainium export.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>A <b>sideloaded</b> app is a normal (not system) app that was not installed by a store such as Google Play, Galaxy Store, Aurora, Amazon, Huawei, Xiaomi, OPPO or vivo. Each such app is looked up in this order, and the first source that knows it decides: your own source (set or imported), then the Obtainium catalog (skipped for apps installed by an F-Droid client), then IzzyOnDroid, then F-Droid (for apps installed by an F-Droid client, F-Droid comes first). This app's own update has its own card.</p>\n\n  <h4>Step by step</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open the tab. The first time, it checks by itself. Later, press <b>Check for Updates</b>.</li>\n    <li>Watch the status line: \"Checking sideloaded apps (GitHub, F-Droid, Obtainium)... 3/12\", then \"Checking the Galaxy Store... 8/30 apps\". It ends with, for example, \"2 updates available - checked 12 Galaxy Store and 9 sideloaded apps\", and a time. If some could not be checked, it says how many and gives the last error.</li>\n    <li>Read the rows (next section). With none, the list says \"Everything is up to date.\"</li>\n    <li>Press <b>Update</b> on one row. The button changes to \"Downloading 40%\", then \"Installing\", then a tick and \"Updated\". A thin bar follows the progress.</li>\n    <li>To update everything, press <b>Update All (N)</b> (it appears when two or more are ready) and confirm. They run one after another: waiting rows say \"Queued\".</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>Reading a row</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The name, the package name, and badges: the source (for example GitHub or Galaxy Store), \"system\" for a system app, and where the source came from (\"via Obtainium catalog\", \"via your Obtainium list\", \"your source\").</li>\n    <li>The installed version, an arrow, and the new version in bold. Short release notes may follow.</li>\n    <li><b>Update</b> starts the update. After an error the button says <b>Retry</b> and a line gives the reason.</li>\n    <li><b>Release</b> replaces Update when the newest release has no APK for your phone; it opens the release page.</li>\n    <li><b>Page</b> opens the app's page; <b>Open in Obtainium</b> hands the app to Obtainium, if you have it.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>Under the list there may be two more groups. <b>Track in Obtainium (N)</b> holds apps whose releases are on sites this app cannot read (a company website, for example); use <b>Open in Obtainium</b> for them. <b>Not tracked (N)</b> holds sideloaded apps no source knows. Press <b>+ Set source</b>, then enter a GitHub link (github.com/owner/repo), a Codeberg link or an F-Droid package link. The app saves it and checks again.</p>\n  <p>You cannot ignore or pin a version, and there is no screen to edit a source you saved. A source you saved is tried first for that app.</p>\n\n  <h4>Other buttons and cards</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Import Obtainium List</b></td><td>Opens the file picker. Choose the export file from Obtainium (Obtainium, Settings, Export). The app uses each app's source from it and checks again.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>GitHub Token</b></td><td>Optional. A token is a long code that you create in your GitHub account settings. One with no permissions ticked is enough, and it raises GitHub's limit from 60 to 5,000 checks an hour. Empty the box to remove it. The button shows a tick when a token is saved.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Card <b>App update</b></td><td>Updates this app itself from its GitHub releases: <b>Check for app update</b>, <b>Update now</b>, <b>Release notes</b>. It checks that the file is this app, newer, and signed with the same key. With a working mode it installs by itself and says \"The app will restart\" (Android closes the app while it is replaced, so open it again if it does not come back). With none it opens Android's install screen, and Android can first ask you to allow installs from this app (Install unknown apps; the Permissions sheet in About has a row for it).</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Card <b>Play Store apps (via Aurora)</b></td><td>The app cannot tell whether a Play Store app has an update. <b>Open Aurora Store</b> (an open-source Play Store client, shown if installed) and <b>List installed apps</b> give you a <b>Store</b> button per app, which opens its store page.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>The same information shows up in other places: the Application Manager's list can show the new version next to an app (turn on the <b>Versions</b> pill), can sort by <b>Updates first</b>, and the app menu shows an update line with an Update, Release or Download button (see <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a> and <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>).</p>\n\n  <h4>Checks before an update installs, and messages</h4>\n  <p>For every update the app downloads the file, then checks that it is the same package, a newer version, and signed with the same key as the installed app. If any check fails, nothing is installed. It then installs over the old version through your working mode, which keeps the app's data, and deletes the downloaded file.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Message</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>\"N couldn't be checked (...)\"</td><td>A site did not answer, or limited you: GitHub allows only 60 checks an hour without a token. Wait, or add a <b>GitHub Token</b>, then check again.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"signed with a different key than the installed app ...\"</td><td>The file comes from another builder than your copy (F-Droid signs its own builds). Update from the source you installed from.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"downloaded version ... is not newer than the installed one\"</td><td>The source's version number is not higher than yours.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"no APK for this phone in the latest release\"</td><td>Use <b>Release</b> to look for a build by hand.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"download failed: HTTP ...\" or \"download incomplete\"</td><td>The site or your connection failed. Press <b>Retry</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"installing updates needs ADB, Shizuku or Root\"</td><td>Set up a working mode first.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Privacy.</b> Checking contacts these sites: Samsung's update service (it receives your phone model, your SIM's network codes, your region code and Android version, with the package name and version of each Samsung app), api.github.com and github.com, codeberg.org, f-droid.org, apt.izzysoft.de and raw.githubusercontent.com (for the Obtainium catalog). The lookups name the apps being checked (their package names, or their GitHub project). Installing an update also downloads the file from the address the release gives. Nothing else leaves the phone. See <a href=\"#privacy\">Privacy</a>.</div>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> An update replaces the working version. If an app matters to you, back it up first (<a href=\"#apps-backup\">Backups</a>). Every update, done or failed, is also written to the History (the button on the UAD-NG Debloater tab).</div>"},{"id":"tab-store","title":"App Stores","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Browse four catalogs of open-source Android apps and install from them: ShizuStore, GitHub, F-Droid and Orion. The apps are not stored by this app; each one is downloaded from its own developer's page.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> the tab named <b>App Stores</b>. Four buttons at its top switch between the catalogs: <b>ShizuStore</b>, <b>GitHub</b>, <b>F-Droid</b> and <b>Orion</b>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Browsing needs the internet only.</li>\n    <li>Installing uses your active working mode (ADB, Shizuku or Root, see <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>). With none, the app hands the downloaded file to Android's install screen and you confirm there. Android can first ask you to allow installs from this app (Android calls the setting <b>Install unknown apps</b>; the Permissions sheet in <b>About</b> has a row for it). That route may be blocked on some phones; if it is, set up a working mode.</li>\n    <li>Each install is one APK file. Apps that come only as bundles cannot be installed here; use the <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a>.</li>\n    <li>Galaxy Store is not a store in this tab. Samsung apps are checked in the <a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a>.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>The four catalogs</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Catalog</th><th>What it holds</th><th>Sorting</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>ShizuStore</b></td><td>The curated catalog of timschneeb: apps that work with <a href=\"#mode-shizuku\">Shizuku</a>. Installed from each app's own source (GitHub, GitLab or F-Droid).</td><td>Most starred, Most downloaded, Recently updated, Recently added, Name (A-Z)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>GitHub</b></td><td>Apps that publish an APK on GitHub Releases, from the Komi Store catalog (up to 5,000 apps).</td><td>Most starred, Recently released, Most downloaded, Name (A-Z)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>F-Droid</b></td><td>Any app in F-Droid or in other known repositories. Pick a repository first.</td><td>Recently updated, Name (A-Z)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Orion</b></td><td>The public Orion catalog by RookieEnough: apps resolved from GitHub, Codeberg or direct links, including Morphe-built apps. Only entries that resolve to an installable APK are shown.</td><td>Name (A-Z), Name (Z-A)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>Step by step</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open the tab and press the catalog you want. The grey line under the card says what it is doing: \"Open to load the catalog.\", \"Loading the ShizuStore catalog...\", \"Loading the catalog...\", \"Loading more... 450 apps so far\".</li>\n    <li>Type in <b>Search apps...</b>. The search looks at names, package names, descriptions and authors (the other three catalogs also look at categories). Use one drop-down for a category and the other for sorting.</li>\n    <li>Each row shows an icon, the name, two lines of description, and small chips such as stars, downloads, version, size and category (ShizuStore shows stars, version, category and a \"root\" chip when the app needs root). A count such as \"12 / 340 apps\" sits next to <b>Refresh</b> (called <b>Refresh catalog</b> in ShizuStore). Lists show 60 rows at a time; press <b>Show 60 more</b> for more.</li>\n    <li><b>ShizuStore:</b> tap a row to open a detail sheet with the description, stars, downloads, author, licence, the install source and size, screenshots and a collapsible <b>Permissions</b> list. Press <b>Install</b>, or <b>Source</b> to open the project's page. The button says <b>No APK</b> when none is available.</li>\n    <li><b>Enlarge a screenshot.</b> The screenshots in the detail sheet are small. Tap one (the line under them says <i>Tap a picture to enlarge it</i>) and it opens full screen on black, to fit the screen, with a counter such as <b>2 / 8</b>. <b>Pinch</b> with two fingers to zoom (up to 6 times), or use the <b>+</b>, <b>−</b> and <b>Fit</b> buttons at the top; <b>double tap</b> zooms in to 2.5 times and a second double tap goes back; <b>drag</b> a zoomed picture to move it (it cannot leave the screen); <b>swipe</b> left or right, or press the arrows at the screen's edges, for the next or previous picture (the neighbours load while you look). <b>Back</b>, the <b>✕</b>, or a tap on the black beside the picture closes the viewer and leaves the detail sheet as it was. A picture that cannot be loaded says <i>This picture could not be loaded. Check the connection and open it again.</i> Only pictures with a web address (http or https) are shown.</li>\n    <li><b>GitHub, F-Droid, Orion and the stores you add:</b> tap a row (not its <b>Install</b> button) to open the same kind of detail sheet. What the list knows is shown at once (description, stars, version, size, categories); the rest comes from the phone while a line says it is loading: the <b>full description</b> (for a GitHub or Codeberg project its README, as far as it is useful, in plain readable text), the <b>screenshots</b>, the <b>license</b>, forks and topics, and the <b>Website</b> and <b>Source</b> links. An F-Droid style repository supplies its own description and screenshots; a GitHub or Codeberg project supplies the screenshots in its <i>fastlane</i> folder or, failing that, the pictures of its README (badges, logos and buttons are left out). Tap a screenshot to enlarge it. <b>Install</b> in the sheet does the same as the button of the row. If the details cannot be loaded (no connection, or GitHub's limit for requests without a token is used up; save a token in the App Updater tab to raise it), the sheet keeps what the list knows and says why.</li>\n    <li>Watch the status line: under the row for GitHub, F-Droid and Orion, and above the buttons of the detail sheet for ShizuStore. It shows \"Starting...\", \"Finding the latest release of ...\", \"Downloading ...\" (then the size so far and a percentage, such as \"12 MB 40%\"), \"Checking the download's SHA-256...\" (a code that proves the file was not changed on the way), \"Installing ...\", then \"Installed ...\". With the system installer: \"Opening the installer...\" and \"Confirm the install of ...\".</li>\n    <li>After a successful install through a working mode, a sheet offers <b>Launch Application</b> and <b>Application Settings</b>. With the system installer there is no sheet: Android's own screen tells you.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>The + tab: add a store of your own</h4>\n  <p>The last tab of the row is a <b>+</b>. Tap it to add one more place to find apps. Paste an address:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>An F-Droid style repository</b>, such as <code>https://apt.izzysoft.de/fdroid/repo</code> (the address of the folder that holds <code>index-v2.json</code> or <code>index-v1.jar</code>; with or without the file name, and the app also tries the address with <code>/repo</code> added). The app first reads the start of its index to check that it is a repository and to learn its name. It then lists all its apps like the F-Droid tab does, with the build that suits your phone, and checks every download against the repository's own hash.</li>\n    <li><b>A GitHub or Codeberg project</b>, such as <code>https://github.com/owner/project</code>: a tab with that one project, which installs the APK of its latest release.</li>\n    <li><b>A GitHub or Codeberg user or organization</b>, such as <code>https://github.com/owner</code>: a tab with every project of theirs that is not archived and not a fork (up to 300 on GitHub, 200 on Codeberg), sorted by stars. Not every project publishes an APK; Install says so when a project has none.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p><b>The name of the tab.</b> Type one in <b>Name of the tab</b> (up to 30 characters), or leave it empty and it is filled in from the address: a repository's own name, a project's name, a user's name. A tab can be renamed later with <b>Rename</b> on its card, and removed with <b>Remove this store</b> (apps you installed from it stay installed). Up to 12 extra stores; they are kept on this phone and come back after a restart. The window refuses an address that is no web address, one the app cannot read as a repository (it says so in the window), and a store that is already there.</p>\n  <p>Only public information is read, and nothing is sent anywhere but to the address you gave (and, for the app's details, to GitHub or Codeberg).</p>\n\n  <h4>GitHub catalog extras</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>If the search does not find an app, press <b>Search</b> in the box \"Not in the list? Search the whole catalog for ...\". It needs at least two letters.</li>\n    <li>Type a GitHub project as <b>owner/repo</b> (the two parts of its web address after github.com/) to get a line \"Install the latest APK release of this GitHub project\" with an <b>Install</b> button. The app picks the APK for your processor. If the latest release has no APK you see \"The latest release of ... has no APK asset.\"</li>\n    <li>If the catalog server cannot be reached, the app shows its offline copy, or a small built-in list, and says so. <b>Refresh</b> tries again. The list is kept for six hours.</li>\n    <li>A GitHub token saved in the <a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a> lifts GitHub's limit on these requests.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>F-Droid extras</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The drop-down at the top chooses the repository. Press <b>Repos</b> to see the list: each entry has <b>Browse apps</b>, <b>Copy address</b> and <b>Copy fingerprint</b>. Press the same button again (it now says <b>&larr; Apps</b>) to come back. <b>Copy address &amp; fingerprint</b> copies both for the open repository.</li>\n    <li>A catalog can be several MB, F-Droid's own tens of MB. On mobile data or a hotspot the tab asks first (\"You're on a metered connection\") and waits for <b>Download</b>. A catalog is kept for 12 hours.</li>\n    <li>Apps with no build for your phone are hidden, and a note tells you how many.</li>\n    <li>When the repository publishes a SHA-256 for a file (F-Droid's index does), the download is checked against it. A mismatch stops the install.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>What the status line says</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Message</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>\"Could not load the catalog: ...\" or \"Could not load: ...\"</td><td>No internet, or the site did not answer. Check the connection and press <b>Refresh</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"No apps match your search.\" or \"No apps match your filters.\"</td><td>Clear the search box, set the category to All categories.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"Stopped early after N apps ...\"</td><td>The catalog download broke off. Press <b>Refresh</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"Install failed: ...\"</td><td>The reason follows: for example \"the download is X, not Y\" (a wrong file was stopped), or Android's own message such as a signature mismatch.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"No matching APK in the latest release of ...\"</td><td>The project's release has no APK that fits your phone.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> A catalog lists apps; it does not vouch for them. Open <b>Source</b> and read about a project you do not know, and look at the permissions. If the same app is already installed from another source with a different signing key, Android refuses the new one. Uninstall the old copy first (its data is deleted), or update from the same source.</div>\n  <p><b>To undo:</b> uninstall the app from the Application Manager. Its data is deleted. <b>Privacy:</b> browsing contacts the catalog's site, and an install contacts the developer's download site; both can see your internet address (see <a href=\"#privacy\">Privacy</a>).</p>"},{"id":"tab-logcat","title":"Logcat Viewer","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Logcat is the phone's running diary. Android and every app write short notes into it all the time: what they are doing, warnings and errors. The Logcat Viewer lets you read it, filter it and save or share it, which is the best way to find out why an app crashes.</p>\n<p><b>Where to find it:</b> the tab named <b>Logcat Viewer</b>. An app's own menu also has a <b>Logs</b> button that opens this tab for that app (see <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>). For the log of another device, such as a watch, see <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>.</p>\n\n<h4>Before you start</h4>\n<p>Reading the log needs a <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a> (ADB, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root). Without one you see <i>Working Mode Required</i> (<a href=\"#mode-readonly\">Read-Only mode</a> cannot read it). Opening the tab starts reading right away.</p>\n\n<h4>How to read a line</h4>\n<p>Each entry is a row with a coloured square at the left holding one letter, the level, then:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the <b>tag</b> in bold: who wrote it (an app part or a system part, for example <i>AndroidRuntime</i>);</li>\n<li>the <b>time</b>, as hours, minutes, seconds and thousandths;</li>\n<li>two numbers such as <i>1234/1250</i>: the process id and the thread id (who ran the code; a process is one running program);</li>\n<li>the <b>message</b> underneath. A message of several lines, such as the list of steps in a crash, stays together as one entry.</li>\n</ul>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Level</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>V</td><td>Verbose</td><td>Every little detail. Very noisy.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D</td><td>Debug</td><td>Notes for developers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>I</td><td>Info</td><td>Normal events</td></tr>\n<tr><td>W</td><td>Warn</td><td>Something odd, probably not serious</td></tr>\n<tr><td>E</td><td>Error</td><td>Something failed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>F</td><td>Fatal</td><td>A failure so bad the program stopped</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>The controls</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Level drop-down</td><td>Starts at <b>Info</b>. Shows this level and every more serious one. <b>Error</b> shows E and F; <b>Verbose</b> shows everything.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lines drop-down</td><td>How many recent lines to read: 200, <b>500</b> (starting choice), 1000 or 2000. While Playing, up to 300 are read each second.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Filter box</td><td>Type a word or a tag and press Enter. Only lines that contain it (capital letters ignored, anywhere in the line) stay, and the word is highlighted.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>All apps</b></td><td>Opens <i>Log of one app</i>: a search box and a list, running apps first. Tap an app to see only the lines written by it. The button then shows the app's name and <b>Show all apps</b> appears to go back. An app that shares its user id with others shows their lines too, with a note.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Play</b> / <b>Pause</b></td><td>Play reads the log once a second and follows the newest line. It starts by itself when you open the tab. Pause stops it. It also stops when you leave the tab, and it does not read while the app is in the background.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Refresh</b></td><td>Reads once, and jumps to the newest line.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Clear</b></td><td>Empties the phone's log buffer and the screen (<i>Log cleared</i>). It clears it for every app, not only the one you chose.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Copy</b>, <b>Save</b>, <b>Share</b></td><td>Copy the lines as text; save them as a text file in <code>Download/ADB App Manager/</code> (on Android 9 and older, in the app's own folder instead), named <code>logcat_all_</code> plus the date and time, with the app's package name in place of <code>all</code> when you picked one app; or share that file with Android's share sheet. Lines of hidden levels are left out, and a short header says the filters used.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Scrolling up stops the view from jumping to new lines. Scroll back to the bottom to follow the log again.</p>\n\n<h4>The colour key</h4>\n<p>Above the log is a row of six chips, one per level, each with a coloured dot, its letter and name, and how many lines of that level were read: for example <i>E · Error 12</i>. Tap a chip to hide that level (it turns dim); tap again to show it. If you hide all of them, the screen says <i>Every level is hidden - tap the color key above to show some.</i></p>\n<p>To keep the screen quick, only the newest 800 entries are drawn. A line at the top says <i>N earlier entries not shown · tap to show them</i>; tap it to show up to 2500. The phone itself keeps only a limited amount of log, so older lines are gone.</p>\n\n<h4>Tap an entry to open it</h4>\n<p>Tap any entry (a coloured row) and it opens in a window of its own, so a long line is easy to read in full. Selecting text in a row with a long press still works and does not open the window. The window shows the level, the tag (the part of the system or the app that wrote it), the time and the process numbers, and the whole message in a box, with three buttons:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Copy</b>: puts the entry's lines, as they were logged, on the clipboard.</li>\n<li><b>More info</b>: asks the Coding Agent you chose in the <a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a> tab to explain the entry in plain words: what it means, the likely cause, whether it matters and what to try. The answer appears under the buttons as it is written, with a <b>Copy the answer</b> button. <b>This sends the entry's text (and your Android version and phone model) to that agent's company</b>; nothing is sent until you tap the button. API keys that appear in the text are shortened first; other private details (account names, addresses) are not, so look at the entry before you ask. With no agent connected, the window says so and offers to open the place where you set one up.</li>\n<li><b>Web search</b>: opens a search for the tag and the first line of the message in your browser. Long numbers and addresses are left out of the search so that other people's reports can match. It needs no agent.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Example: why does my app crash?</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open the app's menu (<a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>) and tap <b>Logs</b>, or open Logcat Viewer and use <b>All apps</b> to pick it. The log is now only that app's.</li>\n<li>Set the <b>Level</b> to <b>Warn</b> or <b>Error</b>, and <b>Lines</b> to 2000 so the crash does not scroll away.</li>\n<li>Optional: tap <b>Clear</b> so the log only holds what comes next. This empties the log for every app.</li>\n<li>Use the app until it crashes.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Pause</b>. Scroll to the end and look for red <b>E</b> or <b>F</b> rows. A crash of an app written in Java or Kotlin has a row <i>FATAL EXCEPTION</i>, and all its lines have the tag <i>AndroidRuntime</i>: type <code>AndroidRuntime</code> in the filter and press Enter to see just the crash. (Filtering for <i>FATAL EXCEPTION</i> itself would hide the lines under it.) A crash of native code shows <i>Fatal signal</i>.</li>\n<li>Read the lines under it: <i>Process:</i> names the app, the next line names the error (for example a null pointer), and <i>Caused by:</i> lines name the real cause. The lines starting with <i>at</i> are where in the code it happened.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Share</b> or <b>Save</b> and send the file to the app's developer.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> A log can hold personal details: account names, addresses, the names of your apps. Read it before you share it, or filter it to one app first.</div>\n\n<h4>If it fails</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><i>Working Mode Required</i> or <i>reading logcat needs ADB, Shizuku or Root</i>: set up a working mode.</li>\n<li><i>(no matching log lines)</i> or <i>(no output)</i>: lower the Level to Verbose, empty the filter, or choose <b>All apps</b>.</li>\n<li><i>(name is not running, so there are no log lines to show)</i>: on an older Android the app has to be running; start it.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"tab-taskmgr","title":"Task Manager","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">See what your phone is doing right now: the apps and processes that are running, how busy the processor is, how much memory is used, and the state of the graphics chip, the battery and the network.</p>\n<p><b>Where to find it:</b> the tab named <b>Task Manager</b>. Six buttons at the top choose the page: <b>Processes</b>, <b>CPU</b>, <b>RAM</b>, <b>GPU</b>, <b>Battery</b> and <b>Network</b>.</p>\n\n<h4>Before you start</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Page</th><th>Needs a working mode?</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Processes, GPU</td><td>Yes. Without one the page says <i>Needs ADB, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root</i> and a <b>Set Up</b> button opens <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working Modes</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>CPU, RAM, Network</td><td>Best with one. The tab's own note says Processes, CPU and network read from the shell. Without a mode the app tries to read CPU, RAM and network itself, which Android allows on some phones only, so a page may show nothing.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Battery</td><td>No. It works in <a href=\"#mode-readonly\">Read-Only mode</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The tab measures only while it is open. When you leave it, measuring stops.</p>\n\n<h4>The settings box</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Auto-refresh</b>: how often to measure: Off, 1 s, 2 s, <b>3 s</b> (starting choice), 5 s or 10 s. <b>Refresh now</b> measures once. A longer time uses less battery.</li>\n<li><b>Graph speed</b>: a slider from 150 to 1000 ms (starting at 300 ms). It only changes how often the graph is redrawn, not how often the phone is measured.</li>\n<li><b>Units</b> (only on the Battery page): temperature in <b>&deg;F</b> or <b>&deg;C</b> (starting at F), and current in <b>mA</b> or <b>µA</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The graphs keep the last 120 readings. Your choices are remembered.</p>\n\n<h4>Processes</h4>\n<p>A process is one running program. The list shows the busiest first. <b>Sort by CPU</b> (starting choice) orders by how much processor each uses; <b>Sort by memory</b> by how much memory each holds. The button <b>Apps first</b> next to them puts the processes that belong to an app (the ones with a package name and an X button) above the system and kernel processes, each group keeping the order of the sort; tap it again for the plain order. Your choice is remembered. A line above says how many processes were read (200 are shown at most).</p>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open <b>Processes</b>. Each row shows the app's name and a line such as <i>com.example.app • PID 4821 • 182 MB • 1.5% CPU</i>. PID is the process's number; the memory is what it holds in RAM now.</li>\n<li>Rows for system and kernel work (names that are not an app) have no button. Rows for apps have a round <b>X</b>. Tap it to <b>Force Stop</b> that app: <i>Stopped com.example.app</i>. There is no question first.</li>\n<li>The list refreshes by itself. An app that restarts at once (a messenger, a clock) comes back.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Force Stop ends the app at once: anything not saved in it is lost, and its alarms and notifications may stop until you open it again. Its data is not erased. Do not stop a system app you do not recognise (the launcher, the keyboard, the phone app): the phone may misbehave until Android starts it again. To undo, open the app again. See <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a> for the same Force Stop.</div>\n\n<h4>CPU</h4>\n<p>The CPU is the phone's processor. The big number is how busy it was since the last reading, over all its cores (a core is one processing unit). The first reading shows 0% because two readings are needed. Under the graph:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>One box per core with its own percentage.</li>\n<li><b>Processor Information</b>: SoC (the chip's name), Architecture, ABI (the kind of code it runs), Governor (the rule that picks the speed) and Temperature (<i>estimated</i>, from a sensor the app thinks belongs to the CPU). Under the tabs, the <b>Units</b> row has a <b>°F / °C</b> menu that picks the unit; the Battery tab uses the same choice.</li>\n<li><b>System Statistics</b>: Processes and Threads (only with a working mode) and Uptime (time since the phone started).</li>\n<li><b>Cluster</b> boxes: cores are grouped by speed range (fast cores and slow, thrifty ones). Each group shows <b>Min</b>, <b>Current</b> and <b>Max</b> frequency in MHz or GHz. A high Current close to Max means the group is working hard; it drops when idle.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>RAM</h4>\n<p>RAM is the working memory. The big number is the share in use; the line under it reads for example <i>Using 4.1 GB of 7.5 GB</i>. The boxes show:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Used</b>: total minus Available.</li>\n<li><b>Available</b>: what apps could get right now, counting cache that Android can give back. This is the number to watch.</li>\n<li><b>Swap used</b> and <b>Swap total</b>: extra memory made from storage or by compression.</li>\n<li>Under <b>Memory Information</b>: <b>Free</b> (memory doing nothing), <b>Buffers</b> and <b>Cached</b> (kept to speed things up), <b>Swap free</b>, <b>Swap cached</b> and <b>Page size</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Android keeps memory busy on purpose. A high Used and a small Free are normal. Worry only when Available gets very low and the phone is slow.</div>\n\n<h4>GPU</h4>\n<p>The GPU draws the picture on your screen. The big number is its load. Phones do not all expose it, so you may see <i>Not available on this phone</i>. Some show <i>~40% (by clock speed)</i>, a rough guess from the chip's speed. Under it, <b>GPU Information</b> says whether <b>Vulkan</b> is supported (and its API version) and the <b>OpenGL ES</b> version (Vulkan and OpenGL ES are the two ways apps draw graphics on the chip).</p>\n<p>The <b>GPU renderer</b> card is at the very top of the tab, above the graph, and shows in large letters which renderer is in use (<b>Default</b>, <b>OpenGL</b> or <b>Vulkan</b>). Its drop-down (working mode needed) offers <b>OpenGL</b>, and <b>Vulkan</b> if the phone supports it (plus <b>Default</b> until you pick one). Choosing one sets the system's drawing engine and restarts System UI (the status and navigation bars) to apply it, with a visible flicker. To go back, choose the other one, or restart the phone (a setting made this way normally does not survive a restart).</p>\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Change the renderer only if you know why, for example to test an app. Some apps and games behave differently or look wrong with the other engine.</div>\n\n<h4>Battery</h4>\n<p>The big number is the charge. Under it: the state (<i>Charging</i>, <i>Discharging</i>, <i>Not charging</i>, <i>Full</i>) and the source (<i>AC</i>, <i>USB</i>, <i>Wireless</i>). The boxes: <b>Temperature</b>, <b>Current</b>, <b>Voltage</b> and <b>Health</b> (Good, Overheat, Dead, Over voltage, Failure, Cold, or Unknown). Some phones show current as negative while the battery is in use, others as positive. <b>Battery Information</b> adds Technology, Source, Power in watts, Charge in mAh, <b>Capacity (estimated)</b>, <b>Time left (estimated)</b> (only while discharging) and <b>Charge cycles</b> (<i>N/A</i> unless Android 14 or later reports it). The estimates are rough.</p>\n\n<h4>Network</h4>\n<p>The big number is the speed now, down and up added together; the line under it shows each: <i>down 1.2 MB/s up 80 KB/s</i> (with arrows on screen). Below are the active connections, busiest first, for example <i>wlan0</i> (Wi-Fi) or <i>rmnet</i> (mobile data), then <b>Network Statistics</b>: <b>Total received</b> and <b>Total sent</b> since the phone started.</p>\n\n<h4>Why the numbers differ from other apps</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Each app measures at its own moments and over its own span, so figures never match exactly.</li>\n<li>The per-app CPU percentage comes from the phone's own <code>ps</code> command (the command that lists running programs), which may be an average over the whole time the process has run, so it can look low or high compared with what happens this second.</li>\n<li>RAM here counts what Android says is Available. Apps that report only Free show smaller numbers.</li>\n<li>Temperature is estimated from a sensor, and GPU load is not available on every phone.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>If it fails.</b> <i>No processes read</i>: connect a working mode, or press <b>Refresh now</b>. <i>Waiting for data...</i>: wait one refresh. <i>reduced ps output on this device</i>: this phone's process list lacks some columns, so the numbers may be missing. <i>Could not stop</i>: the app could not be stopped; try again from <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>.</div>"},{"id":"tab-about","title":"About","group":"The tabs","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The last tab. It tells you which version of the app you have, opens this guide, the project page and the release notes, lets you support the developer if you wish, and shows what your phone says about itself.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> the <b>About</b> tab, always the last one on the tab bar. Like Application Manager, it cannot be switched off or moved (see <a href=\"#feature-list\">Turning tabs on and off and moving them</a>).</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <p>Nothing here needs a working mode, a computer or an account. The buttons that open web pages (GitHub, Releases, PayPal) open them in your phone's browser, so they need an internet connection. The rest works offline.</p>\n\n  <h4>The top card</h4>\n  <p>The card shows the app's name, <b>ADB Application Manager Pro</b>, its version and build number (for example \"Version 7.10.0-Pro · build 820\"; yours will differ), and a one-paragraph description. Under it are the main buttons:</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Issues</b></td><td>Opens the project's issue page, <code>github.com/Bingblop/ADB-Application-Manager/issues</code>, in your browser. Report a bug or ask for a feature there, where other people can see it and add to it. It needs a free GitHub account to write; reading does not.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Contact the developer</b></td><td>Opens a small sheet to write to the developer by email. Type your message, leave <b>Add the app and phone details</b> on (it adds the app version, Android version and phone model, which makes a problem much easier to find; turn it off to send only your words), and tap <b>Send</b>: your email app opens with the message ready, the subject already set to <i>ADB App Manager</i>. <b>Copy message</b> copies the text instead. The message goes to a <b>contact alias</b> (an address that only forwards mail), so the developer's own address is never shown to you or kept in the app. If the phone has no email app, the sheet says so and you can copy the message. The alias is shown in the sheet (<code>bingblop.coral666@simplelogin.fr</code>), so you can also write to it from any email app. A build made without an alias says the contact address is not set up yet and points you to Issues.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>GitHub</b></td><td>Opens the project page, <code>github.com/Bingblop/ADB-Application-Manager</code>, in your browser. Source code, releases and the issue tracker are there.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Help Guide</b></td><td>Opens this guide in a sheet: a table of contents (the <b>Contents</b> button at the top brings it back), a search box, and every tab and button explained. After you follow a link inside the guide, a button <b>Back to where I was</b> shows for about 15 seconds and takes you back to the spot you left. The guide is English only, and it is loaded the first time you open it. Most other tabs, and Settings, have a small round <b>?</b> button on their first card that opens the guide at their topic; <b>Application Manager</b> and <b>Command-Line Interface</b> have none, so this button is how you reach the guide from them.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>What's new</b></td><td>Opens a sheet titled <b>What's New</b> with the release notes of the newest three versions. At the bottom, <b>Full changelog</b> opens the whole file on GitHub and <b>Got it</b> closes the sheet. The same sheet also opens once by itself after the app has been updated. If the build has no notes, a message says <i>No release notes in this build</i>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Permissions</b></td><td>Opens the <b>Permissions</b> sheet: All files access, Usage access, Display over other apps, Install unknown apps and, while a working mode is active, three more. Each row has an <b>Allow</b> button or shows \"Allowed\". Allow what you like and skip the rest. See <a href=\"#app-permissions\">App permissions</a>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Check for update</b></td><td>Jumps to the <a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a> tab, scrolled to the card that checks this app's own releases. The first visit checks by itself; if it was already checked, it checks again. If the App Updater tab is switched off, a message says <i>App Updater is turned off. Turn it on in Settings, Feature List.</i> and nothing else happens.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>To update the app, see <a href=\"#install-and-update\">Installing and updating</a>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Buy me a coffee</h4>\n  <p>The app is free, and the card says it stays that way. If it saved you time, you can give a small amount to the developer. The card says the app stays free and that giving is <b>totally optional</b>. It has:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Buy me a coffee · $1</b> opens PayPal's donation page in your browser for one US dollar.</li>\n    <li><b>or choose your own amount:</b> type an amount in the box with the <b>$</b> sign (the box suggests <code>5.00</code>) and tap <b>Donate</b>. You can type 5, 5.5, 5,50 or $ 12.00. Amounts from 0.01 to 100,000 with at most two decimals are accepted. If the amount is not valid, the box turns red and a message says <i>Enter an amount like 5 or 2.50</i>.</li>\n    <li>The money goes to the PayPal address <code>bingblop@yahoo.com</code>, in US dollars, as a one-time payment. PayPal lets you change the amount on its own page. <b>Copy the PayPal address</b> copies the address if you would rather send it yourself.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>The app does not handle any payment itself: it only opens the PayPal page in your browser, where you can check the address and amount and simply close the page if you change your mind.</p>\n\n  <h4>Developer &amp; project</h4>\n  <p>Shows the developer (<b>Bingblop</b>), the project address, and these buttons:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Releases</b>: the page with the latest APK files (the ones to install or update from).</li>\n    <li><b>Report a bug or idea</b>: opens GitHub's page for a new issue. You need a GitHub account to post. Before you report a problem, tap <b>Copy debug info</b> (below) and paste it into the report.</li>\n    <li><b>Star on GitHub</b>: the project page, where you can star it. <b>Bingblop on GitHub</b>: the developer's profile.</li>\n    <li><b>Share the app</b>: opens Android's share sheet with the app's name and the link to its latest release.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>This build</h4>\n  <p>The card says <i>Handy when you report a problem, or want to be sure the APK is the real one.</i> It lists:</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Version, Package, Build type</td><td>Version name and build number; the app's package name; \"release\" or \"debug\".</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Installed from</td><td>The store or app that installed it, or \"sideloaded (no store)\".</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Updated</td><td>When the app was last updated.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Android, Device, CPU, WebView</td><td>Your Android version and API level, the phone model, the processor type, and the version of the web engine that draws the app.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Working mode</td><td>The mode in use right now.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>Under the list, a line either says <b>Signed with the official release key</b> or <b>Not signed with the official release key (a debug, fork or modified build)</b>, followed by the signature's SHA-256 fingerprint (a long code that identifies the key the app was signed with). The official key's fingerprint begins <code>94:61:87:0F</code>. The second message is not automatically a danger (a copy you built yourself, or a fork, also shows it), but if you meant to install the official app, reinstall from the project's Releases page (see <a href=\"#install-and-update\">Installing and updating</a>). Three buttons: <b>Copy debug info</b> (a short text with version, package, Android, device, WebView, working mode and fingerprint), <b>Share it</b> (the same text through the share sheet) and <b>Copy fingerprint</b>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Device Specs</h4>\n  <p>Everything the phone will say about itself, <b>with no working mode needed</b>. It is read when the tab opens, and the <b>Refresh</b> button reads it again. A line appears only when your phone reports it. The groups are:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Hardware:</b> model, brand, chipset (SoC), CPU architecture, number of cores and clock range, RAM, Vulkan and OpenGL ES, screen resolution, density and refresh rate, storage free and total.</li>\n    <li><b>Software:</b> Android version, security patch, build ID, bootloader, kernel.</li>\n    <li><b>System:</b> uptime, language, time zone.</li>\n    <li><b>Battery:</b> level, status, power source, technology, health, charge cycles.</li>\n    <li><b>Network:</b> connection type and network interfaces.</li>\n    <li><b>Camera:</b> each camera, which way it faces, megapixels and flash.</li>\n    <li><b>Sensors:</b> the count, then each sensor's name and maker.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>If it says <i>Could not read the device specs</i>, tap <b>Refresh</b>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Handy to know</h4>\n  <p>A short list of tips: how Back works, press and hold, File Manager View, installs from File Manager, the Rish shell in the ADB Console, and the per-app log in Logcat. Each tip is explained in its own section of this guide.</p>\n\n  <h4>Privacy and Credits</h4>\n  <p>The <b>Privacy</b> card says: no account, no ads, no analytics and no server of the developer's; everything runs on your phone; the app goes online only for something you open or ask for (update checks, App Stores, the debloat list, and a VirusTotal lookup if you add your own key); the ADB key and the APK-signing key are made on your phone and never leave it. The full text is in <a href=\"#privacy\">Privacy</a>. The <b>Credits</b> card thanks the projects the app builds on; see <a href=\"#credits\">Credits</a>.</p>\n\n  <h4>If something does not work</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>A web button does nothing.</b> Your phone has no browser, or no internet. Check both.</li>\n    <li><b>Check for update only shows a message.</b> The App Updater tab is switched off. Turn it on in the gear (Settings), under <b>Feature List</b>.</li>\n  </ul>"},{"id":"prefs","title":"Settings (the gear)","group":"Settings and looks","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The Settings screen decides how the app looks and behaves: its language, light or dark, colours, font, app icons, the extra button on each app row, and which tabs are shown. Everything here changes only this app, not the rest of your phone.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> tap the <b>gear icon</b> in the header at the top right, to the left of the working-mode badge. Settings is not a tab: it has no place on the tab bar and cannot be switched off. The gear lights up while the screen is open. Press <b>Back</b> to return to the tab you came from.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>No permission, no computer and no working mode are needed. Every control works at once; there is no Save button, and what you choose is kept on the phone.</li>\n    <li>These settings are about this app only. The font, the colours and the language do not change the rest of your phone. (<b>Trim Caches</b>, which clears the caches of the other apps, was a card here until v7.10.10; it now lives in the <a href=\"#tab-sdm\">SD Maid</a> tab.)</li>\n    <li>The cards come in this order: <b>Language</b>, <b>Appearance</b>, <b>Palette Style</b>, <b>My Themes</b>, <b>Curated Theme Palettes</b> (with the <b>Granular Color Pickers</b> inside it), <b>Lists</b>, <b>Motion</b>, <b>Haptic feedback</b>, <b>Progress messages</b>, <b>Reconnecting devices</b>, <b>Font</b>, <b>Icon pack</b>, <b>Action Button</b>, <b>Feature List</b> and, last, <b>Press and hold guide</b>. Below, each card has its own part, with every control, its default and when to change it.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Not in this screen.</b> There is no switch here for keeping the screen on, for update checks, for backups or for logs. Update checks live in <a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a>, backups in the <a href=\"#apps-backup\">apps backup</a> section, logs in <a href=\"#tab-logcat\">Logcat Viewer</a>. The Command-Line Interface has its own gear (<a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a>), and the <b>Keep selection after running</b> switch is inside the batch panel (see <a href=\"#apps-batch\">batch actions</a>).</div>\n\n  <h4>Language</h4>\n  <p>A drop-down list, under the words \"The text of this app. Names of apps, files and commands are not translated.\"</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th><th>Default</th><th>When to change it</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Language</b> (drop-down)</td><td>Chooses the language of the app's buttons and messages. The list has English, Español, Français, Deutsch, Português (Brasil), Italiano, Русский, 简体中文, 日本語, 한국어, العربية, हिन्दी, Türkçe and Bahasa Indonesia. Arabic is shown right to left. App names, file names, commands and what Android itself prints stay as they are.</td><td>English</td><td>If you read another language more easily. This guide is English only and uses the English button names, so switch back to English if you cannot find a button it describes.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p><b>What you will see:</b> the whole app is redrawn in the new language and a message says <i>Language: ...</i>. If the language file cannot be loaded, a message says <i>That language could not be loaded. The text stays in</i> and the name of the language you had, and the list returns to that language.</p>\n\n  <h4>Appearance</h4>\n  <p>Light, dark, or follow your phone. Details and the colour rules are in <a href=\"#themes\">Themes and colours</a>.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th><th>Default</th><th>When to change it</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Light</b></td><td>Always light.</td><td>-</td><td>Bright rooms, or if dark is hard to read.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Dark</b></td><td>Always dark.</td><td>-</td><td>Dim rooms, or if you simply prefer it.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>System</b></td><td>Follows your phone's dark mode setting. A line under the buttons says what it is now, for example \"Following your phone's setting (currently Dark)\".</td><td>Selected</td><td>Leave it unless you want a fixed look.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Schedule</b></td><td>Switches by the clock. Two time boxes appear when it is selected.</td><td>-</td><td>If you want light by day and dark by night regardless of the phone.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Light from</b> and <b>Dark from</b> (time boxes, only with Schedule)</td><td>The time the app turns light, and the time it turns dark. It follows the clock while the app is open, and it works across midnight.</td><td>07:00 and 19:00</td><td>To match your own day. If both times are equal, the app stays light.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Pure black in dark mode</b> (switch)</td><td>Makes the dark backgrounds true black (#000000) for AMOLED screens, with cards lifted just enough to stay visible. It applies only while dark mode is active.</td><td>Off</td><td>On an OLED or AMOLED screen (a screen type where black pixels are switched off), to save some battery and get deeper blacks.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>Palette Style</h4>\n  <p>One <b>source colour</b>, nine ways to turn it into a whole theme: the accent, the background, the cards and the text, each in a light and a dark version. Tap a style card and the app is rebuilt from the source colour at once; a message says <i>Palette style: Vibrant</i>. The nine styles are the ones Android's own Material You uses, plus Fidelity, Content, Neutral and Monotone from Google's Material Color Utilities.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Tonal Spot</b></td><td>Calm and balanced: soft colours with a gentle tint. What Android uses by default. With Material purple as the source, this is the Material 3 baseline.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Vibrant</b></td><td>Strong, saturated colours with a clearly tinted background.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Fidelity</b></td><td>Stays as close to your source colour as it can, in its own strength. The third (tertiary) colour is the opposite one on the colour wheel.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Content</b></td><td>Like Fidelity, with a neighbouring colour for the third accent.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Neutral</b></td><td>Almost grey, with only a trace of your colour.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Expressive</b></td><td>Playful: the accents are turned far away from your colour.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Fruit Salad</b></td><td>A lively mix: the main colour is turned back 50° on the colour wheel and the accents are bold.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Rainbow</b></td><td>A strong accent on a plain grey background.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Monotone</b></td><td>Greys only, no colour at all. The green of Running and the red of Bloatware stay, because they tell you a state.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th><th>Default</th><th>When to change it</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Source color</b> (a circle and its code)</td><td>The colour every style starts from. Tap it for the colour popup (grid, or Custom with sliders and a hex box). Choosing a colour switches the style palette on.</td><td>Automatic: the main colour of your wallpaper on Android 12 and newer, otherwise Material purple (#6750A4). The line under <i>Source color</i> says which.</td><td>To get the same style in your own colour. A colour that is already vivid, such as #E91E63 or #009688, shows the styles best.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Automatic</b> (button)</td><td>Goes back to the automatic source colour. It shows only while you have picked a colour.</td><td>-</td><td>To follow the wallpaper again.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p><b>What you will see:</b> every tile is a small preview (accent, second accent, third accent and card colour) of that style for the source colour and for the mode you are in, so the previews change when you switch light and dark or pick another source. The style in use is highlighted and the line under the tiles says, for example, <i>Vibrant from #6750A4</i>. While a curated palette is in use the line says <i>Not in use</i>.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Choosing a style (or a source colour) clears your colour tweaks, like choosing a curated palette does. You can tweak the result with the <b>Granular Color Pickers</b> afterwards.</li>\n    <li>The text on the page is always easy to read: the styles keep text at least 7:1 against the background, and the accent at least 4.5:1, for any source colour, in light and in dark.</li>\n    <li><b>Light and dark</b> follow the <b>Appearance</b> card. <b>Pure black in dark mode</b> still applies on top.</li>\n    <li>The style, the source colour and the palette in use are remembered. Tapping a curated palette card takes over again.</li>\n    <li>The colours are calculated with the HCT colour space and tonal palettes of Google's Material Color Utilities. The third accent of Fidelity and Content is an approximation of Google's, so those two can differ a little from the same style in Android.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>My Themes</h4>\n  <p>Save the look you made under a name and switch between your themes with one tap. A theme holds the palette (a curated palette, Material You, or a palette style with its source colour), your <b>colour tweaks</b> for light and for dark, and <b>Pure black in dark mode</b>. It does <b>not</b> hold <b>Appearance</b> (Light, Dark, System, Schedule): that stays as you set it, so one theme shows in both its light and its dark version.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Save current theme…</b></td><td>Asks for a name (up to 40 characters; it suggests one such as <i>Vibrant #E91E63</i> or <i>Midnight Slate tweaked</i>) and saves the look in use now. A name that is already used, in capitals or not, asks <i>Replace it with the look in use now?</i>; the replaced theme moves to the top. Up to 30 themes: the 31st is refused with <i>Up to 30 themes: delete one first</i>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>A theme (tap it)</td><td>Applies it at once: the palette, source colour, tweaks and Pure black come back and a message says <i>Theme: ...</i>. The theme in use is highlighted, and the <b>Palette Style</b> card follows. The four swatches (accent, background, card, second accent) preview the theme in the light or dark mode you are in. Under the name a line says what it is, for example <i>Vibrant · #E91E63</i> or <i>Midnight Slate · tweaked · pure black</i>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Rename</b></td><td>Changes the name. Another theme's name (any case) is refused: <i>Another theme is already called ...</i>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Update</b></td><td>Asks, then puts the look in use now into that theme, keeping its name and place.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Delete</b></td><td>Asks, then removes the theme.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>Themes are kept on the phone with the other settings. A look made with a very old custom palette cannot be saved (the card says to choose a palette or a palette style first).</p>\n\n  <h4>Curated Theme Palettes</h4>\n  <p>Eight ready-made colour schemes. Tap a card to use it. Each has a light and a dark version, and the little strip of four swatches shows how it looks in the mode you are in now. The active card has a highlight.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Palette</th><th>What it looks like</th><th>Default</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Material 3</b> (badge M3 BASELINE)</td><td>Google's baseline purple scheme.</td><td>-</td><td>The fallback on Android 11 and older.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Material You</b> (badge M3 DYNAMIC • WALLPAPER)</td><td>Colours taken from your wallpaper, through Android's system palette.</td><td>Selected for new installs</td><td>Needs Android 12 or newer. On older phones the badge says <i>NEEDS ANDROID 12+ • USES M3 BASELINE</i>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>AMOLED Cyan</b></td><td>Cyan on black.</td><td>-</td><td>Made for OLED screens.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Cyberpunk Violet</b></td><td>Pink-red accent on a deep violet background.</td><td>-</td><td></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Nordic Emerald</b></td><td>Green accent on a very dark green.</td><td>-</td><td></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Sunset Amber</b></td><td>Orange accent on a dark brown.</td><td>-</td><td></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Midnight Slate</b></td><td>Blue accent on dark slate.</td><td>-</td><td></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Blood Moon</b></td><td>Red accent on black.</td><td>-</td><td></td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p><b>What you will see:</b> the colours change at once and a message says <i>Applied Theme: ...</i>. Choosing a palette also clears every colour tweak you made with the colour pickers below. On a phone older than Android 12, choosing <b>Material You</b> shows <i>Material You needs Android 12+. Using Material 3 baseline.</i></p>\n\n  <h4>Granular Color Pickers</h4>\n  <p>Under the palettes, a card for fine-tuning. <i>Tweaks apply to the current mode (light or dark)</i>: a colour you change in dark mode does not change light mode, and the other way round. Each row has a coloured circle and its code; tap it to open the <b>colour popup</b>:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>The hex box.</b> A <b>#</b> is already written in front of it, so you only type the six characters, for example <code>00DFFF</code>. Only the digits 0 to 9 and the letters A to F are accepted, and they always appear in capitals, even if you type small letters. You can also paste a code with or without a #. The preview above updates as you type. Under six characters the box turns red and says how many you have; <b>Apply</b> stays off until there are six.</li>\n    <li><b>Colors.</b> A grid of ready-made colours: tap one to put its code in the hex box.</li>\n    <li><b>Custom.</b> Three sliders, <b>Hue</b>, <b>Saturation</b> and <b>Brightness</b>, with the same hex box above them. Move a slider and the code changes; type a code and the sliders move. The two views share the colour, so you can switch between them.</li>\n    <li><b>Cancel</b> closes the popup and changes nothing. <b>Apply</b> sets the colour.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Picker</th><th>What it colours</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Primary System Accent</b></td><td>Buttons, active indicators and borders.</td><td>The main colour of the app.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>App Background</b></td><td>The page behind everything.</td><td>With <b>Pure black</b> on, the background in dark mode is black whatever you pick here.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Card Surface Color</b></td><td>Cards and dialog containers.</td><td></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Running App Badge</b></td><td>The marker for an app that is running in the background.</td><td></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Frozen App Badge</b></td><td>Disabled or suspended packages.</td><td></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>System App Badge</b></td><td>Core framework and maker system packages.</td><td></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Bloatware Badge</b></td><td>System apps that were uninstalled.</td><td></td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Reset tweaks</b> (button, shown only while the current mode has tweaks)</td><td>Removes your colour tweaks for the current mode (light or dark) and goes back to the palette's own colours. A message says <i>Color tweaks reset</i>.</td><td>Does not touch the other mode.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>The default of each picker is whatever the selected palette uses. There is no \"save\"; the tweak is kept as soon as you pick the colour.</p>\n\n  <h4>Lists</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th><th>Default</th><th>When to change it</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Remember my filters</b> (switch)</td><td>On: the Application Manager, the UAD-NG Debloater and Saved Applications open with the filters you left switched on. Off: the Application Manager always opens on <b>Total Installed</b>.</td><td>Off</td><td>Turn it on if you always look at the same slice, such as \"3rd Party\" apps. Leave it off if you want a fresh start every time (see <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>).</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>Motion</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th><th>Default</th><th>When to change it</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Expressive Animations</b> (switch)</td><td>Material 3 Expressive's bouncier, springier motion throughout the app, instead of a plain fade or slide. A message says <i>Expressive animations on</i> or <i>off</i>.</td><td>On</td><td>Turn it off if the motion bothers you or you prefer plain movement.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>Haptic feedback</h4>\n  <p>One switch, <b>Vibrate on taps</b>, <b>on by default</b>. While it is on, every tap on a button, a tab, a switch, a menu or a list row gives a very short vibration, so you feel that the tap was taken. A tap that already vibrated for another reason (a press and hold, for example) is still felt once, not twice. Turn it off and the app stays quiet; turning it on again gives one tick as proof. It is the app's own tick: the touch-feedback setting of your phone is not changed, and if your phone has its vibration switched off, you will not feel it either. Needs no permission.</p>\n\n  <h4>Progress messages</h4>\n  <p>One switch, <b>Say what is going on</b>, <b>on by default</b>. While an app is uninstalled, frozen, suspended, cleared and so on (from the app menu, the action button, a batch or the Connected Devices apps), the app shows small messages while it works: <i>Working on it&hellip;</i>, then <i>Checking the phone&hellip;</i> (or <i>Checking the device&hellip;</i>) while it asks the phone what became of the app. Turn the switch off if they are noise. What stays: the result (for example <i>Alpha: Uninstalled</i> or <i>Bravo: Still installed</i>), every problem and its result window, and the line on a batch's progress sheet.</p>\n\n  <h4>Reconnecting devices</h4>\n  <p>What the <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a> tab does when a device you were connected to drops. The same switch is under the <b>Not connected</b> cards in that tab.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th><th>Default</th><th>When to change it</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Reconnect on its own</b> (switch)</td><td>When a Wi-Fi, Wireless debugging or Bluetooth device drops while the tab is open, the app tries to bring it back without you pressing anything. A cable (USB) is never retried on its own. A message says <i>Reconnecting on its own: on</i> or <i>off</i>.</td><td>On</td><td>Turn it off if you take devices away on purpose and do not want the app to keep knocking.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Retry timings</b> (a box)</td><td>The seconds to wait before each attempt, separated by commas or spaces. <b>0, 8, 25</b> means: at once, again 8 seconds after that attempt and a last time 25 seconds later. Up to 8 attempts, each wait from 0 to 600 seconds (10 minutes); a decimal such as 0.5 is allowed. A line under the box repeats what is in use. When the box is not valid the line says why (<i>\"x\" is not a number of seconds</i>, <i>At most 8 attempts.</i>, <i>Each wait is 600 seconds (10 minutes) at most.</i>) and the timings you had stay.</td><td>0, 8, 25</td><td>A watch that needs longer to wake up: <b>0, 15, 45, 90</b>. A device that comes back fast: <b>0, 3</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Reset</b> (button)</td><td>Back to 0, 8, 25. It shows only while you use your own timings.</td><td>-</td><td>To undo your timings.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>The box is greyed out while <b>Reconnect on its own</b> is off. The timings count from the moment the device dropped, one attempt after the other, and the series stops at the first attempt that works. While the app is in the background the attempts wait for you to come back.</p>\n\n  <h4>Font</h4>\n  <p><b>The list of fonts found is for one visit.</b> What <b>Find fonts on this phone</b> finds is shown under the button, with a <b>Clear this list</b> link. The list is emptied when you leave Settings (and a search that finishes after you left is thrown away), so it does not sit in memory; search again when you need it. The font you chose stays.</p>\n  <p>The font of the text in this app only. A line shows what is in use: <i>In use: the system font</i>, or <i>In use: Name (file name, size)</i>.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th><th>Default</th><th>When to change it</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Use system font</b></td><td>Goes back to your phone's own font. It only shows while a custom font is in use.</td><td>System font in use</td><td>To undo a font you chose.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Find fonts on this phone</b></td><td>Searches your storage for <code>.ttf</code> and <code>.otf</code> font files (up to 12 MB each) and lists them. A progress bar and a count show while it works. A <b>Filter found fonts...</b> box appears when more than eight are found. Needs <b>All files access</b>; if it is not allowed, the app asks (see <a href=\"#app-permissions\">App permissions</a>).</td><td>-</td><td>When you have font files on the phone and want to see them.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Choose a file...</b></td><td>Opens Android's file chooser so you pick one font file by hand. Needs no extra access.</td><td>-</td><td>When you know where the file is, or do not want to give file access.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Tap <b>Find fonts on this phone</b> (or <b>Choose a file...</b>). If you used the search, tap a font in the list.</li>\n    <li>A sheet opens with a sample (\"Aa Bb 123\", a sentence and \"ADB Application Manager\") in that font, and the note <i>Only the text of this app changes; names, paths and commands in code-style boxes keep their own font.</i></li>\n    <li>Tap <b>Use this font</b>, or <b>Cancel</b> to look at another. A message says <i>Font: ...</i> and the whole app changes.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>If a file is not a usable font, a message says <i>Could not use that file</i> with a reason. If your chosen font cannot be loaded the next time the app starts (for example the file was damaged), the app uses the system font again and says so.</p>\n\n  <h4>Icon pack</h4>\n  <p>Draws the app icons in the lists with an icon pack you have installed (ADW, Nova, Apex and Go packs). Apps the pack does not cover get the pack's background and shape when it has one, and otherwise keep their own icon.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th><th>Default</th><th>When to change it</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Icon pack</b> (drop-down)</td><td>Lists <b>Default icons</b> and each icon pack found on the phone. Choosing one redraws the icons; a message says <i>Icon pack applied</i> or <i>Default icons</i>. If a chosen pack is uninstalled, the app falls back to default icons.</td><td>Default icons</td><td>If you use an icon pack and like the look in the app too. With none installed, only Default icons is offered.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Clear icon cache</b></td><td>The icons are drawn once and kept on disk. This throws the saved ones away so they are drawn again. A message says <i>Icon cache cleared, icons are being redrawn</i>, or that it was already empty.</td><td>-</td><td>If an icon looks wrong or old after you updated an app or changed pack.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>Action Button</h4>\n  <p>The extra button on each row of the Apps list and the Debloater. It is always an icon. You choose what a tap on it does, and, with a press and hold, which actions its sheet offers.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th><th>Default</th><th>When to change it</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Action Button</b> (drop-down)</td><td>Nine choices: <b>App Settings</b> (opens Android's settings page for the app), <b>Force Stop</b>, <b>App Launcher</b> (opens the app), <b>Enable / Disable (dynamic)</b>, <b>Install / Uninstall (dynamic)</b>, <b>Unsuspend / Suspend (dynamic)</b>, <b>Permission Manager</b>, <b>Activity Launcher</b> and <b>None (no button)</b>. The dynamic ones change their icon and action with the app's state: a frozen app shows Enable, an uninstalled app shows Reinstall, a suspended app shows Unsuspend. Uninstall asks <i>Uninstall name?</i> first. A line under the list previews the icon(s).</td><td>App Settings</td><td>Choose the action you use most. Choose <b>None</b> for plainer rows.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Hold menu</b> (list)</td><td>Press and hold the Action Button on a row and a sheet lists actions for that app. This list decides which actions the sheet offers and in what order. Each row has an up arrow, a down arrow and a switch. At least one action must stay on (otherwise <i>Keep at least one action in the hold menu</i>). Tapping an entry in the sheet runs it; the one that is also your Action Button is highlighted.</td><td>All eight on, in this order: App Settings, App Launcher, Force Stop, Enable / Disable, Install / Uninstall, Unsuspend / Suspend, Permission Manager, Activity Launcher</td><td>To remove actions you never use, or to put your favourites first.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Reset to Default</b></td><td>Puts the hold menu back to all eight actions in the default order. A message says <i>Hold menu reset</i>. It does not change which action the button itself performs.</td><td>-</td><td>If you made a mess of the order.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Force Stop, Disable, Uninstall and Suspend change real apps on your phone when you tap the button. If you keep one of them as the Action Button, a stray tap does something. App Settings (the default) is the safest choice. For the actions themselves see <a href=\"#apps-menu\">the app menu</a>.</div>\n\n  <h4>Feature List</h4>\n  <p>Which tabs are on the tab bar, and in what order. It has its own section, <a href=\"#feature-list\">Turning tabs on and off and moving them</a>.</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th><th>Default</th><th>When to change it</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>A <b>switch</b> on each of the 12 movable tabs</td><td>On: the tab is on the bar. Off: it is taken off the bar.</td><td>All on</td><td>To hide tabs you do not use.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The <b>up</b> and <b>down arrows</b> on each row</td><td>Move the tab one place earlier or later on the bar.</td><td>The default order</td><td>To put your favourite tabs first.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Reset to Default</b></td><td>Switches every tab on and puts them back in the default order. A message says <i>Tabs are back in their default order, all turned on</i>.</td><td>-</td><td>To start over.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>Press and hold guide</h4>\n  <p>This card has no controls. It lists what responds to a press and hold of about half a second (you feel a short tap when it works):</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>What you hold</th><th>Where</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>An app row</td><td>Apps</td><td>Opens that app's menu (the same as tapping the three dots). Tap the checkbox to select instead.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>An app's icon</td><td>Apps</td><td>Saves the icon as a PNG to Download/ADB App Manager/Icons.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The Action Button</td><td>Apps, Debloater</td><td>Opens the sheet with the actions you chose for the hold menu.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The round checkmark button (when apps are selected)</td><td>Apps</td><td>Clears every selection at once.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>A file or folder row</td><td>File Manager</td><td>Starts selecting. Tap more rows to add them, then Copy, Move or Delete.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>A file the APK search found</td><td>Installer</td><td>Deletes it from the phone, with an Undo right after.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>A setting that is 1 or 0</td><td>Hidden Settings</td><td>Flips it. See <a href=\"#tab-settings\">Hidden Settings</a>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>An overlay row</td><td>Overlays</td><td>Switches that overlay on or off. See <a href=\"#tab-overlays\">RRO/Monet Customization</a>.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>If something here does not work</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>I cannot find a button the guide mentions.</b> You may have chosen another language. Switch to English under <b>Language</b>.</li>\n    <li><b>A tab has disappeared.</b> It is switched off in the <b>Feature List</b>. Switch it on or use <b>Reset to Default</b>.</li>\n    <li><b>The colours look wrong.</b> Tap <b>Reset tweaks</b>, or tap a palette card to clear every tweak.</li>\n    <li><b>An app icon is missing or wrong.</b> Tap <b>Clear icon cache</b>, or pick <b>Default icons</b>.</li>\n    <li><b>The text is hard to read after a font change.</b> Tap <b>Use system font</b>.</li>\n    <li><b>I cannot find Trim Caches any more.</b> It moved to the <a href=\"#tab-sdm\">SD Maid</a> tab.</li>\n  </ul>"},{"id":"feature-list","title":"Turning tabs on and off and moving them","group":"Settings and looks","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The tab bar has fourteen tabs, and you may not use them all. The Feature List lets you take tabs off the bar and put the rest in the order you like.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> tap the gear icon in the header to open <a href=\"#prefs\">Settings</a>, then scroll to the card named <b>Feature List</b> (after the Action Button card).</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>No working mode and no permission are needed. Every change shows on the tab bar at once, and it is kept when you close the app and when you restart the phone.</li>\n    <li>The Feature List only decides what is <b>shown</b>. Switching a tab off does not delete anything: the tab's own settings and saved things stay where they are, and the tab comes back exactly as you left it when you switch it on again.</li>\n    <li>It affects the tab bar only. The <a href=\"#prefs\">Settings</a> screen is opened by the gear, is not a tab, and cannot be switched off, so you can always get back to this card.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>What stays fixed</h4>\n  <p><b>Application Manager</b> is always the first tab and <b>About</b> is always the last. They have no switch or arrows in the list because they cannot be turned off or moved. Everything between them can be switched off and moved.</p>\n\n  <h4>The list and the default order</h4>\n  <p>The card says: <i>Turn a tab off to take it out of the tab bar and use the arrows to put the tabs in the order you like. Application Manager and About always stay where they are.</i> Each of the twelve movable tabs has a row with its name, an up arrow, a down arrow and a switch. The first row's up arrow and the last row's down arrow are greyed out because they have nowhere to go. A row whose switch is off has a dimmed name. This is the default order, which is also what <b>Reset to Default</b> gives you:</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Name in the list</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td></td><td><b>Application Manager</b> (fixed, always first)</td><td><a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td>1</td><td><b>Saved Applications</b></td><td><a href=\"#tab-saved-lists\">Saved Applications</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td>2</td><td><b>UAD-NG Debloater</b></td><td><a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td>3</td><td><b>APK Installer</b></td><td><a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td>4</td><td><b>File Manager</b></td><td><a href=\"#tab-files\">File Manager</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td>5</td><td><b>Command-Line Interface</b></td><td><a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a> (with the ADB Console)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>6</td><td><b>Hidden Settings</b></td><td><a href=\"#tab-settings\">Hidden Settings</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td>7</td><td><b>RRO/Monet Customization</b></td><td><a href=\"#tab-overlays\">RRO/Monet Customization</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td>8</td><td><b>App Updater</b></td><td><a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td>9</td><td><b>App Stores</b></td><td><a href=\"#tab-store\">App Stores</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td>10</td><td><b>Logcat Viewer</b></td><td><a href=\"#tab-logcat\">Logcat Viewer</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td>11</td><td><b>Task Manager</b></td><td><a href=\"#tab-taskmgr\">Task Manager</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td>12</td><td><b>Connected Devices</b></td><td><a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a></td></tr>\n      <tr><td></td><td><b>About</b> (fixed, always last)</td><td><a href=\"#tab-about\">About</a></td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>On the tab bar, long names break into two lines (for example Hidden / Settings). The names in the list are the full names.</p>\n\n  <h4>Switch a tab off</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Tap the gear icon, then scroll to <b>Feature List</b>.</li>\n    <li>Tap the switch at the right end of the tab's row, so it turns off. The tab's name in the list turns dim.</li>\n    <li>Look at the tab bar under the header: the tab is gone. The tabs that were after it move up to take its place.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p><b>What happens to a tab you switch off:</b></p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>It leaves the tab bar at once, and Back no longer goes to it.</li>\n    <li>Anything inside the app that points at it does not open it. A message says, for example, <i>App Updater is turned off. Turn it on in Settings, Feature List.</i> and nothing else happens: no sheet closes and no download starts. Examples are <b>Check for update</b> in About (App Updater), the update button in an app's menu (App Updater) and <b>Logs</b> in an app's menu (Logcat Viewer).</li>\n    <li>One exception: if you open an APK file from outside the app (for example from your file manager or a download, choosing this app to install it), the app switches <b>APK Installer</b> back on by itself, because you asked for it. A message says <i>APK Installer was turned off. It is on again to open this file.</i></li>\n    <li>You can switch off every one of the twelve if you like. The bar then shows only Application Manager and About.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>Get a tab back</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open Settings with the gear icon and scroll to <b>Feature List</b>.</li>\n    <li>Turn the tab's switch on. It returns to the bar in the position its row has in the list.</li>\n    <li>If you cannot remember which tab you hid, or want everything as it was, tap <b>Reset to Default</b>.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>Move a tab</h4>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>In the Feature List, tap the <b>up arrow</b> on a row to move that tab one place earlier, or the <b>down arrow</b> to move it one place later.</li>\n    <li>The row moves and the tab bar follows at once. The screen scrolls so the row stays under your thumb, so you can tap the same arrow again to keep moving it.</li>\n    <li>Repeat for other tabs until the order suits you.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>A tab that is switched off still keeps its place in the list. If you switch it on again later, it returns to that place. Moving a tab never changes the position of Application Manager or About.</p>\n\n  <h4>Reset to Default</h4>\n  <p>The <b>Reset to Default</b> button under the list switches all twelve tabs on and puts them back in the default order shown above. A message says <i>Tabs are back in their default order, all turned on</i>. There is no question first, and it does not touch anything else in Settings.</p>\n\n  <h4>When the app is updated</h4>\n  <p>Your choice is checked against the tabs the new version has. A tab that no longer exists is dropped from your choice. A tab the update adds is placed after the tab that comes before it in the default order, so your saved order keeps working.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> If you use only a few tabs, hide the rest and put the ones you use most right after Application Manager. The tab bar then shows more of what you need at once, without sideways scrolling.</div>\n\n  <h4>If something goes wrong</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>\"... is turned off. Turn it on in Settings, Feature List.\"</b> You tried to open a tab that is off. Switch it on as described above.</li>\n    <li><b>A tab is missing and I do not know why.</b> Open the Feature List: a row with a dimmed name and its switch off is a hidden tab.</li>\n    <li><b>I pressed an arrow and the row did not move.</b> It is already first or last; its arrow is greyed out.</li>\n    <li><b>The order looks wrong after an update.</b> Tap <b>Reset to Default</b> and arrange it again.</li>\n  </ul>"},{"id":"themes","title":"Themes and colours","group":"Settings and looks","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Choose how the app looks: light or dark, one of eight ready-made colour schemes, one of nine palette styles built from a source colour of your choice, or your own colours. These choices change only this app, not the rest of your phone.</p>\n  <p><b>Where to find it:</b> tap the gear icon in the header to open <a href=\"#prefs\">Settings</a>. Five cards are about looks: <b>Appearance</b>, <b>Palette Style</b>, <b>My Themes</b>, <b>Curated Theme Palettes</b> and, inside the same card below the palettes, <b>Granular Color Pickers</b>.</p>\n\n  <h4>Before you start</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>No working mode, permission or computer is needed, and there is no Save button: each choice applies at once and is remembered.</li>\n    <li>The <b>Material You</b> palette needs Android 12 or newer. On older phones it falls back to the Material 3 baseline colours.</li>\n    <li>To change the colours of Android itself (the whole phone), see <a href=\"#tab-overlays\">RRO/Monet Customization</a> instead. This section is only about the app's own look.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>How the app chooses its colours</h4>\n  <p>Four things work together, in this order:</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li><b>The mode:</b> light or dark. <b>Appearance</b> decides which one is in use now.</li>\n    <li><b>The palette:</b> one of the eight cards, or a <b>palette style</b> (Tonal Spot, Vibrant, Fidelity, Content, Neutral, Expressive, Fruit Salad, Rainbow or Monotone) built from the source colour. Either way there is a dark version and a light version.</li>\n    <li><b>Your tweaks:</b> any colours you changed with the colour pickers. They are saved for one mode only, so a tweak made in dark mode does not change light mode.</li>\n    <li><b>Pure black:</b> if it is on, then in dark mode the backgrounds become black whatever the palette and your tweaks say.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>The text colour on buttons is chosen automatically, black or white, whichever is easier to read on the accent colour. The phone's status bar takes the colour of the app's background.</p>\n\n  <h4>Light, dark or system</h4>\n  <p>In the <b>Appearance</b> card, tap one of four buttons. The active one is highlighted, and a message says what you chose (for example <i>Appearance: dark</i>).</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Light</b></td><td>Always the light version.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Dark</b></td><td>Always the dark version.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>System</b> (the default)</td><td>Follows your phone's dark mode. When the phone switches, the app switches with it. A line under the buttons says, for example, <i>Following your phone's setting (currently Dark)</i>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Schedule</b></td><td>Switches by the clock. Two boxes appear: <b>Light from</b> (default 07:00) and <b>Dark from</b> (default 19:00). Tap a box and choose a time. The line under the buttons says, for example, <i>Light from 07:00, dark from 19:00 (currently Dark)</i>. It works across midnight and updates while the app is open. If you set both times the same, the app stays light.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p>With <b>Light</b> or <b>Dark</b>, the line under the buttons says <i>Dark mode • color tweaks are saved separately for light and dark</i> (or Light mode).</p>\n  <p><b>Pure black in dark mode</b> (a switch below the line): gives true black (#000000) backgrounds on AMOLED screens, which the card says saves battery. Cards and panels are lifted just enough to stay visible. If you switch it on while the app is light, a message says <i>Pure black applies whenever dark mode is active</i>. It is off by default.</p>\n\n  <h4>The eight palettes</h4>\n  <p>Under <b>Curated Theme Palettes</b> (\"Every palette has a light and a dark version\") there are eight cards. Each card shows a name and a strip of four small swatches: the accent colour, the background, the card colour and the system-app colour. The swatches are a preview of how that palette looks in the mode you are in now, so they change when you switch between light and dark. <b>They are not buttons of their own; tap the card.</b></p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Tap a card, for example <b>Midnight Slate</b>.</li>\n    <li>The app changes colour at once. The card is highlighted and a message says <i>Applied Theme: Midnight Slate</i>.</li>\n    <li>Any colour tweaks you made before are cleared, for both light and dark. The palette's own colours are used.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Palette</th><th>Where its colours come from</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Material 3</b> (M3 BASELINE)</td><td>Google's baseline purple scheme, with an official light version and an official dark version.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Material You</b> (M3 DYNAMIC • WALLPAPER)</td><td>The colours your phone builds from the wallpaper (Android 12 and newer), for both light and dark. The default for new installs.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>AMOLED Cyan</b>, <b>Cyberpunk Violet</b>, <b>Nordic Emerald</b>, <b>Sunset Amber</b>, <b>Midnight Slate</b>, <b>Blood Moon</b></td><td>Six dark schemes made for the app. Their light version is made from the dark one: lighter backgrounds, and the accent and badge colours darkened until they are easy to read on white.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <h4>My Themes</h4>\n  <p>The <b>My Themes</b> card (below Palette Style) saves a look under a name: <b>Save current theme…</b>, then tap a saved theme to wear it again. It keeps the palette or palette style, the source colour, your colour tweaks and Pure black, and leaves Light, Dark and System alone. Each theme can be renamed, updated with the look in use now, or deleted. See <a href=\"#prefs\">Settings</a> for every control.</p>\n\n  <h4>Palette styles</h4>\n  <p>The <b>Palette Style</b> card (above the eight palettes) builds a whole theme from <b>one source colour</b> in one of nine styles: <b>Tonal Spot</b>, <b>Vibrant</b>, <b>Fidelity</b>, <b>Content</b>, <b>Neutral</b>, <b>Expressive</b>, <b>Fruit Salad</b>, <b>Rainbow</b> and <b>Monotone</b>. The source colour is automatic (your wallpaper's main colour on Android 12 and newer, otherwise Material purple) until you pick one with the circle in the card. See <a href=\"#prefs\">Settings</a> for what each style does and for the controls.</p>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Open Settings (the gear) and find <b>Palette Style</b>.</li>\n    <li>Optional: tap the <b>Source color</b> circle and choose a colour, for example #009688.</li>\n    <li>Tap a style. The app changes colour at once, the tile is highlighted and a message says <i>Palette style: ...</i>.</li>\n    <li>Switch <b>Appearance</b> between Light and Dark to see both versions. Tap one of the eight palette cards to leave the style palette.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h4>Material You</h4>\n  <p>Material You is Android 12's way of building the phone's colours from one source colour, normally your wallpaper. When you choose this palette, the app borrows the phone's accent and neutral colours, so it matches the rest of the phone.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>When you change your wallpaper, or come back to the app after changing the system colours, the app reads the colours again and updates itself.</li>\n    <li>If you change the phone's colours with the <a href=\"#tab-overlays\">RRO/Monet Customization</a> tab, this app's Material You palette follows them too. Pick another palette if you want the app to keep its own colours regardless.</li>\n    <li>On a phone older than Android 12 the card's badge says <i>NEEDS ANDROID 12+ • USES M3 BASELINE</i>, and choosing it gives the Material 3 baseline with the message <i>Material You needs Android 12+. Using Material 3 baseline.</i></li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>Your own colours</h4>\n  <p>The <b>Granular Color Pickers</b> change single colours of the palette that is in use. The card says <i>Fine-tune the current palette. Tweaks apply to the current mode (light or dark).</i> Tap a picker to open the colour popup (a grid of colours, or Custom with hue, saturation and brightness sliders, and a hex box that starts with a # and takes capitals only; see <a href=\"#themes\">Themes and colours</a>).</p>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Picker</th><th>What it colours</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><b>Primary System Accent</b></td><td>Buttons, active indicators and borders.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>App Background</b></td><td>The page behind everything. Ignored in dark mode while Pure black is on.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Card Surface Color</b></td><td>App cards and dialog containers.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Running App Badge</b></td><td>The marker for apps that are running in the background.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Frozen App Badge</b></td><td>Disabled or suspended packages.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>System App Badge</b></td><td>Core framework and maker system packages.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Bloatware Badge</b></td><td>System apps that were uninstalled.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n    <li>Choose the mode you want to tweak first: <b>Light</b> or <b>Dark</b> in Appearance (with System or Schedule, tweak whatever mode is showing now).</li>\n    <li>Tap the coloured circle (or its code) in the row of the colour you want to change. A colour chooser opens.</li>\n    <li>Pick a colour and confirm. The app changes at once, and the circle and the code (such as <code>#00E5FF</code>) show the new colour.</li>\n    <li>Repeat for other colours. To tweak the other mode as well, switch the mode and do it again.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p><b>What you will see:</b> a <b>Reset tweaks</b> button appears at the top right of the pickers card while the current mode has tweaks.</p>\n\n  <h4>Undo a colour change and reset</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Reset tweaks</b> removes your tweaks for the mode that is showing. A message says <i>Color tweaks reset</i>. The other mode's tweaks stay.</li>\n    <li><b>Tap a palette card</b> (even the one already in use) to clear the tweaks for both modes.</li>\n    <li>There is no single \"reset everything\" button. To return to the starting point, set <b>Appearance</b> to <b>System</b>, tap the <b>Material You</b> card (this also leaves a palette style), switch <b>Pure black in dark mode</b> off and, if you used it, leave Schedule at its default times 07:00 and 19:00.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h4>Tips</h4>\n  <div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Pick the accent first, then the background. Keep the background and card colours clearly different from the text colour. Very light text on a very light background is hard to read.</div>\n  <div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> It is possible to pick colours that are hard to read, for example text-coloured cards on a similar background. Nothing is lost: tap <b>Reset tweaks</b> (top right of the pickers card), or tap any palette card to clear all tweaks. The gear icon in the header always stays reachable.</div>\n\n  <h4>If something looks wrong</h4>\n  <table class=\"hg-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>The app is dark and I chose Light</td><td>You may have System or Schedule selected, not Light.</td><td>Tap <b>Light</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pure black seems to do nothing</td><td>It only applies while dark mode is active.</td><td>Switch to Dark, or wait for the schedule to turn dark.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>My tweak vanished</td><td>Tweaks are kept per mode and per palette, and choosing a palette clears them.</td><td>Make the tweak again after choosing the palette.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Material You looks like Material 3</td><td>The phone is older than Android 12, so the app uses the Material 3 baseline (the card's badge says so).</td><td>Choose another palette.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The colours changed by themselves</td><td>Material You, System or Schedule is selected and the phone's colours or dark mode changed.</td><td>Choose a fixed palette and Light or Dark.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>"},{"id":"recipe-first-debloat","title":"Remove bloatware from a new phone, safely","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, your phone has lost a few pre-installed apps you never use, every step was reviewed before it ran, and you know how to put each one back.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a> that is ready: the badge at the top right is green or violet. Without one, the buttons below open a sheet called <b>Working Mode Required</b>.</li>\n<li>The <b>UAD-NG Debloater</b> tab, and internet the first time, to download the community list (about 1.6 MB).</li>\n<li>About an hour, and a battery above half.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li><b>Save your own things first.</b> Copy photos, contacts and messages to your cloud account or a computer. Removing an app deletes that app's own data.</li>\n<li>Open <b>UAD-NG Debloater</b>. If the status line says the list has not been downloaded, tap <b>Update List</b>. When it finishes, the line says how many packages the list knows (a package is one app or system component) and how many are on your phone.</li>\n<li>Leave only the <b>Recommended</b> chip on, and <b>On device</b> under State. In the row of brand chips, tap your phone's own brand (it comes right after <b>All brands</b>) to shorten the list.</li>\n<li>Tap a row to read its whole description. Skip any app you cannot explain, and any with an orange <b>Needed by</b> line that lists apps you use.</li>\n<li>Tap the circle on the left of three to five apps. Do not use <b>Select shown</b> on a long list.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Disable</b>, not <b>Uninstall</b>. A sheet called <b>Review: Disable Packages</b> lists every app it will touch. Nothing has happened yet. Read it, then tap the green button, for example <b>Disable 4</b>.</li>\n<li>A message says \"Running on 4 packages...\". Then <b>Debloater Results</b> shows <b>Success</b> or <b>Failed</b> for each app. <b>Failed</b> means the phone refused and nothing changed. Tap <b>Done</b>.</li>\n<li>Restart the phone and use it for a day: a call, a text, the camera, Wi-Fi, the keyboard, notifications. If all is well, do the next small batch.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>After a week without trouble, you may use <b>Uninstall</b> for the apps you are sure about. It uses the same review sheet. For a system app, neither Disable nor Uninstall frees much storage, because the file stays in the protected system area. What you gain is less background activity.</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Undo.</b> Open <b>History</b> (top card of the same tab) and tap <b>Undo</b> on the entry, or set State to <b>Disabled</b> or <b>Uninstalled</b>, tick the app and tap <b>Restore</b>. Never remove anything marked <b>Unsafe</b>, or the keyboard, launcher, phone, messages or Settings. If the phone will not start, see \"If the phone will not start\" in <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloating safely</a>.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a>, <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloating safely</a>, <a href=\"#apps-profiles\">Profiles and drift watch</a> (to be told when a system update brings apps back), <a href=\"#safety\">Safety</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-freeze-app","title":"Freeze an app, and bring it back","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, one app is switched off without anything being deleted, and you know the single tap that switches it back on.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>. In Read-Only mode, tapping <b>Freeze</b> opens <b>Working Mode Required</b> instead. That is the app protecting you, not an error.</li>\n<li>The <b>Application Manager</b> tab (the first tab).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Type a few letters of the app's name in the search box (it says \"Search by name or package\"). The list shrinks as you type.</li>\n<li>Tap the <b>...</b> button at the right end of its row, or press and hold the row for about half a second. A sheet slides up with the app's name, package name, version and a grid of buttons.</li>\n<li>Check the name at the top, then tap <b>Freeze</b>. There is no question first. A message at the bottom says \"Checking the phone&hellip;\" and then what the phone reports (\"Frozen\"), and the button turns into <b>Enable</b>.</li>\n<li>Close the sheet with the X. The row now has a <b>FROZEN</b> tag, the app has gone from your home screen and app drawer, and the <b>Frozen</b> count at the top went up by one. Tap the <b>Frozen</b> pill to see all frozen apps.</li>\n<li>To bring it back, use the <b>Frozen</b> pill to find the app, open its <b>...</b> menu and tap <b>Enable</b>. The <b>FROZEN</b> tag goes and the app returns exactly as it was, with its data and settings.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If Android refuses, you see \"Action reported a problem\" and a <b>Command Result</b> box with Android's own words. Nothing was changed. Some system apps are protected and cannot be frozen.</p>\n<p><b>A lighter pause:</b> <b>Suspend</b> in the same menu keeps the app enabled but greys out its icon and stops it opening. <b>Unsuspend</b> undoes it. To freeze many apps at once, see <a href=\"#apps-batch\">Selecting many apps and batch actions</a>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Undo.</b> <b>Enable</b> always works, and every Freeze is also listed under <b>History</b> in the <b>UAD-NG Debloater</b> tab, with an <b>Undo</b> button. Freeze only apps marked <b>USER</b> that you recognise until you have read <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloating safely</a>. Never freeze your keyboard, home screen, phone or messages app, Settings or System UI.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>, <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>, <a href=\"#safety\">Safety</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-uninstall-restore","title":"Uninstall a system app, and get it back","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, a pre-installed app is gone from your phone, and you have seen the ways to bring it back.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>, and the <b>Application Manager</b> tab.</li>\n<li>To know what \"uninstall\" means for a system app: it is removed for your user only. Its file stays in the protected system area, so it can come back. A factory reset, and sometimes a system update, also bring it back.</li>\n<li>For an app you installed yourself, the rules are different: see the last warning below.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Try <b>Freeze</b> first (<a href=\"#recipe-freeze-app\">Freeze an app, and bring it back</a>) and live without the app for a few days. Only go on if nothing broke.</li>\n<li>Find the app and open its <b>...</b> menu. Check that its row says <b>SYSTEM</b>. If a <b>UAD-NG</b> chip shows, tap it and read the advice. Stop if it says <b>UNSAFE</b>.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Uninstall</b>. There is no \"Are you sure?\" box. A message says \"Checking the phone&hellip;\" and then \"Uninstalled\". The button turns into <b>Reinstall</b>, the row gets an <b>UNINSTALLED</b> tag, and the <b>Bloatware (Uninstalled)</b> count goes up by one.</li>\n<li>Restart the phone and use it normally for a day.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>Getting it back</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Tap the <b>Uninstalled</b> pill (or the <b>Bloatware (Uninstalled)</b> box), open the app's <b>...</b> menu and tap <b>Reinstall</b>. The tag goes and the app returns as if freshly installed: its old data is gone and it may ask for permissions again.</li>\n<li>Other ways: in the <b>UAD-NG Debloater</b> tab set State to <b>Uninstalled</b>, tick the app and tap <b>Restore</b>; or open <b>History</b> there and tap <b>Undo</b> on the entry.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If the result says \"only root can delete system app for a particular user\", Android would not allow it from the shell. The app already tried a second way. Use <b>Freeze</b> instead, or switch to <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> For an app you installed yourself, <b>Uninstall</b> removes it for good and <b>Reinstall</b> cannot bring it back. To get it back you need a backup (<a href=\"#recipe-restore-backup\">Restore an app from a backup</a>) or its store. Make the backup before you uninstall (<a href=\"#recipe-backup-before\">Back up an app before you change it</a>).</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>, <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a>, <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloating safely</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-backup-before","title":"Back up an app before you change it","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, you have one backup file that holds an app, its permission choices and, with root, its data, saved in your Download folder.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>. The app uses it to read the app's permission settings, so Read-Only mode refuses.</li>\n<li>For the app's <b>data</b> (logins, saved games, settings) you need <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a> on the phone. The app asks your root manager itself, so your working mode does not have to be Root. Without root, the backup holds the app and its settings, not its data.</li>\n<li>One app at a time. There is no button to back up many apps at once. Make sure you have free storage.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>In the <b>Application Manager</b>, find the app and open its <b>...</b> menu.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Backup</b>. The menu closes and a box opens. At the top is a card, \"Back up (app name)\", with its package name and version.</li>\n<li>Look at the switch <b>Include app data</b>. With root it says \"Uses Root. The app is stopped while its data is copied.\" Without root it is greyed out and says \"Needs Root, which was not found on this phone.\" Switch it on only if you need the data, and do not use the app meanwhile.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Create backup</b>. A progress line and bar show \"Reading (name)...\" and \"Saving the backup...\".</li>\n<li>A note says \"Saved (name) ((size)) to (path)\". The file ends in <code>.adbbackup</code> and sits in <code>Download/ADB App Manager/Backups</code>, named like <code>Calculator_8.5_20261006-101530-123.adbbackup</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Read any warning under the note. For example, \"Some data changed while it was being read; the backup may be incomplete\". On Android 8 and 9 the file goes to the app's own storage instead, and it is removed if you uninstall this app, so tap <b>Share</b> in the backups list and keep a copy somewhere else.</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Good to know.</b> A backup changes nothing on your phone. It does not hold files the app keeps in shared storage, your online accounts, or secrets kept in Android's Keystore, so a restored app may ask you to sign in again. A data backup holds private information, so keep the file safe.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#apps-backup\">Backups and restore</a>, <a href=\"#recipe-restore-backup\">Restore an app from a backup</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-restore-backup","title":"Restore an app from a backup","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, the app is installed again from your backup file, with its permissions and app ops put back, and with its data if you chose that.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>, and a backup made by this app (a file ending in <code>.adbbackup</code>). See <a href=\"#recipe-backup-before\">Back up an app before you change it</a>.</li>\n<li>To restore the app's <b>data</b> as well, the backup must contain data and the phone must have <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>.</li>\n<li>The <b>Application Manager</b> tab, and its <b>Backups</b> button (in the row above the filter pills).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Tap <b>Backups</b>. The box lists every backup this app knows: the app name and version, the date, the size, and \"with data\" or \"APK + settings\".</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Restore</b> on the one you want. For a file that is not in the list (for example from another phone), tap <b>Choose backup file...</b> at the bottom and pick it. A file that is not a backup is refused with \"Not a usable backup\".</li>\n<li>A panel asks \"Restore (name) (version)?\" It says it installs the backed-up APK over the installed one, then puts back its permissions and app ops.</li>\n<li>If the backup has data and you have root, you may tick <b>Also restore app data</b>. It replaces the app's current data. Otherwise the box is greyed out and says \"(needs Root)\" or \"(none in this backup)\".</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Confirm restore</b>. A bar shows \"Reading the backup...\", \"Installing...\", \"Restoring permissions...\" and, with data, \"Restoring data (Root)...\".</li>\n<li>A note shows the result, for example \"(name): installed 8.5\" and \"Permissions 5 of 6 granted, 2 app ops restored\". The app list refreshes. An app you had deleted is back in it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If the same version is still installed, the install is skipped and only permissions, app ops and data are put back. A system app that was removed for you is brought back instead of installed.</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Android refuses an older version over a newer one, and an app signed with a different key. The note then says to uninstall the app first, which deletes the data it holds. Restoring data replaces the current data, so if that matters, make a fresh backup first. Restore only files you made yourself or got from someone you trust. <b>Delete</b> in the backups list removes the file at once, with no undo.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#apps-backup\">Backups and restore</a>, <a href=\"#recipe-new-phone\">Move to a new phone and set everything up again</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-update-apps","title":"Update the apps on your phone","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, you know which of your apps have a newer version, and the ones this app can update are updated, each checked before it installs.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Internet, and the <b>App Updater</b> tab.</li>\n<li>A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a> to install updates. Without one, a sheet called <b>Working Mode Required</b> opens. Only this app's own update can use Android's install screen.</li>\n<li>To know what is not covered: Google Play apps. This app cannot tell whether a Play app has a newer version.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open <b>App Updater</b>. The first time it checks by itself. Later, tap <b>Check for Updates</b> in the card <b>Updates Available</b>.</li>\n<li>Watch the status line: \"Checking sideloaded apps (GitHub, F-Droid, Obtainium)...\", then \"Checking the Galaxy Store...\". It ends with something like \"2 updates available &#8226; checked 12 Galaxy Store and 9 sideloaded apps\", followed by the time of the check. The tab's name shows the number in brackets. With none, the list says \"Everything is up to date.\"</li>\n<li>Each row shows the app, where the update comes from (for example <b>GitHub</b> or <b>Galaxy Store</b>), and the installed version, an arrow and the new version. Tap <b>Update</b>. The button changes to \"Downloading 40%\", then \"Installing\", then a tick and \"Updated\".</li>\n<li>For several, tap <b>Update All (N)</b> (it appears only when two or more apps can be updated) and confirm \"Update N apps?\". They go one after another. Waiting rows say \"Queued\".</li>\n<li>For this app itself, use the top card <b>App update</b>: <b>Check for app update</b>, then <b>Update now</b>.</li>\n<li>For Google Play apps, use the card <b>Play Store apps (via Aurora)</b>. <b>Open Aurora Store</b>, or tap <b>List installed apps</b> and then <b>Store</b> beside an app to open its store page. Or update them in the Play Store itself.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A <b>Release</b> button instead of <b>Update</b> means the newest release has no file for your phone. It opens the release page. Apps under <b>Not tracked</b> have no known source: tap <b>+ Set source</b> and enter a GitHub, Codeberg or F-Droid link.</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> An update replaces the working version, and there is no one-tap way back. Before it installs, the app checks that the file is the same package, newer, and signed with the same key, and installs nothing if a check fails. For an app that matters, make a backup first (<a href=\"#recipe-backup-before\">Back up an app before you change it</a>).</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a>, <a href=\"#tab-store\">App Stores</a>, <a href=\"#install-and-update\">Installing and updating the app</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-install-apk","title":"Install an APK file","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, the app inside your APK file is installed, after you have seen what the file is and who signed it.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The APK file on your phone (an APK is the file an app is installed from; see <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>), from a source you trust.</li>\n<li>The <b>APK Installer</b> tab. With a <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a> you get every option. Without one, set the Authorizer to <b>No privilege</b>: Android's own install screen then asks you to confirm, and first it may ask you to allow this app to install apps (the row <b>Install unknown apps</b> in <b>About</b>, <b>Permissions</b> allows that in advance).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open <b>APK Installer</b> and tap <b>Choose APK / APKS / APKM / XAPK</b>. Pick the file in Android's file chooser. (Other ways: tap a row in the list after <b>Find APKs on this device</b>; or tap the file in the <b>File Manager</b> and then <b>Install</b>; or open the file from your browser and choose this app.)</li>\n<li>The hint says \"Reading package...\", then \"Loaded APK - review and install below.\" The page scrolls to a card with the app's name, package name, <b>Version</b>, <b>Installed</b> (if it is already on the phone), <b>Min SDK</b> and <b>Size</b>.</li>\n<li>Read the signature line under it. Every app is signed by its maker with a key, like a seal, and an update must carry the same seal. \"Signature matches the installed app.\" is good. \"Signed with a different key than the installed app - Android will reject this as an update.\" means the file comes from someone else.</li>\n<li>Optional: in the <b>VirusTotal scan</b> card, paste your own free API key and tap <b>Scan this package</b>.</li>\n<li>Look at <b>Install options</b>. <b>Authorizer</b> (the working mode that carries out the install) is already your active mode. Leave <b>Block signature mismatch</b> and <b>Block unknown signature</b> on.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Install</b>. A message says \"Installing ...\", and a grey line under the button shows the command, for example <code>pm install -r (1 file)</code>.</li>\n<li>A sheet opens, titled \"Install\" with the package name. On success it offers <b>Launch Application</b> and <b>Application Settings</b>. Tap <b>Done</b>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Installing over an installed copy of the same app keeps its data.</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Undo.</b> Open the app's <b>...</b> menu in the <b>Application Manager</b> and tap <b>Uninstall</b>. Its data is deleted. An APK can do anything a normal app can, so install only files you trust. If the install fails with <code>INSTALL_FAILED_UPDATE_INCOMPATIBLE</code>, the installed copy has another signing key and only uninstalling it first fixes that, which deletes its data: back it up first. Other messages are explained in <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a>.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a>, <a href=\"#recipe-install-bundle\">Install a split bundle (APKS, APKM, XAPK)</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-install-bundle","title":"Install a split bundle (APKS, APKM, XAPK)","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, an app that came as several files is installed in one go, with only the parts your phone needs, and any game data is copied into place.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The bundle file on your phone: <b>.apks</b>, <b>.apkm</b> or <b>.xapk</b> (see <a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split packages</a> for what these are).</li>\n<li>A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a> (ADB, Shizuku or Root) if the bundle has game data: on Android 11 and newer, only they can copy data into <code>Android/obb</code>.</li>\n<li>Free space of up to twice the file size, and the <b>APK Installer</b> tab.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open <b>APK Installer</b>. Tap <b>Choose APK / APKS / APKM / XAPK</b> and pick the file. For an XAPK with game data you may tap <b>Install XAPK (APK + OBB data)</b> instead.</li>\n<li>The hint says \"Loaded APKS\" (or APKM, or \"Loaded XAPK with 2 game data files\"). More cards appear: the package card, <b>Splits</b>, <b>Game data (OBB)</b> if the file has some, and <b>Install options</b>.</li>\n<li>In <b>Splits</b>, the <b>Base APK</b> row is always on. Tap <b>Match this phone</b>. It ticks the base and the parts that fit: one processor part, one screen-density part and the languages of your phone. Rows say \"fits this phone (CPU arm64-v8a)\" or \"another CPU\". If no processor row fits, the bundle may not support your phone.</li>\n<li>With game data, keep <b>Copy data files after install</b> on. The app installs first and copies the data only if the install worked.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Install</b>. The grey line shows the command, for example <code>pm install-multiple -r (4 files)</code>. With game data, a line shows \"Copying data file 1 of 2: ...\". Keep the phone awake and plugged in: a big copy takes time.</li>\n<li>The result sheet says <b>Success</b>, and \"Game data (OBB): 2 of 2 data files copied\". Tap <b>Done</b>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If the app crashes at once, or the install says a split is missing, the app needs a part that was not ticked. Tap <b>Select all</b> and install again. If the install fails for another reason, the error table in <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a> says what to do.</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Undo.</b> Uninstall the app from its <b>...</b> menu in the <b>Application Manager</b>. Its data is deleted. Every part of a bundle must be signed with the same key. Keep the <b>Block</b> switches on.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split packages, APKS, APKM, XAPK and game data</a>, <a href=\"#recipe-install-apk\">Install an APK file</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-find-big-apps","title":"Find the apps that take the most space","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, you see your apps in order of size, biggest first, and you know what you can do about the big ones.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The <b>Application Manager</b> tab. Looking needs no <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>.</li>\n<li>Optional: <b>Usage access</b>. With it, the size counts the app, its data and its cache. Without it, the size is only the app's own file, and a message says \"Sorted by APK size. Allow usage access in the app menu to include data.\"</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>In the row above the filter pills, open the <b>Sort</b> menu and choose <b>Size</b>. A message says \"Measuring app sizes...\". The list is then biggest first, and each row shows its size after a dot, for example \"&#8226; 1.4 GB\".</li>\n<li>To include data and cache, open any app's <b>...</b> menu. The line under its name reads \"APK 12 MB\" and ends in a link, <b>Show data usage</b>. Tap it. With a working mode on, the app switches usage access on for you. Otherwise Android's Usage access screen opens: switch it on for this app and press Back.</li>\n<li>Close the app and open it again. The <b>Size</b> sort is remembered and the sizes are measured again. The line in an app's menu now reads like \"APK 12 MB &#8226; Data 340 MB &#8226; Cache 80 MB\".</li>\n<li>Tap the <b>3rd Party</b> pill to see only apps you installed yourself, or type in the search box.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>What you can do about a big app</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>An app you do not use:</b> <b>Uninstall</b> in its menu. Its data is deleted, so back it up first if it matters.</li>\n<li><b>An app whose data or cache is big:</b> the <b>App Info</b> button opens Android's own screen for the app, where its storage options are. <b>Clear Data</b> in the app's menu deletes logins and settings and cannot be undone. To clear only the cache of every app in one go, with no data lost, see <a href=\"#recipe-trim-caches\">Free up space by clearing every app's cache</a>.</li>\n<li><b>A system app:</b> Uninstall and Freeze free almost nothing, because the file stays in the protected system area.</li>\n<li><b>Big files rather than apps:</b> in the <b>File Manager</b>, search for <code>size:&gt;100mb</code> (see <a href=\"#files-search\">Searching files</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Sorting and measuring change nothing on your phone. But <b>Uninstall</b> and <b>Clear Data</b> in an app's menu change your apps (each asks you to confirm first). <b>Clear Data</b> cannot be undone, and <b>Uninstall</b> of an app you installed yourself cannot be undone either, except from a backup (<a href=\"#recipe-backup-before\">Back up an app before you change it</a>). Freeze is the undoable choice.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>, <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>, <a href=\"#app-permissions\">The permissions this app asks for</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-stop-app","title":"Stop an app that keeps running in the background","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, the app is stopped now and, if you choose, told not to run in the background again.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>. The <b>RUNNING</b> tag and the <b>Running</b> filter also need one.</li>\n<li>The <b>Application Manager</b> tab.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Step 1: stop it now</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Tap the <b>Running</b> pill. The list shows apps that have a live process, each with a <b>RUNNING</b> tag.</li>\n<li>Open the app's <b>...</b> menu and tap <b>Force Stop</b>. A message says \"Action executed\" and the <b>RUNNING</b> tag goes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Another way: open <b>Task Manager</b>, then <b>Processes</b>, and tap the round <b>X</b> on the app's row. It stops at once, with no question first.</p>\n\n<h4>Step 2: ask Android not to let it run in the background</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Close the menu and tap the app's row to select it. A round button with a number appears at the bottom right. Tap it, then tap <b>Batch Ops</b>.</li>\n<li>In the sheet <b>Batch Ops (App Ops)</b>, tap the preset <b>Stop background activity</b>. An app op is a small Android switch that says whether an app may do one thing, such as run in the background. Three app ops are ticked, each set to <b>Ignore</b>: <code>RUN_IN_BACKGROUND</code>, <code>RUN_ANY_IN_BACKGROUND</code> and <code>WAKE_LOCK</code>. The button at the bottom says <b>Apply to 1 app</b>.</li>\n<li>Tap it and confirm \"Set 3 app ops on 1 app?\". <b>Batch Results</b> opens and the app shows <b>Success</b>.</li>\n<li>Use the phone for a while, then look at the <b>Running</b> pill again.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Force Stop is only for now: the app can start again by itself, from an alarm or another app. How much the app ops hold back depends on the app and on your phone maker's own battery rules, and this has not been tried on every phone. If the app still comes back, <b>Freeze</b> is the firm answer (<a href=\"#recipe-freeze-app\">Freeze an app, and bring it back</a>).</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Undo.</b> Open the app's <b>...</b> menu and the <b>App Ops</b> tab. In the field \"Other op\", type one of the three names, choose <b>default</b> in the list beside it and tap <b>Set</b>. Repeat for the other two. Force Stop needs no undo: just open the app again. Do not stop a system app you do not recognise.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#apps-batch\">Batch Ops</a>, <a href=\"#tab-taskmgr\">Task Manager</a>, <a href=\"#quick-tiles\">Quick Settings tiles and widget</a> (a <b>Stop apps</b> tile stops a whole list in one tap).</p>"},{"id":"recipe-permission","title":"Take a permission away from an app","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, one app no longer has a permission you chose, for example the camera, and you know how to give it back.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>. Without one you can read the list, but the buttons show a padlock.</li>\n<li>The <b>Application Manager</b> tab.</li>\n<li>To know the limit: you can switch only <i>runtime</i> permissions, the kind Android asks you about (camera, location, contacts). Install-time permissions are fixed when the app is installed. They show a padlock and no one can change them.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open the app's <b>...</b> menu. It opens on the <b>Permissions</b> tab.</li>\n<li>Tap the <b>Changeable</b> chip, or type <code>camera</code> in the search box.</li>\n<li>Find the row, for example <b>CAMERA</b>. The button at the right says <b>GRANTED</b>. Tap it. It changes to <b>DENIED</b> and a message says \"Revoked CAMERA\".</li>\n<li>Android may close the app when you take a permission away. Open it and see how it behaves. If it needs the permission, it asks again.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For finer control, the <b>App Ops</b> tab has the buttons <b>Allow</b>, <b>FG</b> (only while the app is on screen), <b>Ignore</b>, <b>Deny</b> and <b>Reset</b> for each op. For many apps at once, <b>Batch Ops</b> has presets such as <b>No location</b> and <b>Privacy lockdown</b> (see <a href=\"#apps-batch\">batch actions</a>).</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Undo.</b> Tap the same button again: it changes back and a message says \"Granted CAMERA\". Permission changes made one by one are not in the History, so note what you changed. In <b>Batch Ops</b>, the preset <b>Undo Privacy lockdown</b> sets those ops back to <b>Reset</b>. A padlock on a <b>GRANTED</b> or <b>DENIED</b> button means you have no working mode right now. A padlock with <b>NOT GRANTED</b> means an install-time permission.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>, <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>, <a href=\"#safety\">Safety</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-hidden-setting","title":"Change a hidden Android setting, and undo it","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, you have changed one of Android's hidden settings, seen the effect, and put the old value back.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>. Without one, the <b>Hidden Settings</b> tab shows a card, \"Needs ADB, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root\", with a <b>Set Up</b> button.</li>\n<li>The <b>Hidden Settings</b> tab. The example here is the speed of window animations, <code>window_animation_scale</code> in the <b>Global</b> table. It is harmless, and 1 is the normal value.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open <b>Hidden Settings</b>. Grey bars show while the tables are read, then the buttons <b>Global</b>, <b>Secure</b> and <b>System</b> each show a count. Tap <b>Global</b>.</li>\n<li>Type <code>animation</code> in the box <b>Search settings...</b>. The list shrinks to the matches, with the matching letters highlighted.</li>\n<li>Tap the row <code>window_animation_scale</code>. A sheet slides up with its name, table, description and a <b>Value</b> box. First tap <b>Copy value</b>, to keep the old value on the clipboard.</li>\n<li>Replace the text in the <b>Value</b> box with <code>0.5</code>. A line under the box says what kind of value it looks like and what it was before. A red line means it cannot be saved yet.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Save</b>. The sheet closes, the row shows the new value with a small dot, and an <b>Undo</b> bar appears at the bottom for about 7 seconds. Open an app: its windows should now move quicker. Some phone makers' software ignores some settings.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>Undo it</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Within about 7 seconds, tap <b>Undo</b> on the bar. A message says \"Put back: window_animation_scale\".</li>\n<li>Later: tap <b>...</b> (the three dots next to the search box), then <b>Changes</b>. Each entry shows the old and new value. Tap <b>Revert</b> under the one you want to undo.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A setting with an orange edge and the word \"careful\" asks for confirmation. Some of them can cut the connection the app uses (<code>adb_enabled</code>, <code>wifi_on</code>, <code>airplane_mode_on</code>) or lock you out of the keyboard. Leave those alone. A setting that is 1 or 0 can also be flipped by pressing and holding its row.</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> The history only knows about changes made with this app, keeps the last 150, and is lost if you clear the app's data. There is no backup or restore of a whole table: before you experiment widely, use <b>...</b>, <b>Copy shown</b> and paste the text into a note. Some values are owned by the system or the phone maker and come back by themselves.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#tab-settings\">Hidden Settings</a>, <a href=\"#settings-journal\">Undoing settings changes</a>, <a href=\"#safety\">Safety</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-colors","title":"Change Android's colours (Monet and overlays)","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, the system screens and the apps that follow Material You (also called Monet) use a colour you picked, and you can go back with one tap.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Android 12 or newer for the colours. On older phones the tab opens on the <b>Overlays</b> half, <b>Apply theme</b> and <b>Default</b> are greyed out, and only overlays work.</li>\n<li>A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a> to change anything. The colours in use are shown without one.</li>\n<li>The <b>RRO/Monet Customization</b> tab. Phone makers differ: some add their own palette on top, so try one colour first.</li>\n<li>Save your work in other apps first: Android usually restarts apps when the colours change, this one included.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open <b>RRO/Monet Customization</b> and tap <b>Theme</b>. The top card, <b>Colors in use</b>, shows where the colours come from now, and five rows of squares (<b>Accent 1</b> to <b>Neutral 2</b>).</li>\n<li>Under <b>Color</b>, tap <b>Custom color</b> (<b>Wallpaper</b> is the default). A box appears with a big square and a code field.</li>\n<li>Pick the colour: type a six-digit code such as <code>6750A4</code>, move the <b>Hue</b>, <b>Saturation</b> and <b>Lightness</b> sliders, tap a recent colour, or open <b>Preset colors</b>, search by name and tap a round swatch.</li>\n<li>Under <b>Style</b>, choose one: <b>Tonal Spot</b> (the default), <b>Vibrant</b>, <b>Expressive</b>, <b>Fruit Salad</b>, <b>Rainbow</b> or <b>Spritz</b>.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Apply theme</b>. It reads \"Applying...\", then Android repaints within a second or two and may restart apps. An <b>Undo</b> bar saying \"Theme applied\" waits for about 7 seconds. If Android restarted this app, it comes back to this tab with the bar still waiting. The squares update a few seconds later.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>Overlays</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Tap <b>Overlays</b> (an overlay is a small package that changes how Android draws part of the system), and search by name or target. Tap the switch on a row, or press and hold the row.</li>\n<li>The list is read again and the row shows what Android reports. The <b>Undo</b> bar says \"On: name\" or \"Off: name\". An overlay marked <b>Unavailable</b> cannot be switched.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Undo.</b> Tap <b>Undo</b> on the bar within about 7 seconds, or tap <b>Default</b> and confirm \"Go back to the system default?\". Later, <b>Hidden Settings</b>, <b>...</b>, <b>Changes</b> lists <code>theme_customization_overlay_packages</code> with a <b>Revert</b>. A wrong overlay can make the screen harder to use: switch it off again in the <b>Overlays</b> half. If the colours look unchanged, your phone maker's palette wins. This tab does not change the app's own colours: see <a href=\"#themes\">Themes and colours</a>.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#tab-overlays\">RRO/Monet Customization</a>, <a href=\"#settings-journal\">Undoing settings changes</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-read-crash","title":"Find out why an app crashes","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, you have the app's crash report on screen, you know which lines give the cause, and you have a file to send to the developer.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>. Without one, the <b>Logcat Viewer</b> tab says <b>Working Mode Required</b>.</li>\n<li>The <b>Logcat Viewer</b> tab switched on (the <b>Logs</b> button in an app's menu opens it).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>In the <b>Application Manager</b>, open the crashing app's <b>...</b> menu and tap <b>Logs</b>. The <b>Logcat Viewer</b> opens showing only that app's lines. (Or open the tab, tap <b>All apps</b> and pick the app.) The tab starts following the log by itself: the green <b>Play</b> button turns orange and says <b>Pause</b>.</li>\n<li>Set the level drop-down to <b>Warn</b> or <b>Error</b> (the level is how serious a line must be to show), and the lines drop-down to <b>2000</b>, so the crash does not scroll away.</li>\n<li>Optional: tap <b>Clear</b>, so the log holds only what comes next. This empties the phone's log for every app, and it also pauses the live reading: the screen says \"(cleared)\" and the button says <b>Play</b> again.</li>\n<li>If the button says <b>Play</b>, tap it. Use the app until it crashes. The log follows the newest line while the button says <b>Pause</b>.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Pause</b>. Scroll to the end and look for red <b>E</b> or <b>F</b> rows. Type <code>AndroidRuntime</code> in the filter box and press Enter to see just the crash. A crash in native code shows \"Fatal signal\" instead.</li>\n<li>Read the lines. A row <b>FATAL EXCEPTION</b> starts the report. The line <b>Process:</b> names the app. The next line names the error, for example a null pointer. A <b>Caused by:</b> line gives the real cause. Lines starting with <b>at</b> say where in the code it happened.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Share</b> and send the text to the developer, or tap <b>Save</b>: the file goes to <code>Download/ADB App Manager</code> as <code>logcat_all_date_time.txt</code> (with the app's package name when one app is picked).</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> A log can hold personal details: account names, addresses, the names of your apps. Read it before you share it, and filter to one app first. <b>Clear</b> cannot be undone, but it only empties the log, not your apps. For a Wear OS watch use <a href=\"#recipe-device-log\">Read a watch's log</a>.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#tab-logcat\">Logcat Viewer</a>, <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>, <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-copy-files","title":"Copy, move or unzip files","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, your files are in the folder you wanted, or the contents of a zip file are unpacked next to it.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The <b>File Manager</b> tab. For your own storage you need no <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>, but you need <b>All files access</b>. If a box says \"Storage looks inaccessible\", tap <b>Grant storage access</b>, switch it on in Android's screen and press Back.</li>\n<li>For an SD card or USB drive, add it once with <b>+ Add storage</b> (<a href=\"#files-storage\">SD cards, USB drives and other storage</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Copy or move</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Tap a folder to open it. The row <b>..</b> goes up one level. Or type a path in the box under the title and tap <b>Go</b>.</li>\n<li>Press and hold a row for half a second, or tap <b>Select</b>. A bar appears, \"1 selected\", with <b>All</b>, <b>Copy</b>, <b>Move</b>, <b>Compress</b> and <b>Delete</b>. Tap more rows to add them.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Copy</b> (the original stays) or <b>Move</b>. The bar says \"3 items to copy: open the target folder, then tap Paste\".</li>\n<li>Open the target folder. The bar stays while you browse.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Paste here</b>. If a name is already used there, a box titled \"A name is already taken\" offers <b>Replace</b>, <b>Skip</b> or <b>Keep both</b>. A progress line shows with a <b>Cancel</b> button, and at the end a message says, for example, \"Copied 3 of 3\".</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>Unzip</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Tap the archive (zip, 7z, rar, tar and more), then <b>Extract...</b>. To look first without unpacking, tap <b>View contents</b>.</li>\n<li>Under <b>Extract into</b> choose <b>This folder</b>, <b>A new folder named after the archive</b> (the starting choice) or <b>Another folder</b>. Under \"If a file is already there\" choose <b>Replace it</b>, <b>Skip it</b> or <b>Keep both</b> (the starting choice).</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Extract</b>. A message says, for example, \"Extracted 12 files (3.4 MB) to /storage/emulated/0/Download/photos\".</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> There is no recycle bin. <b>Delete</b> is final, and so is <b>Replace</b>. To undo a copy, delete the copy. To undo a move, move the items back. The <b>Copy</b> and <b>Move</b> buttons in a single file's sheet ask for a destination path and replace a file of the same name without asking, so use <b>Select</b> for anything important. Keep away from <code>/system</code> and <code>/data</code>.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#tab-files\">File Manager</a>, <a href=\"#files-archives\">Zip, 7z, rar, tar and other archives</a>, <a href=\"#files-search\">Searching files</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-run-command","title":"Run an ADB command from the app","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, you have run a command on your phone from inside the app, with no computer, and read its answer.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a> for commands such as <code>pm</code> and <code>dumpsys</code>. Without one, the shell <b>This app (sandbox)</b> still runs file and text commands.</li>\n<li>The <b>Command-Line Interface</b> tab. Two big buttons at the top, <b>Terminal</b> and <b>ADB Console</b>, switch between its two panes. This recipe uses <b>Terminal</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open <b>Command-Line Interface</b> and tap <b>Terminal</b>. A black screen prints a welcome.</li>\n<li>Next to <b>Shell</b>, choose <b>Working mode (ADB, Shizuku, Root)</b>. The screen prints a line such as \"sh ready through ADB, running as the shell user (uid 2000)\". (A uid is the account number Android runs the command under. 2000 is the shell user: it can do a lot, but it is not root.)</li>\n<li>Tap the box at the bottom, type <code>getprop ro.product.model</code> and tap <b>RUN</b>. Your line is echoed, and under it comes the answer: your phone's model name.</li>\n<li>Try a command that lists the apps you installed: <code>pm list packages -3</code>. It prints one <code>package:</code> line for each.</li>\n<li>For ready-made commands, tap <b>Cheat Sheet</b>. It lists them in groups such as Device info, Packages and App control. Tap one: it lands in the input box with its placeholder, such as <code>&lt;package&gt;</code>, selected so you can type over it. Nothing runs until you tap <b>RUN</b>.</li>\n<li>To end a command that is still running, tap <b>STOP</b> (the <b>RUN</b> button turns into it). <b>Copy</b> under the screen copies the output.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>To run commands on <i>another</i> device, such as a watch, use the <b>Console</b> sub-tab of <b>Connected Devices</b> instead (<a href=\"#devices-console\">The device console</a>).</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> A command does exactly what it says, with the rights of the shell you chose, and most have no undo. <code>rm</code> deletes at once, and <code>pm clear</code> erases an app's data. Start with commands that only read (<code>list</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>ls</code>, <code>df</code>). Never paste a command you do not understand. Some pairs undo each other: <code>pm disable-user --user 0 name</code> is undone by <code>pm enable name</code>.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a>, <a href=\"#safety\">Safety</a>, <a href=\"#glossary\">Glossary</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-profile","title":"Save your setup and apply it again later","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, you have a named profile that remembers which apps you turned off, you can apply it again in one reviewed step, and the app can warn you when a system update undoes it.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The <b>Application Manager</b> tab. Saving needs no working mode. <b>Applying</b> needs a <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a>.</li>\n<li>To know what a profile holds: only apps that are <i>not</i> enabled (frozen, suspended or uninstalled). It does not hold permissions, app ops or settings.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>First freeze, suspend or uninstall the apps the way you like them.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Profiles</b> (in the row under the count boxes, next to <b>Backups</b>). A box called \"App Profiles\" opens. A line says \"Every app that is not enabled is saved.\" and how many apps that is. If you selected some apps first, it says \"Only the selected apps are saved.\"</li>\n<li>Type a name in the box \"Profile name (e.g. Lean Samsung)\" and tap <b>Save</b>. A message says \"Saved profile (name) ((number) apps)\". The card shows a summary such as \"12 apps, 8 disabled, 3 suspended, 1 uninstalled\".</li>\n<li>Later, open <b>Profiles</b> and tap <b>Apply</b> on the card. A preview says what will change, for example \"Will disable 4, suspend 2\", then how many already match and how many are not on this phone and are skipped.</li>\n<li>Read it and tap <b>Confirm apply ((number))</b>. The button is greyed out if nothing needs changing. A box shows each app and a <b>Stop</b> button, then <b>Profile Results</b> lists <b>Success</b> or <b>Failed</b> for each app.</li>\n<li>Optional: tap <b>Watch</b> on a profile. The button changes to <b>Watching</b>. After a system update brings apps back, a card at the top of the Application Manager says, for example, \"3 apps from \"Lean Samsung\" no longer match it\". Tap <b>Review</b>. Nothing is applied until you tap <b>Confirm apply</b>.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Undo.</b> Every run is written to the history: open <b>History</b> in the <b>UAD-NG Debloater</b> tab and tap <b>Undo</b>. A profile can disable and uninstall many apps in one go, including system apps, so read the preview every time. <b>Delete</b> on a profile removes it at once, with no question. Never apply a profile from someone else without checking its apps.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#apps-profiles\">Profiles and drift watch</a>, <a href=\"#recipe-new-phone\">Move to a new phone and set everything up again</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-after-reboot","title":"Get the app working again after a reboot","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, the badge at the top right is green (or violet for Root) again, and <b>Freeze</b> and the other buttons work.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>To know which working mode you use. Android switches off some of the ways this app reaches your phone when it restarts. The app does not start by itself, so it connects when you open it.</li>\n<li>Your frozen and removed apps stay as they were. A restart does not undo them. A system update can bring removed apps back: that is what a watched profile is for (<a href=\"#recipe-profile\">Save your setup and apply it again later</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open the app. The badge is orange and says \"(not ready)\" after the mode's name, or \"Setup Mode (Read-Only)\". The Application Manager shows the orange banner \"Active Mode: Read-Only (Unprivileged)\". Its <b>Set Up</b> button opens the setup sheet. The X in its top right corner only hides the banner until the next start.</li>\n<li>Tap the badge. The sheet <b>Working Modes &amp; ADB Setup</b> opens. The line under its title shows \"Selected\" and \"Active\". Find the card of your mode and read its tag.</li>\n<li>Do the part for your mode:\n<ul>\n<li><b>Root:</b> usually nothing. If the Root tag says <b>DENIED</b> or actions fail, tap <b>Request Root Access</b> and allow it in your root manager's prompt (<a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a> says what to do if it stays DENIED).</li>\n<li><b>Wireless Debugging:</b> your pairing is kept. In Android's Developer options switch <b>Wireless debugging</b> on again and read the new <b>IP address &amp; Port</b>. In the app's card type the port (or tap <b>Auto-Detect Ports</b>, or <b>Use My Wi-Fi IP</b> for the address) and tap <b>Connect Wireless ADB</b>.</li>\n<li><b>ADB over TCP:</b> port 5555 is closed again. On Android 11 or newer, connect with Wireless Debugging first, then tap <b>Switch to 5555 (tcpip)</b>. On Android 8 to 10, a computer must run <code>adb tcpip 5555</code> once more, then tap <b>Connect</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Shizuku:</b> open the Shizuku app and start it again the way you did before. The card then shows <b>AUTHORIZED</b> by itself, or tap <b>Authorize &amp; Use Shizuku</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Auto:</b> it uses the first of ADB over TCP, Wireless Debugging and Shizuku that is ready, and never Root. Get one of those ready, as above.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Close the sheet. The badge turns green, for example \"Wireless ADB (37099)\", the orange banner goes, and the list of apps reloads.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> The Quick Settings tile <b>Working mode</b> switches to the next ready mode without opening the app. Connecting only gives the app its power back. It changes none of your apps, and without a mode the app is simply Read-Only.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>, <a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP port 5555</a>, <a href=\"#mode-shizuku\">Shizuku</a>, <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>, <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-wear-debloat","title":"Remove apps you do not need from a Wear OS watch","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, an app you do not use is switched off on your watch, and you have a one-tap way to bring it back.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The watch connected in the <b>Connected Devices</b> tab with the badge <b>Connected</b>. If you have not done that yet, follow <a href=\"#devices-wear\">Wear OS, start to finish</a>. For the Wi-Fi way, this phone needs no working mode.</li>\n<li>The watch on its charger, and a few minutes. The reading of its apps can take up to 90 seconds.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open <b>Connected Devices</b> and pick the watch. Read the name in the device card: everything here happens on <i>that</i> device, not on this phone.</li>\n<li>Open the <b>Apps</b> sub-tab. The line above the list says \"Reading the apps of the device...\" until it is done. Tap <b>3rd Party</b> (apps added after the watch was made) or <b>System</b> (apps that came with it) to narrow the list, or type in <b>Search apps on the device</b>.</li>\n<li>Find an app you know. If its name means nothing to you, leave it alone and read <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloating safely</a>.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Disable</b> on its row. A message says, for example, \"Disabled: Fit\". The row gets a <b>DISABLED</b> badge and the button becomes <b>Enable</b>. To do several, tap the boxes on the left and use <b>Disable</b> in the bar that appears. It asks first when more than one is selected.</li>\n<li>Use the watch for a few days. If anything breaks, find the app with the <b>Disabled</b> filter and tap <b>Enable</b>.</li>\n<li>Only if nothing broke and you want it gone: tap the three dots, then <b>Uninstall</b>, and confirm. A removed system app shows <b>UNINSTALLED</b>, and <b>Reinstall</b> brings it back from the copy still on the watch. If the watch first answers that only root can remove a system app, the app sends a tiny helper file to it, runs it there and deletes it again by itself, so you have nothing extra to do (<a href=\"#devices-apps\">Managing the apps of the device</a> explains it). If that fails too, the sheet <b>That did not work</b> shows the watch's words: <b>Disable</b> the app instead.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> Never remove the watch face, <b>Settings</b>, the Google or Wear OS services, the keyboard, or the Bluetooth and phone-pairing apps. A watch that loses them may not work until a factory reset, which erases it. When unsure, <b>Disable</b>, and only that. If the device refuses, a sheet called <b>That did not work</b> shows its answer: leave that app alone.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#devices-apps\">Managing the apps of the device</a>, <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-wear-install","title":"Install an app on a watch","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, an app from an APK or bundle file on your phone is installed on your watch.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The watch connected in <b>Connected Devices</b>, showing <b>Connected</b> (<a href=\"#devices-wear\">Wear OS, start to finish</a>).</li>\n<li>A file made for Wear OS on this phone: an <b>.apk</b>, <b>.apks</b>, <b>.apkm</b>, <b>.xapk</b> or a zip of APKs, from a source you trust. The app cannot download watch apps for you: <b>App Stores</b> installs on this phone only.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open the <b>Send</b> sub-tab. The card <b>Send apps to this device</b> says \"Nothing chosen yet.\"</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Choose package files</b> and pick one or more files. Each becomes a row with its type and size. (To send an app this phone already has, tap <b>From this phone</b>, tick it and tap <b>Add to the list</b>. Those files are sent as they are. An app that this phone keeps in several parts says \"splits made for this phone, they may not fit the device\" in its row: it may not fit the watch.)</li>\n<li>Check the four switches. The defaults suit most files: <b>Replace an app that is already there</b> on, <b>Allow an older version</b>, <b>Grant every permission</b> and <b>Allow test apps</b> off.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Send to device</b>. A bar and a line show progress, for example \"1 of 3 &middot; face.apk &middot; Installing 2 files\". A bundle is trimmed to the parts the watch needs. The button turns into <b>Stop</b> while it runs. Tapping <b>Stop</b> ends the one that is running and makes the rest of the list fail with \"Error: stopped\".</li>\n<li>Each row ends with <b>Installed</b> in green, or the end of the device's error in red. A line says \"2 of 3 packages installed\" and a message says \"Sent to the device\" or \"Some packages did not install\".</li>\n<li>Open the <b>Apps</b> sub-tab and tap <b>Reload</b>. The app is in the list.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A red row quotes Android's own words. The table in <a href=\"#devices-send\">Sending apps to the device</a> says what each one usually means. The card <b>Send with Bluetooth</b> does not work for Wear OS watches: they do not accept shared files that way. Use <b>Send to device</b>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Undo.</b> In the <b>Apps</b> sub-tab, tap the app's three dots, then <b>Uninstall</b>. Install only files you trust: the app runs with the same powers as any other. A phone app may install but be unusable on a small round screen.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#devices-send\">Sending apps to the device</a>, <a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split packages</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-wear-density","title":"Make a watch's screen show more (or less)","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, the watch draws everything smaller, so more fits (or larger, so it is easier to tap), and you either kept the change or it went back by itself.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The watch connected in <b>Connected Devices</b>, and in your hand with its screen on, so you can see the result.</li>\n<li>To know the idea: <b>density</b> is measured in dpi. A lower number draws things smaller and shows more. A higher number draws them larger and shows less. Sharpness does not change.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open the <b>Display</b> sub-tab (the last one). The card <b>Screen density</b> says, for example, \"Now 320 dpi (the device's own).\"</li>\n<li>Tap <b>&minus;</b> a few times. Each tap moves the drop-down 5 lower, to about 20 or 40 below the value marked <b>(default)</b>, for example 280 or 240. For bigger things, tap <b>+</b>. Nothing is applied yet.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Apply selected</b>. The watch redraws at once and some apps restart.</li>\n<li>A yellow banner appears: \"The density is now 280 dpi. Keep it? It goes back by itself in 15 s.\" It has two buttons, <b>Keep</b> and <b>Put it back</b>, and counts down every second.</li>\n<li>Look at the watch. Tap <b>Keep</b> (a message says \"Kept\") if you like it. Tap <b>Put it back</b>, or do nothing, if not: when the countdown ends the app puts it back and says \"Put back\".</li>\n</ol>\n<p>To use a number the list does not have, type it in <b>Your own number</b> and tap <b>Apply number</b>. It accepts 72 to 1000, but stay inside the list's range of 120 to 640.</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Undo.</b> <b>Reset to default</b> puts the device's own density back at once, with no countdown. If the screen becomes unreadable, wait 15 seconds, or open the <b>Console</b> sub-tab and run <code>wm density reset</code>. The 15-second countdown runs inside the app's page on this phone: if the app is paused or closed, or the connection drops, it may not happen, so use the console. The change normally stays after the watch restarts. Leave the <b>Screen size</b> card alone unless you know why: a wrong size can cut off a round screen.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#devices-display\">Screen density and size</a>, <a href=\"#devices-console\">The device console</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-device-log","title":"Read a watch's log","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, you have read the watch's own log, found the lines about one problem, and copied or shared them.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The watch connected in <b>Connected Devices</b>, showing <b>Connected</b>. No working mode is needed on this phone for the Wi-Fi way.</li>\n<li>To know that the log is a short circular buffer: when it is full, the oldest lines are overwritten, so an old problem may be gone.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open the <b>Logcat</b> sub-tab. Opening it reads the log once by itself.</li>\n<li>Set the level drop-down (it starts at <b>Info</b>) to <b>Error</b>, and the lines drop-down to <b>1000</b>. Changing either reads the log again.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Clear</b>. A message says \"Log cleared on the device.\" Only fresh lines will show from now on.</li>\n<li>Make the problem happen on the watch, for example open the app that crashes.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Refresh</b>. A few red rows appear. (<b>Play</b> reads again every 2.5 seconds, but it keeps the watch's Wi-Fi busy and uses its battery: tap <b>Pause</b> when you have what you need.)</li>\n<li>Look for a row with the tag <b>AndroidRuntime</b> whose message starts with <b>FATAL EXCEPTION</b>. The lines joined under it are the crash report: <b>Process:</b> and the package name, the name of the error, and a <b>Caused by</b> line that is usually the real reason. Type the app's package name in the <b>filter text / tag</b> box to cut the noise. A native crash shows \"Fatal signal\".</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Copy</b>, or <b>Share</b> to send it as the file <code>device_logcat.txt</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This sub-tab is simpler than the <b>Logcat Viewer</b> tab for this phone: it has no per-app picker, no colour key to hide a level, no <b>Save</b> button and no 2000-line choice, and it draws at most 700 rows. Live reading stops by itself when you leave the sub-tab.</p>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> <b>Clear</b> empties the watch's log buffer, and the old lines cannot be brought back. A log can hold personal details: read it before you share it. If the area shows adb's complaint instead of rows, the watch dropped or went offline: fix the connection (<a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>) and tap <b>Refresh</b>.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#devices-logcat\">The device's log</a>, <a href=\"#recipe-read-crash\">Find out why an app crashes</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-new-phone","title":"Move to a new phone and set everything up again","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, the new phone has this app set up, the apps you used to turn off are turned off again, and the backups and lists you chose to carry over are in place.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The old phone, still working, and a way to send text and files to yourself (a message, a note or your cloud).</li>\n<li>A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a> on both phones for anything that changes apps. There is no \"transfer everything\" button, so this recipe moves the parts that can move. The table says which.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Moves?</th><th>How</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Which apps you turned off</td><td>Yes</td><td>A profile (copy and paste its text)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Saved lists</td><td>Yes</td><td><b>Copy</b>, then <b>New List</b> and paste</td></tr>\n<tr><td>App backups</td><td>Yes, with limits</td><td><b>Share</b>, then <b>Choose backup file...</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Batch Ops presets, saved commands</td><td>Yes</td><td><b>Export presets</b>, then <b>Import...</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hidden settings, colours, the app's own look</td><td>No</td><td>Do them again by hand</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>On the old phone</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>In the <b>Application Manager</b>, tap <b>Share CSV</b> and save the list of all your apps somewhere safe (a cloud drive, an email to yourself), a useful checklist.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Profiles</b>. If you have no profile yet, save one (<a href=\"#recipe-profile\">Save your setup and apply it again later</a>). Tap <b>Copy</b> on it and paste the text into a note or message to yourself.</li>\n<li>In <b>Saved Applications</b>, tap <b>Copy</b> on each list and paste it into a note.</li>\n<li>For apps whose data matters, tap <b>Backups</b>, then <b>Share</b> on each backup, and keep the files (<a href=\"#recipe-backup-before\">Back up an app before you change it</a>).</li>\n<li>If you made your own presets or commands: select any app, tap the round button, then <b>Batch Ops</b> and <b>Export presets</b>, or <b>Command</b> and <b>Export saved commands</b>. Send the files to yourself.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>On the new phone</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Install the app (<a href=\"#install-and-update\">Installing and updating the app</a>) and set up a working mode again. Pairing and the ADB key belong to each phone. When you open the app, the title at the top reads \"Full ADB/Root System Manager\". This guide is in the <b>About</b> tab, under <b>Help Guide</b>.</li>\n<li>Install your own apps first, from their stores. A profile skips apps that are not on the phone.</li>\n<li>Open <b>Profiles</b>, paste the text into \"Paste a shared profile here to import it\", tap <b>Import</b>, then <b>Apply</b>. Read the preview and tap <b>Confirm apply</b>.</li>\n<li>In <b>Saved Applications</b>, tap <b>New List</b>, paste the package names and tap <b>Save List</b>.</li>\n<li>To restore an app, tap <b>Backups</b>, then <b>Choose backup file...</b> and pick the file (<a href=\"#recipe-restore-backup\">Restore an app from a backup</a>).</li>\n<li>For presets and commands, open the same <b>Batch Ops</b> or <b>Command</b> sheet and tap <b>Import...</b>. Then set your hidden settings, colours and the app's own look by hand.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Phones differ. A profile from another make may name system apps this phone does not have (they are skipped) or ones that matter more here, so read the preview. A backup of a system app is refused on a phone whose own copy is signed with a different key, and app data can only be restored with Root. Do not copy hidden-setting values from one phone to another.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#apps-profiles\">Profiles and drift watch</a>, <a href=\"#apps-backup\">Backups and restore</a>, <a href=\"#tab-saved-lists\">Saved Applications</a>.</p>"},{"id":"recipe-trim-caches","title":"Free up space by clearing every app's cache","group":"How do I...?","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">At the end, the cache of every app on your phone has been cleared in one go, and you have seen how much free space it gave back. No app, setting, login or file is deleted.</p>\n\n<h4>What you need</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a> that is ready: the badge at the top right is green or violet. Without one, the button shows a padlock and tapping it opens a sheet called <b>Working Mode Required</b>.</li>\n<li>A minute or two. The app stays usable while it works.</li>\n<li>To know what a cache is: temporary files that an app keeps to save time, such as downloaded thumbnails, web pages a browser kept, map tiles and video that was only buffered. Apps make them again when they need them (see <b>cache</b> in the <a href=\"#glossary\">glossary</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Steps</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li>Open the <b>SD Maid</b> tab and find the card <b>Trim Caches</b>.</li>\n<li>Tap <b>Trim Caches in All Applications</b>. (The text under the button says what is trimmed and what is not.)</li>\n<li>A question opens: \"Trim the caches of every app now? Only cache files are deleted. Apps, their data, logins and your files stay as they are.\" Tap <b>OK</b>. <b>Cancel</b> does nothing.</li>\n<li>The button turns grey and reads \"Trimming caches&hellip;\". A line under it says this can take up to a minute or two. You can keep using the app.</li>\n<li>A box titled <b>Trim Caches in All Applications</b> opens. <b>Caches trimmed</b> with <b>SUCCESS</b> means it worked, and a line such as \"Free space on the phone: 120 GB before, 123 GB after (2.4 GB freed).\" tells you the result. If nothing more could be freed, the line ends \"(nothing more could be freed)\" instead. Close the box.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If the box says <b>It did not finish</b> with <b>FAILED</b>, Android refused and the words under it are its own. See <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>, \"Trim Caches says it did not finish\".</p>\n\n<h4>What to expect</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The button runs <code>pm trim-caches 128G</code>, the same as <code>adb shell pm trim-caches 128G</code> on a computer. It asks Android to delete app caches until the phone has 128 GB free. No phone has that much free space, so the target is never reached, and Android clears every cache it is allowed to clear, in every app. The order is Android's own choice.</li>\n<li>How much it frees depends on how much cache your apps hold. It can be a little or a lot, and the box shows the free space before and after.</li>\n<li>The first start of an app afterwards can be a little slower, and it may use mobile data to fetch images again.</li>\n<li>An app that keeps offline downloads in its cache folder (some music, video and map apps do) may need to download them again.</li>\n<li>Apps that are running right now may keep some of their cache until they close.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>What it cannot do</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It does not delete files, downloads, photos, videos or app data, and it uninstalls nothing. So it frees only what caches took up.</li>\n<li>For big amounts of space, find what is large and decide: <a href=\"#recipe-find-big-apps\">Find the apps that take the most space</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Undo.</b> There is no undo: a cache that was cleared cannot be put back. That is safe, because apps make it again by themselves, but an app with offline music, videos or maps in its cache may need to download them again. If that worries you, do it on Wi-Fi.</div>\n\n<p><b>Read more:</b> <a href=\"#prefs\">Settings (the gear)</a>, <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>, <a href=\"#recipe-find-big-apps\">Find the apps that take the most space</a>, <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>.</p>"},{"id":"safety","title":"Staying safe: what can go wrong and how to undo it","group":"Safety and help","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">This app can change things that Android normally protects. Almost every change can be undone, and a few cannot. This page tells you which is which, how to work in small safe steps, and what to do if the phone stops behaving.</p>\n\n<h4>The idea in a few lines</h4>\n<p>Android keeps apps apart from each other and from the system on purpose. A <a href=\"#modes-overview\">working mode</a> lets this app reach across that line, so it can switch off, remove or change things that Android's own Settings will not touch. The Debloater and the batch actions that remove or switch off apps show a list and ask first, and the app keeps a history with Undo for the common changes. But the buttons in one app's menu act at once, and the app cannot know which apps <i>your</i> phone needs. You decide, so it pays to go slowly. Looking never harms anything: reading the app list, permissions, manifests and logs changes nothing. Trouble starts only when you change something.</p>\n\n<h4>The four golden rules</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li><b>Change little.</b> Touch a few things at a time, and start with the safest: apps marked <b>USER</b>, apps the <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a> rates <b>Recommended</b>, a setting you can explain. If you cannot say in one sentence what an app or a setting does, leave it alone. Prefer the gentlest tool: <b>Force Stop</b>, <b>Suspend</b> and <b>Freeze</b> delete nothing, and Suspend and Freeze are undone with one tap. <b>Uninstall</b> and <b>Clear Data</b> are the heavy ones.</li>\n<li><b>Back up first.</b> Copy your photos, contacts and messages somewhere else, the way you normally do. In the app, make a <a href=\"#apps-backup\">backup</a> of an app before you remove or clear it, and save a <a href=\"#apps-profiles\">profile</a> or a <a href=\"#tab-saved-lists\">saved list</a> so you remember what you changed. Before you edit a <a href=\"#tab-settings\">hidden setting</a>, use <b>Copy value</b> to keep the old value. Know your screen lock and Google account password: after a factory reset you will need them.</li>\n<li><b>One change at a time.</b> Change one app, one setting, one overlay or one density step, then look at the phone. If you change five things and something breaks, you will not know which did it. Keep batches short (three to five apps) and read the confirmation list.</li>\n<li><b>Test, then wait.</b> After a change, restart the phone and try what you use every day: a call, a text, the camera, Wi-Fi and mobile data, Bluetooth, the keyboard, unlocking, notifications, an alarm, charging. Live with the change for a day before the next one, because a problem can show up later.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Freeze first, uninstall later. A frozen app keeps its data, costs one tap to bring back, and shows you whether anything breaks. For a system app, neither action frees much storage (its file stays on the protected system part), so there is rarely a reason to hurry.</div>\n\n<h4>What can go wrong, and how to undo it</h4>\n<p>\"How likely\" is a rough guide for someone who follows the four rules, not a promise: the same action can be harmless on one phone and a problem on another.</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>How likely to cause trouble</th><th>How to undo</th><th>Read more</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Force Stop</b>, <b>Suspend</b> or <b>Freeze</b> an app you installed</td><td>Very unlikely. Nothing is deleted; unsaved work in a stopped app is lost.</td><td>Force Stop needs no undo: open the app again. For Suspend or Freeze, tap <b>Unsuspend</b> or <b>Enable</b> in the app's menu or in a batch, or use Undo in the History.</td><td><a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Freeze</b> (disable) a system app</td><td>Unlikely for an app you can explain. Possible for a core part: a missing feature is usual, and a vital part can cause a boot loop.</td><td>The same ways. Android's own Settings, Apps, can often switch it on too.</td><td><a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloating safely</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Uninstall</b> a system app (for your user)</td><td>Possible. Its data for you is deleted, and a vital one can cause a boot loop.</td><td><b>Reinstall</b>, <b>Restore</b>, or Undo in the History. A factory reset also brings it back.</td><td><a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloating safely</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Uninstall</b> an app you installed</td><td>Possible. It goes with its data, and Reinstall cannot bring it back.</td><td>Install it again; restore a backup if you made one.</td><td><a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Clear Data</b>, or restoring app data</td><td>Likely to hurt if the app held things you need. On a contacts, messages or phone app it can delete what is kept only on the phone.</td><td>No undo, except a backup that included the data (that needs Root).</td><td><a href=\"#apps-backup\">Backups</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Rem Updates</b> (takes a system app's updates off), a <b>permission</b> or <b>app op</b>, disabling a <b>component</b></td><td>Unlikely to possible. The app may stop working or close.</td><td>Tap the same button again, press ENABLE, or update the app from its store.</td><td><a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Batch actions</b> and applying a <b>profile</b></td><td>Higher: one wrong tick reaches many apps.</td><td><b>Stop</b> ends a batch (apps not reached yet stay as they were). <b>Undo</b> in the Debloater's History covers Freeze, Enable, Suspend, Unsuspend, Uninstall and Reinstall. Clear Data, Force Stop and typed commands cannot be undone.</td><td><a href=\"#apps-batch\">Batch actions</a>, <a href=\"#apps-profiles\">Profiles</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Hidden Settings</b></td><td>Unlikely for ordinary ones. Real for those marked \"careful\": they can cut the connection this app uses or lock you out of the keyboard or screen.</td><td>The Undo bar (about 7 seconds), then <b>Changes</b> and <b>Revert</b>. If locked out, use the phone's own Settings, or a computer (below).</td><td><a href=\"#tab-settings\">Hidden Settings</a>, <a href=\"#settings-journal\">Undoing settings changes</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Overlays</b> and system colours</td><td>Unlikely to possible. The wrong overlay can make the screen harder to use.</td><td>The Undo bar, <b>Default</b>, or switch the overlay off.</td><td><a href=\"#tab-overlays\">RRO/Monet Customization</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Screen <b>density or size</b></td><td>Unlikely on a connected device (it undoes itself after 15 seconds unless you tap Keep). Real on this phone typed in a terminal: no safety net, and it normally survives a restart.</td><td><b>Put it back</b>, <b>Reset to default</b>, or <code>wm density reset</code> and <code>wm size reset</code>.</td><td><a href=\"#devices-display\">Screen density and size</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Commands</b> typed in a terminal or console</td><td>From none (commands that only read) to severe (<code>rm</code>, <code>pm clear</code>, <code>settings put</code>, <code>reboot</code>). No undo.</td><td>The opposite command, if there is one. Otherwise only a backup.</td><td><a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Coding agents</b></td><td>Depends on what you approve. What you type goes to the provider.</td><td>File changes: <code>/undo</code>. Commands cannot be undone.</td><td><a href=\"#terminal-agents\">Coding agents</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Delete</b> in the File Manager</td><td>No recycle bin. Serious in system folders: the app refuses to delete a storage's root or a top-level system folder, but the files inside them are not protected.</td><td>Only a copy made earlier.</td><td><a href=\"#tab-files\">File Manager</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Installing an <b>APK file</b></td><td>Possible: an APK can do whatever an app can. The Block switches stop look-alikes.</td><td>Uninstall it. Keep both Block switches on.</td><td><a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Reboot to recovery</b>, <b>Reboot to bootloader</b> or <b>Power off</b> on a connected device</td><td>Low, but this phone cannot bring that device back.</td><td>Restart the device by hand.</td><td><a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a></td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a> raises every risk above, because each action then runs with full rights. Do not root a phone just to use this app: Wireless Debugging or Shizuku does most of the same work on Android 11 and newer.</div>\n\n<h4>If the phone will not start: a boot loop and the recovery ladder</h4>\n<p>A <b>boot loop</b> is when the phone restarts again and again, or sits on its logo, and never reaches the home screen. It happens when something Android needs at start-up is missing or broken. With this app, removing or disabling a vital system app is the usual cause. It is rare if you follow the four rules, and you will know which few apps to blame.</p>\n<div class=\"hg-danger\"><b>Risky.</b> If the phone is looping, stop. Do not try random things or keep removing apps. Work down this ladder one step at a time and stop at the first step that works. The steps are in the order of what they cost you.</div>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What to do</th><th>What it loses</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>1. Undo the last change</b></td><td>If the phone starts, or reaches the home screen even for a moment, switch back on what you changed: <b>Enable</b>, <b>Reinstall</b>, <b>Restore</b>, Undo in the History. Android's own Settings can switch a frozen app on too.</td><td>Nothing.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2. Restart</b></td><td>Hold the power button until the phone switches off, wait ten seconds, switch it on.</td><td>Nothing.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3. Safe mode</b></td><td>Starts the phone with only the apps that came with it. How to start it differs by maker: often you hold Volume Down while the logo shows, or press and hold <b>Power off</b> on the power menu. If it starts, an app you installed is probably the problem: remove it in Android's own Settings, then restart normally.</td><td>Nothing is deleted. It does not undo a removal, and this app does not run there.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>4. Recovery mode: wipe cache partition</b></td><td>With the phone off, hold your maker's key combination to open the recovery menu, and look for \"wipe cache partition\". Not every phone has it.</td><td>Only temporary system files. It does not bring back a removed app, so it rarely cures this, but it costs nothing to try.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>5. A computer</b></td><td>If debugging was on beforehand and the phone answers adb: <code>adb shell pm install-existing PACKAGE</code> brings back a removed app, <code>adb shell pm enable PACKAGE</code> a frozen one.</td><td>Nothing. A phone stuck on its logo often does not answer.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>6. Factory reset (last resort)</b></td><td>In the recovery menu choose the entry that wipes data or resets to factory settings.</td><td>Everything on the phone: installed apps and their data, photos and files not copied elsewhere, messages, accounts, settings, and every change this app made. It puts back system apps you removed for your user, because they never left the system part. Afterwards you need your screen lock and account passwords.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>7. The maker's own software</b></td><td>Reinstalling the official software for your exact model from a computer, with the maker's tool (flashing).</td><td>Usually everything. Steps and risks differ by maker, and this guide does not cover them.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> Look up now, while the phone works, how to start safe mode and recovery mode on your exact model. The key combinations differ and are hard to find in a panic.</div>\n\n<h4>Things that need a computer to fix</h4>\n<p>A computer helps only if you prepared for it: <b>USB debugging</b> must have been on before the trouble, and the phone must already trust the computer (the \"Allow USB debugging?\" question, answered with Always allow). The computer needs Google's SDK Platform-Tools, which contain <code>adb</code> (<a href=\"https://developer.android.com/tools/releases/platform-tools\">download page</a>; <a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP</a> has the first steps). If you plan big changes, do both first, and switch USB debugging off afterwards.</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Run on the computer</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>ADB over TCP is off after a restart (Android 8 to 10)</td><td><code>adb tcpip 5555</code></td><td>On Android 11 and newer, use Wireless Debugging instead.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A removed system app or a frozen app must come back</td><td><code>adb shell pm install-existing PACKAGE</code> or <code>adb shell pm enable PACKAGE</code></td><td>The phone must start far enough to answer.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The screen is too small or too large</td><td><code>adb shell wm density reset</code> and <code>adb shell wm size reset</code></td><td>A restart alone will not fix it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A hidden setting locked you out</td><td><code>adb shell settings put TABLE NAME OLDVALUE</code>, or <code>settings delete TABLE NAME</code> for one you created</td><td>TABLE is global, secure or system. This is why you copy the old value first.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>An overlay made the screen hard to use</td><td><code>adb shell cmd overlay disable OVERLAYNAME</code></td><td>The overlay's detail sheet has <b>Copy command</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The phone will not start and adb does not answer</td><td>Nothing from this app</td><td>Steps 6 and 7 of the ladder.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Before a phone update</h4>\n<p>A system update replaces parts of Android. It can bring back apps you removed, switch frozen apps on again, and leave you with working modes that must be set up again after the restart.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Save what you did.</b> In <a href=\"#apps-profiles\">Profiles</a>, save a profile and tap <b>Watch</b>. After the update the app tells you if apps came back. <b>Review</b> shows what would change, and <b>Confirm apply</b> removes them again. <b>Share</b> sends the profile as a file you can keep somewhere safe.</li>\n<li><b>Be careful with Advanced and Expert removals.</b> Nobody can promise how a maker's update copes with apps removed for your user. Before a big update (a new Android version), the cautious choice is to <b>Restore</b> anything removed above the Recommended level, and remove it again once the update works.</li>\n<li><b>Back up.</b> Copy your own data off the phone, and keep the app backups you care about (in <code>Download/ADB App Manager/Backups</code>).</li>\n<li><b>Charge and free space.</b> Keep the battery above half, preferably plugged in, with several gigabytes free.</li>\n<li><b>Expect to set up the working mode again.</b> After the restart ADB over TCP is off, Shizuku has stopped and the Wireless Debugging address may have changed. On a rooted phone an update may remove root: follow your root manager's instructions first.</li>\n<li><b>Afterwards,</b> check the <b>Frozen</b> and <b>Bloatware (Uninstalled)</b> counts at the top of the Application Manager, and the card that says apps no longer match your watched profile.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>What never to do</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Do not remove, freeze or clear the data of the parts the phone needs</b>, unless you know the consequences and have a replacement: the system interface (System UI, <code>com.android.systemui</code>), your launcher, the phone and dialer, contacts storage and messages, your keyboard, Settings, the permission controller and package installer, Google Play services and the Play Store (<code>com.android.vending</code>) if you use Google apps, the Android System WebView, and anything for emergency alerts, the SIM, mobile data, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, fingerprint or face unlock and the camera. Clearing the data of a contacts or messages app deletes what it keeps on the phone.</li>\n<li><b>Never remove an app rated Unsafe</b>, and treat a \"Needed by\" line that names apps you use as a stop sign. Do not use <b>Select shown</b> on a long list you have not read.</li>\n<li><b>Do not paste a command you do not understand</b>, even from a friendly website, and do not press <b>Always allow in this chat</b> for a coding agent unless you accept whatever follows.</li>\n<li><b>Do not apply a profile, list or command preset from someone else</b> without reading it.</li>\n<li><b>Do not switch off the two Block switches</b> in the APK Installer, or tick \"Always allow\" for an ADB fingerprint you do not recognise.</li>\n<li><b>Do not change <code>adb_enabled</code>, <code>adb_wifi_enabled</code>, Wi-Fi or airplane mode</b> in Hidden Settings while the app uses ADB over that connection. You can cut yourself off.</li>\n<li><b>Do not delete in system folders</b> (<code>/system</code>, <code>/data</code>, <code>/vendor</code>) with the File Manager, or type extreme density or size numbers.</li>\n<li><b>Do not leave a door open.</b> Switch Wireless debugging off when you do not need it, and restart after using ADB over TCP: the port listens on your Wi-Fi while it is open.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>When something goes wrong</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li><b>Stop.</b> Do not make another change.</li>\n<li><b>Undo the last thing you did,</b> using the table above. Read Android's own message first: it is in a <b>Command Result</b> box.</li>\n<li><b>Look in <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a></b> for the exact words on the screen, or in the <a href=\"#faq\">questions and answers</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Ask for help</b> with the details collected at the end of <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>.</li>\n</ol>"},{"id":"troubleshooting","title":"Troubleshooting: it does not work","group":"Safety and help","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Find the area that matches your problem, then the words you see on the screen. Each row says what is usually going on and what to do. Nothing here needs a computer unless it says so. The last parts say how to reset the app and what to collect when you report a problem.</p>\n\n<h4>Three quick checks first</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li><b>Look at the badge</b> at the top right. Green or violet means a working mode is ready. Orange means it is not, and anything that changes an app will not work yet (<a href=\"#screen-tour\">A tour of the screen</a>).</li>\n<li><b>Read the exact words.</b> When something fails, a sheet stays open with Android's own answer, which usually says what is wrong. Search this page for those words.</li>\n<li><b>Close the app and open it again.</b> Swipe it away from your recent apps and start it. This reads the app list, the mode and the connection again, which clears many stale screens. Nothing is lost.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>It says Read-Only, or buttons are locked</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Orange badge \"Setup Mode (Read-Only)\" and an orange banner</td><td>No working mode is ready. The app can look and cannot change.</td><td>Tap the badge, or <b>Set Up</b>, and pick a mode (<a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A sheet <b>Working Mode Required</b></td><td>The button you tapped changes something and needs a mode.</td><td>Tap <b>Set Up Working Mode</b>. Nothing was changed.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Badge orange, ending \"(not ready)\"</td><td>You chose that mode but it is not connected now: Wireless Debugging went off with the Wi-Fi, Shizuku stopped, the phone restarted, or (for Root) your root manager has not allowed this app.</td><td>Open the badge sheet, read the tag on that card, finish the setup (below).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A padlock on a button</td><td>No working mode. On an install-time permission the padlock is permanent.</td><td>Set up a mode. Install-time permissions stay locked.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The orange banner has gone</td><td>You tapped its X (it returns at the next start), or a mode is on.</td><td>Nothing is wrong.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>It worked yesterday, orange today</td><td>A restart switches off ADB over TCP and stops Shizuku, and Wireless Debugging gets a new port.</td><td>See the table in <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>. Root survives a restart.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Wireless Debugging will not pair or connect</h4>\n<p>It needs Android 11 or newer and Wi-Fi. The <b>pairing</b> address and code go in step 1, the <b>connect</b> address (IP address &amp; Port on the main Wireless debugging screen) in step 2. Mixing them up is the commonest mistake (<a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>).</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Pairing fails, or \"Wrong password\"</td><td>The code is wrong, or the pairing window closed and the code expired.</td><td>Open <b>Pair device with pairing code</b> again and keep it visible: split screen, a pop-up window, or <b>Pair via Notification</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"failed to connect\", or refused</td><td>The port is old (it changes each time Wireless debugging is switched on), you used the pairing port, it is off, or the Wi-Fi differs.</td><td>Read <b>IP address &amp; Port</b> again and type it in step 2. <b>Auto-Detect Ports</b> can fill it in.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Target ... is not ready (check the authorization prompt on the device)\"</td><td>Android is waiting for you to approve.</td><td>Accept the prompt or notification, then tap <b>Connect Wireless ADB</b> again.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wireless debugging switches itself off</td><td>The phone is not on Wi-Fi. It does not work over mobile data.</td><td>Join a Wi-Fi network (no internet needed). Another phone's hotspot often works.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>No <b>Wireless debugging</b> in Developer options</td><td>Android 10 or older.</td><td>Use <a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP</a> or <a href=\"#mode-shizuku\">Shizuku</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Green, then orange after a restart or Wi-Fi change</td><td>Android switched it off or chose a new port. The pairing is kept.</td><td>Switch it on, read the new address, connect. No new pairing.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>It asks to pair again</td><td><b>Regenerate Key</b> or cleared storage made the old pairing useless, or Android forgot it.</td><td>Pair again (step 1).</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>ADB over TCP will not connect</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>\"Connection refused\", \"failed to connect\"</td><td>Nothing listens on port 5555: the one-time step was not done, or the phone restarted.</td><td>Repeat Way A or Way B in <a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"...not ready (check the authorization prompt...)\"</td><td>An \"Allow USB debugging?\" question is waiting.</td><td>Approve it and tap <b>Connect</b>. If none appears, use <b>Revoke USB debugging authorizations</b> in Developer options, then retry.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The prompt's fingerprint differs from the app's</td><td>Something else is connecting.</td><td>Tap Deny.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tag <b>PORT 5555 OPEN</b> in orange</td><td>The phone listens, but the app is not connected, often an approval pending.</td><td>Tap <b>Connect</b>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Shizuku stopped, or root was denied</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Tag <b>NOT INSTALLED</b></td><td>No Shizuku app was found on the phone.</td><td>Install Shizuku, start it, then tap <b>Authorize &amp; Use Shizuku</b> (<a href=\"#mode-shizuku\">Shizuku</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tag <b>NOT RUNNING</b>, or \"Shizuku is not running...\"</td><td>Shizuku stops whenever the phone restarts, unless started with root and set to start itself.</td><td>Open Shizuku, start it the same way as before, then tap <b>Authorize &amp; Use Shizuku</b> (<a href=\"#mode-shizuku\">Shizuku</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tag <b>RUNNING (UNAUTHORIZED)</b></td><td>Shizuku runs but has not approved this app.</td><td>Tap <b>Authorize &amp; Use Shizuku</b> and <b>Allow</b>. If you denied it, switch the approval on in the Shizuku app.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tag <b>UPDATE SHIZUKU</b></td><td>The running Shizuku is too old.</td><td>Update the Shizuku app and start it again.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nothing appears when you tap Authorize</td><td>The question can hide in the notification shade, or a battery saver stopped Shizuku.</td><td>Check the shade, then tap <b>Check Status</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Root tag <b>NOT FOUND</b></td><td>No <code>su</code> program found: the root manager may be missing or off.</td><td>Fix that, then tap <b>Check Status</b> on the Shizuku card.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Root was denied or is not available\"</td><td>You tapped Deny, or the root manager did not answer in about fifteen seconds. Root stays your chosen mode, and the Root tag turns to <b>DENIED</b>.</td><td>Tap <b>Request Root Access</b> again and approve, or allow the app in the root manager's list.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Root shows DENIED</b>: the Root tag is orange and says DENIED, and the badge reads \"Root (Superuser) (not ready)\"</td><td>A <code>su</code> program exists, but it did not answer as root for this app: your root manager refused it, or you did not tap Allow in time (the app waits about eight seconds). Nothing privileged runs, and the app keeps that answer until you check again.</td><td>Open your root manager (Magisk, KernelSU or APatch), find ADB Application Manager Pro in its list of apps and set it to allow. Then choose Root again with <b>Request Root Access</b> in Working Modes (<a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Badge says Root (violet), but actions fail</td><td>Root was working when the app last asked. If you have since taken the app off the allowed list in your root manager, the app does not know until it asks again. Otherwise Android refused that one action.</td><td>Tap <b>Request Root Access</b> (or <b>Check Status</b> on the Shizuku card) so the app asks again. If the tag turns to DENIED, allow the app in your root manager. If not, read the words in the Command Result box (next table).</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>An action says \"not allowed\", \"permission denied\" or SecurityException</h4>\n<p>These words are Android's, not the app's. Android or the phone's maker said no, and nothing was changed. The app shows them in a <b>Command Result</b> box after \"Action reported a problem\".</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>\"Permission Denial\", SecurityException or WRITE_SECURE_SETTINGS on a setting, theme or overlay</td><td>Android refused. Some keys are locked by the system or the maker.</td><td>On Xiaomi, Redmi and POCO, switch on <b>USB debugging (Security settings)</b> in Developer options. Otherwise leave it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"only root can delete system app for a particular user\"</td><td>Android would not let a normal shell remove it. The app already tried a second way.</td><td>Use <b>Freeze</b> instead, or <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a> if the phone has it. (On another device in <b>Connected Devices</b> the app tries a second way by itself too: see <a href=\"#devices-apps\">Managing the apps of the device</a>.)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>DELETE_FAILED_DEVICE_POLICY_MANAGER</code> or <code>DELETE_FAILED_USER_RESTRICTED</code></td><td>A work profile, device owner, enterprise setup or parental control blocks removal.</td><td>It cannot be bypassed from here while it applies.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Operation not allowed\", \"Shell cannot change component state\"</td><td>The app or component is protected on purpose.</td><td>Leave it. Try <b>Force Stop</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Permission Denial: ... not exported\" on launching a screen</td><td>The screen is not meant to be opened from outside.</td><td>Try <b>LAUNCH (SHELL)</b>; it does not work on every phone (<a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Android refused the change and gave no reason\" (overlays)</td><td>The system fixes that overlay, or it belongs to another app.</td><td>Leave it.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>The list of apps is empty, incomplete or out of date</h4>\n<p>The list is read when the app starts, after you change the working mode, and after the app changes an app itself. It shows the apps of the main user of the phone, which is normally you.</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>\"Querying system packages...\" never ends, or \"No Applications Loaded\"</td><td>Android did not answer. The reason is printed under the message.</td><td>Tap <b>Reload Packages</b>. If it keeps failing, close and reopen the app.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fewer apps than you expect</td><td>A filter or search is on, perhaps from <b>Remember my filters</b>, or the search matched less than you meant (<b>Use regex matching</b> is on at first, so special characters count).</td><td>Tap <b>Clear filters</b> or <b>All Apps</b> and empty the search box (<a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Running is 0 and nothing is UNINSTALLED</td><td>Both need a working mode.</td><td>Set one up (<a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Apps of a work profile, Secure Folder or second user are missing</td><td>They live in another user space.</td><td>Nothing to do here.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Many apps missing on a phone with privacy features</td><td>The app asks Android for the full list of apps, and Android grants that when the app is installed. This guide cannot say whether some phone makers add a privacy switch of their own that hides apps.</td><td>Look in Android's Settings, Apps, ADB Application Manager Pro, Permissions for anything about apps or the installed apps list, and allow it. Close and reopen the app. If it is still short, report it (see the end of this page).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>An app you installed or removed elsewhere is wrong in the list</td><td>The list was read before the change.</td><td>Close the app and open it again.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Icons are blank</td><td>They are drawn in the background, so they fill in later. An unreadable icon stays blank.</td><td>Wait, then use <b>Clear icon cache</b> or <b>Default icons</b> in <a href=\"#prefs\">Settings</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Installing an app fails</h4>\n<p>The result sheet quotes Android's reply, and the app adds a \"Note:\" for the common ones. The full table of messages and options is in <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a>.</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>\"App not installed\" (Android's own installer)</td><td>Wrong file for the processor, a different signing key, a broken download or no space.</td><td>Try the universal file, download again, free space (<a href=\"#install-and-update\">Installing and updating the app</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>INSTALL_FAILED_UPDATE_INCOMPATIBLE</code>, \"signatures do not match\"</td><td>The installed copy has a different signing key.</td><td>Only uninstalling it first helps, and that deletes its data. Back it up first.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>INSTALL_PARSE_FAILED_NO_CERTIFICATES</code></td><td>The signature cannot be verified: edited after signing, or unsigned.</td><td>Install it from the <a href=\"#tab-files\">File Manager</a>, which signs a copy with this app's key.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>INSTALL_FAILED_VERSION_DOWNGRADE</code>, <code>INSTALL_FAILED_TEST_ONLY</code>, <code>INSTALL_FAILED_DEPRECATED_SDK_VERSION</code></td><td>An older version, a test-only app, or an app built for a very old Android.</td><td>Turn on <b>Allow downgrade</b>, <b>Allow test packages</b> or <b>Bypass low target SDK block</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>INSTALL_FAILED_OLDER_SDK</code>, <code>INSTALL_FAILED_NO_MATCHING_ABIS</code></td><td>The app needs a newer Android, or has no code for this processor.</td><td>Find another version or build.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>INSTALL_FAILED_INSUFFICIENT_STORAGE</code></td><td>Not enough space. The installer first copies the file, so allow up to twice its size.</td><td>Free some space.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>INSTALL_FAILED_VERIFICATION_FAILURE</code></td><td>Play Protect refused or timed out.</td><td>Turn off its app scanning, or \"Verify apps over USB\" in Developer options.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"no APKs found inside this archive\"</td><td>A damaged or incomplete download.</td><td>Download it again.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The app crashes at once, or \"a split is missing\"</td><td>A part (feature module) was not ticked.</td><td>Press <b>Select all</b> and install again (<a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split packages</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Game data: \"Android doesn't let this app create that folder...\"</td><td>OBB data needs a working mode on Android 11 and newer.</td><td>Set up a mode, or copy the data by hand.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Android asks about \"Install unknown apps\" for this app, or an install without a working mode is refused at once</td><td>With no working mode, this app hands the file to Android's own installer. Android asks you the first time whether to allow that, and refuses until you do.</td><td>Tap <b>Allow</b> on the row <b>Install unknown apps</b> in <b>About</b>, <b>Permissions</b>: with a working mode it is granted at once, otherwise Android's own screen for this app opens, so switch it on there and come back. You can also find it in Android's Settings (Apps, ADB Application Manager Pro; the wording varies by phone). Or set up a working mode so the app installs by itself (<a href=\"#app-permissions\">The permissions this app asks for</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Refused with no reason (No privilege)</td><td>Some phones block the system installer. Samsung's <b>Auto Blocker</b> can too.</td><td>Set the Authorizer to ADB, Shizuku or Root. For Auto Blocker see Settings, Security and privacy.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>An update check or an App Stores catalog fails</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>\"N couldn't be checked (...)\"</td><td>A site did not answer, or limited you. GitHub allows 60 checks an hour without a token.</td><td>Wait, or add a <b>GitHub Token</b> (5,000 an hour), then check again (<a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"signed with a different key than the installed app\"</td><td>Another builder made that file. F-Droid, for example, signs its own builds.</td><td>Update from the source you installed from. Nothing was installed.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"no APK for this phone in the latest release\"</td><td>The newest release has no file for your processor.</td><td>Press <b>Release</b> and look by hand.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"download failed: HTTP ...\", \"download incomplete\"</td><td>The site or your connection failed.</td><td>Press <b>Retry</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"installing updates needs ADB, Shizuku or Root\"</td><td>No mode is ready.</td><td>Set one up. Only this app's own update can use Android's install screen.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Confirm the install to update\", then Android refuses</td><td>The phone blocked the system installer, or Install unknown apps is not allowed for this app.</td><td>Allow it with the row <b>Install unknown apps</b> in <b>About</b>, <b>Permissions</b> (or in Android's Settings, Apps, ADB Application Manager Pro, Install unknown apps), install the APK from your browser, or set up a working mode.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A Play Store app is not listed</td><td>Play Store apps are not checked.</td><td>Use the card <b>Play Store apps (via Aurora)</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Could not load the catalog: ...\", \"Stopped early after N apps\"</td><td>No internet, the site did not answer, or the download broke off.</td><td>Check the connection and press <b>Refresh</b> (<a href=\"#tab-store\">App Stores</a>).</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>The debloater list will not download, or looks wrong</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>\"The UAD-NG list has not been downloaded yet\", \"Waiting for the UAD-NG list...\"</td><td>The first download did not work.</td><td>Connect to the internet and tap <b>Update List</b> (<a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"UAD-NG list update failed: ... (using cached list)\"</td><td>No internet, or GitHub could not be reached. Something on your network can block it, such as a VPN, a firewall, an ad blocker or a private DNS setting. The words after \"failed\" give the reason. A damaged download never replaces a good copy.</td><td>Try later, or on another network.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"None of the packages on this phone are in the UAD-NG list.\"</td><td>The list loaded but nothing matches.</td><td>Tap <b>Update List</b>. If it stays, use the Application Manager.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"No packages match these filters.\"</td><td>Your chips are too narrow.</td><td>Turn on more levels, or set State to All.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>An app is missing, or has no UAD-NG tag</td><td>Only apps in the list and on this phone appear.</td><td>Manage it in the <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Backups and restores</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Working Mode Required</b> on Create backup or Confirm restore</td><td>The app needs a mode to read an app's permission settings.</td><td>Set one up (<a href=\"#apps-backup\">Backups and restore</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Include app data</b> is grey: \"Needs Root...\"</td><td>Only root can read another app's data.</td><td>Without root a backup holds the app and its settings.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"A newer version is installed than the one in the backup\", or \"signed with a different key\"</td><td>Android refuses an older version, or an update signed by someone else.</td><td>Uninstall the app first, then restore. That deletes its data.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"... it does not match this device\"</td><td>A system-app backup is refused on a phone whose own copy has another key.</td><td>It mostly helps on the same phone model.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Not enough storage\", \"A split APK is missing\", \"Not a usable backup\"</td><td>No space, a damaged file, or not a backup from this app.</td><td>Free space, or choose or make another backup.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Some data changed while it was being read\"</td><td>The app was in use during the copy.</td><td>Back up again while you do not use it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>You cannot find your backups</td><td>They are in <code>Download/ADB App Manager/Backups</code> (on Android 8 and 9, in the app's own storage).</td><td>Use <b>Choose backup file...</b> for a file you moved.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>The File Manager cannot see or open something</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>\"Storage looks inaccessible\", \"Can't read this folder...\", a folder that looks empty</td><td>All files access is off, or it is a system folder and no mode is connected.</td><td>Tap <b>Grant storage access</b>, or set up a mode (<a href=\"#tab-files\">File Manager</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"The app cannot write here...\"</td><td>That folder is not writable for the app.</td><td>Grant All files access, or connect a mode.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>An SD card or USB drive is not shown</td><td>Android gives an app only a folder you choose.</td><td>Tap <b>+ Add storage</b> (<a href=\"#files-storage\">SD cards and other storage</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Search finds nothing</td><td>Wrong scope, a hidden name, or All files access is off. Search does not use a working mode.</td><td>Check the drop-down, turn on <b>Show hidden files</b> (<a href=\"#files-search\">Searching files</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"A file job is still running\"</td><td>Only one runs at a time.</td><td>Wait, or tap <b>Cancel</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Not a readable archive\", \"That password did not work\"</td><td>A damaged or cut-short file, not an archive, or a wrong password.</td><td>Check the file and retype the password (<a href=\"#files-archives\">Archives</a>).</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Terminal commands hang or do not run</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>A command never ends</td><td>Some run until stopped: <code>logcat</code> without <code>-d</code>, <code>top</code> without <code>-n 1</code>, <code>ping</code> without a count.</td><td>Tap <b>STOP</b> or <b>CTRL</b>. A command stops itself after 30 minutes (<a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"The shell is still busy with an earlier command\"</td><td>An earlier command has not finished.</td><td>Tap <b>STOP</b>. If \"The shell is not running\", press Enter or tap <b>Restart shell</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"No working mode is connected: connect ADB, Shizuku or Root first\"</td><td>You chose the working-mode shell with no mode ready.</td><td>Tap <b>Working Modes</b>, or choose another shell.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"needs a real terminal\" (nano, vim, top)</td><td>Full-screen programs cannot run here.</td><td>Tap <b>Open in Termux</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Termux is not installed\", \"may not run commands in Termux yet\"</td><td>The Termux shell is not set up.</td><td>Follow <b>Termux setup</b>. Use Termux from F-Droid or GitHub.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A coding agent seems stuck</td><td>An approval card waits for your tap, or it paused after 25 steps.</td><td>Tap <b>Run</b>, <b>Apply</b> or <b>Skip</b>, or send \"go on\" (<a href=\"#terminal-agents\">Coding agents</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"The key was not accepted\", \"no credit left\", \"model may not exist\", \"too long\", \"Too many requests\", \"No connection\"</td><td>The provider's answers about your key, account, model, text size, quota or the network.</td><td>Check or renew the key, add credit, pick another model, tap <b>New chat</b>, wait, or check the connection. <b>Details</b> has the exact reply.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Logcat or the Task Manager is empty</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Working Mode Required</b>, \"reading logcat needs ADB, Shizuku or Root\"</td><td>Read-Only mode cannot read the log.</td><td>Set up a mode (<a href=\"#tab-logcat\">Logcat Viewer</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"(no matching log lines)\", \"(no output)\"</td><td>The level is too high, the filter matches nothing, or the app wrote nothing.</td><td>Set the level to Verbose, empty the filter, or choose <b>All apps</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Empty after <b>Clear</b></td><td>Clear empties the log for every app, and the log is short.</td><td>Make the problem happen again, then <b>Refresh</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Task Manager: \"Needs ADB, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root\"</td><td>Processes and GPU need a mode. Battery needs none. CPU, RAM and Network work without one only on some phones.</td><td>Tap <b>Set Up</b> (<a href=\"#tab-taskmgr\">Task Manager</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>CPU shows 0%, GPU \"Not available on this phone\"</td><td>The CPU needs two readings, and many phones hide the GPU load.</td><td>Wait one refresh. Nothing is wrong.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Hidden Settings will not save, or overlays and colours do nothing</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>\"Needs ADB, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root\"</td><td>These tabs run Android's own commands through a working mode.</td><td>Tap <b>Set Up</b> and connect a mode (<a href=\"#tab-settings\">Hidden Settings</a>, <a href=\"#tab-overlays\">RRO/Monet Customization</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Could not read the global settings\", or \"!\" on a button</td><td>No answer, or the list was cut short.</td><td>Tap <b>Try again</b>, or <b>Working modes</b> to check the connection.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Save</b> stays grey, with a red line</td><td>The value breaks a limit: 20,000 characters, no control characters, and a size ADB can carry.</td><td>Shorten the value.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Change refused</b>, \"Theme change refused\", \"Permission Denial\"</td><td>Android said no, in its own words. Some keys and themes are locked by the system or the maker.</td><td>Read the advice. On Xiaomi, Redmi and POCO, switch on <b>USB debugging (Security settings)</b> in Developer options.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"No answer from the device (timed out)\", \"No answer: the theme may still change\"</td><td>The change may still have been applied.</td><td>Wait for the tab to read again, check, then retry only if needed.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The old value comes back, or the new value shows but nothing looks different</td><td>The system or the maker's software owns that setting, or Android reads it only at certain moments.</td><td>Restart the app that should react, or the phone. If nothing helps, your phone does not use it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Colours applied but look the same</td><td>Your maker's own palette wins, or apps have not repainted.</td><td>Reopen an app or restart. Material You needs Android 12 or newer.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Could not switch it on\" with \"commit failed\", or an overlay marked <b>Unavailable</b></td><td>The system fixes that overlay, it belongs to another app, or its target is missing.</td><td>Leave it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The Undo bar has gone</td><td>It stays about 7 seconds.</td><td>Use <b>Changes</b> and <b>Revert</b> (<a href=\"#settings-journal\">Undoing settings changes</a>), or <b>Default</b> for the theme.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>You turned off ADB or Wi-Fi from here and lost the connection</td><td>The app can no longer reach the phone to change it back.</td><td>In Android's own Developer options switch USB debugging or Wireless debugging (and Wi-Fi) back on, then reconnect.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Trim Caches says it did not finish</h4>\n<p>This is the card <b>Trim Caches</b> in the SD Maid tab. It runs <code>pm trim-caches 128G</code> through your working mode and then opens a box. If the box says <b>It did not finish</b>, with <b>FAILED</b> on the command, the words under it are Android's own (<a href=\"#prefs\">Settings</a> describes the card).</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>A padlock on the button, and a sheet <b>Working Mode Required</b></td><td>Trim Caches needs a working mode (ADB over TCP, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root). Nothing ran.</td><td>Tap <b>Set Up Working Mode</b> and connect one (<a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>), then try again.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>It did not finish</b>, with words such as \"Exception\", \"denied\", \"not allowed\" or \"Failure\"</td><td>Android refused the command. Some phones do not let the shell user (the one ADB and Shizuku run as) trim caches, or the maker's security rules block it.</td><td>Read the words in the box. Try once more. If it refuses again, switch to another working mode (Root, if the phone has it) and try again. If every mode is refused, your phone does not allow it: Android's own Settings, Apps, then the app, Storage, <b>Clear cache</b>, does one app at a time.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"The command gave no answer.\"</td><td>The working mode returned nothing, usually because the connection to the phone dropped while it ran (for example Wireless Debugging went off with the Wi-Fi).</td><td>Check that the badge is green or violet again, then try again.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Error: the command could not be started\"</td><td>The app could not hand the command to the working mode.</td><td>Close the app and open it again, then try again.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Caches trimmed</b>, but \"nothing more could be freed\"</td><td>There was little cache left to clear. This is not an error.</td><td>Nothing is wrong. To free a lot of space, see <a href=\"#recipe-find-big-apps\">Find the apps that take the most space</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>The Connected Devices tab cannot find or keep a watch</h4>\n<p>These rows fit a watch and any other device. See <a href=\"#devices-wear\">Wear OS, start to finish</a>.</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Why</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>No device</b>, or \"Nothing found\" after a scan</td><td>Debugging over Wi-Fi is off, the address is wrong, the devices are on different networks, or the router hides devices from each other (a guest network, client isolation).</td><td>Check debugging is on and both are on the same Wi-Fi. Type the address by hand under <b>Wi-Fi</b> (<a href=\"#devices-add\">Adding a device</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Wrong password\"</td><td>The pairing code is wrong or expired.</td><td>Open <b>Pair new device</b> again and use the new code and address.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Paired, but <b>Connect</b> fails, or \"Connection refused\"</td><td>You typed the pairing address, or debugging is off, or the port changed.</td><td>Use the address on the <b>Debug over Wi-Fi</b> screen.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Connection timed out\", \"No route to host\"</td><td>The device is asleep, on another network, or blocked by the router.</td><td>Wake it and check the Wi-Fi.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Not allowed yet</b></td><td>The device has not accepted this phone's key (also after <b>Regenerate Key</b>).</td><td>On the device tap <b>Allow</b> and tick <b>Always allow</b>, then <b>Refresh</b>. If no question comes, turn debugging off and on.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>offline</b></td><td>It stopped answering: Wi-Fi asleep, out of range, or restarted.</td><td>Tap <b>Reconnect</b>. If it stays offline, turn Wireless debugging off and on, then <b>Connect</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>It drops after a while, or the address changed after a restart</td><td>Many watches turn Wi-Fi off to save battery. A restart can give a new address and port.</td><td>Use the charger, keep the screen on, read the new address or use <b>Nearby</b>. The old one under <b>Recent:</b> will not work.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The Bluetooth link fails</td><td>Debugging over Bluetooth is off in the Wear OS app or on the watch, this phone has no ADB mode, or the watch does not support it.</td><td>Check both switches and the mode, or use Wi-Fi. It is the least tested part.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A <b>That did not work</b> sheet, or a red row when sending</td><td>The device refused, or Android's own install message (for example <code>INSTALL_FAILED_OLDER_SDK</code>). For an Uninstall of a system app, the app has already tried its second way (a small helper sent to the device) before the sheet opened.</td><td>See <a href=\"#devices-apps\">Managing the apps of the device</a> and <a href=\"#devices-send\">Sending apps</a>. If a system app will not uninstall, <b>Disable</b> it instead.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>The screen became too small or too large</h4>\n<p>This follows a screen density (dpi) or size change, on a connected device or on this phone.</p>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li><b>Wait 15 seconds</b> if you changed a connected device from its <b>Display</b> sub-tab. The change undoes itself unless you tapped <b>Keep</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Tap Put it back</b>, or <b>Reset to default</b> (<b>Reset</b> for the size). A reset has no countdown.</li>\n<li><b>Run the rescue command</b> in the device's Console sub-tab (or the ADB Console for this phone): <code>wm density reset</code> and, for the size, <code>wm size reset</code>. They work while the connection is up, even when you cannot read the screen.</li>\n<li><b>If the connection is gone,</b> connect again, then do step 3. If it is this phone and the screen is unusable, use a computer (<a href=\"#safety\">Staying safe</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> A density or size change normally stays after a restart, so restarting does not fix it. If the app is closed during the 15 seconds, the automatic undo may not happen (<a href=\"#devices-display\">Screen density and size</a>).</div>\n\n<h4>Android closed the app in the background</h4>\n<p>Android may close an app that is not on screen, some makers sooner than others. The connection it held is lost, anything still running (a backup, an install, a command) may be cut short, and the list is read again when you open it. Long file jobs continue, with a notification and a <b>Cancel</b> button.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>With Auto or ADB over TCP the app reconnects at start-up if port 5555 is open. With Wireless Debugging it tries the last address once, which works only if the port has not changed (<a href=\"#mode-readonly\">Auto and Read-Only</a>).</li>\n<li>If Android keeps closing it, open its battery settings (Settings, Apps, ADB Application Manager Pro, Battery) and choose the option that does not restrict it. The names vary by maker.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>How to reset the app's settings</h4>\n<p>Choose the smallest reset that does the job. None of them undo what the app already changed in Android: a frozen app stays frozen and a hidden setting keeps its value until you undo them (<a href=\"#safety\">Staying safe</a>).</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>To reset</th><th>How</th><th>What it loses</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>The tab bar, the hold menu</td><td>Settings (the gear): <b>Feature List</b> or <b>Action Button</b>, then <b>Reset to Default</b>.</td><td>Your tab order, hidden tabs, or hold-menu order (<a href=\"#feature-list\">Turning tabs on and off</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Colours</td><td><b>Reset tweaks</b>, or tap a palette card. There is no single reset button (<a href=\"#themes\">Themes and colours</a>).</td><td>Your colour tweaks.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hidden settings, the system theme</td><td><b>Changes</b> then <b>Revert</b>, newest first. <b>Default</b> for the theme.</td><td>Nothing, but each revert is a change too.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The ADB key and every pairing</td><td>Badge sheet, <b>Regenerate Key</b>.</td><td>Every ADB connection drops, devices ask again, and Wireless Debugging must be paired again.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Everything in the app</td><td>Android Settings, Apps, ADB Application Manager Pro, Storage, <b>Clear storage</b> (some phones say Clear data).</td><td>Saved lists, profiles, history, settings, saved tokens and keys, the downloaded debloat list and the ADB key. It starts like a first launch. On Android 10 and newer, files in <code>Download/ADB App Manager</code> are not part of this.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A fresh start</td><td>Uninstall and install again (<a href=\"#install-and-update\">Installing and updating the app</a>).</td><td>The same, plus the permissions you gave and the key that signs edited APKs. On Android 8 and 9 the saved files go too.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>How to report a problem</h4>\n<p>A report that says what you did, what you expected and what happened can be fixed. Collect these first:</p>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li><b>The app's details.</b> In the <b>About</b> tab, <b>This build</b> card, tap <b>Copy debug info</b> (or <b>Share it</b>). It holds the app's version and package name, your Android version, the phone model and processor, the WebView version, the working mode and the signing fingerprint, but no names, accounts or app lists. Paste it into the report.</li>\n<li><b>The exact message.</b> Copy it, or screenshot the note or the <b>Command Result</b> box. Add your phone's make and which working mode was on.</li>\n<li><b>The steps.</b> Which tab and button, in what order, whether it happens every time, and what you expected.</li>\n<li><b>For a crash or restart,</b> the log: in the <b>Logcat Viewer</b> tap <b>All apps</b>, choose ADB Application Manager Pro, set the level to Error, make it happen again, then <b>Share</b> or <b>Save</b>. Read the file first: a log can hold personal details.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Where to report.</b> In the About tab tap <b>Report a bug or idea</b>. It opens GitHub's page for a new issue on <code>github.com/Bingblop/ADB-Application-Manager</code>. You need a GitHub account to post. Look through the existing issues first.</p>\n<div class=\"hg-warn\"><b>Careful.</b> Issues on GitHub are public. Never paste an API key, a token, a password or a pairing code, and remove personal details from screenshots and logs.</div>"},{"id":"faq","title":"Frequently asked questions","group":"Safety and help","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Short, honest answers to the questions beginners ask most, with a link to the place that explains each one properly. If a word is new to you, the <a href=\"#glossary\">glossary</a> has it.</p>\n\n<h4>The basics</h4>\n\n<h5>What does this app do?</h5>\n<p>It lists every app on your phone and lets you freeze, remove, back up and inspect them. It also installs app files, browses files, runs commands, changes Android's hidden settings and manages another Android device such as a watch. All of it runs on the phone, and in the usual case you need no computer. See <a href=\"#welcome\">Welcome</a>.</p>\n\n<h5>Is it safe?</h5>\n<p>The app itself has no ads, no accounts and no analytics (<a href=\"#privacy\">Privacy</a>). What matters is what you do with it. Freezing is easy to undo. Removing system apps and clearing data can hurt if you pick the wrong thing. Follow the four golden rules in <a href=\"#safety\">Staying safe</a>, start with apps rated Recommended, and change a few things at a time. No guide can promise that a change is safe on every phone.</p>\n\n<h5>Is it free?</h5>\n<p>Yes. The About tab says the app is free and stays free, and this guide describes no paid feature. The <b>Buy me a coffee</b> card is optional and only opens PayPal in your browser. If you use a coding agent with your own key, the provider may bill that account, which is between you and the provider (<a href=\"#terminal-agents\">Coding agents</a>).</p>\n\n<h5>Does it need root?</h5>\n<p>No. Root is one of four working modes, and the quickest if your phone already has it. Wireless Debugging (Android 11 and newer) and ADB over TCP need no root. Only a few things need it, mainly backing up and restoring an app's data (<a href=\"#apps-backup\">Backups</a>). Without root you can still freeze, suspend, bring back apps and, on most phones, remove system apps for your user (<a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a> says what to do when Android refuses). The app cannot root your phone (<a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>).</p>\n\n<h5>Does it need a computer?</h5>\n<p>Often not. On Android 11 or newer, Wireless Debugging needs only Wi-Fi. On a rooted phone you need nothing. On Android 8 to 10 without root, you need a computer once to switch on ADB over TCP (or to start Shizuku), and again after every restart. The table in <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a> lays it out.</p>\n\n<h5>Which versions of Android does it work on?</h5>\n<p>Android 8.0 or newer. The app will not install on older versions. It declares Android 14 as the version it is built for, and this guide cannot promise that every feature behaves the same on newer versions. Some features need more than 8.0: Wireless Debugging needs Android 11, and the Material You theme needs Android 12.</p>\n\n<h5>Will it work on my Samsung, Pixel, Xiaomi or other phone?</h5>\n<p>It uses Android's own commands, so in principle it works on any phone with Android 8.0 or newer. Makers differ in small ways. On some recent Samsung phones, <b>Auto Blocker</b> can block installs that do not come from a store, and Samsung's own apps are checked through the Galaxy Store. On Xiaomi, Redmi and POCO, changes to hidden settings and themes may need <b>USB debugging (Security settings)</b> switched on in Developer options. Where you find Build number differs by maker (<a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>, step 1). The app has not been tried on every phone. If something misbehaves on yours, report it (<a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>).</p>\n\n<h5>Will it void my warranty?</h5>\n<p>It depends on the phone maker and on your country, and nobody here can promise either way. Freezing, suspending and removing apps for your user go through Android's own commands. The app has no feature that unlocks the bootloader or flashes firmware. Rooting is different: the guide says it usually voids the warranty, and on Samsung phones it trips the Knox counter (<a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>). Check your maker's terms. Before a repair, undo what you can (<b>Enable</b>, <b>Restore</b>); a factory reset also puts back system apps you removed for your user.</p>\n\n<h5>Can it brick my phone?</h5>\n<p>The app has no feature that flashes firmware or unlocks the bootloader. But removing or disabling a vital system app can leave the phone in a boot loop, and a factory reset normally cures that (<a href=\"#safety\">Staying safe</a>). The Terminal, above all as root, can run any command, including harmful ones. That is why the guide asks you never to paste a command you do not understand.</p>\n\n<h5>Why do I have to switch on Developer options?</h5>\n<p>Android keeps ADB, USB debugging and Wireless debugging in a hidden menu, so people who do not need them do not stumble on them. Tapping <b>Build number</b> seven times makes the menu appear, and every option inside stays off until you switch it on (<a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>, step 1).</p>\n\n<h4>Working modes and connections</h4>\n\n<h5>What is a working mode, and which one should I pick?</h5>\n<p>It is the way the app borrows enough power to change other apps: ADB over TCP, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku or Root, or Auto, which picks the first that is ready. Rooted phone: pick Root. Android 11 or newer, not rooted: Wireless Debugging. Android 8 to 10, not rooted: ADB over TCP with a computer once, or Shizuku. <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a> has the full decision list.</p>\n\n<h5>What is the difference between ADB over TCP and Wireless Debugging?</h5>\n<p>Both let the app talk to the phone's ADB service. Wireless Debugging is Android's own Wi-Fi feature (Android 11 and newer): you pair once with a code, and the connect port changes whenever it is switched off and on. ADB over TCP uses the fixed port 5555 and does not depend on the Wi-Fi once it is on, but a restart switches it off, and on Android 8 to 10 you need a computer to switch it on (<a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP</a>).</p>\n\n<h5>Why does the Wireless Debugging address keep changing?</h5>\n<p>Android picks a new random connect port each time Wireless debugging is switched off and on, and it often switches it off itself when you leave the Wi-Fi or restart the phone. The pairing stays, so you only read the new <b>IP address &amp; Port</b> and tap Connect. To avoid this, you can switch on ADB over TCP from Wireless Debugging with one button, until the next restart (<a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>).</p>\n\n<h5>Does it keep working after a restart?</h5>\n<p>It depends on the mode. Root: yes, nothing to redo. Shizuku: it stops, so start it again, unless it was started with root and set to start itself. ADB over TCP: switched off again, so repeat the one-time step. Wireless Debugging: the pairing is kept, but you switch it on again and connect to the new port. A freeze, a suspend or a removal is stored by Android and not by this app, so it normally stays after a restart (a system update can undo it).</p>\n\n<h5>Is it safe to leave Wireless debugging or ADB over TCP switched on?</h5>\n<p>Switch them off when you do not need them. While port 5555 is open, the phone listens on the Wi-Fi, and another device on the same network could try to connect. It would still need your approval in the \"Allow USB debugging?\" question, so never tick \"Always allow\" for a fingerprint you do not recognise. A restart switches ADB over TCP off (<a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP</a>).</p>\n\n<h4>Freezing, removing and getting apps back</h4>\n\n<h5>What is the difference between freezing, suspending and uninstalling?</h5>\n<p><b>Freeze</b> (Android calls it disabling) keeps the app installed with its data, but it cannot run and disappears from your launcher. <b>Enable</b> undoes it. <b>Suspend</b> keeps the app enabled but paused: its icon is greyed out and it cannot be opened until you <b>Unsuspend</b>. <b>Uninstall</b> on a system app removes it for your user only and deletes its data for you; the file stays in the system, so <b>Reinstall</b> can bring it back. On an app you installed, uninstalling removes it for good. When unsure, freeze (<a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>, <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>).</p>\n\n<h5>Can I get an app back?</h5>\n<p>Usually. A frozen app: <b>Enable</b>. A suspended app: <b>Unsuspend</b>. A system app you removed: <b>Reinstall</b>, <b>Restore</b> in the Debloater, or Undo in the History. Such apps stay in the list with the UNINSTALLED badge so you can find them. A factory reset also restores them. An app you installed and removed must be installed again from a store or a file. Data you cleared is gone unless you have a backup that included it (that needs Root).</p>\n\n<h5>Can I undo everything at once?</h5>\n<p>There is no \"undo all\" button. Undo works one History entry or one batch at a time (the History keeps the last 150 entries), and the hidden settings have their own list of changes to revert. A saved <a href=\"#apps-profiles\">profile</a> lets you apply a known state again. The only all-in-one undo is a factory reset, and that also erases your own data.</p>\n\n<h5>Which apps are safe to remove?</h5>\n<p>No list can say that for every phone. The <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a> shows a community rating for each app (Recommended, Advanced, Expert or Unsafe) and describes it. Start with Recommended, read each description, change a few apps at a time, and never remove one rated Unsafe. The ratings are advice, not a promise (<a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloating safely</a>).</p>\n\n<h5>Will it free up storage, save battery or make my phone faster?</h5>\n<p>Not by much for system apps: their file stays on the protected system part, so little space is freed. A frozen app does not run in the background, which can cut background activity, but whether you notice depends on the apps. Removing an app you installed does free its space, and <b>Trim Caches</b> in Settings clears the temporary files of every app, which frees some space too (see the next question). Do not expect miracles.</p>\n\n<h5>Does Trim Caches delete my data?</h5>\n<p>No. <b>Trim Caches</b>, a card of the SD Maid tab, deletes only cache: the temporary files each app keeps in its own cache folders, such as thumbnails, saved web pages, map tiles and buffered video. It does not touch apps, their data and settings, logins and accounts, saved games, photos, videos, downloads or your files, and nothing is uninstalled. Afterwards an app may start a little slower and may fetch images again over mobile data, and an app that keeps offline downloads in its cache (some music, video and map apps) may need to download them again. It needs a working mode, and it cannot be undone, though apps simply rebuild what they need (<a href=\"#recipe-trim-caches\">Free up space by clearing every app's cache</a>, <a href=\"#prefs\">Settings</a>).</p>\n\n<h5>Will a system update undo my changes?</h5>\n<p>It can. A big Android or phone-maker update can bring back apps you removed and switch frozen apps on again, and this differs from phone to phone. Save a profile and tap <b>Watch</b>: after an update the app tells you what came back and lets you review and remove it again (<a href=\"#apps-profiles\">Profiles</a>, and the section \"Before a phone update\" in <a href=\"#safety\">Staying safe</a>).</p>\n\n<h5>Why do some apps come back?</h5>\n<p>Most often because of a system update, as above. A factory reset restores system apps removed for your user, and <b>Reinstall</b>, <b>Restore</b>, Undo or restoring a backup brings one back on purpose. If an app returns with none of these, phones differ and this guide cannot cover every maker's software. Remove it again, and consider watching a profile.</p>\n\n<h4>Installing and updating</h4>\n\n<h5>What are APKS, APKM and XAPK?</h5>\n<p>They are bundles of several app files that belong together: a base file plus small parts for your processor, screen density and language. The <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a> opens all of them and ticks the parts that fit your phone. XAPK may also carry game data (OBB), which is copied after the install (<a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split packages, APKS, APKM, XAPK and game data</a>).</p>\n\n<h5>What is the 32-bit APK, and which download do I need?</h5>\n<p>Each release has three APK files: <code>arm64-v8a</code> for 64-bit phones (nearly all), <code>armeabi-v7a</code> for 32-bit phones, and <code>universal</code>, which carries both. A small <code>SHA256SUMS.txt</code> file lists their checksums. If you are not sure, take the universal one. The difference is the small ADB program inside the app. The 32-bit build is new and has been tried on far fewer phones (<a href=\"#install-and-update\">Installing and updating the app</a>).</p>\n\n<h5>How do I update the app?</h5>\n<p>Install the new APK over the old one: your settings, lists and profiles are kept. Or open <b>About</b>, tap <b>Check for update</b>, then <b>Update now</b>. Either way the new file must be this app, newer, and signed with the same key (<a href=\"#install-and-update\">Installing and updating the app</a>).</p>\n\n<h5>Can I install apps from Google Play with it?</h5>\n<p>No. The app does not download from Google Play. It installs app files you give it, and the <a href=\"#tab-store\">App Stores</a> tab browses four open-source catalogs (ShizuStore, GitHub, F-Droid and Orion) and installs from them. Play Store apps keep updating through the Play Store or Aurora Store; the <a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a> only points you there.</p>\n\n<h5>Can I move the app to the SD card?</h5>\n<p>No. By default Android keeps an app in internal storage unless the app allows otherwise, and this app does not ask for that. Your own files are different: the <a href=\"#files-storage\">File Manager</a> can add an SD card or a USB drive, and you can move backups and downloads there with Copy and Paste.</p>\n\n<h4>Watches, tablets and other devices</h4>\n\n<h5>Can I use it on a watch?</h5>\n<p>Yes. The <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a> tab manages a Wear OS watch from your phone over adb (the developer tool, see the <a href=\"#glossary\">glossary</a>), mostly through Wi-Fi, and also through a USB cable or a watch's Bluetooth link: apps, installs, a console, the log, files, hidden settings and screen density. The app itself is not installed on the watch. The Bluetooth link is the least tested part. <a href=\"#devices-wear\">Wear OS, start to finish</a> walks through it.</p>\n\n<h5>Can I use it on a tablet or a TV, or on someone else's phone?</h5>\n<p>A tablet or a second phone is managed from the same tab, the same way as a watch. Installing the app on a tablet should work like on a phone, but this guide has not been checked on one. The app has no TV launcher entry. A TV that offers ADB over the network could be reached from Connected Devices, but that has not been tried. Use it only on devices you own or may manage: the other device must have debugging on and must approve your phone.</p>\n\n<h4>Privacy and money</h4>\n\n<h5>Does it collect data or show ads?</h5>\n<p>No ads, no account, no analytics and no server of the developer's. The app goes online only for things you open or ask for, such as the debloat list, update checks and the App Stores. <a href=\"#privacy\">Privacy</a> lists every contact.</p>\n\n<h5>Are my AI keys safe?</h5>\n<p>A coding agent's key is sealed with AES-256-GCM using a key held in the Android Keystore, stored in the app's private settings, and sent only to that provider's own address. Android's backup is switched off for the app. The VirusTotal key and the GitHub token are kept in the app's private settings without that extra sealing, so leave them empty if that worries you (<a href=\"#privacy\">Privacy</a>).</p>\n\n<h5>What does the donation do?</h5>\n<p><b>Buy me a coffee</b> opens PayPal's donation page in your browser, for a one-time amount in US dollars to the developer's PayPal address. The app handles no payment itself, and you can close the page and nothing happens (<a href=\"#tab-about\">About</a>).</p>\n\n<h4>The app itself</h4>\n\n<h5>How do I change the language?</h5>\n<p>Tap the gear in the header, then <b>Language</b>. The list has 14 languages: English, Español, Français, Deutsch, Português (Brasil), Italiano, Русский, 简体中文, 日本語, 한국어, العربية, हिन्दी, Türkçe and Bahasa Indonesia. The whole app changes at once. App names, file names and commands are not translated (<a href=\"#prefs\">Settings</a>).</p>\n\n<h5>Why is this guide in English only, and does it work offline?</h5>\n<p>The guide is written once, in English, and uses the English button names. If you switched the app to another language, the names on your screen will differ from the ones here, so switch back to English if you cannot find a button. The guide is stored inside the app and opens without internet: <b>About</b> tab, <b>Help Guide</b> button (<a href=\"#welcome\">Welcome</a> says how to use it).</p>\n\n<h5>Can I hide tabs I do not use, or change their order?</h5>\n<p>Yes. Open Settings with the gear, find the <b>Feature List</b> card, and use its switches and arrows. Application Manager always stays first and About last. Switching a tab off deletes nothing, and <b>Reset to Default</b> puts everything back (<a href=\"#feature-list\">Turning tabs on and off and moving them</a>).</p>\n\n<h5>Where do I report a bug or ask for a feature?</h5>\n<p>In the <b>About</b> tab, tap <b>Report a bug or idea</b>. It opens GitHub's page for a new issue, and you need a GitHub account to post. The end of <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a> says what to collect first.</p>\n\n<h5>Is the app on Google Play, and how do I know my copy is genuine?</h5>\n<p>It is not on Google Play. Download it only from its <a href=\"https://github.com/Bingblop/ADB-Application-Manager/releases/latest\">releases page on GitHub</a>. In the About tab, the <b>This build</b> card should say \"Signed with the official release key\", and the fingerprint begins <code>94:61:87:0F</code>. Each release also has a <code>SHA256SUMS.txt</code> file (<a href=\"#install-and-update\">Installing and updating the app</a>).</p>\n\n<h5>How do I remove the app, and what happens to my changes?</h5>\n<p>Uninstall it like any app in Android's Settings. Its saved lists, profiles and settings go with it. What it changed in Android does not: frozen apps stay frozen, hidden settings keep their values and overlays stay on until you undo them. Undo them first, and see \"How to delete everything\" in <a href=\"#privacy\">Privacy</a>.</p>\n\n<h5>Is the source code open?</h5>\n<p>The project's GitHub page holds the source code, the releases and the issue tracker (the <b>GitHub</b> button in About opens it), so you can read it there. The app and the files this guide was written from state no licence for the app's own code, so this guide cannot say what you may do with it. If you need to know, ask the developer through <b>Report a bug or idea</b> in the About tab. See also <a href=\"#credits\">Credits</a>.</p>"},{"id":"glossary","title":"Glossary","group":"Safety and help","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">Every technical word the app and this guide use, in plain language, in alphabetical order. Each entry points to the section that explains it. If you cannot find a word, try the search box at the top of the guide.</p>\n\n<h4>0-9</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>3rd Party</b></td><td>The app list's name for an app that you or a store installed. Its row carries the tag USER. The opposite is a system app. See <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>A</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>ABI</b></td><td>The kind of machine code a phone's processor runs, such as arm64-v8a (64-bit) or armeabi-v7a (32-bit). An app must match it. See <a href=\"#install-and-update\">Installing and updating the app</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Allow Restricted Settings</b></td><td>A special access that lifts Android's block on a few sensitive switches for apps installed from outside a store (Android 13 and newer). Optional. See <a href=\"#app-permissions\">The permissions this app asks for</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>activity</b></td><td>One screen of an app. The Activities tab of an app's menu lists them, and LAUNCH opens one. See <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>ADB</b></td><td>Android Debug Bridge, the developer tool that sends commands to a phone as the shell user (UID 2000). This app carries its own copy. See <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>ADB key</b></td><td>The key pair this app makes on its first start, which is how a phone recognises it. See <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>ADB over TCP</b></td><td>Making the phone's ADB listen on port 5555, so the app can connect from the inside. A restart switches it off. See <a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>AES</b></td><td>A strong encryption method. It locks 7z archives and zip archives (when you choose AES), and it seals the keys of coding agents. See <a href=\"#files-archives\">Archives</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>All files access</b></td><td>Android's special permission to see all files on storage. It is optional. See <a href=\"#app-permissions\">The permissions this app asks for</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>AOSP</b></td><td>The Android Open Source Project: the plain Android system, without a maker's additions. It is one of the list filters of the UAD-NG Debloater. See <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>API key</b></td><td>A long secret text from an AI provider that lets the app use your account with them. It is stored sealed. See <a href=\"#terminal-agents\">Coding agents</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>API level</b></td><td>Android's version as a number: 26 is Android 8, 30 is Android 11, 34 is Android 14. See <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>APK</b></td><td>The file an app is installed from, ending in .apk. See <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>APKM</b></td><td>The bundle format of APKMirror: a zip file of APK files that belong together. See <a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split packages</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>APKS</b></td><td>A bundle made by Google's bundletool and similar tools: a zip file of APK files. See <a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split packages</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>app op</b></td><td>A finer switch that Android keeps behind the permissions, such as running in the background. Its values are Allow, FG, Ignore, Deny and Reset. See <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>archive</b></td><td>One file that holds many files, such as zip, 7z, rar and tar. The File Manager can look inside one without unpacking it. See <a href=\"#files-archives\">Archives</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Aurora Store</b></td><td>An open-source client for the Google Play catalog. The App Updater offers to open it for Play Store apps. See <a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>authorization</b></td><td>A device's approval of this phone's key. Until given, it shows as unauthorized (Not allowed yet). See <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Authorizer</b></td><td>In the APK Installer, how a file is installed: ADB, Shizuku, Root or No privilege. See <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Auto Blocker</b></td><td>A feature on some recent Samsung phones that can block installs that do not come from a store. See <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Auto mode</b></td><td>The working mode that uses the first of ADB over TCP, Wireless Debugging or Shizuku that is ready. It never picks Root. See <a href=\"#mode-readonly\">Auto and Read-Only mode</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>B</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>backup</b></td><td>A saved copy of one app in an .adbbackup file: its APK, permissions and app ops, and with Root its data. See <a href=\"#apps-backup\">Backups and restore</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>badge</b></td><td>A small coloured label. The one at the top right names the working mode, and app rows have tags such as FROZEN. See <a href=\"#screen-tour\">A tour of the screen</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>base APK</b></td><td>The core file of an app that comes in parts. It is always installed. See <a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split packages</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>bloatware</b></td><td>Apps added by the phone's maker or network that many people never use and cannot easily remove. See <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Bluetooth link</b></td><td>A way to reach a Wear OS watch through its Bluetooth connection to the phone, with no Wi-Fi. See <a href=\"#devices-add\">Adding a device</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>boot loop</b></td><td>The phone restarts again and again, or sits on its logo, and never reaches the home screen. See <a href=\"#safety\">Staying safe</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>bootloader</b></td><td>The small program that runs before Android and is used for flashing. This app does not unlock it. See <a href=\"#faq\">Questions and answers</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>broadcast receiver</b></td><td>The part of an app that listens for events, such as the phone starting up. The Components tab of an app's menu lists them as Receivers, next to Services and Providers. See <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>build number</b></td><td>The line in About phone you tap seven times to unlock Developer options. The app has its own build number in About. See <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>bundle</b></td><td>Several app files packed into one file (APKS, APKM or XAPK). See <a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split packages</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>C</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>cache</b></td><td>Temporary files kept to save time, such as downloaded thumbnails or saved web pages. Each app has its own cache folder, and apps rebuild what they need if it is removed. This app also keeps an icon cache and a thumbnail cache, and <b>Trim Caches</b> in Settings clears the cache of every app (see <b>trim caches</b> below and <a href=\"#prefs\">Settings</a>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>certificate fingerprint</b></td><td>A short code, usually the SHA-256, worked out from the certificate that an app is signed with. It identifies the key the app was signed with, and it is shown in the Signatures tab of <a href=\"#apps-menu\">the app menu</a>. See <b>signature</b> and <b>SHA-256</b> below.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>checksum</b></td><td>A short code, such as a SHA-256, that identifies the exact contents of a file. Downloads are checked with it. See <a href=\"#tab-store\">App Stores</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>chip</b></td><td>A small rounded button that turns a filter or an option on or off, or copies a piece of text. A row of them that scrolls sideways is also called a row of pills. See <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>clear data</b></td><td>Wipes everything an app has saved: logins, settings and files kept inside it. It cannot be undone. See <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>cluster</b></td><td>On the Task Manager's CPU page, a group of processor cores of similar speed, with its own lowest, current and highest speed. See <a href=\"#tab-taskmgr\">Task Manager</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Codeberg</b></td><td>A free website, like GitHub, where developers share code and app releases. The App Updater checks it for newer versions. See <a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>coding agent</b></td><td>An AI assistant you chat with inside the Terminal. It can suggest commands, read files and propose changes, and you approve each step. See <a href=\"#terminal-agents\">Coding agents</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>component</b></td><td>One working part of an app: an activity, a receiver, a service or a provider. See <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>config split</b></td><td>A part of a split app made for one processor type, one screen density or one language. See <a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split packages</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>content provider</b></td><td>The part of an app that shares data with other apps. The Components tab of an app's menu lists them as Providers, next to Receivers and Services. See <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>core</b></td><td>One processing unit of the phone's processor. The Task Manager shows a box for each. See <a href=\"#tab-taskmgr\">Task Manager</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>CPU</b></td><td>The phone's processor, the part that runs programs. The Task Manager shows how busy it is, and the About tab names its type. See <a href=\"#tab-taskmgr\">Task Manager</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>CSV</b></td><td>A plain table file that opens in any spreadsheet. The app list can be exported as one. See <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>D</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>debloat</b></td><td>To turn off or remove bloatware. See <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Debug over Bluetooth</b></td><td>A setting on a Wear OS watch, and in the Wear OS app, that lets adb reach the watch through its phone link. See <a href=\"#devices-add\">Adding a device</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Debug over Wi-Fi</b></td><td>The watch's setting that lets adb connect over Wi-Fi. It shows the watch's address and port. See <a href=\"#devices-wear\">Wear OS, start to finish</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>debug-signed</b></td><td>Signed with the common developer test key. It is a sign that an app was re-signed by a tool. See <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>density</b></td><td>Also called dpi (dots per inch): how big Android draws things. Lower draws smaller, so more fits. See <a href=\"#devices-display\">Screen density and size</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Developer options</b></td><td>Android's hidden menu with debugging switches. You unlock it by tapping Build number seven times. See <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>device owner</b></td><td>A work or school setup that controls a phone and can block removing apps. The app shows DELETE_FAILED_DEVICE_POLICY_MANAGER then. See <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>dex optimization</b></td><td>Recompiling an app's code for speed. It is what the Optimize button does. See <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>disable</b></td><td>Android's word for freeze. A disabled app cannot run. See <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Display over other apps</b></td><td>A special access that lets this app open another app's screen from the background. Optional. See <a href=\"#app-permissions\">The permissions this app asks for</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>downgrade</b></td><td>Installing an older version of an app over a newer one. Android refuses unless <b>Allow downgrade</b> is on in the APK Installer. See <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>dpi</b></td><td>Dots per inch, the way screen density is counted. See <b>density</b> above and <a href=\"#devices-display\">Screen density and size</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>drift</b></td><td>When the phone no longer matches a watched profile, usually because a system update brought apps back. See <a href=\"#apps-profiles\">Profiles and drift watch</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>dry run</b></td><td>A practice run of a batch command that only lists what it would do and runs nothing. See <a href=\"#apps-batch\">Selecting many apps and batch actions</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>E</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>exported</b></td><td>A component that other apps may open. An unexported one is normally reachable only by the app itself. See <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>F</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>factory reset</b></td><td>Erases everything on the phone and returns it to how it left the factory. It restores system apps you removed for your user. See <a href=\"#safety\">Staying safe</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>F-Droid</b></td><td>A catalog of free, open-source Android apps. The App Stores tab can browse it and many other repositories of the same kind, and the App Updater checks it. See <a href=\"#tab-store\">App Stores</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>feature module</b></td><td>An optional part of a split app. The installer does not tick it by default; if an app crashes at once after installing, try <b>Select all</b>. See <a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split packages</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>fingerprint</b></td><td>A short code that identifies a key, such as the ADB key or the key an app is signed with. See <a href=\"#tab-about\">About</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>firmware</b></td><td>The phone's own built-in system software, written by the maker. This app never changes it. See <a href=\"#safety\">Staying safe</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>flashing</b></td><td>Writing the maker's official software onto a phone from a computer. This guide does not cover it. See <a href=\"#safety\">Staying safe</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>force stop</b></td><td>Ends an app's running processes now. Nothing is deleted. See <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>foreground service</b></td><td>A background job that shows a notification, as long file jobs do. See <a href=\"#app-permissions\">The permissions this app asks for</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>freeze</b></td><td>Switch an app off without removing it. Its files and settings stay, and Enable brings it back. See <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>G</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Galaxy Store</b></td><td>Samsung's app store. The App Updater checks it for Samsung apps. See <a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>gist</b></td><td>A small page on GitHub where someone shares a note or a list. The ADB Cheat Sheet can load Pulimet's gist of ADB commands. See <a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>GitHub</b></td><td>A website where developers keep their code and publish app releases. This app's own releases, the UAD-NG list and many update sources are there. See <a href=\"#privacy\">Privacy</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>GitHub token</b></td><td>An optional code that raises GitHub's limit for update checks from 60 to 5,000 an hour. See <a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>GPU</b></td><td>The chip that draws the picture on your screen. See <a href=\"#tab-taskmgr\">Task Manager</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>H</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>hidden settings</b></td><td>Android's settings tables (Global, Secure and System) that its own Settings app does not show. See <a href=\"#tab-settings\">Hidden Settings</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>History</b></td><td>The Debloater's list of what you did, from app menus and batches, with Undo on each entry. The last 150 are kept. See <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloating safely</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>hotspot</b></td><td>A phone that shares its mobile data as a small Wi-Fi network for other devices. Wireless Debugging can use another phone's hotspot when no Wi-Fi is around. See <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>HTTPS</b></td><td>The secure way a program talks to a website, so that others on the network cannot read the traffic. Plain <code>http</code> is not secure. See <a href=\"#privacy\">Privacy</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>I</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>icon pack</b></td><td>An installed set of app icons that the app can draw in its lists. See <a href=\"#prefs\">Settings</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>install-time permission</b></td><td>A permission fixed when the app is installed. Nobody can change it, and the app shows a padlock on it. See <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>intent</b></td><td>A request one part of Android sends to another, such as \"open this screen\" or \"install this file\". See <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>IP address</b></td><td>The number that identifies a device on a network, such as 192.168.1.20. A port follows after a colon. See <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>IzzyOnDroid</b></td><td>A catalog that works like F-Droid, with many apps taken from developers' own GitHub releases. The App Updater and the App Stores tab use it. See <a href=\"#tab-store\">App Stores</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>K</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Keystore</b></td><td>Android's protected storage for keys. This app keeps its APK-signing key there, and the key that seals your agent keys. See <a href=\"#privacy\">Privacy</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Knox</b></td><td>Samsung's security system. Rooting a Samsung phone trips a counter in it. See <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Komi Store</b></td><td>A community catalog of GitHub projects that publish an installable APK. The <b>GitHub</b> button in App Stores browses it. See <a href=\"#credits\">Credits</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>L</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>launcher</b></td><td>The home screen app. Do not remove yours unless another is chosen. See <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloating safely</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>logcat</b></td><td>The phone's running diary, in which Android and every app write short notes. See <a href=\"#tab-logcat\">Logcat Viewer</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>M</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Magisk</b></td><td>A root manager, an app that decides which apps may use root. KernelSU and APatch are the others the app names. See <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>manifest</b></td><td>An app's own description of itself, in XML: its screens, permissions and parts. See <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Material 3</b></td><td>Google's current design system for Android apps. The app's own look follows it, and its baseline purple palette is called Material 3. See <a href=\"#themes\">Themes and colours</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Material You</b></td><td>Android 12's way of building all system colours from one source colour, normally your wallpaper. See <a href=\"#themes\">Themes and colours</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>MCP</b></td><td>Model Context Protocol: a standard way of giving an AI assistant extra tools, such as access to files or the web. The Terminal's <b>MCP</b> button adds them to agents that run in Termux. See <a href=\"#terminal-agents\">Coding agents</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>mDNS</b></td><td>The way devices announce themselves on a network. The Nearby scan and Auto-Detect Ports use it. See <a href=\"#devices-add\">Adding a device</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>metered connection</b></td><td>Mobile data or a hotspot, where downloads may cost money. The app asks before large ones. See <a href=\"#tab-store\">App Stores</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>model</b></td><td>In a coding agent, the particular AI model that answers you. The <b>Model</b> drop-down lists the ones the provider offers. See <a href=\"#terminal-agents\">Coding agents</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Monet</b></td><td>The name of the colour engine behind Material You. See <a href=\"#tab-overlays\">RRO/Monet Customization</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>N</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>native library (.so)</b></td><td>A file ending in .so that holds machine code made for one processor type, such as arm64-v8a or armeabi-v7a, instead of Java code. The Libraries tab of <a href=\"#apps-menu\">the app menu</a> lists the ones inside an app's file.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>notification shade</b></td><td>The panel you pull down from the top of the screen to see notifications. The code box for <b>Pair via Notification</b> is there. See <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>O</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>OBB</b></td><td>Extra data files of a big game or app, kept under Android/obb. An XAPK can carry them. See <a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split packages</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Obtainium</b></td><td>A separate open-source app that fetches updates from developers' pages. This app uses its community catalog. See <a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>OEM</b></td><td>The phone's maker (original equipment manufacturer). It is one of the list filters of the UAD-NG Debloater, and shows the apps the maker added. See <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>OpenGL ES</b></td><td>One of the two ways apps draw graphics on the GPU (the other is Vulkan). The Task Manager's GPU page shows which versions your phone supports. An app can also say which version it needs (see <b>uses-feature</b>), and the Features and Configurations tabs of <a href=\"#apps-menu\">the app menu</a> compare that with your phone. See <a href=\"#tab-taskmgr\">Task Manager</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Orion Store</b></td><td>A public catalog by RookieEnough that the <b>Orion</b> button in App Stores browses. See <a href=\"#credits\">Credits</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>overlay</b></td><td>Also called RRO (Runtime Resource Overlay): a small package that changes the look of another app or of Android. See <a href=\"#tab-overlays\">RRO/Monet Customization</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>P</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>package name</b></td><td>The unique ID of an app, such as com.google.android.youtube. See <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>package visibility</b></td><td>Android 11 and newer limit which other apps an app can see. This app asks to see all, so its list should be complete. See <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>pairing code</b></td><td>The six-digit code Android shows when you pair for Wireless Debugging. It only works while that window is open. See <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>patched app</b></td><td>An app changed or repackaged by a tool such as ReVanced. The Patched filter finds it. See <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>permission</b></td><td>What an app is allowed to do, such as use the camera. See <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>pill</b></td><td>The same as a chip: one of the small rounded filter buttons under the count boxes at the top of the Application Manager. Tap it to turn that filter on or off. See <b>chip</b> above and <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Play Protect</b></td><td>Google's scanner for apps. It can refuse or delay an install. See <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>pm</b></td><td>Android's package manager command, behind many buttons: <code>pm disable-user</code>, <code>pm uninstall</code> and others. See <a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>port</b></td><td>The number after the colon in an address. It says which service on the device you are talking to. See <a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>process</b></td><td>One running program. See <a href=\"#tab-taskmgr\">Task Manager</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>profile</b></td><td>A saved list of which apps are frozen, suspended or removed, which you can apply again later. See <a href=\"#apps-profiles\">Profiles and drift watch</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>pull</b></td><td>To copy a file or an app's APK from another device to this phone. The Connected Devices tab has <b>Pull</b> buttons; they save into <code>Download/ADB App Manager/Devices/</code>. See <a href=\"#devices-files\">The device's files</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Q</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Quick Settings</b></td><td>The panel of buttons you pull down from the top of the screen (swipe down twice to see all of it). This app adds two tiles to it. See <a href=\"#quick-tiles\">Quick Settings tiles</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>R</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>RAM</b></td><td>The phone's working memory. See <a href=\"#tab-taskmgr\">Task Manager</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>read-only mode</b></td><td>The state with no working mode: the app can look and cannot change anything. See <a href=\"#mode-readonly\">Auto and Read-Only mode</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>recovery mode</b></td><td>A small maintenance menu the phone can start instead of Android. It has the wipe options: wipe cache partition (temporary system files only) and factory reset. See <a href=\"#safety\">Staying safe</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>regex</b></td><td>A small pattern language for searching text. The search box can use it. See <a href=\"#tab-apps\">Application Manager</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>removal level</b></td><td>The UAD-NG rating of how risky it is to remove an app: Recommended, Advanced, Expert or Unsafe. See <a href=\"#debloater-safety\">Debloating safely</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Rish</b></td><td>Shizuku's remote shell. The ADB Console's Rish mode keeps it open between commands. See <a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>root</b></td><td>Full administrator access to the phone, beyond what ADB can do. The phone must already be rooted. See <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>root manager</b></td><td>The app on a rooted phone that decides which apps may use root, such as Magisk, KernelSU or APatch. See <b>Magisk</b> above and <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>runtime permission</b></td><td>A permission Android asks you about, which can be switched on and off. See <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>S</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>SAF</b></td><td>Storage Access Framework: Android's own file picker, through which you give an app one folder. See <a href=\"#files-storage\">SD cards and other storage</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>safe mode</b></td><td>Starts the phone with only the apps that came with it. This app does not run there. See <a href=\"#safety\">Staying safe</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>sandbox</b></td><td>In the Terminal, the shell that runs as this app in its private folder and cannot touch other apps. See <a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>SDK</b></td><td>Software development kit. In the APK Installer, <b>Min SDK</b> is the oldest Android an app runs on and <b>Target SDK</b> the one it was built for; both are API levels. See <b>API level</b> above and <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>SDK Platform-Tools</b></td><td>Google's small download for a computer that contains the <code>adb</code> program. You need it only for the computer steps. See <a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>SecurityException</b></td><td>Android's way of saying that an app was not allowed to do something. Nothing was changed. See <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>service</b></td><td>The part of an app that works in the background. See <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>SHA-256</b></td><td>A common kind of checksum: a long code worked out from a file's exact contents. Downloads, the VirusTotal lookup and signing fingerprints use it. See <b>checksum</b> above and <a href=\"#privacy\">Privacy</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>shared library</b></td><td>A piece of code that several apps use together instead of each carrying its own copy. Android links it into an app that asks for it. The Libraries tab of <a href=\"#apps-menu\">the app menu</a> lists the ones an app asks for and the ones linked in.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>sheet</b></td><td>A panel that slides up over the screen. App menus, Working Modes and this guide are sheets. See <a href=\"#screen-tour\">A tour of the screen</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>shell</b></td><td>A program that reads your lines and runs them. Also the name of the Android user (UID 2000) that ADB runs as. See <a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Shizuku</b></td><td>A separate free app that starts a helper with ADB-level power and lets other apps use it. See <a href=\"#mode-shizuku\">Shizuku</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>ShizuStore</b></td><td>A catalog by timschneeb of apps that work with Shizuku. The <b>ShizuStore</b> button in App Stores browses it. See <a href=\"#credits\">Credits</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>sideload</b></td><td>To install an app from a file instead of a store. An app installed that way is called sideloaded. See <a href=\"#install-and-update\">Installing and updating the app</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>signature (app signature)</b></td><td>The seal an app is signed with, made with its maker's key. Android accepts an update only if it has the same one. The Signatures tab of <a href=\"#apps-menu\">the app menu</a> shows it. See also <b>certificate fingerprint</b> above and <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>signature scheme (v1, v2, v3)</b></td><td>The way a signature is stored in the app's file: v1 is the old way (files in a META-INF folder), and v2, v3 and v3.1 are newer ones kept in a block inside the file, where v3 and later let the maker change the signing key. The Signatures tab of <a href=\"#apps-menu\">the app menu</a> shows which ones an app has.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>SIM</b></td><td>The small card that identifies you to your mobile network. The app reads only its network codes (the country and operator numbers), and sends them only in a Samsung update check. See <a href=\"#privacy\">Privacy</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>SoC</b></td><td>System on a chip: the main chip of a phone, holding the processor, graphics and more. See <a href=\"#tab-taskmgr\">Task Manager</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>split APK</b></td><td>One part of an app that comes in several files that must be installed together. See <a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split packages</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>split screen</b></td><td>Two apps shown on the screen at the same time. It keeps Android's pairing code window visible while you type the code into this app. See <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>su</b></td><td>The program on a rooted phone that gives root rights to an app. The Root card looks for it. See <a href=\"#mode-root\">Root</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>suspend</b></td><td>A lighter pause than freezing: the app stays enabled but cannot be opened, and its icon is greyed out. See <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>swap</b></td><td>Extra memory made from storage or by compression. It is shown on the Task Manager's RAM page. See <a href=\"#tab-taskmgr\">Task Manager</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>system app</b></td><td>An app that came with the phone. See <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>System UI</b></td><td>The part of Android that draws the status bar, the notification shade, the lock screen and the navigation. Never remove it. See <a href=\"#safety\">Staying safe</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>T</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>table</b></td><td>In Hidden Settings, one of the three groups of settings: Global, Secure or System. See <a href=\"#tab-settings\">Hidden Settings</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>target</b></td><td>The app, or part of Android, that an overlay changes. See <a href=\"#tab-overlays\">RRO/Monet Customization</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Termux</b></td><td>A terminal app for Android. The Terminal tab can use it as a shell. See <a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>tile</b></td><td>A button in Android's Quick Settings panel. This app adds two, Working mode and Stop apps. See <a href=\"#quick-tiles\">Quick Settings tiles</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>toast</b></td><td>A small message that slides up at the bottom of the screen and fades. See <a href=\"#screen-tour\">A tour of the screen</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>token</b></td><td>In a coding agent chat, a piece of text that the AI counts, and that the provider may bill for. The state line shows how many tokens were sent and received. A <b>GitHub token</b> is a different thing, see above. See <a href=\"#terminal-agents\">Coding agents</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>trim caches</b></td><td>The button <b>Trim Caches in All Applications</b> in the SD Maid tab. It runs <code>pm trim-caches 128G</code>, which asks Android to delete app caches until 128 GB is free. No phone has that much free space, so Android clears every cache it is allowed to. Only cache is deleted; apps, their data, logins and your files stay. See <a href=\"#prefs\">Settings</a> and <a href=\"#recipe-trim-caches\">Free up space by clearing every app's cache</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>U</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>UAD-NG</b></td><td>Universal Android Debloater Next Generation: the community list that rates how safe each pre-installed app is to remove. See <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>UID 2000</b></td><td>The user number of the shell user that ADB runs as. Root is UID 0. See <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>unauthorized</b></td><td>What adb calls a device that has not accepted this phone's key. See <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>uninstall for user</b></td><td>Removing a system app for your user only. Its file stays in the system, so it can be brought back. See <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>usage access</b></td><td>A special access that lets this app read how big each app is and when it was last used. Optional. See <a href=\"#app-permissions\">The permissions this app asks for</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>USB debugging</b></td><td>A Developer options switch that lets a computer, or ADB over TCP, connect to the phone. See <a href=\"#mode-tcp\">ADB over TCP</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>user app</b></td><td>An app you or a store installed. The app list calls these 3rd Party. See <a href=\"#basics\">Words you will see</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>uses-feature</b></td><td>A line in an app's manifest that names hardware or software the app needs or can use, such as a camera, Bluetooth Low Energy or an OpenGL ES version. It is marked required or optional. The Features tab of <a href=\"#apps-menu\">the app menu</a> lists these lines and says which ones your phone has.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>V</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>VirusTotal</b></td><td>An online service that checks files against many virus scanners. The APK Installer can use it with your own key. See <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Vulkan</b></td><td>One of the two ways apps draw graphics on the GPU (the other is OpenGL ES). The Task Manager's GPU page says whether your phone supports it. See <a href=\"#tab-taskmgr\">Task Manager</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>W</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Wear OS</b></td><td>Google's version of Android for watches. See <a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>WebView</b></td><td>The web engine that draws this app's screens. Its version is in the debug info. See <a href=\"#tab-about\">About</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>widget</b></td><td>A panel you place on the home screen. This app has one. See <a href=\"#quick-tiles\">Quick Settings tiles</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Wireless Debugging</b></td><td>Android's built-in way to let an ADB tool connect over Wi-Fi (Android 11 and newer). See <a href=\"#mode-wireless\">Wireless Debugging</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>wm</b></td><td>Android's window-manager command. <code>wm density</code> and <code>wm size</code> change the screen density and size. See <a href=\"#devices-display\">Screen density and size</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>work profile</b></td><td>A separate space for work apps. Its apps are normally not in this app's list. See <a href=\"#troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>working mode</b></td><td>The way this app gets the power to change other apps: ADB over TCP, Wireless Debugging, Shizuku, Root, or Auto. See <a href=\"#modes-overview\">Working modes</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Write Secure Settings</b></td><td>A special permission that lets this app change a few protected system settings. With a working mode the app can grant it to itself. Optional. See <a href=\"#app-permissions\">The permissions this app asks for</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>X</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>XAPK</b></td><td>A bundle: a zip file with one or more APKs, a manifest and usually game data (OBB). See <a href=\"#installer-splits\">Split packages</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>XML</b></td><td>A plain-text way of writing structured information. An app's manifest is written in it. See <b>manifest</b> above and <a href=\"#apps-menu\">The app menu</a>.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"id":"privacy","title":"Privacy and what the app contacts","group":"Safety and help","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">The short version: no account, no ads, no analytics and no server of the developer's. Everything runs on your phone, and the app goes online only for something you open or ask for. This page lists every contact and says where your data lives and how to delete it.</p>\n\n<h4>In short</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>No account and no sign-up.</b></li>\n<li><b>No ads, no analytics, no crash reporting.</b> The libraries shipped with the app (named in <a href=\"#credits\">Credits</a>) are for archives, text patterns, general helpers, Shizuku and logging.</li>\n<li><b>Nothing phones home in the background.</b> The app has no timer that contacts a server. Every contact below starts when you open a tab, press a button or send a chat message. (After a restart, the app compares the phone's build number with the last one. If you are watching a profile, it may post a reminder. It sends nothing.)</li>\n<li><b>The keys the app makes</b> (the ADB key and the APK-signing key) are created on your phone and never leave it. No key is shipped inside the app: every install makes its own.</li>\n<li><b>Android's own backup is switched off</b> for the app, so nothing of it goes to a cloud backup.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>What the app can see on your phone</h4>\n<p>With a working mode and the permissions you give, the app can read your app list, files, logs and settings. It shows them to you and does not send them anywhere by itself. What leaves the phone is in the next table, and each item needs something you did. The app list is not sent as a whole, but an update check does name each app it looks up, as the table says.</p>\n\n<h4>What the app contacts, and when</h4>\n<p>Every website below can see your internet address, as any website can.</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>When</th><th>What goes out</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>GitHub</b> (<code>raw.githubusercontent.com</code>): the UAD-NG list</td><td>You open the <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a> and the app has no list or one over a week old, or tap <b>Update List</b>.</td><td>A request for one file (about 1.6 MB comes back). Nothing about your apps.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>GitHub</b> (<code>api.github.com</code>, <code>github.com</code>): this app's own update</td><td>You open the <a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a> for the first time after the app starts, or tap <b>Check for update</b>, <b>Check for app update</b>, <b>Check for Updates</b> or <b>Update now</b>.</td><td>A request for the newest release and, if you update, the download of the APK for your processor. GitHub may pass the download on to its own file servers.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>GitHub, Codeberg, F-Droid, IzzyOnDroid, the Obtainium catalog</b> (<code>api.github.com</code>, <code>github.com</code>, <code>codeberg.org</code>, <code>f-droid.org</code>, <code>apt.izzysoft.de</code>, <code>raw.githubusercontent.com</code>): update checks for sideloaded apps</td><td>The App Updater checks other apps: on its first visit after the app starts, when you tap <b>Check for Updates</b>, and again after you set a source or import an Obtainium list.</td><td>Lookups that name each app checked: its package name, or its GitHub or Codeberg project. A saved GitHub token goes to GitHub only.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Samsung's update service</b> (<code>vas.samsungapps.com</code>)</td><td>The same check, for Samsung apps only.</td><td>Your phone model, SIM network codes, region code and Android version, with the package name and version of each Samsung app.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>App Stores catalogs</b>: ShizuStore (<code>shizustore.timschneeberger.me</code>), the GitHub catalog (<code>api.github-store.org</code>, with an offline copy on <code>raw.githubusercontent.com</code>, and <code>api.github.com</code> for a GitHub project you type in), the F-Droid repository you pick (F-Droid, IzzyOnDroid or one of about 40 others, each on its own server), Orion (<code>raw.githubusercontent.com</code>)</td><td>You open the <a href=\"#tab-store\">App Stores</a> tab (it loads ShizuStore at first, or the catalog you used last since the app started), switch catalog, refresh, search the GitHub catalog or type a GitHub project.</td><td>Catalog requests, your search words (GitHub catalog only), and pictures such as app icons and GitHub owner photos (<code>github.com</code>, <code>avatars.githubusercontent.com</code>) from those servers.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Developers' download sites</b> (GitHub, GitLab, F-Droid, Samsung's servers and others)</td><td>You press <b>Install</b> or <b>Update</b> in the App Stores or the App Updater.</td><td>A download request. For a Samsung app, your phone model, SIM network codes, region code and Android version go with it. The file is checked before it installs.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>VirusTotal</b> (<code>www.virustotal.com</code>)</td><td>Only if you paste your own key in the <a href=\"#tab-installer\">APK Installer</a> and press <b>Scan this package</b>.</td><td>The file's SHA-256 fingerprint and your key. The whole file only if you then press <b>Upload it for analysis</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>An AI provider</b> (<code>api.anthropic.com</code>, <code>api.openai.com</code>, <code>generativelanguage.googleapis.com</code>, <code>api.cursor.com</code>, <code>api.perplexity.ai</code>, <code>api.x.ai</code>, <code>api.llama.com</code>, <code>api.deepseek.com</code>, and <code>api.github.com</code> to test a GitHub token for Copilot), or your own server (Jan.ai, AnythingLLM, Ollama)</td><td>Only after you add a key or a server address in the Terminal, and then when you test it, chat, or load the provider's list of models (at most once a day). Your own server is contacted at the address you typed; Ollama on this phone stays on the phone.</td><td>Your key (to that provider only) and what <a href=\"#terminal-agents\">Coding agents</a> lists: your messages, a short description of your phone, and what the agent reads or runs, with key-like text hidden.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>GitHub's gist of ADB commands</b> (<code>gist.github.com</code>)</td><td>Only if you press <b>Load the full reference from the gist</b> in the ADB Cheat Sheet.</td><td>An ordinary request to GitHub for that page. GitHub's reply is shown inside the app.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Installers and downloads run by Termux</b> (<code>claude.ai</code>, <code>gh.io</code>, <code>cursor.com</code>, <code>opencode.ai</code>, <code>astral.sh</code>, <code>github.com</code>, and the package servers that Termux and the installed tools use)</td><td>Only if you tap an <b>Install</b> step of an agent, of DroidMind or of Ollama in the Terminal.</td><td>Termux runs the commands and does the downloading, not this app. They fetch the tool's installer and packages, whatever those then download, and for Ollama a model of about 1 GB.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Your web browser, or this phone's store app</b></td><td>You tap <b>Buy me a coffee</b> or <b>Donate</b> (PayPal), <b>GitHub</b>, <b>Releases</b>, <b>Report a bug or idea</b>, <b>Star on GitHub</b>, <b>Bingblop on GitHub</b>, the UAD-NG Wiki, <b>Get a key</b>, <b>Make a token</b>, <b>Website</b>, <b>Page</b>, <b>Source</b>, <b>Release</b>, <b>Open in Obtainium</b> (without Obtainium installed, this opens <code>apps.obtainium.imranr.dev</code>), a <b>Store</b> button, a credit link (such as the ones under the App Stores catalogs), or the F-Droid or GitHub button in the Termux setup.</td><td>The app only opens the address. Your browser then talks to that site, which sees what the address carries, such as the PayPal address and amount. A <b>Store</b> button asks the Play Store or Aurora Store on this phone to open that app's page.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The built-in addresses all use <code>https</code>. The app allows plain <code>http</code> only so that you can reach an AI server of your own, and a key sent that way is not encrypted on your network.</p>\n<p>Two things are not this app's own contacts. Commands you run in the Terminal, above all in Termux, and the tools you install there can reach the internet if the command itself does. The <b>MCP</b> button writes server entries into the settings of an agent tool in Termux, and that tool, not this app, then contacts the server you chose (the GitHub preset points at <code>api.githubcopilot.com</code>).</p>\n\n<h4>What Connected Devices sends</h4>\n<p>Nothing goes through the internet. The tab uses the app's own adb to talk to the other device over your local network (Wi-Fi), a USB cable or, for a watch, its Bluetooth link. Its scan asks your own network, not the internet, which devices are announcing wireless debugging. <b>Send with Bluetooth</b> hands files to Android's own Bluetooth sharing. Files you pull are saved on this phone, in <code>Download/ADB App Manager/Devices/&lt;device&gt;/</code> (<a href=\"#tab-devices\">Connected Devices</a>).</p>\n\n<h4>What stays on your phone, and where</h4>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Where</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Settings, filters, saved lists, profiles, the debloater history, the history of hidden-setting changes, the saved GitHub token and VirusTotal key</td><td>The app's private storage.</td><td>Other apps cannot read it without root. The token and key are not sealed: leave them empty if that worries you.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The UAD-NG list, icon cache, thumbnails (up to 48 MB), installers and update downloads</td><td>The app's private storage and cache.</td><td>Installers and updates are deleted after use. Settings has <b>Clear icon cache</b>, and the File Manager <b>Clear thumbnail cache</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The ADB key</td><td>A private folder of the app.</td><td>Made on first start.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The key that signs edited APKs</td><td>The Android Keystore.</td><td>Generated inside it, so it cannot be copied off the phone.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The keys of coding agents</td><td>Sealed with AES-256-GCM under a key held in the Android Keystore, kept in the app's private settings.</td><td>The page never gets a key back, only its start and last four characters. The app adds a key to a request itself, and only when it goes to that provider's own address, or the server address you saved with it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The terminal's home folder and, if you keep it, the last 60 chat messages</td><td>The app's private storage.</td><td>Turn off <b>Keep the conversation between launches</b>, or tap <b>Clear the conversation</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Backups, saved APKs, icons, CSV lists, manifests, saved logs and files pulled from other devices</td><td><code>Download/ADB App Manager</code> and its folders (<code>Backups</code>, <code>APKs</code>, <code>Icons</code>, <code>Devices</code>).</td><td>Ordinary files in your Downloads. A backup can hold an app's data, so treat it like that data. On Android 8 and 9, all of these except the pulled device files are in the app's own storage instead and go with the app.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>The permissions the app holds, and does not</h4>\n<p>See <a href=\"#app-permissions\">The permissions this app asks for</a> for the full table.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>It declares</b> internet, network state and Wi-Fi state, the list of all apps, usage access, display over other apps, storage and all files access, write secure settings, Install unknown apps, notifications, Bluetooth connection, vibration, keeping the phone awake during file jobs, boot completed, a foreground service for file jobs, running commands in Termux, and Shizuku's. The special ones stay off until you allow them, and you can take each back in Android's Settings.</li>\n<li><b>It does not ask for</b> the camera, the microphone, your location, contacts, calendar, messages, call log or accounts, and it is not an accessibility service or a device administrator.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The text from <b>Copy debug info</b> in About holds only the app's version and package name, your Android version, the phone model and processor, the WebView version, the working mode and the signing fingerprint. It holds no names, accounts, app lists or files.</p>\n\n<h4>How to delete everything</h4>\n<ol class=\"hg-steps\">\n<li><b>Undo what the app changed in Android first.</b> Frozen apps, removed system apps, hidden settings, overlays and themes are stored by Android, not by this app, and they stay when the app goes. Use <b>Enable</b>, <b>Restore</b>, <b>Changes</b>, <b>Revert</b> and <b>Default</b> (<a href=\"#safety\">Staying safe</a>).</li>\n<li><b>Remove secrets inside the app.</b> <b>Forget the key</b> for each coding agent, <b>Clear the conversation</b>, and empty the VirusTotal key and GitHub token boxes.</li>\n<li><b>Delete the files you saved.</b> Open <code>Download/ADB App Manager</code> in the <a href=\"#tab-files\">File Manager</a> and delete what you do not want.</li>\n<li><b>Clear the app's storage,</b> or uninstall it. In Android's Settings, Apps, ADB Application Manager Pro, Storage, tap <b>Clear storage</b>: this deletes everything in the app's private storage listed above. Uninstalling also removes its Keystore keys.</li>\n<li><b>Take back the trust you gave.</b> In Developer options tap <b>Revoke USB debugging authorizations</b>, and remove the pairing under <b>Wireless debugging</b>. Remove the app from Shizuku's and your root manager's lists, and switch off its special accesses (such as All files access and Install unknown apps) in Android's Settings.</li>\n<li><b>Outside the phone.</b> A provider keeps what you sent it, under its own rules. Delete your chats there and revoke the API keys on its site.</li>\n</ol>"},{"id":"credits","title":"Credits and licences","group":"Safety and help","html":"<p class=\"hg-lead\">ADB Application Manager Pro is made by Bingblop, and it stands on the work of many other people and projects. This page names the ones the app's own sources name. A licence is stated here only where those sources state it. If none is named, that does not mean there is none: look at the project itself.</p>\n\n<h4>The app</h4>\n<p>The developer is Bingblop. The project page, <code>github.com/Bingblop/ADB-Application-Manager</code>, holds the source code, the releases and the issue tracker; the <b>GitHub</b> button in the About tab opens it. The app does not show a licence text of its own, and the project files this guide was written from do not state one (there is no LICENSE file in the project's main folder). So this guide does not name a licence for the app's own code. To learn the terms, ask the developer through the project page.</p>\n\n<h4>The debloat list: UAD-NG</h4>\n<p>The ratings, descriptions and \"needed by\" lines in the <a href=\"#tab-debloater\">UAD-NG Debloater</a> come from <b>Universal Android Debloater Next Generation</b>, a community project of the Universal-Debloater-Alliance. The About tab and the project's README give its licence as <b>GPL-3.0</b>. The list is not bundled in the app: it is downloaded when you ask for it (<a href=\"#privacy\">Privacy</a>). The advice is that project's, and the <b>UAD-NG Wiki</b> button opens its wiki.</p>\n\n<h4>Shizuku</h4>\n<p>Shell access through Shizuku uses the <b>Shizuku API</b> by RikkaApps. The app ships the Shizuku API libraries (version 13.1.5) and two files named <code>rish</code> and <code>rish_shizuku.dex</code>, which it keeps as a fallback way to run commands through Shizuku. The Shizuku app itself is a separate project, not made by this app's developer (<a href=\"#mode-shizuku\">Shizuku</a>).</p>\n\n<h4>The Task Manager tab</h4>\n<p>The <a href=\"#tab-taskmgr\">Task Manager</a> tab is modeled on <b>RohitKushvaha01/TaskManager</b> on GitHub, as the About tab says.</p>\n\n<h4>The Morphe Patcher tab</h4>\n<p>The <a href=\"#tab-morphe\">Morphe Patcher</a> tab is built on the <b>Morphe</b> project by MorpheApp (<code>github.com/MorpheApp</code>, GPL-3.0). The engine that applies the patches is <b>morphe-patcher</b>, built into this app unchanged; the patch bundle format, patch sources, Simple and Advanced patching, the patch log and the list of patched apps follow <b>Morphe Manager</b>; the patching pipeline follows <b>morphe-cli</b>; and the patch bundles come from <b>Morphe Patches</b> and from the community sources listed by <code>morphe-patches.software</code>, each by its own authors. The APK download sources, Fast mode and the VirusTotal check of <a href=\"#morphe-helper\">Morphe Helper</a> follow <b>Helper for Morphe</b> by rushiranpise (GPL-3.0). The engine's own libraries (Kotlin, Guava, Bouncy Castle, Google's apksig and apkzlib, smali, ARSCLib) keep their own licences; they are listed in the file NOTICE of the project. This app is independent of these projects and of the APK sites it can download from.</p>\n\n<h4>The SD Maid tab</h4>\n<p>The <a href=\"#tab-sdm\">SD Maid</a> tab is a port of the four tools SystemCleaner, AppCleaner, CorpseFinder and Deduplicator of <b>SD Maid SE</b> by darken (<code>github.com/d4rken-org/sdmaid-se</code>, GPL-3.0): the filters and their defaults, the expendables databases, the CorpseFinder marker database, the Deduplicator rules and the accessibility steps for clearing caches follow the original, rewritten in Java for this app. SD Maid's icons, logo, mascot and translations are not used, and this tab is not the SD Maid app. SD Maid SE is on GitHub and on Google Play (the card in the tab has both links).</p>\n\n<p>The descriptions of Android's own settings in <a href=\"#tab-settings\">Hidden Settings</a> are taken in part from the documentation in the source code of the <b>Android Open Source Project</b> (<code>source.android.com</code>, Apache License 2.0; the file is <code>Settings.java</code>). The text of the settings people meet most often was written for this app.</p>\n\n<h4>Update sources and catalogs</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Galaxy Store</b>, <b>GitHub</b>, <b>Codeberg</b>, <b>F-Droid</b> and <b>IzzyOnDroid</b>: where the <a href=\"#tab-updates\">App Updater</a> looks for newer versions.</li>\n<li><b>Obtainium</b> by ImranR98, whose community catalog says where many apps' releases live, and whose export file the app can import.</li>\n<li><b>ShizuStore</b> by timschneeb, which indexes the awesome-shizuku list. The app reads its public catalog and installs from each app's own source.</li>\n<li><b>Komi Store</b> (komi-store/komi-store), whose catalog of GitHub projects with an installable APK is what the <b>GitHub</b> catalog in <a href=\"#tab-store\">App Stores</a> browses.</li>\n<li><b>Orion Store</b> by RookieEnough, whose public catalog the <b>Orion</b> button in App Stores browses.</li>\n<li><b>Pulimet's ADB gist</b>, from which the ADB Cheat Sheet is compiled and which it can load live.</li>\n<li><b>Termux</b>, whose documented <code>RUN_COMMAND</code> feature the Terminal's Termux shell uses (<a href=\"#tab-terminal\">Command-Line Interface</a>), and <b>Aurora Store</b>, which the App Updater offers to open for Play Store apps.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>The 32-bit adb</h4>\n<p>The 32-bit and universal APKs carry the <code>adb</code> client of Termux's <b>android-tools</b> 37.0.0-2 for <code>arm</code> (from packages.termux.dev), with the 56 shared libraries it needs, taken from the same repository: protobuf 35.1, abseil 20260526.0, libc++ 30, brotli 1.2.0, fmt 11.2.0, lz4 1.10.0, zstd 1.5.7 and zlib 1.3.2. The notes kept with them in the project's source give these licences:</p>\n<table class=\"hg-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Licence stated</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>android-tools, abseil</td><td>Apache-2.0</td></tr>\n<tr><td>protobuf</td><td>BSD-3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>libc++</td><td>Apache-2.0 with the LLVM exception</td></tr>\n<tr><td>brotli, fmt</td><td>MIT</td></tr>\n<tr><td>lz4, zstd</td><td>BSD</td></tr>\n<tr><td>zlib</td><td>zlib (the licence is named after the library)</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The 64-bit APK carries its own statically linked arm64 build of adb (the adb program, built so that it needs no other files). The project's files do not record where that build comes from or which licence it has, so this guide cannot say.</p>\n\n<h4>Libraries inside the app</h4>\n<p>The libraries the app is built with are <code>commons-compress</code> 1.27.1, <code>commons-io</code> 2.16.1, <code>commons-lang3</code> 3.16.0, <code>aircompressor</code> 0.27, <code>xz</code> 1.10, <code>junrar</code> 7.5.5, <code>re2j</code> 1.8, <code>slf4j-api</code> 1.7.36, and the four Shizuku libraries above (<code>shizuku-api</code>, <code>shizuku-aidl</code>, <code>shizuku-provider</code> and <code>shizuku-shared</code>, all 13.1.5). The project's notes name them Apache Commons Compress, Commons IO and Commons Lang3, Tukaani XZ, Aircompressor, junrar, RE2J and the SLF4J API. Commons Compress and its helpers (Commons IO, Commons Lang3, XZ and the SLF4J logging interface) handle 7z and tar archives, junrar reads rar archives, Aircompressor handles zstd, and RE2J matches text patterns that come from catalog data, in a safe way. The app's own files do not repeat their licences. Each project states its own.</p>\n\n<h4>Techniques borrowed</h4>\n<p>The code notes that its fallback for removing a stubborn system app uses the same technique as the App Manager and Canta apps. It also notes that launching a hidden screen is how Activity Manager does it over Shizuku, a technique that dedicated activity launchers use.</p>\n\n<h4>Design</h4>\n<p>The app's looks follow Google's Material 3 and Material You (the colours Android builds from your wallpaper), with Material 3 Expressive motion (<a href=\"#themes\">Themes and colours</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"hg-note\"><b>Tip.</b> If you think a project is missing here, or is credited wrongly, tell the developer: the About tab has a <b>Report a bug or idea</b> button.</div>"}]};
